    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns1:description "Wu Hongliang is a politician of the People's Republic of China. He is currently the president of the Beijing Fine Art Academy (Beijing huayuan 北京画院), the director of the Art Museum of the Beijing Fine Art Academy (Beijing huayuan meishuguan 北京画院美术馆), and the director of the Memorial Hall of Qi Baishi's Former Residence (Beijing Huayuan Qi Baishi jinianguan 北京画院齐白石纪念馆). "@en ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q117747155> ;
    ns1:isCuratorOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1001> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1010> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Ink Bamboo (墨竹)"@en,
        "墨竹"@zh,
        "Mò zhú"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q710049> ;
    ns1:description "The poem “Ink Bamboo” (Mo zhu 墨竹) was written by Jin Nong 金农 (1687–1763), a famous painter and calligrapher of the early Qing dynasty (1644-1911).  The poet explicitly referred to bamboo in the first line of the poem. In his paintings, Jin Nong generally used traditional images full of symbolism, such as the 'Four Gentlemen' (Si junzi 四君子), i.e., orchids, bamboo, chrysanthemums, and plums. He preserved his independence by selling works in an open market, rather than relying on an individual patron. His calligraphy was also highly regarded, and his distinctive syle, the \"lacquer calligraphy\" (Qi shu 漆书), is very well-known. "@en ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """"On fine paper from the Five Dynasties, within the library, open the window to paint bamboo, where clouds gently unravel.\r
Do not lightly compare to reeds and rushes; this is Yang Fengzi's bold calligraphy."""@en,
        """五代隃糜内库纸，开轩画竹云舒舒。\r
莫将蒲苇轻相比，此是杨风子草书。"""@zh,
        """\r
Wǔdài shù mí nèi kù zhǐ, kāi xuān huà zhú yún shū shū.\r
Mò jiāng pú wěi qīng xiāng bǐ, cǐ shì yángfēngzǐ cǎoshū."""@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1012> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Painting Bamboo (画竹)"@en,
        "画竹"@zh,
        "Huà zhú"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q890211> ;
    ns1:description "The poem “Painting Bamboo” (Hua zhu 画竹) was written by the polyhedric artist Xu Wenzhang (徐文长, 1521 – 1593), also known as Xu Wei 徐渭,  of the late Ming dynasty (1368-1644). This poem explicitly refers to Wen Yuke (文与可), the courtesy name of Wen Tong (文同), a celebrated painter of bamboo from the Song dynasty. Wen Tong is often praised as a master for his ability to “transmit the spirit” of bamboo through the movement and vitality of the brushstroke. The poem reflects on the artistic style of painting  that \"does not seek resemblance in form but rather captures the charm of life\" (bu qiu xingsi, qiu shengyun 不求形似求生韵). Xu Wei sought to strip away heaviness and complexity in painting, aiming to capture the spirit through simplified forms. His approach emphasized expressive vitality over meticulous likeness, embodying a philosophy where essence took precedence over appearance, emphasizing an expressive approach that transcends mere likeness."@en ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """All things are precious for the shadows they cast; bamboo is even more so when painted in this way.\r
In dense shade no bird can pass; emerald waves rise naturally to meet the sky.\r
With sharp, crisp sounds the stones resound; in the misty distance, there is another realm of smoke.\r
One only needs Wen Yuke, brush in hand, to capture the spirit and pass it on."""@en,
        "万物贵取影，画竹更宜然，浓阴不通鸟，碧浪自翻天，戛戛俱鸣石，迷迷别有烟，只需文与可，把笔取神传。"@zh,
        "萬物貴取影，畫竹更宜然，濃陰不通鳥，碧浪自翻天，戛戛俱鳴石，迷迷別有煙，只需文與可，把筆取神傳。"@zh,
        "Wànwù guì qǔ yǐng, huà zhú gèng yí rán, nóng yīn bùtōng niǎo, bì làng zì fāntiān, jiájiá jù míng shí, mí mí bié yǒu yān, zhǐ xū wén yǔ kě, bǎ bǐ qǔ shén chuán."@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1013> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Ink Bamboo for a Friend (画竹寄友人)"@en,
        "画竹寄友人"@zh,
        "Huà zhú jì yǒurén"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q731030> ;
    ns1:description "The poem \"Ink Bamboo for a Friend\" (Hua zhu ji youren 画竹寄友人) was written by the famous painter and calligrapher Ni Zan (1301 – 1374) of the Yuan dynasty (1271-1368). Along with Huang Gongwang (黄公望, 1269–1354), Wu Zhen (吴镇, 1280–1354), and Wang Meng (王蒙, 1308–1385), Ni Zan was considered one of the \"Four Masters of the Yuan Dynasty\" (Yuan si jia 元四家). During the collapse of the Yuan dynasty,  he traveled extensively through southern China, devoting himself to painting. This poem reflects Ni Zan’s personal and artistic philosophy, where bamboo serves as a metaphor for moral integrity and spiritual resilience. Through these verses, Ni Zan not only offers a gift to his friend but also conveys his own inner cultivation, aligning artistic expression with ideals of simplicity and detachment."@en ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """In early spring, peach and plum vie in splendor;\r
By summer’s height, willows and rushes fade in lament.\r
I thank you, friend, for seeking stillness;\r
Planting bamboo, what better to guard your heart?\r
\r
Tall and straight, its mind remains pure and tranquil;\r
Dense and verdant, even after frost and snow have passed.\r
Composing these verses, I send my distant thoughts;\r
This noble companion—bamboo—truly endures through time."""@en,
        """先春竞桃李，凌阳叹蒲柳。 \r
谢君静者徒，种竹安所守。 \r
亭亭清净心，郁郁霜雪后。 \r
赋诗寄远怀，此君真可久。"""@zh,
        """先春競桃李，凌陽嘆蒲柳。\r
謝君靜者徒，種竹安所守。\r
亭亭清淨心，鬱鬱霜雪後。\r
賦詩寄遠懷，此君真可久。"""@zh,
        """Xiān chūn jìng táolǐ, líng yáng tàn púliǔ. \r
Xièjūnjìng zhě tú, zhǒng zhú'ān suǒ shǒu. \r
Tíngtíng qīngjìng xīn, yùyù shuāng xuě hòu. \r
Fù shī jì yuǎn huái, cǐjūn zhēn kě jiǔ."""@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1014> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Spontaneous Verses at a Mountain Retreat in Late Spring (春晚山庄率题) "@en,
        "春晚山庄率题"@zh,
        "Chūn wǎn shān zhuāng lǜ tí"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q461800> ;
    ns1:description "The poem \"Spontaneous Verses at a Mountain Retreat in Late Spring\" (Chun wan shanzhuang lü ti 春晚山庄率题) by Lu Zhaolin 卢照邻 (ca. 634 – ca. 684 or 686) consists of two quatrains. Lu Zhaolin, a famous poet of the early Tang dynasty (618-907),  was celebrated as one of the \"Four Paragons of the Early Tang\" (Chu Tang si jie 初唐四杰), along with Wang Bo 王勃, Yang Jiong 杨炯, and Luo Binwang 骆宾王.  In the poem, Lu Zhaolin vividly depicts the natural scenery of a mountain villa in late spring. In the third line (竹懒偏宜水，花狂不待风), he explicitly mentions bamboo, highlighting its natural inclination towards water: \"Bamboo, at leisure, thrives by the water; flowers, in wild exuberance, do not wait for the wind.\" Lu Zhaolin emphasizes the harmony between bamboo and water, and the unrestrained exuberance of the flowers, which bloom without the prompting of the wind. This imagery captures the pleasant essence of the late spring landscape."@en ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """No. 1\r
Leisurely I stroll in late spring, gazing across fields where all horizons open.\r
Floating silk threads drift across the trees; playful butterflies weave chaotically among the bushes.\r
Bamboo, at ease, prefers the water; flowers, in wild abandon, bloom without waiting for the wind.\r
Only the lingering mood of poetry and wine seems enough to fulfill a lifetime’s longing.\r
\r
No. 2\r
In the countryside, no neighbors in sight; alone I sit, embraced by a garden in spring.\r
Orioles sing, not confined to any chosen tree; fish frolic, unafraid of the angler’s line.\r
Amid mountains and waters, music is played to its end; wind and flowers inspire frequent toasts of wine.\r
These years are already full of joy; such deep delight makes me wish to linger on.\r
"""@en,
        """(1)\r
顧步三春晚，田園四望通。\r
遊絲橫惹樹，戲蝶亂依叢。\r
竹懶偏宜水，花狂不待風。\r
唯餘詩酒意，當了一生中。\r
\r
(2)\r
田家無四鄰，獨坐一園春。\r
鶯啼非選樹，魚戲不驚綸。\r
山水彈琴盡，風花酌酒頻。\r
年華已可樂，高興復留人。"""@zh,
        """(1)\r
顾步三春晚，田园四望通。\r
游丝横惹树，戏蝶乱依丛。\r
竹懒偏宜水，花狂不待风。\r
唯馀诗酒意，当了一生中。\r
\r
(2)\r
田家无四邻，独坐一园春。\r
莺啼非选树，鱼戏不惊纶。\r
山水弹琴尽，风花酌酒频。\r
年华已可乐，高兴复留人。\r
"""@zh,
        """(1)\r
Gù bù sān chūnwǎn, tiányuán sì wàng tōng.\r
Yóusī héng rě shù, xì dié luàn yī cóng.\r
Zhú lǎn piān yí shuǐ, huā kuáng bùdài fēng.\r
Wéi yú shī jiǔyì, dāngle yīshēng zhōng.\r
\r
(2)\r
Tiánjiā wú sìlín, dú zuò yī yuán chūn.\r
Yīng tí fēi xuǎn shù, yú xì bù jīng lún.\r
Shānshuǐ tánqín jǐn, fēng huā zhuó jiǔ pín.\r
Niánhuá yǐ kě lè, gāoxìng fù liú rén."""@zh-Latn .

