        "Yǔ zhú tú"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q461800> ;
    ns1:description "The poem \"Bamboo in the Rain\" was written by the calligrapher and painter Huang Yi 黄易（1744-1802）on his painting \"Bamboo in the Rain\" (Yu zhu tu 雨竹图). Huang Yi, originally from Qiantang 钱塘, lived in Shaoxing 绍兴 for a long time. He was one of the \"Eight Schools of Xiling (Xiling ba jia 西泠八家)\". Together with Xi Gang 奚冈 and Wu Lu 吴履, he was known as one of  the \"Three Wonders of Western Zhejiang (Zhe xi san miao 浙西三妙)\", and together with Ding Jing 丁敬, Jiang Ren 蒋仁 and Xi Gang 奚冈, he was also known as one of the \"Four Famous Masters of Hangzhou Commandery  (Hang jun si jia 杭郡四家)\". In this poem, Huang Yi reflects on the tranquil and resilient qualities of bamboo under the cleansing rain, using it as a metaphor for purity of spirit and steadfastness. "@en ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """Rain washes slender bamboo, leaving it vividly green;\r
Rustling softly, as if near a riverside pavilion.\r
In this world, all things seem without feeling,\r
Only autumn’s voice is the sweetest to hear."""@en,
        """雨洗修篁分外青，萧萧如在过溪亭。\r
世间都是无情物，只有秋声最好听。\r
"""@zh,
        """雨洗修篁分外青，蕭蕭如在過溪亭。\r
世間都是無情物，只有秋聲最好聽。"""@zh,
        """Yǔ xǐ xiū huáng fèn wài qīng, xiāoxiāo rú zàiguò xī tíng.\r
Shìjiān dōu shì wúqíng wù, zhǐyǒu qiūshēng zuì hǎo tīng."""@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1023> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "At Yuqian, Monk’s Green Bamboo Pavilion ( 於潜僧绿筠轩)"@en,
        " 於潜僧绿筠轩"@zh,
        "Yú qián sēng lǜ yún xuān"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q36020> ;
    ns1:description """"At Yuqian, Monk’s Green Bamboo Pavilion" (Yu qian seng lv yun xuan 於潜僧绿筠轩) is a poem by Su Shi 苏轼 (1037—1101), a famous poet, calligrapher, painter, and statesman of the Northern Song dynasty (960–1127). He is also known by his literary name Su Dongpo (苏东坡), and was a central figure of the Song literati culture, celebrated for his talents and philosophical depth.\r
"At Yuqian, Monk’s Green Bamboo Pavilion" was composed during one of Su Shi’s travels and reflects his deep appreciation for bamboo as a symbol of resilience, elegance, and the cultivated spirit of the gentleman (junzi 君子). The “Green Bamboo Pavilion” (Lü Yun Xuan 绿筠轩) evokes a tranquil retreat where natural beauty and moral cultivation converge."""@en ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q18886189> ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """One may live without meat, but not without bamboo.\r
No meat makes one thin; no bamboo makes one coarse.\r
The thin may grow fat again, but the vulgar cannot be cured.\r
Others laugh at these words, they seem lofty, or perhaps mad.\r
If one still gobbles greedily before the bamboo,\r
how could there be any Yangzhou crane in this world?"""@en,
        """可使食无肉，不可居无竹。\r
无肉令人瘦，无竹令人俗。\r
人瘦尚可肥，俗士不可医。\r
旁人笑此言，似高还似痴。\r
若对此君仍大嚼，世间哪有扬州鹤。"""@zh,
        """可使食無肉，不可居無竹。\r
無肉令人瘦，無竹令人俗。\r
人瘦尚可肥，俗士不可醫。\r
旁人笑此言，似高還似痴。\r
若對此君仍大嚼，世間哪有揚州鶴。"""@zh,
        """Kě shǐ shí wú ròu, bùkě jū wú zhú.\r
Wú ròu lìng rén shòu, wú zhú lìng rén sú.\r
Rén shòu shàng kě féi, sú shì bùkě yī.\r
Pángrén xiào cǐ yán, shì gāo hái shì chī.\r
Ruò duì cǐjūn réng dà jué, shìjiān nǎ yǒu yángzhōu hè."""@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1024> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Bamboo and Rocks (竹石)"@en,
        "竹石"@zh,
        "Zhú shí"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1480 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5727902> ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q712007> ;
    ns2:P571 "1740"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """Bamboo and Rocks is a seven-character quatrain (qiyan jueju 七言绝句) composed by Zheng Xie 郑燮  (1693—1765), also known by his literary name Zheng Banqiao 郑板桥, a renowned calligrapher and painter of the Qing dynasty (1644-1911). Originally from Xinghua 兴化 (in present-day Jiangsu), he served as District Magistrate in Shandong. Renowned for his integrity and sense of justice, he was forced to resign after opposing high-ranking officials on matters of disaster relief. In his later years, he settled in Yangzhou 扬州, where he earned his living through painting and calligraphy. He became one of the renowned “Eight Eccentrics of Yangzhou” (Yangzhou baguai 扬州八怪), a group noted for their unorthodox styles and independent spirit.\r
This poem, composed as an inscription for a painting (ti hua shi 题画诗), celebrates the resilience of bamboo rooted in fractured rocks. It vividly evokes bamboo's tenacious vitality and resolute character, making it a symbol of steadfastness and moral strength in the face of adversity."""@en ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """Firmly gripping the green mountain without letting go, \r
Its roots are originally set in fractured rocks.\r
 Enduring countless hardships and strikes, it remains strong, \r
Unaffected by winds from all directions. \r
"""@en,
        """咬定青山不放松，立根原在破岩中。\r
千磨万击还坚劲，任尔东西南北风。"""@zh,
        """咬定青山不放鬆，立根原在破巖中。\r
千磨萬擊還堅勁，任爾東西南北風。"""@zh,
        """Yǎodìng qīngshān bù fàngsōng, lìgēn yuán zài pò yán zhōng.\r
Qiān mó wàn jī hái jiān jìn, rèn ěr dōngxī nánběi fēng."""@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1026> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P144 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1027>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1111> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q131395>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q165939>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q282>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q405>,
