    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Love Writing 98-18 (signs)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU consists of the graphic signs  which form the visual structure of the oil painting in "Love Writing 98-18" (1998). Although these signs may initially resemble traditional Chinese calligraphy, but they are actually abstract and stylized forms inspired by ancient scripts, particularly the ancient bronze script (jinwen 金文). Simplified and expressive, they bridge the gap between historical writing systems and contemporary visual language. Luo Qi employs oil painting techniques within a calligraphic framework. The characters are no longer legible texts but transformed into pictographic elements, giving the composition a symbolic and rhythmic structure. The use of vibrant primary colors intensifies the visual impact and helps unify the graphic signs within a cohesive abstract field.\r
The “writing” that Luo Qi creates is not meant to be read through the lens of linguistic logic but rather to be felt. It is a form of emotional, non-verbal communication, representing a visual language rooted in emotion, intuition, and artistic vitality. This concept of ‘Love Writing’ (情书 qíngshū) or "poetic writing" is underscored by a recurring motif across the series: the stylized heart, which is often drawn in a stylized, symbolic manner and highlighted in bright fuchsia. These hearts clearly reflect the first character of the series’ title, 情 (qíng, "love/emotion"), and offer a key to interpreting this series. Luo Qi’s calligraphy becomes a language of love, one that bypasses rationality and speaks directly to the viewer’s sensibility.""" ;
