    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1451.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Tian Xiang (节气)" ;
    ns1:description """The 24 stones of Tian Xiang are engraved with the Chinese twenty-four solar terms (jieqi 节气), which divide the solar year into 24 two-week periods, such as jingzhe 惊蛰, or “insects awaken”, marking the first stirring of hibernating insects. Each stone reflects a unique aspect of the infinite universe, crafted from massive natural rocks sourced from the depths of the boundless sea near Shandong province, historically known as Lu 鲁, the birthplace of Confucianism. For this reason, the rocks are referred to as “ru rocks” (ru shi 儒石, meaning Rocks of Confucianism). The calligraphy wraps around each side of the ru rocks, revealing only one of ar few strokes from any given angle. It is only through observing the entire installation that viewers realise each stone represents a distinct solar term, symbolising the vastness of the universe. “The characters carved into the ru rocks are smooth on the surface but firm in essence, embodying timeless Oriental elegance, in a pursue of a perfect balance of stiffness and fluidity” (Gu 2018, 5). The uncoloured strokes are delicately carved into the rocks’ natural texture with gentle transitions, reflecting the artist’s intention to integrate Chinese views of the universe and daily life into these naturally shaped rocks.\r
The Chinese twenty-four solar terms (jieqi 节气), which divide the solar year into 24 two-week periods, derives from the traditional Chinese calendar, also known as 'agricultural calendar' (nongli 农历) or 'lunar calendar' (yinli 阴历). It is a lunisolar calendar, combining the solar, lunar, and other cycles for various social and agricultural purposes. It was firstly introduced during the Taichu calendar of the Han Dynasty, which paired the solar terms with the 12 combined periods known as climate terms.""" ;
