<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1527> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P144 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1529> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1454272>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q146095> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3257809>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q429220> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/ugly_calligraphy> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1527.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Violet (紫罗兰)" ;
    ns1:description """This Calli-Writing Unit is composed of the three Chinese characters “Ziluolan 紫羅蘭” (Violet). Unlike his typically spontaneous color applications, this piece exhibits a calculated and thoughtful use of violet, a color symbolic of both nature and the tragic love story of Chinese writer Zhou Shoujuan. The choice of violet not only draws on the visual inspiration of the flower but also reflects a deeper emotional resonance, underscoring themes of loss and beauty in decline.\r
Set against the backdrop of a demolition site, the work introduces a striking contrast between the vibrant violet hue and the desolate, decaying urban environment. This juxtaposition amplifies Dohak625’s critique of urban spaces and their disconnection from nature, a key aspect of graffiti culture. By placing this natural symbol in a site of destruction, the artist critiques the erasure of nature in modern cities, while simultaneously attempting to reclaim it. \r
The "Violet" characters, with their jagged, irregular forms, align with Dohak625's anti-style aesthetic and the “ugly calligraphy” (choushu 丑书), suggesting organic, untamed growth amidst the urban decay.\r
A particularly powerful element in the artwork is the central stroke of the character "lan 蘭 (orchid)," which extends beyond the surface and toward the ground, symbolically connecting the piece to the earth. This stroke, reminiscent of a plant’s root reaching into the soil, suggests a re-establishment of nature's presence in the city. The characters’ vertical lines, grounding them to the earth, create a visual link between the artwork and the natural world, while the upper strokes form a shelter-like structure, symbolizing protection. This fusion of the organic and urban suggests not only the persistence of nature but its capacity to reclaim space within the industrialized city.\r
Through this CWU, Dohak625 moves beyond personal artistic expression, engaging in a broader conversation about the role of nature in urban spaces. "Violet (Ziluolan 紫羅蘭)" becomes both a commentary on the degradation of the environment and an act of reclamation, bringing nature into spaces from which it has been erased. The work transcends mere aesthetic concerns, positioning itself as a statement on the ongoing tension between urban development and the natural world."""@en ;
