<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1529> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Violet 紫罗兰"@en,
        "紫罗兰"@zh,
        "Zǐluólán"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q10920885> ;
    ns2:P571 "1925-12"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    ns1:description """"Violet" was a popular urban fashion and mass-market literary magazine, widely circulated in the 1920s and 1930s. First published in December 1925, it ceased publication in June 1930, after releasing a total of four volumes and 96 issues. The magazine carried forward the tradition of late Qing and early Republican-era popular literature, while also being deeply rooted in the modernizing urban environment of Shanghai during the 1920s and 1930s, a period of rapid social and cultural transformation. Throughout its publication run, Violet maintained a broad readership, reflecting the rising consumer culture and the increasing demand for urban leisure and lifestyle content among Shanghai’s middle and upper classes.\r
The cover title of Violet was inscribed by the famous Chinese opera performer Mei Lanfang, and the magazine was founded by the writer Zhou Shoujuan. Zhou, a central figure in early 20th-century popular literature, had a personal attachment to the name "Violet" due to a youthful romantic experience. While in middle school, Zhou attended a school performance and was immediately captivated by a female student named Zhou Yinping, who was both graceful and lively. The two exchanged love letters, pledging undying devotion to one another, with Zhou vowing not to marry anyone else. However, Yinping’s wealthy family disapproved of Zhou’s modest background and forced her into another marriage, leaving Zhou heartbroken. Yinping’s English name was Violet, and after this, Zhou developed a lifelong obsession with the flower and its symbolism. He kept violets on his desk, wrote with violet-colored ink, and titled many of his works after the flower, including the magazine he edited, Violet."""@en .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1532> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P144 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1536> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q177749>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q183670> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q429220> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1532.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU The heavenly wind blows upon me (天风吹我)" ;
    ns1:description """This Calli-Writing Unit features the phrase “Tian feng chui wo 天风吹我” ("The Heavenly Wind Blows Upon Me"), in non-simplified characters, derived from Gong Zizhen’s poem “Xiang Yue · Sky Wind Blows Me (《湘月·天风吹我》)” from the Qing Dynasty (1792–1841). Dohak625 created this artwork using pink chalk on the ground as a tribute to Gong Zizhen's self-assurance and artistic legacy.\r
According to Dohak625 (interview with M. R. Bisceglia 2024), the nuances of Chinese poetry can be difficult for non-native speakers to grasp. While the phrase might simply translate to “I was here” in English, it conveys a deeper significance, evoking the image of a deity descending from the heavens, declaring, “Here I am.” Although the artist does not claim a divine status, they admire Gong Zizhen's boldness, particularly this line, which emphasizes the poet's extraordinary confidence.\r
Stylistically, he mixed his personal jagged/anti-style with the contemporary trend in Chinese calligraphy known as “ugly calligraphy” (choushu 丑书), which is characterized by disharmony, asymmetry, and inconsistency, while, the use of pink chalk carries both symbolic and practical meanings. \r
Pink, associated with softness and compassion, contrasts with the poem's themes of divine presence and grandeur, highlighting the tension between humility and boldness. This color also symbolizes life and creativity, aligning with Gong Zizhen’s challenge to traditional modesty.\r
Chalk, being temporary and easily erased, emphasizes the fleeting nature of the artwork, reflecting the transient essence of human existence—central themes in classical Chinese poetry. The medium's vulnerability to the elements suggests the impermanence of art and life. Dohak625’s choice of chalk evokes associations with childhood and playfulness, transforming a simple material into something profound. Utilizing the ground as a canvas invites public interaction and engagement.\r
The delicate quality of pink chalk juxtaposed with the weighty significance of Gong Zizhen’s poetry creates an interplay between contemporary and historical contexts, bridging the ephemeral with the eternal. Ultimately, the use of pink chalk serves as a personal interpretive tool for Dohak625, emphasizing the fleeting nature of art while resonating across time."""@en ;
