<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1536> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "The Wind Blows Me (湘月·天风吹我)"@en,
        "Xiang Yue: The Wind Blows Me"@en,
        "湘月·天风吹我"@zh,
        "Xiāng yuè·tiān fēng chuī wǒ"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1041328> ;
    ns2:P571 "1832"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """"Xiang yue: tian feng chui wo 湘月·天风吹我" is a poem written by Gong Zizhen (1792–1841), a prominent Chinese poet and thinker from the late Qing dynasty. In this poem, Gong reflects on his return to West Lake after a decade-long absence, and his feelings of nostalgia, unfulfilled ambitions, and emotional solitude.\r
The first stanza reflects the poet’s personal aspirations, recalling his youthful years, during which his ambitions remained unfulfilled and his career achievements fell short. The second stanza contrasts the scenery with his feelings, as he revisits familiar landscapes like the lake and mountains. This triggers a deep sense of melancholy as the unchanged surroundings intensify his emotional struggles. The poem seamlessly blends narrative, philosophical reflection, emotion, and nature, all conveying a mood of desolation and grandeur. Gong Zizhen's writing style is light and elegant, making this work a masterpiece of his poetic collection.\r
The poem's themes include regret, lost time, and the sorrow of unfulfilled dreams, beautifully expressed through Gong's vivid and ethereal writing.\r
The poem begins with a powerful image of the wind blowing the speaker onto a corner of West Lake's scenic hills. The sight evokes memories of youth, which are now distant and filled with regrets. Gong contrasts his past youthful ambitions with the modest reality of his life, where achievements such as fame and literary accomplishments feel hollow.\r
As he gazes at the setting sun and fragrant grasses by the lake, his melancholy deepens. The symbolic imagery of "blowing the flute" and "talking about the sword" in the latter part of the poem represents two sides of the poet's nature: artistic elegance and heroic vigor. These elements evoke his inner conflict between pursuing beauty and grace versus bold action.\r
The poem captures both personal sorrow and a broader reflection on life's transience, blending emotional depth with vivid imagery."""@en ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q30006487> ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """Xiang Yue · The Wind Blows Me\r
In the summer of Renshen, I sailed West Lake, writing to express my long-held feelings, after ten years away from Hangzhou.\r
The wind blows me, casting me into a corner of the lake and hills, and indeed, it is beautiful. Once, I was but a young guest in Donghua, now, looking back, the vastness is endless. Is it my life’s work to chase empty fame, to butcher dogs and carve words into dragons? My old friends, Su Xiao, would surely laugh at my folly.\r
I catch a glimpse of the setting sun, half a bank of fragrant grass, and suddenly, sorrow rises within. Where can I now hear the soft steps of silk shoes? My heart drifts, lonely and untethered. A mournful flute, a mad talk of swords—two tastes that stir the soul. Two fleeting spring dreams, and the sound of oars fades into the clouds and waters.\r
(Translator: Marta R. Bisceglia)"""@en,
        """湘月·天风吹我\r
壬申夏泛舟西湖，述怀有赋，时予别杭州盖十年矣\r
天风吹我，堕湖山一角，果然清丽。曾是东华生小客，回首苍茫无际。屠狗功名，雕龙文卷，岂是平生意？乡亲苏小，定应笑我非计。\r
才见一抹斜阳，半堤香草，顿惹清愁起。罗袜音尘何处觅，渺渺予怀孤寄。怨去吹箫，狂来说剑，两样销魂味。两般春梦，橹声荡入云水。"""@zh,
        """Xiāng yuè·tiān fēng chuī wǒ \r
rén shēn xià fànzhōu xīhú, shùhuái yǒu fù, shí yǔ bié hángzhōu gài shí nián yǐ \r
tiān fēng chuī wǒ, duò húshān yījiǎo, guǒrán qīnglì. Céng shì dōng huá shēng xiǎo kè, huíshǒu cāngmáng wújì. Tú gǒu gōngmíng, diāo lóng wén juǎn, qǐ shì píng shēngyì? Xiāngqīn sū xiǎo, dìng yīng xiào wǒ fēi jì. \r
Cái jiàn yīmǒ xiéyáng, bàn dī xiāngcǎo, dùn rě qīng chóu qǐ. Luō wà yīn chén hé chù mì, miǎomiǎo yǔ huái gū jì. Yuàn qù chuī xiāo, kuáng lái shuō jiàn, liǎngyàng xiāohún wèi. Liǎng bān chūnmèng, lǔ shēng dàng rù yún shuǐ."""@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1540> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q104482419>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q190273>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4105337>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q468777>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7798302> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1074584>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3133> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/ugly_calligraphy> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1540.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Just live (活着就行)" ;
    ns1:description """This Calli-Writing Unit consists of four characters “Huo zhe jiu xing 活着就行,” meaning "Just live" or "To breathe is to thrive." The phrase carries a philosophical tone, suggesting that mere existence is a form of thriving. It emphasizes the intrinsic value of life, highlighting resilience and survival, and implies that, despite challenges, the key is to keep going.\r
Stylistically, this CWU adheres to Dohak's jagged/anti-style and the contemporary trend in Chinese calligraphy known as "ugly calligraphy" (choushu 丑书), characterized by disharmony, asymmetry, and inconsistency.\r
The characters are painted in green with a black outline and each of them occupies a single shutter of a shop. Because of their big dimension, they evoke a big character calligraphy (dazi shufa). The use of green and the jagged strokes reminiscent of leaves may serve as a “green” message, providing a splash of color against the otherwise gray and dreary shutters of the metropolis. Each character resembles a plant, contrasting not only with the gray of the shutter in the background but also with the squared characters of the shop signs. This highlights an interesting contrast between art and the urban environment, utilizing a style that not only captures attention but also conveys an ecological message. Moreover, the contrast between the fluid, organic characters and the squared shop signs underscores a tension between the rigidity of commerce and the naturalness of life. This type of artistic intervention may also stimulate reflection on sustainability and the importance of preserving green spaces in cities.\r
Another important aspect of this CWU is that each individual character is perfectly and harmoniously contained within a square, akin to the practice of writing within the hanzigezi 汉字格子 grid. The hanzigezi is a grid composed of intersecting horizontal and vertical lines that create squares, traditionally utilized for practicing Chinese writing and calligraphy.\r
The artist enjoys creating graffiti on roll-up doors for two main reasons: i) First, these doors are only visible after the shop closes, minimizing their impact on the environment. Compared to graffiti in other locations, the reduced visibility can increase the chances of the artwork lasting longer. ii) Second, the size of roll-up doors is particularly suitable for Chinese graffiti, especially when multiple doors appear in succession. On this occasion, the artist encountered four consecutive roll-up doors and decided it would be perfect to write a motto. Thus, they inscribed four Chinese characters meaning “Huo zhe jiu xing 活着就行” (Just live), which reflects one of their personal values. iii) Additionally, the artist enjoys designing signs for shop names on these roll-up doors. This approach can lead environmental protection workers, who are tasked with covering graffiti, to mistakenly believe the artwork was created by the shop owner, potentially protecting it from removal. """@en ;
