        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q429220>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q943> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1559.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Wood Dragon (灵丹 +YP)" ;
    ns1:description """In this CWU, the graffiti artist Exas employs a distinctive technique, transforming the two characters of his Chinese tag Lingdan 灵丹, which means "elixir" or "magic pill," into animated "puppets." Through this zoomorphic transformation, the characters themselves become dynamic and lifelike, as if animated with the spirit of the dragon. Zoomorphism, the process by which letters or characters are given animal-like forms, plays a central role in this artwork. Exas' interpretation of the characters takes inspiration from the concept of letterform illustration, where the shapes of letters or symbols are designed to resemble living beings, whether human or animal. This method effectively bridges language with visual art, allowing the written word to acquire physicality and presence.\r
What stands out in this animation is the striking contrast between warm and cool tones. The dragon's body is depicted in vibrant red, while its flame-like tentacles are rendered in shades of sky blue, creating a dramatic interplay of colors. Interestingly, the dragon’s tail takes on the appearance of a frozen tree, enhancing the contrast between fire and ice.\r
The forms themselves are rounded yet sharp, embodying both fluidity and precision, much like the dragon’s claws and teeth. As in traditional calligraphic compositions, Exas masterfully juxtaposes Yin and Yang elements: heat and cold, water and fire, blending opposing forces through chromatic contrast and symbolic imagery. This tension between elements ‒ sharp and soft, warm and cold ‒ imbues the artwork with a deeper, dualistic narrative, symbolizing balance in contrast."""@en ;
