<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1689> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P144 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1699> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q125928>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q43059> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q207849>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q29052406> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q41670> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1860> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/great_seal_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1689.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU More Than Human World (More Than Human World + 8 red tag/seals)" ;
    ns1:description """This Calli-Writing Unit spans eight pillars of a bridge, where the artist employs a calligraphic style reminiscent of  the great seal script (dazhuan 大篆), using bold black brushstrokes and acrylic paint to inscribe the phrase "more than human world."\r
This expression originates from David Abram's influential book "The Spell of the Sensuous," linking the artwork to his philosophy of the "more-than-human world," which emphasizes the interconnectedness of human beings and the natural environment. As a result, the piece can be understood as a socio-political work of art.\r
The composition of the artwork is deliberately fragmented, with letters spread across different pillars. This arrangement creates a dynamic, multifaceted experience for viewers, who encounter the message gradually as they traverse the bridge. For instance, the first pillar bears the word "mor," accompanied by TxT's signature red tag/seal, which is inspired by ancient Chinese calligraphic seals, reinforcing the themes of permanence and authority. A lemon-yellow scribbled background—an element repeated on alternating pillars—adds a striking contrast to the black brushstrokes. The subsequent pillars continue in a similar pattern, with the second pillar completing the word "more" and alternating the use of the red tag and yellow background, ensuring rhythmic visual balance throughout the installation.\r
This artistic choice - using black brushstrokes and a modern interpretation of traditional Chinese calligraphy - connects visually to Abram's philosophy, as it bridges the ancient, symbolic language of calligraphy with the contemporary urgency of environmental concerns. The use of letters, rather than Chinese characters, further modernizes the calligraphic aesthetic.\r
The piece responds directly to the devastating drought of the River Po in 2022, an event exacerbated by climate change, which profoundly altered the natural landscape. The drought serves as a reminder of the fragility of ecosystems and humanity's responsibility to recognize its place within the broader web of life. TxT’s work reflects this shift, drawing attention to the inextricable link between human and non-human entities, a central theme in Abram’s philosophy. In The Spell of the Sensuous, Abram advocates for a reevaluation of how humans engage with the "more-than-human world," urging a more profound respect for the natural world that sustains human life.\r
Through this large-scale outdoor installation, TxT highlights the fragile, yet powerful, relationship between humans and nature. The artwork becomes a visual reminder of the impact of climate change, while also inviting contemplation on the role of human beings as part of a broader, interconnected ecological system. By bridging the philosophical with the visual, the piece serves as a meditation on how human actions - particularly in the context of environmental crises such as the River Po drought - reverberate through the natural world, blurring the boundaries between the human and the more-than-human."""@en ;
