<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1767> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/698> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Vienna Art Week 2023"@en ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1741> ;
    ns2:P580 "2023-11-10"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2023-11-17"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P856 <https://www.viennaartweek.at/en/vienna-art-week-2023/> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1767.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """VIENNA ART WEEK 2023 centered around the theme "INCITING PASSION", exploring the many facets of this powerful emotion. The festival delved into how passion, while a driving force that motivates individuals to pursue goals with dedication, can also be destructive and dangerous, applying this concept to various areas such as love, work, politics, and religion.\r
Over 100 free events were held in institutions, independent spaces, galleries, and other exhibition venues, offering a rich and diverse program. Art education was a key focus, with particular emphasis on inclusivity: for the first time, an extensive program was offered for people with disabilities and chronic illnesses under the motto “VIENNA ART WEEK for all”. The festival thus reflected the ambiguity of passion, representing both private and public spaces and their impact on our everyday lives."""@en ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1767.jpg> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1768> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Deploring the Death of My Aunt (姨母帖)"@en,
        "姨母帖"@zh,
        "Yímǔ tiē"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1480 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5727902> ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q378564> ;
    ns2:P571 "0349"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """"Yimu Tie 姨母帖", known as "Letter to the Aunt" or "Letter for the Aunt," is a famous calligraphy work by Wang Xizhi (王羲之). The original content of this letter is not entirely clear, but it is believed to have been an informal and affectionate message intended for Wang Xizhi's aunt. Its significance in the history of calligraphy lies not so much in its content but in the style with which it was written. The version preserved in the Liaoning Provincial Museum is a copy made during the Tang Dynasty, specifically in 696 AD, under the order of Empress Wu Zetian, who sought to preserve and promote Wang Xizhi's calligraphy. The manuscript was created using a technique called "double line" (shuanggou), which made the characters more pronounced and distinctive.\r
\r
The manuscript, which contains 6 lines and 42 characters, is an example of a type of calligraphy that, while remaining true to the characteristics of Wang Xizhi, has a more solid and less elegant appearance compared to his other works. Although it retains some typical features of Wang's calligraphy, such as fluid brushstrokes, Yimu Tie stands out for its robustness and influence from clerical script (隶书), a more "rigid" style that contrasts with the grace and elegance found in Wang's more fluid cursive script.\r
Empress Wu Zetian, the only female emperor in Chinese history, promoted the collection and reproduction of many calligraphic works. Yimu Tie was included in the "Wan Sui Tong Tian Manuscript" (万岁通天帖), an official compilation of calligraphy intended to celebrate and preserve the art for future generations."""@en .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1779> a ns3:Q1400264 ;
    ns2:P2561 "Guangdong Modern Dance Company 广东现代舞团" ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q16572> ;
    ns2:P453 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1263> ;
    ns2:P571 "1992"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "The Guangdong Modern Dance Company is the China’s first professional modern dance company, founded in 1992 in Guangzhou as the “Guangdong Experimental Modern Dance Company” (GMDC) under the leadership of the director Yang Meiqi 杨美琦 (b.1945) and the artistic director Cao Chengyuan 曹诚渊 (in art Willy Tsao, b. 1955).  The GMDC is internationally renowned for its exquisite and captivating performances and its vision that modern dance is a tool for self-expression attracts some of the most talented dancers and choreographers in China. "@en ;
    ns1:hasArtisticCollectiveType <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2393314> ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5613637> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1783> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1232>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/592> ;
    ns2:P1476 " Sans papier/son papier"@en ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q490> ;
    ns2:P580 "2011-05-27"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2011-07-14"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P664 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q105979072> ;
    ns1:description "The exhibition “Sans papier/son papier” was held from May 27 to July 14, 2011. An original exhibition at the Polytechnic University of Milan-Bovisa uses paper to define the utopia and the contradictions of contemporary society starting from the hopes of the “sans papier”. Paper, the privileged material of the culture of recycling, takes on multiple symbolic meanings, from the exploration of new forms of expression and construction to the search for identity and citizenship of the “sans papier”, migrants who aspire to integration into the societies of the rich West. In this exhibition, twenty-four paintings on handmade paper and cardboard prototypes of works by artists such as the creator of the project Gabriele Amadori, Giuseppe Spagnulo (1936-2016), Rodolfo Aricò (1930-2002), Davide Cantoni (b. 1965), Angela Glajcar (b. 1970), Hans Hermann (1858 – 1942) and Daniele Nitti Sotres (b. 1977) were exhibited. Some of the installations were created by the students of Gabriele Amadori, then professor of scenography at the Polytechnic of Milan. " .

