    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Deer crying (鹿鸣)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU is composed of multiple pictographic variants of the character 'lu'  鹿 (deer) and ‘ming’ 鸣 (cry)  freely  arranged on the sheet of paper in order to recreate the din of a herd of wailing deer. The artist took draws on several attestations of the character written in “bronze script” (jinwen 金文), a variety of Chinese scripts written on ritual bronzes from the Shang dynasty (c. 1600 BC - 1045 BC) to the Zhou dynasty (c. 1046 BC - 256 BC). As a result, viewers can appreciate the naïf representation of the primitive fears, even if does not know that they are actually Chinese characters. The artist also added colors, such as brown, orange and yellow, to accentuate the pictorial charge of the representation.\r
The term 'Luming 鹿鸣' (Deer crying) is the title of the opening section of 'Xiaoya 小雅' (Minor Odes) in the poetry anthology 'Shijing 诗经' (Classic of Poetry). The poems included in this section describe deer gathering and enjoying fresh grass, which is likened to the harmonious gathering of noble individuals. """ ;
