    ns2:P941 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1845>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1847>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1849>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1852>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1854> ;
    ns1:description """This musical composition of approximately 12 minutes long is composed by Cheng Yeung-ping for a mixed ensemble with inspiration from a set of 5 momentous calligraphic pieces created by Tong Yang-tze for the Main Hall of M+ of Hong Kong in occasion of the opening of the museum in 2020.  The titles of the five calligraphies are:\r
1) “The Movement of Heaven is Powerful” (Tian xing jian天行健); \r
2) “Renew oneself daily” (Ri xin日新); \r
3) “At the auspicious moment, act without delay” (Le tian zhi ming 乐天知命);\r
4) “Delight in the existence of heaven and understand its order” (Jian ji er zuo 见机而作);\r
5) “Embracing the way of heaven brings progress” (He tian zhi qu, dao da xing ye 何天之衢，道大行也). \r
Sentences do not have a fixed reading order, which allowed the audience to enjoy the calligraphies freely and without a predefined starting point. The five quotes are taken from the I Ching (Yijing 易经, Book of Changes) an ancient Chinese philosophical text. \r
Following Tong Yang-tze calligraphies's structure, the composition realized by Chen Yeung-ping is divided into five sections, as the composer’s personal response to the texts of the calligraphy. These sections are: \r
1) “Heaven” (Tian 天);\r
2) “Human” (Ren人);\r
3) “Self-awareness” (Zi zhi 自知);\r
4) “Overcome ego” (Zi sheng 自勝);\r
5)“Unity” (He yi 合一). \r
As Chen Yeung-ping explained: "Tong Yang-tze’s calligraphies are highly experimental. My composition simply echoes the calligraphic gestures and movements evoked by his calligraphies. The message they carry is equally significant, so I decided to make a musical movement corresponding to each of the five calligraphic pieces made by Tong Yang-tze based on the meaning expressed by his calligraphies. In the calligraphies you can capture the ‘momentum’ of each stroke, as well as the texture and intensity of the ink. In music too, we talk about ‘density’, ‘texture’, ‘gesture’ in some way and this creates a strong connection between music and calligraphy" (interview 2024).\r
The composition was played for the first time  September 30, 2022 at the University of Hong Kong  by Tete Bae 𧜏志恩 (flute), Euna Kim (violin), Leung Ka-lok 梁家洛 (pipa) , Kaisan (guqin) and Alexander Wong (piano) as part of the "Ink Art and New Music Project" in an event entitled "Ink Art and New Music - New Works by Faculty and Student Composers".\r
"""@en ;
    ns1:hasPerformingArtType <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3328797> .

