<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1870> a ns3:Q184485 ;
    ns2:P1303 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11405>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5994> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Cursive (行书)"@en,
        "行书"@zh,
        "Xíngshū"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P2047 "PT11M"^^xsd:duration ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q60> ;
    ns2:P585 "1964"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P86 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1871> ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q607543> ;
    ns1:description "This musical composition for flute and piano is inspired by the cursive script in which, as decleared by the composer Chou Wen-Chung, \"the joined strokes and rounded angles result in expressive and contrasting curves and loops\" (Chou Wen-Chung official website). In his composition, this concept influenced “the use of specified but indefinite pitches and rhythm, regulated but variable tempo and dynamics, as well as various timbres possible on the two instruments [the flute and the piano].  The piano serves as reflection of the flute by “extending” its range into the lower register and by matching the flute’s varied timbral resources, such as microtonal trills and flutter tonguing, using plucked piano strings and foreign materials between these strings” (ibid.)."@en ;
    ns1:hasPerformingArtType <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3328797> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1871> a ns3:Q215627 ;
    ns2:P103 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P106 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q121594>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q36834> ;
    ns2:P1412 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1860> ;
    ns2:P19 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q43407> ;
    ns2:P20 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1384> ;
    ns2:P21 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6581097> ;
    ns2:P2561 "Chou Wen-Chung 周文中" ;
    ns2:P27 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P569 "1923-07-28"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P570 "2019-10-25"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P812 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q638> ;
    ns2:P856 <https://chouwenchung.org/> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q60> ;
    ns1:description """Chou Wen-Chung (1923 – 2019) was born in Yantai (Chefoo), Shandong. He grew up in China and developed an early love for music. His first musical “imprinting” occurred at an early age. Chou came to the United States in 1946 as a young refugee from World War II. He was awarded a scholarship to study architecture at Yale (having already earned a degree in the subject from Chongqing University in his homeland), but he dropped out before the end of the semester. In 1952, he arrived in New York to study music at Columbia University. His music, ranging from arrangements of ancient Chinese poems to masterful string quartets and an evocative cello concerto, marries Western modernism with Eastern styles and sound textures in a captivating and personal manner. His first musical composition, “Landscapes,” an orchestral setting of three Chinese songs, was premiered by the San Francisco Symphony in 1953. His numerous residencies at the Yaddo art colony  in upstate New York led to a collaboration with poet Ted Hughes (1930-1998) based on “The Tibetan Book of the Dead” and a lifelong friendship with the female writer Dawn Powell (1896-1965). However, his most significant professional relationship was with modernist composer Edgard Varèse (1883-1965). Chou met Varèse in 1949 and became his assistant while the older composer was creating his final works. After Varèse’s death in 1965, Chou completed the maestro’s last piece, “Nocturnal,” which premiered in 1968. As Varèse’s most celebrated student, Chou has remained a dedicated advocate for his mentor’s work and has lived for the past four decades in the same Sullivan Street house in Greenwich Village that Varèse purchased in 1925.\r
Chou recognized the intrinsic contribution of qin music and the concept of the single tone to Chinese music, and, more importantly, he acknowledged their value to composers. Before becoming a musician, Chou was primarily a calligrapher. After years of practicing various styles and scripts of Chinese calligraphy, he began to see parallels between this art form and music. In Chou’s view, the layers of meaning within calligraphy served as a philosophical foundation for music. Rather than seeking to amalgamate the divergent traditions of East and West, he aimed to internalize and transcend contemporary idioms and techniques, creating a personal style that reflects a genuine modern sensibility. The “musicality” of Chinese characters is another concept Chou experimented with as he attempted to combine Western and Eastern elements and reproduce Chinese sounds through Western instruments. In particular, Chou compares the ink flow to the density of the music and experimented with the characters written in cursive style in the piece “Cursive” (1964, see the archive)."""@en ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Present (not specified)"@en ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1349386> ;
    ns1:isComposerOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1870> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1878> a ns3:Q215627 ;
