    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1941.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """“Brush-Stroke” (筆法) is a musical composition that stands out for its deep inspiration from Chinese calligraphy, creating a fascinating intertwining of visual art and sound. This work not only celebrates the beauty of calligraphic strokes but also translates them into a musical language, where each note and pause reflect the fluidity and expressiveness of the calligraphic gesture. \r
“Brush-Stroke” was commissioned by and dedicated to Stephen Drury and the Callithumpian Consort.   This première took place on February 23, 2005, in Calderwood Hall of the Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum of Boston.  The title “Brush-Stroke” reveals the influence of Chinese calligraphy in music, in particular, the “slender gold script” by Emperor Huizong 徽宗 (1082-1135)  of the Song dynasty and the wild cursive script by Monk Huaisu. In this work, Liang tried to develop an idea that he called “One-Note-Polyphony”. \r
As Lei Liang explains:\r
"Inspired by my sonogram analysis of the music of the guqin, my initial fascination in sound has found its expression in an idea that I call “one-note polyphony.” Each note is conceived as a kernel, or a container: a single note functions as the “intersection” where various musical dimensions can be accesseded. My interest lies in the potential for perceptible timbral transformation through the varying possibilities for instrumental re-synthesis on these single notes" (Interview 2024).\r
This concept is inspired by his sonogram analysis of the music of the ancient Chinese seven-string zither – the guqin. Each single note functions as an “intersection” where various musical dimensions can be accesseded. Liang explores the timbral spectrum of a wide range of instruments through his technique of One-Note-Polyphony, shaping the composite texture through overlapping entries and unifying it through continually transforming a set of six pitches. Furthermore, by exploring the spectral sounds associated with guqin, Liang allows the listener to immerse in the transience of each sound as it comes into being and passes away, like the effects found in Chou Wen-Chung’s “Yü ko” (Yuge 渔歌 , 1965).  Furthermore, the slow, ritualistic quality of the first section of “Brush-Stroke” contains features reminiscent of gagaku,  Japanese court music imported during the Tang dynasty, as well as the Aak (Korean court music),  ensembles that similarly comprise woodwinds, strings, and percussion. The ritualistic features are specially made evident by the shaping of the phrase based on collective “breath” rhythm, the woodwinds’ role in leading the ensemble with a head motive at a commencement of a musical phrase, and the subtle shifts in timbre within the texture of One-Note-Polyphony.\r
The second section is rhythmically more animated, featuring two percussionists that engage in a fast, interlocking rhythmic pattern (forming a mirror canon) with pebbles, while the remaining instruments provide various textures in sustaining a fluid continuity of sounds. As the collective texture builds in intensity, the percussionists switch over to shouting out interlocking vocables like in kecak (Balinese monkey chant)  and hand drumming on bongos and congas. \r
In the final section, the piano appears in the foreground with its stochastic and pointillistic texture, coalescing into a rapid chromatic figuration in the highest register of the piano. The explosive rhythmic energy attributed to the percussion and piano are suggestive of the wild cursive style of calligraphy associated with Huaisu. Liang’s music is deeply philosophical, yet sensual, evocative, yet abstract, and disciplined, yet spontaneous. Suffice to say, his music is autobiographical: it is as if with each brushstroke, Liang reclaims his cultural identity through refracting memories of people, concepts, and objects onto a vast musical canvas. And in this way, he pays homage to tradition while embracing a global perspective and invites the listener to participate in a journey that transcends cultural boundaries. \r
Through a careful exploration of the forms and dynamics of Chinese writing, “Brush-Stroke” invites listeners to immerse themselves in a sensory experience that evokes ancient artistic traditions. The composition unfolds as a dialogue between mark and sound, allowing the delicacy and precision of calligraphic strokes to be perceived through melodies and rhythms. In this way, “Brush-Stroke” is not just a tribute to calligraphy but a work that invites reflection on the connections between different art forms and their capacity to express emotions and cultures.\r
""" ;
