<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2040> a ns3:Q215627 ;
    ns2:P103 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P106 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3303330>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q483501> ;
    ns2:P1412 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1860> ;
    ns2:P19 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P20 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3593416> ;
    ns2:P21 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6581097> ;
    ns2:P2561 "Zhang Dawo 张大我" ;
    ns2:P27 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P569 "1943"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P570 "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P856 <https://zhang-dawo.com/en/> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q34366>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns1:description "Zhang Dawo (born 1943) was a Chinese calligrapher and artist, born into a cultured family. He grew up in Tianjin, where he received private lessons in calligraphy from the city’s two most distinguished practitioners, Li Henian and Wu Yuru. After graduating from high school in 1963, Zhang volunteered to leave Beijing and became a teacher in northeastern China, where he faced significant challenges during the Cultural Revolution, including imprisonment by the Red Guards. After 18 months, he was released and assigned to manual labor. In 1979, Zhang and his family were allowed to return to Beijing, where he resumed his career in education, teaching calligraphy at a secondary school and later at the Capital University of Beijing. Since 1992, Zhang has split his time between Beijing and Tasmania, Australia. His experiences in Tasmania deepened his appreciation for the abstract lines in nature, influencing his work. He began experimenting with denser ink and vibrant color, using a fast-moving brush to evoke a sense of flight in his calligraphy. By the late 1990s, Zhang’s works had evolved into a form of Oriental abstract expressionism, blending traditional and contemporary influences from both Eastern and Western cultures. He describes his style as \"Dawo Black in White Miaomo,\" focusing on the tranquil power of solid black and abstract forms. Zhang's large-scale works, some up to 16 meters wide, express a dynamic yet deeply Chinese sensibility, resonating with both Eastern and Western audiences." ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "School"@en .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2042> a ns3:Q215627 ;
    ns2:P103 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
