        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3133>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q47071> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2052.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Writing Music – Silent Melodies: \"La Traviata\" (signs)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU consists of Luo Qi's original transcription of an aria of the Italian opera "La Traviata" using a series of signs that belong to the the "Dunhuang Musical Score System" (Dunhuang qupu / yuepu 敦煌曲谱/乐谱). This ancient music notation system is made up of 20 tablature-signs, which represented the tablature notations for the four-stringed Tang dynasty pipa (Chinese lute), indicating finger positions and their corresponding pitches. These symbols are simple characters (six are Chinese numerals), radicals or character components/parts. They are accompanied by two other symbols (a dot · and a mouth 口) that denote rhythm and meter. In this work, even though these signs follow the verticality of the format, they are arranged freely, unlike Dunhuang musical manuscripts and traditional calligraphy. The addition of green and brown is also an innovative element of this work. The artist reinterprets and reshapes these symbols in a unique and personal way, transforming the musical composition into a "visual and visible musical score" that is deeply rhythmic, dynamic, and intrinsically calligraphic.\r
""" ;
    ns1:directionality "Free",
        "Traditional" ;
