    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2070.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Writing Music - Silent Melodies \"Se l'aura spira\" (signs)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU is composed of Luo Qi's original transcription of an Italian aria entitled "Se l'aura spira" by Girolamo Frescobaldi (1583–1643) using a series of signs that belong to the the "Dunhuang Musical Score System" (Dunhuang qupu / yuepu 敦煌曲谱/乐谱). This ancient music notation system is made up of 20 tablature-signs, which represented the tablature notations for the four-stringed Tang dynasty pipa (Chinese lute), indicating finger positions and their corresponding pitches. These symbols are simple characters (six are Chinese numerals), radicals or character components/parts. As in the Dunhunag musical pieces, in this CWU these symbols are transcribed one below the other from right to left, to form columns, and are accompanied by two other symbols (a dot · and a mouth 口) that denote rhythm and meter.  Even if the writing flow is traditional, the materials are new (acrylic on plastic). The use of black and white, although traditional, is recombined in the transcription of the signs: it is not the black ink on white paper but there are black and white signs on a transparent support. Even the fact of tracing only the outlines of the signs creates a play of empty and full spaces typical of calligraphy but rendered in a new form.\r
In this CWU, the Dunhuang musical scores are reshaped in a way that the musical composition is transformed into a  rhythmic and cadenced “visual and visible musical score” in dialogue with the surrounding space.  \r
""" ;
    ns1:directionality "Traditional" ;
