    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Sound Images - Luo Qi 2023 (signs)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU consists of a set of  pictographic signs entirely re-invented by Luo Qi.  Eyes, hearts, fish, birds, stylized human and animal figures, flags, overflowing vessels, dwellings, arrows, triangle roofs, and crosses reshape ancient Chinese pictograms. These signs act as indipendent visual symbols, emphasizing their pictographic essence rather than their semantic meaning. This conceptual shift was  already explored extensively in Luo Qi's earlier "Love Writing" series (Qingshu 情书, 1990-2002) and "Bird" and "Fish" series (Niao yu yu xilie “鸟”与"鱼”系列, 2005–2009). The core of Luo Qi’s work lies in the deformation and reconfiguration of selected characters and components, in order to strengthen and revitalize their aesthetic vitality and visual impact. He starts from the seal-script form of a character and then reimagines it creatively.  His invented signs draw particularly on forms of jiaguwen (oracle bone script), jinwen (bronze script), dazhuan (large seal script), and xiaozhuan (small seal script), the most pictographic styles in the Chinese calligraphic tradition. For example, the second character from the bottom in the first column is easily recognizable as the word for “fish” (yu 鱼), written in oracle bone script. \r
The characters are arranged in columns, read from right to left and from top to bottom, like a classical piece of calligraphy. However, the overal layout of the signs is free and fluid. In some cases, characters are enclosed by a big rectangular frame that resembles an ancient seal. The characters within this frame are a prominent feature within the overall composition organized in vertical columns.  \r
 The filling of parts of the characters with red colour not only deepens the three-dimensional feeling of the writing but also adds to its pictorial quality. The alternation between black and red generates a rhythmic balance, echoed by the contrast between large and small characters. These contrasts are reminiscent of the arrangement in manuscripts and old printed books , where the main content of the text is written in big characters and the commentary inserted in smaller script. \r
Curved lines makes the shapes appear softer and more inviting, evoking the fluid lines found in Miró’s visual language.  The risult is the creation of a vivid kaleidoscope of imagined and yet-to-be-imagined narratives, immersed in a dynamic and pulsating visual rhythm. """ ;
