        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2148_2.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Writing Landscapes - Drafting from Extensive Research on Ancient Model Scrolls (历代名碑帖)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU consists of the calligraphic strokes and the Chinese characters written in a range of traditional scripts, shaping a contemporary reinterpretation of a Chinese landscape painting (shanshui 山水). Calligraphic strokes and ink lines construct natural elements such as mountains, tree branches, flowers and rocks. The work therefore embodies the the classical aesthetic principle of 'the shared origin of painting and calligraphy' (shuhuatongyuan 书画同源). \r
The overall composition is structurally inspired by "Precipitous Peak" (Sou jin qi feng da caogao 搜尽奇峰打草稿, literally "Searching All the Strange Mountains for Sketches"), a landscape painting by Shi Tao 石涛 (1642 – 1707). Peaks and all other natural elements emerge from layered clusters of characters and strokes.\r
The textual content is drawn from 'historical collections of rubbings and model calligraphic works' (lidai ming bei tie 历代名碑帖), written through a variety of scripts and styles. These include the great seal script (dazhuan 大篆) of the Shang and Zhou dynasties, the small seal script of Li Si 李斯, the running script of Wang Xizhi 王義之, the wild cursive of Zhang Xu 张旭 and Huai Su 怀素, to the styles of Ming-Qing masters like Dong Qichang 董其昌 and Fu Shan 傅山 (artist's interview 2025).""" ;
