<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2213> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q10379677>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q273120>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7281> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11472>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q207849> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/regular_script> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Red Humor (无人说道)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU includes the textual content composed of slogans from advertisements, lines of classical poetry, news headlines, Buddhist scriptures, government notices, and weather forecasts, all incorporated in Wu Shanzhuan's installation artwork "Red Humor: Big-Character Posters". These heterogeneous materials collide in a chaotic, post-semantic space where traditional meaning breaks down. The texts are rendered in ink and commercial paint on xuan paper. The installation was created in the artist’s studio and is currently housed at the Zhoushan Municipal Cultural Center (Qunzhong Yishuguan 群众艺术馆), Zhoushan City.\r
The characters used by the artist are often distorted, modified, or simplified beyond recognition, rendering them unreadable. Then, the placement, repetition, and layering of characters across the entire space (walls, floors, ceilings) of Zhoushan studio (Hearn 2014: 53) create immersive environments that confuse and overwhelm, reinforcing the collapse of legibility. This aligns with the artist's goal of breaking down the communicative function of language and emphasizing visual ambiguity. He also uses visual jokes and puns based on homophones, making each seemingly intelligible fragment open to reinterpretation. As noted in Hearn (2014: 53), the four large characters arranged in a grid on the floor cleverly exploit Chinese wordplay. They can be read, or better interpreted, in multiple ways.  For instance,  read as "wú shuō bā dào" (无说八道), the phrase means "don't speak nonsense".  Its pronunciation immediately evokes the well-known  four-character idiom "hú shuō bā dào" (胡说八道), translated as "talking nonsense" or "speaking absurdities." Here, hú (胡), which suggests something “reckless” or “nonsensical” substitues phonetically the original wú (无), meaning “not” or “without”. Since wu (无) is a homophone of the artist's surname Wu (吴), the phrase may also be read as "Wu speaks nonsense." Additionaly, the style of character ba (八) can easily, and likely deliberately, be confused with ren (人, person), transforming the phrase into wú rén shuō dào" (无人说道), meaning “no one can speak the way” or “no one can explain it”. \r
Another of the artist's manipulation involves the rare character 辺, which can be a graphic variant of either dào (道) or  biān (边), allowing for further interpretations, such as "recklessly speak in eight directions" or again "nonsense". As Hearn summarizes, Wu “undermines the logographic meaning of this text. What we are left with is the artistic equivalent of Babel" (Hearn 2014: 53).  """ ;
