<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2313> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Vivienne Tam “Ready-to-Wear 2013” Exhibition"@en ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1384> ;
    ns2:P580 "2013-02-10"^^xsd:date ;
    ns1:description """Vivienne Tam “Ready-to-Wear 2013” Exhibition was held at New York Fashion Week. It was a vibrant fusion of East and West, blending bold contemporary aesthetics with cultural symbolism. The show took place on February 10th, coinciding with the Chinese New Year, adding a meaningful backdrop to the presentation. Vivienne Tam cleverly mixed elements of Western pop culture—like a pop-art print of Barack Obama wearing sunglasses accompanied by QR codes—with traditional Chinese influences, including stylized Chinese characters and Maoist references such as "long live" (wan sui 万岁) featured on elegant garments.\r
The color palette was dominated by striking reds, blacks, and whites, accented with touches of blue. Silhouettes ranged from ’60s-inspired sheath dresses to modern reinterpretations of military jackets. Luxurious fabrics like silk and leather were enhanced by intricate details such as accordion pleats and leather appliqués, giving depth and texture to the looks.\r
The beauty styling matched the edgy vibe, with bold dark eyeliner, deep burgundy lipstick, and sleek high ponytails, creating a strong, punk-inspired finish. Beyond the fashion itself, Vivienne Tam used the platform to support social causes, collaborating with an organization that converts electronic waste into microloans for women in need.""" ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2305> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2319> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Chinese Emotional Graffiti - Mental illness"@en ;
    ns2:P580 "2021"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "‘Chinese Emotional Graffiti’ featuring ‘mental illness expressions’ bring to light this critical social issue, highlighting the urgent need for greater support and awareness around mental health, especially in the aftermath of COVID-19. Mental illness remains a significant issue in China, where cultural stigma and limited mental health resources have historically hindered open discussions about psychological well-being. Although awareness has been gradually increasing, societal attitudes still often view mental illness as a weakness or a personal failing rather than a medical condition deserving of empathy and treatment. The prevalence of mental health issues in China has been on the rise, with anxiety and depression being some of the most common disorders, particularly among young people and those in urban areas. The impact of the COVID-19 pandemic has further exacerbated mental health challenges worldwide, and China is no exception. Extended periods of isolation, fear of infection, economic uncertainty, and the social restrictions imposed during lockdowns have significantly contributed to the increase in mental health disorders, including depression, anxiety, and post-traumatic stress symptoms. "@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2320> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2335> a ns3:Q215627 ;
    ns2:P103 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P106 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q121594> ;
    ns2:P19 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q57958> ;
    ns2:P21 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6581072> ;
    ns2:P2561 "Zhang Yanzi 章燕紫" ;
    ns2:P27 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P569 "1967"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns1:description """Zhang Yanzi is a Chinese artist whose practice explores the intersections of art, medicine, and healing. Trained in traditional Chinese ink painting, she holds a Master’s degree from the Capital Normal University and currently serves as a professor at the Central Academy of Fine Arts (CAFA) in Beijing.\r
Her work combines ink, mixed media, and medical materials—such as gauze, pills, and herbs—to reflect on pain, fragility, and the search for spiritual and physical restoration. Through poetic and minimalist visual language, Zhang bridges classical aesthetics and contemporary concerns, creating art as an act of empathy and introspection.""" ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Academic"@en ;
    ns1:isDesignerOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2134> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2347> a ns3:Q43229 ;
    ns2:P2561 "Fu Qiumeng Fine Art" ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1384> ;
    ns2:P856 <https://fuqiumeng.com/> ;
    ns1:description """Fu Qiumeng Fine Art is a gallery positioned at the intersection of East and West, bridging contemporary languages and historical traditions. It is dedicated to the study and promotion of the visual and geographic globalization of contemporary Asian art, documenting the work of artists whose practices engage with classical Asian philosophy, aesthetics, and culture, across a wide range of expressive media.\r
Amid an ever-evolving artistic landscape, the gallery seeks to present the creative trajectories of significant artists to an international audience through curated exhibitions, in-depth interviews, critical essays, and academic forums. Fu Qiumeng Fine Art serves as a space for reflection and education, where visual pleasure is accompanied by intellectual rigor, offering insight into the dynamics of globalization and into transnational and diasporic experiences.""" ;
    ns1:hasOrganisationType <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6881511> ;
    ns1:isDesignerOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/730> .

