<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/237> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "The Foolish Old Man Removes the Mountain (愚公移山)"@en,
        "愚公移山"@zh,
        "Yu Kong Yi Shan"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2984064> ;
    ns2:P571 "-0400"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """Yu Kong Yi Shan 愚公移山 ("The Foolish Old Man Removes the Mountains") is a well-known fable from Chinese mythology about the virtues of perseverance and willpower. The tale first appeared in Book 5 of the Liezi, a Daoist text of the 4th century BC written by the philosopher Lie Yukou. The story was also used by Mao Zedong in a famous speech in 1945. \r
This traditional Chinese story is known by all common people and it speaks about a family story. It is about determination and challenge: if you really want to do something, then it could really happen."""@en ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11073609> ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation "The Foolish Old Man of  nearly ninety years of age, lived behind two high mountains. He was unhappy about the fact that the mountains blocked his way to the south, so he called the whole family to level the two mountains. While they were working, The Wise Old Man at the River Bend stopped the old man. He laughed and said: \"How unwise you are! At your age, old and feeble as you are, you cannot even remove one hair on the mountain, let alone so much earth and so many rocks!\" The Foolish Old Man had a long sign and said: “When I die, there will be my sons, who will have their sons and grandsons. Those grandsons will have their sons and grandsons, and so on to infinity. But the mountains will not grow. Why is it impossible to level them?\". The Wise Old Man could not answer him. Finally, a god of the mountains was touched by their actions and he helped them finished the road quickly. "@en,
        """古时候，愚公的房子前面有两座大山，一座叫太行山，一座叫王屋山。每次愚公出门到南边去，都得绕过这两座山，很不方便。于是，他把家里所有的人都叫来，对他们说： “我下决心要挖掉我们家门口的两座大山，你们说好不好？”大家都同意，只有愚公的妻子觉得不行， 她问：“你这样的力气挖一座小山都很困难，别说太行王屋这样的大山了。 而且，挖出来的泥土，石头放在哪里?"。"把它们搬到北海去”，她的儿子回答。\r
第二天，愚公的一家开始挖山，他们用篮子，水桶把泥土和石头搬到北海。 去一次北海要花很长时间，冬天出发，第二年夏天才回来。愚公的邻居老太太有一个孙子，也过来帮忙。\r
愚公的另一个邻居知道了这事，觉得愚公太傻了，可是愚公说：“虽然我已经九十九岁了，但是我死了以后有儿子，儿子死了有孙子，子子孙孙不停地挖，但山不会再高了，怎么挖不平？” 他带领全家继续挖山。\r
天上的大神听到了愚公说的话，很感动，就派两个巨人背走了两座大山。"""@zh,
        "Gu shihou, yu gong de fangzi qianmian you liang zuo dashan, yizuo jiao taixingshan, yizuo jiao wang wushan. Mei ci yu gong chumen dao nanbian qu, dou de raoguo zhe liang zuo shan, hen bu fangbian. Yushi, ta ba jiali suoyou de ren dou jiao lai, dui tamen shuo: “Wo xia juexin yao wa diao women jia menkou de liang zuo dashan, nimen shuo hao bu hao?” Dajia dou tongyi, zhiyou yu gong de qizi juede buxing, ta wen: “Ni zheyang de liqi wa yizuo xiaoshan du hen kunnan, bie shuo taixing wang wu zheyang de dashanle. Erqie, wa chulai de nitu, shitou fang zai nali?”. “Ba tamen ban dao beihai qu\", ta de er zi huida. Di er tian, yu gong de yijia kaishi wa shan, tamen yong lanzi, shuitong ba nitu he shitou ban dao beihai. Qu yici beihai yao hua hen chang shijian, dongtian chufa, di er nian xiatian cai huilai. Yu gong de linju lao taitai you yige sunzi, ye guolai bangmang. Yu gong de ling yige linju zhidaole zhe shi, juede yu gongtai shale, keshi yu gong shuo: “Suiran wo yijing jiushijiu suile, danshi wo sile yihou you erzi, erzi sile you sunzi, zi zisun sun bu ting de wa, dan shan bu hui zai gaole, zenme wa buping?”. Ta dailing quanjia jixu wa shan. Tianshang de dashen ting daole yu gong shuo dehua, hen gandong, jiu pai liang ge juren beizoule liang zuo dashan."@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2383> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11736>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1867> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Chinese Emotional Graffiti - Desire to escape / dissatisfaction with present reality "@en ;
    ns2:P580 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "This category is represented by pieces that convey a profound sense of disconnection and dissatisfaction with the surrounding world. During the COVID-19 pandemic, individuals around the world faced restrictions on their freedoms and a suspension of daily activities, placing a significant burden on their lives. This context makes the messages of these graffiti pieces clear: they express feelings of inadequacy, alienation, dissatisfaction, and a desire to seek happiness elsewhere. This imagined escape reflects a deeper psychological response to the collective trauma experienced during the pandemic, highlighting the role of creativity and fantasy in coping with adversity."@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2386> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2387> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q50388354> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q262828> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/hard_tipped_calligraphy>,
        <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/regular_script> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Reality is meant to be escaped (现实就是用来逃避的)" ;
    ns1:description "The \"CWU Reality is meant to be escaped (现实就是用来逃避的)\" was executed in black spray paint on a repainted wall that had previously been used to host another work (likely a puppet- or cartoon-style figure), typical of graffiti culture. The artist deliberately employed traditional Chinese characters, reflecting the linguistic and cultural environment of Taipei. This choice firmly situates the work within its local context while also engaging in a broader dialogue with Chinese-language graffiti practices across different regions. By writing directly over a freshly buffed surface, the gesture becomes both an act of defiance and a moment of reflection. Faint traces of the earlier graffiti remain visible beneath the new layer of paint, creating a visual and conceptual dialogue between erasure and re-inscription. Through this interplay, the piece interrogates the cyclical nature of visibility and disappearance in public art. The graffiti style of this work combines spray paint techniques with traditional Chinese writing practices, incorporating Conventional/Traditional characters (changgui fantizi), Regular script (kaishu), and hard-tipped calligraphy (yingbi shufa)."@en ;
    ns1:directionality "Free" ;
    ns1:hasBackgroundColour <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444> ;
    ns1:hasBigCharactersCalligraphy false ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicLine true ;
    ns1:hasCharacterForm <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q178528> ;
    ns1:hasColophon false ;
    ns1:hasGraffitiStyle <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/anti_style>,
        <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/gothic_calligraphy> ;
    ns1:hasSignificance "Meaningful writing" ;
    ns1:isUnitOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2386> ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q659495> ;
    ns1:numberOfCharacters 8 ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation "Reality is meant to be escaped"@en,
        "现实就是用来逃避的"@zh,
        "現實就是用來逃避的"@zh,
        "Xiànshí jiù shì yòng lái táobì de"@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2390> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Chinese Emotional Graffiti - (Aspiration for) Freedom "@en ;
    ns2:P580 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """Within the framework of Chinese Emotional Graffiti, the characters "ziyou 自由 (freedom)" frequently appear on urban walls. The recurring presence of this word reflects graffiti writers’ invocation of freedom in its most universal and existential sense. Yet, some inscriptions also echo well-known quotations or allude to pivotal moments in Chinese history, suggesting that the concept of freedom functions simultaneously as a personal aspiration and as a form of collective memory shaped by sociopolitical experience.\r
A series of graffiti works featuring "ziyou 自由 (freedom)" explores the multifaceted meanings of the term through variations in form, style, and spatial context. Rendered in diverse calligraphic and graffiti-inflected scripts, the word becomes both a visual experiment and a philosophical statement. Each iteration negotiates the tension between inner liberation and external constraint, between the intimate and the public.\r
The repetition of "ziyou 自由 (freedom)" across multiple urban Chinese surfaces creates a dispersed yet interconnected narrative of yearning and defiance. In some instances, the characters are written hastily on abandoned walls or underpasses, their rawness emphasizing immediacy and emotional urgency. In others, they are integrated into more elaborate compositions, where decorative motifs or textual fragments evoke literary or historical references, deepening the semantic resonance of the word.\r
As a recurring motif within Chinese Emotional Graffiti, "ziyou 自由 (freedom)" thus transcends linguistic meaning to become an aesthetic and affective gesture: a reaffirmation of agency and individuality within a rapidly transforming urban and political landscape."""@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2393> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2392> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2979> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q104969796>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/regular_script> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Freedom (自由)" ;
    ns1:description "The \"CWU Freedom (自由)\" consists of two characters spray-painted in red on a weathered gray surface, visually enclosed within a metal cage. The composition intensifies the socio-political resonance of the work: the act of inscribing “freedom” directly onto an urban infrastructure, only to have it visually confined by fencing, can be read as an implicit commentary on the restrictions placed upon individual liberties in the public sphere. Notably, the inscription is arranged vertically, written from top to bottom, recalling the traditional orientation of Chinese calligraphy while asserting its presence within a contemporary graffiti context. The style leans toward a throw-up/softie graffiti aesthetic, and the dripping of the spray paint symbolizes the “overflow” of freedom despite its confinement."@en ;
    ns1:directionality "Traditional" ;
    ns1:hasBackgroundColour <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42519> ;
    ns1:hasCharacterForm <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q185614> ;
    ns1:hasColophon false ;
    ns1:hasGraffitiStyle <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/anti_style>,
        <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/bubble_style_softie>,
        <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/throw_up> ;
    ns1:hasSignificance "Meaningful writing" ;
    ns1:isUnitOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2393> ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q659495> ;
    ns1:numberOfCharacters 2 ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation "Freedom"@en,
        "自由"@zh,
        "Zìyóu"@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2401> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3130>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q490> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Chinese Emotional Graffiti - Loneliness"@en ;
    ns2:P580 "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """Some examples of Chinese Emotional Graffiti that convey the sentiment of loneliness were created outside China. In these instances, the expression of solitude is multifaceted: it reflects not only the emotional isolation experienced during and after pandemic lockdowns, but also the profound sense of estrangement felt by Chinese immigrants living in foreign environments. These works articulate a transnational form of loneliness, one that intertwines personal displacement, cultural detachment, and the search for identity through visual language. \r
The graffiti often employs restrained color palettes, fragmented calligraphic strokes, or disjointed textual compositions that mirror psychological distance and disconnection. \r
The choice of location, such as abandoned walls, underpasses, or urban peripheries, further reinforces a feeling of marginality and invisibility. \r
By translating emotion into written form, these artists transform public surfaces into intimate confessional spaces, revealing the tension between presence and absence, belonging and isolation, within the broader context of diasporic Chinese identity."""@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2403> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2404> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q223270> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/hard_tipped_calligraphy>,
        <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/ugly_calligraphy> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU I feel lonely (我感到孤单)" ;
    ns1:description "The \"CWU I feel lonely (我感到孤单)\" was created with a marker on a rough wall, in the style of Hard-tipped calligraphy (yingbi shufa). This graffiti presents a striking repetition of the pronoun \"wo 我 (I)\" sixteen times, followed by the phrase \"wo gan dao gu dan 感到孤单 (I feel lonely).\" The visual and linguistic accumulation of wo 我 intensifies the self-referential tone, evoking a sense of obsessive introspection and emotional isolation. The piece exemplifies how written language, particularly Chinese characters, can function as a direct medium of affect in contemporary urban expression."@en ;
    ns1:directionality "Modern" ;
