    ns1:madeUsingTool <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q659495> ;
    ns1:numberOfCharacters 5 ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation "I am very sad today"@en,
        "今天很伤心"@zh,
        "Jīntiān hěn shāngxīn"@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2419> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q84>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8646> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Chinese Emotional Graffiti - Sadness"@en ;
    ns2:P580 "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """Sadness is a recurring motif in Chinese Emotional Graffiti, often functioning as both a subtle warning and a reflective reminder. On one hand, it encourages individuals to recognize the emotional depth and human value embedded in the experience of sadness; on the other, perhaps with a trace of irony, it implies that sadness is an ordinary, even inevitable, component of daily existence.\r
In a series of graffiti works centered on this theme, sadness is not depicted through overt melancholic imagery, but rather through restrained visual language and textual ambiguity. The compositions often feature fragmented characters, faded pigments, and irregular drips, formal choices that evoke a sense of erosion or emotional exhaustion. The use of Chinese characters intensifies this affective charge, as strokes appear to tremble or dissolve, suggesting both vulnerability and endurance.\r
Thematically, these works transform public walls into emotional landscapes, where private feelings become part of a collective urban dialogue. Rather than dramatizing despair, the artists seem to reclaim sadness as a shared human condition, resisting the expectation of perpetual optimism. The result is a quiet, introspective form of expression that challenges conventional graffiti’s association with aggression or rebellion, positioning emotional honesty itself as a form of resistance."""@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2418> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2423> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q14773> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Chinese Emotional Graffiti - Discomfort of youth"@en ;
    ns2:P580 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """The series "Chinese Emotional Graffiti – Discomfort of Youth" examines the psychological and cultural repercussions of the COVID-19 pandemic through the language of contemporary Chinese graffiti. The pandemic brought abrupt losses (of work, relationships, and a sense of normalcy) leaving many, especially youth, with the feeling that precious, irretrievable time had been stolen. Young people faced unique challenges: isolation, disrupted education, and pervasive uncertainty about the future. These formative years, marked by restriction and fear, often led to disconnection and a diminished sense of agency.\r
The series translates these experiences into visual form, using the expressive potential of language as both subject and medium. Each piece transforms the urban wall into a site of emotional inscription, where hybridized Chinese characters embody the contradictions of a generation caught between cultural continuity and existential fragmentation. \r
In this sense, the “discomfort” of youth emerges not only as an individual sentiment but as a collective condition: a manifestation of disrupted temporality and constrained autonomy. These works function as public confessions, reclaiming visibility for emotions suppressed during isolation. By turning private disquiet into shared visual experience, "Chinese Emotional Graffiti – Discomfort of Youth" transforms public space into a mirror of generational resilience, mourning, and emotional truth."""@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2426> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2425> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q12101178>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q190007> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/ugly_calligraphy> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Where Is Youth Going? (青年去往何处? )" ;
    ns1:description "The “CWU Where Is Youth Going? (青年去往何处?)” was sprayed in red paint onto the crumbling wall of a ruined house at a street corner. The inscription is split across two sections: “Qingnian 青年” (youth) appears on the left wall, while “qu wang he chu 去往何处” (where is it going?) occupies the right, visually emphasizing a sense of rupture and disjunction. The characters themselves convey a dynamic instability: their strokes shift between assertive, calligraphic precision and erratic, dissolving forms, embodying the inner turbulence of contemporary youth. The surface shows visible layers of paint and traces of erasure, evoking the passage of time and the emotional residue left by isolation and uncertainty. Dripping marks and uneven textures suggest a gradual loss of solidity, as though the very notion of “youth” were disintegrating before the viewer’s eyes."@en ;
    ns1:directionality "Modern" ;
    ns1:hasBackgroundColour <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444> ;
    ns1:hasBigCharactersCalligraphy true ;
    ns1:hasCharacterForm <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q185614> ;
    ns1:hasColophon false ;
    ns1:hasGraffitiStyle <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/anti_style> ;
    ns1:hasSignificance "Meaningful writing" ;
    ns1:isUnitOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2426> ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q659495> ;
    ns1:numberOfCharacters 7 ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation "Where Is Youth Going?"@en,
        " 青年去往何处?"@zh,
        "Qīngnián qù wǎng hé chù?"@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2429> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q28996>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8684> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Chinese Emotional Graffiti - Desperate Love"@en ;
    ns2:P580 "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """The themes of love, affection, and romance are prominently featured within the phenomenon of Chinese Emotional Graffiti. These motifs, long celebrated by poets, musicians, and scenographers throughout history, also find a natural place among contemporary street artists and writers. Yet, within this form of expression, such emotions are often portrayed through a negative or ironic lens. Chinese Emotional Graffiti, typically found on urban walls, bridges, and other public spaces, embodies a raw and unfiltered outpouring of personal sentiment. The artists who create these works frequently dwell on the pain, heartbreak, and longing associated with love, revealing the melancholic underside of romantic experience.\r
This approach stands in contrast to the traditional depiction of love as joyous and fulfilling, instead emphasizing the emotional complexity and ambivalence that this universal theme can evoke in street art. Graffiti phrases such as “Romantic Virus,” “COVID lasted longer than your love,” or “Love Throat” exemplify this tendency to merge irony, despair, and emotional vulnerability. Through these expressions, the artists transform private suffering into public testimony, using walls as a space for catharsis and shared reflection.\r
Several examples further illustrate the emotional toll of the COVID-19 lockdowns. The pandemic not only imposed physical separation but also deepened emotional alienation, contributing to the dissolution of many relationships. As lockdown measures curtailed gestures of intimacy, like hugs, handshakes, kisses, individuals were deprived of the very forms of connection most needed in times of crisis. This state-imposed isolation intensified feelings of loneliness and underscored the fundamental human need for both emotional and physical closeness. In this context, Chinese Emotional Graffiti stands as a poignant visual record of contemporary affect, bridging the gap between personal sentiment and collective experience."""@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2432> .

