<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2436> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2044>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8646> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Chinese Emotional Graffiti - Existential and universal questions "@en ;
    ns2:P580 "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """This series explores the millions of existential and universal questions we encounter in daily life, translated into the visual language of Chinese graffiti. A defining feature of “Chinese Emotional Graffiti,” particularly as showcased on platforms like ChineseGraffitiHub, is the dynamic interaction that emerges in the comments section of each post. Every question, reflection, or emotional outburst depicted in these works sparks engagement from followers, who contribute their own perspectives, experiences, and emotions.\r
This participatory aspect transforms the graffiti from static visual art into a catalyst for communal dialogue and collective reflection. The comments section becomes a virtual space of connection, where people share support, contrasting viewpoints, and empathy, enriching the conversation around universal human concerns. This interaction highlights a distinctive dimension of contemporary Chinese street art: digital platforms amplify the impact of graffiti far beyond its physical presence, creating a living, evolving discourse.\r
By blending traditional graffiti techniques with online engagement, “Chinese Emotional Graffiti” enables a deeper exploration of human emotions, societal issues, and existential questions. Through this synergy of visual art and social media, the series transcends cultural and linguistic boundaries, inviting a global audience to reflect on the fundamental questions that unite us all."""@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2437> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2441> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8646> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Chinese Emotional Graffiti - Fatigue"@en ;
    ns2:P580 "2021"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """The notion of fatigue, primarily psychological yet deeply intertwined with the pressures of contemporary existence, emerges as a central and recurring motif within the framework of Chinese Emotional Graffiti. These works articulate a condition of profound moral and emotional exhaustion, reflecting the pervasive sense of weariness that characterizes life in the post-pandemic era.\r
The graffiti convey not merely physical tiredness but a collective psychological depletion, a state intensified by social isolation, uncertainty, and the erosion of individual autonomy. The experience of the Covid-19 pandemic has transformed human beings into passive witnesses of their own vulnerability, subject to invisible forces that govern both public and private life. In this context, the written expressions found in "Chinese Emotional Graffiti – Fatigue" function as both a lament and a cry for relief.\r
Through fragmented phrases, repetitive strokes, and expressive calligraphic gestures, these works expose the inner fragility of the human condition. The language inscribed on the walls, often terse and emotionally charged, acts as a medium for unspoken despair and collective mourning. Each mark embodies resistance against silence, transforming public space into a site of introspection and emotional release.\r
Ultimately, "Chinese Emotional Graffiti – Fatigue" should be understood as an act of vulnerability and self-exposure. It is both personal and universal—a visual articulation of exhaustion that transcends linguistic and cultural boundaries, seeking empathy and recognition in the shared experience of enduring and surviving fatigue."""@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2444> .

