<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2447> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/748> ;
    ns2:P1476 "CAFA Exhibition: Silk Road Yunshang (丝路云裳)"@en ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P580 "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """The Silk Road Yunshang exhibition was held at the Taimiao Art Museum in Beijing in May 2017, coinciding with the Belt and Road International Cooperation Forum. Curated by Fan Di’an, with Lü Yue and Zhang Yanzi, the exhibition explored the intersection of art, fashion, and silk culture along the Silk Road, connecting tradition with contemporary practice.\r
The exhibition comprised two main sections: 1) “Gathering of Garments”: showcasing artists and designers working with clothing to highlight the cultural, social, and aesthetic dimensions of dress; and 2) “East-West Collaboration”: presenting contemporary artists using silk robes as a “canvas” for artistic creation, including those with limited experience in fashion.\r
The exhibition traced the historical development and transmission of silk and garments, while emphasizing contemporary reinterpretations in art. Fashion art, emerging in the 1960s–1970s in the United States, transcends mere wearability to engage with cultural meaning, artistic ideas, and the creator’s emotions.""" ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2134> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2457> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P144 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7071> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1518315>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q282>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3010205> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42519> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/regular_running_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2457.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Les Poèmes du Mandarin for Hermès - Invitation to Wine" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU is part of the white ceramic plate from the “Les Poèmes du Mandarin” collection by Hermès. It features the full text of Li Bai’s poem "Invitation to Wine" (Jiang jin jiu 将进酒) written by Fung Mingchip in non-simplified characters. The calligraphy is rendered in subtle shades of black and grey with delicate platinum accents to evoke the effect of freshly applied, wet ink, and punctuated with small red seals carved by the artist. The calligraphy was first created freehand on traditional rice paper and later digitally adapted by the design studio to fit the ceramic surface. The writing reflects a personal style inspired by xingkai (semi-cursive script), reinterpreted on a reduced and carefully controlled scale. The strokes are exceptionally delicate, at times resembling fine hatchings, giving the script a highly unconventional and experimental quality compared to traditional calligraphic norms. The composition transforms each character into a visual rhythm, highlighting both the expressive potential of the brush and the artist’s innovative approach to classical Chinese calligraphy.\r
The plate is presented inside Hermès’ iconic square orange box, emphasizing the combination of classical Chinese poetry with the brand’s contemporary luxury aesthetic. The subtle, almost ethereal quality of the calligraphy, together with the pristine white ceramic, transforms the functional object into a work of art, highlighting both the beauty of the literary text and the innovative approach to calligraphy.""" ;
    ns1:directionality "Traditional" ;
    ns1:hasBackgroundColour <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444> ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicLine true ;
    ns1:hasCharacterForm <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q178528> ;
    ns1:hasColophon true ;
    ns1:hasSignificance "Meaningful writing" ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/2457.JPG> ;
    ns1:isUnitOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/730> ;
    ns1:numberOfCharacters 190 ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """Have you not seen –\r
that the waters of the Yellow River come from Heaven,\r
surging into the ocean, never to return again;\r
Have you not seen –\r
in great halls’ bright mirrors, they grieve over white hair,\r
at dawn like black threads, by evening becoming snow.\r
In human life, accomplishment must bring total joy,\r
do not allow an empty goblet to face the moon.\r
Heaven made me – my abilities must have a purpose;\r
I spend a thousand gold pieces completely, but they'll come back again.\r
Boil a lamb, butcher an ox – now we shall be joyous;\r
we must drink three hundred cups all at once!\r
Master Cen,\r
Dan Qiusheng,\r
bring in the wine!\r
The cups must not stop!\r
I’ll sing you a song –\r
I ask that you lend me your ears.\r
Bells, drums, delicacies, jade – they are not fine enough;\r
I only wish to be forever drunk and never sober again.\r
Since ancient times, sages have all been solitary;\r
only a drinker can leave his name behind!\r
The Prince of Chen, in times past, held feasts at Pingle;\r
ten thousand cups of wine – abandon restraint and be merry!\r
Why would a host speak of having little money? –\r
you must go straight and buy it – I’ll drink it with you!\r
My lovely horse,\r
my furs worth a thousand gold pieces,\r
call the boy and have him take them to be swapped for fine wine,\r
and together with you I’ll wipe out the cares of ten thousand ages."""@en,
        """君不見，\r
黃河之水天上來，\r
奔流到海不復回；\r
君不見，\r
高堂明鏡悲白髮，\r
朝如青絲暮成雪。\r
人生得意須盡歡，\r
莫使金樽空對月。\r
天生我材必有用，\r
千金散盡還復來。\r
烹羊宰牛且為樂，\r
會須一飲三百杯。\r
岑夫子，\r
丹丘生，\r
將進酒，\r
杯莫停。\r
與君歌一曲，\r
請君為我傾耳聽。\r
鐘鼓饌玉不足貴，\r
但願長醉不復醒。\r
古來聖賢皆寂寞，\r
惟有飲者留其名。\r
陳王昔時宴平樂，\r
斗酒十千恣欢谑。\r
主人何為言少錢，\r
徑須沽取對君酌。\r
五花馬，\r
千金裘，\r
呼兒將出換美酒，\r
與爾同銷萬古愁。"""@zh,
        """君不见，\r
黄河之水天上来，\r
奔流到海不复回；\r
君不见，\r
高堂明镜悲白发，\r
朝如青丝暮成雪。\r
人生得意须尽欢，\r
莫使金樽空对月。\r
天生我材必有用，\r
千金散尽还复来。\r
烹羊宰牛且为乐，\r
会须一饮三百杯。\r
岑夫子，\r
丹丘生，\r
将进酒，\r
杯莫停。\r
与君歌一曲，\r
请君为我倾耳听。\r
钟鼓馔玉不足贵，\r
但愿长醉不复醒。\r
古来圣贤皆寂寞，\r
唯有饮者留其名。\r
陈王昔时宴平乐，\r
斗酒十千恣欢谑。\r
主人何为言少钱，\r
径须沽取对君酌。\r
五花马，\r
千金裘，\r
呼儿将出换美酒，\r
与尔同销万古愁。"""@zh,
        """Jūn bù jiàn,\r
huánghé zhī shuǐ tiān shànglái,\r
bēnliú dào hǎi bù fù huí;\r
Jūn bù jiàn,\r
gāotáng míngjìng bēi bái fà,\r
zhāo rú qīngsī mù chéng xuě.\r
Rénshēng déyì xū jìn huān,\r
mò shǐ jīn zūn kōng duì yuè.\r
Tiān shēng wǒ cái bì yǒu yòng,\r
qiānjīn sàn jǐn hái fù lái.\r
Pēng yáng zǎi niú qiě wéi lè,\r
huì xū yī yǐn sānbǎi bēi.\r
Cén fūzǐ,\r
dān qiū shēng,\r
jiāng jìn jiǔ,\r
bēi mò tíng.\r
Yǔ jūn gē yī qū,\r
qǐng jūn wèi wǒ qīng ěr tīng.\r
Zhōng gǔ zhuàn yù bùzú guì,\r
dàn yuàn cháng zuì bù fù xǐng.\r
Gǔlái shèngxián jiē jìmò,\r
wéiyǒu yǐn zhě liú qí míng.\r
Chén wáng xī shí yàn Pínglè,\r
dǒujiǔ shíqiān zì huān xuè.\r
Zhǔrén hé wèi yán shǎo qián,\r
jìng xū gū qǔ duì jūn zhuó.\r
Wǔ huā mǎ,\r
qiānjīn qiú,\r
Hū ér jiāng chū huàn měijiǔ,\r
yǔ ěr tóng xiāo wàngǔ chóu."""@zh-Latn ;
    ns1:vectorisedOrDigitised true .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2462> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8646> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Chinese Emotional Graffiti - Social and political commentaries"@en ;
    ns2:P580 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """Social and political commentary occupies one of the most sensitive and contested domains within ‘Chinese Emotional Graffiti.’ Unlike works that prioritize aesthetic experimentation or cultural symbolism, politically charged graffiti foregrounds text as its primary medium, articulating political dissent and public dissatisfaction with government policies.\r
These expressions emerge within a highly constrained environment shaped by censorship. Platforms that document such graffiti, such as Chineseuncensored, are frequently and swiftly shut down, underscoring the precarious position of writers who operate at the threshold between expression and suppression. As a result, many artists adopt indirect language, slang, or local idioms to convey critique while reducing personal risk. Under these conditions, state surveillance not only restricts content but also actively shapes form, rendering subversion an intrinsic component of the artwork itself.\r
Politically oriented graffiti thus demonstrates the capacity of graffiti to function as a vehicle for socio-political critique in a context where overt opposition is fraught with danger. These works often address broader societal concerns, such as public health and personal freedoms, that resonate widely, fostering a sense of shared experience and solidarity among those who feel marginalized or constrained.\r
Ultimately, the tension between artistic expression and political dissent in Chinese graffiti reveals the complex interplay of censorship, social commentary, and expressive resilience. Such writings operate simultaneously as reflections of contemporary social conditions and as acts of resistance within restrictive political environments."""@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2463> .

