<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2478> a ns3:Q215627 ;
    ns2:P103 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P106 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5322166> ;
    ns2:P19 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q57866> ;
    ns2:P21 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6581097> ;
    ns2:P2561 "Shao Honggeng 邵宏庚" ;
    ns2:P27 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P569 "1970"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "Hao Honggeng, born in 1970 in Yancheng, Jiangsu Province, is an internationally renowned designer and semiotician. He studied at the Department of Arts and Crafts at the Nanjing University of the Arts in 1990. Hao has contributed to the design of several iconic symbols, including the emblem of the Beijing Olympic Games and the Expo 2010 Shanghai emblem. His work seamlessly integrates art, calligraphy, and functional design, exemplified in creations such as the “Magnolia Blossom” tableware for state banquets and the Zisha teapot inspired by the World Expo logo. His artworks are preserved in the National Museum of China, and he has received the highest honors awarded to Chinese designers. Additionally, Hao has served on international juries for design awards and exhibitions, reflecting his prominent role in shaping contemporary Chinese visual culture." ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Present (not specified)"@en ;
    ns1:isDesignerOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2477> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2484> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q14773>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8646> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Chinese Emotional Graffiti - Hope"@en ;
    ns2:P580 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """In contrast to the predominantly negative emotional states articulated within Chinese Emotional Graffiti, a distinct subset of works foregrounds motivational language and expressions of hope. These inscriptions employ concise, affective phrases that function not merely as personal affirmations but as collective psychological interventions within public space.\r
Hope, in this context, emerges as a crucial counterforce to the psychological distress and emotional fragmentation intensified by the COVID-19 pandemic. Rather than operating as a passive or abstract sentiment, it manifests as an active and sustaining energy, one that encourages perseverance and reaffirms the possibility of change, even under prolonged conditions of uncertainty and restriction. The belief that each passing day may carry the potential for renewal becomes central to these works, transforming written language into a vehicle of emotional resilience.\r
Through their presence in the urban landscape, such graffiti articulate a desire to return to forms of social intimacy temporarily rendered inaccessible, reaffirming fundamental human needs for connection, affection, and autonomy. In doing so, these hopeful and motivational texts anticipate a broader thematic shift toward freedom, which constitutes the focus of the subsequent category under analysis."""@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2486> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2490> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q190507> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q207849>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/regular_script> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU There is Hope (有希望 )" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU employs large-scale red Chinese characters (you xiwang 有希望 "there is hope"), painted directly onto ...\r
This CWU is composed of the three characters you xiwang 有希望, which means ".....".\r
The CWU There is Hope (有希望 )" employs large-scale red Chinese characters painted directly onto an urban wall using a paint roller rather than spray paint. This technical and visual choice explicitly recalls the aesthetic and ideological legacy of dazibao (big-character calligraphy).\r
The graffiti is additionally framed by a blue outline, a detail that invites multiple interpretations. On a symbolic level, blue may connote trust, calm, stability, or endurance, contrasting with the urgency and intensity of red. Within Chinese visual culture, blue can also evoke notions of continuity, protection, or transcendence, subtly tempering the confrontational force of the red characters. Alternatively, the blue frame may function as a visual boundary, separating the message from the surrounding urban decay and granting it a sense of enclosure, preservation, or intentionality, almost as if isolating hope itself within a fragile perimeter.\r
The chromatic pairing of red and blue is unlikely to be arbitrary. Red traditionally signifies fortune, vitality, and happiness, while blue may suggest rationality, distance, or emotional restraint. Together, they produce a visual tension that mirrors the broader sociopolitical climate: a moment marked by uncertainty, exhaustion, and suspended futures. In such a context, the declaration that “there is hope” reads less as naïve optimism than as an urgent, almost defiant emotional assertion."""@en ;
