        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3502310> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q12019351>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1683724>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2143346>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q368533> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42519> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2591.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Intangible Tangent"@en ;
    ns1:description """This CWU is conceptually associated with the dress "Intangible Tangent" (曲曲). It derives from the expressive calligraphic language of Tong Yang-tze and can be understood as an abstract linguistic element translated into visual form. In this context, the calligraphic stroke functions not only as part of written language but as a structural and aesthetic unit characterized by direction, pressure, expansion, and interruption. Removed from its original textual sequence, the sign becomes an autonomous element whose meaning is conveyed through gesture, rhythm, and spatial presence. The enlarged calligraphic "conceptual" signs visible on the garment emphasize the physicality of the brush movement, revealing variations in ink density and the dynamic tension between thick and thin lines. These characteristics allow the CWU to operate as both a trace of writing and a visual composition. Rather than being read as conventional characters, the strokes are perceived as abstract forms that retain the memory of linguistic structure while functioning primarily through their visual and gestural qualities.\r
In this sense, the calligraphy associated with the dress represents a transformation of writing into an expanded visual language, where the CWU becomes a modular element capable of generating rhythm, movement, and continuity across the surface of the garment."""@en ;
