    ns1:isUnitOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/276> ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q614467> ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation "Bright snow"@en,
        "晴雪"@zh,
        "Qíng xuě"@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/288> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q252000>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6665312> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11472>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Surrounding (围)" ;
    ns1:description "This CWU is part of the artwork “Surrounding”  executed in 1991 by Shao Yan. The character \"Wei 围 \" is written in traditional form (圍). It is composed by two opposing heavy vertical lines that incline from upper left to lower right, framing the artwork, and two horizontal lines that continue beyond the edge of the paper and appear cropped or abbreviated. Inside the frame, the first two horizontal lines are executed in centered-tip brushwork with “flying white” technique while the two horizontal lines in the lower part of the work are drown with moist ink, spread toward the right and merge in a more heavy stroke. In the upper part of the work Shao Yan executes a vertical line that parallels the two lines framing the work and another one to the right side that inclines from left to right. In the lower part he brushes a short and thick vertical line placed off-centerd (compared with the central position it takes in the character “wei”). In the middle part Shao Yan executes the element “口”, in a trapezoidal shape rather than in the original square shape. This element works with the four lines of the frame to balance the entire work. Although the outlines of this work seem to represent an ancient city wall where people are forced to live, the reference to reality is minimal and the work tends towards abstraction. "@en ;
