    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Flying Good Luck (飞来好运)" ;
    ns1:description "This CWU is part of the artwork “Flying Good Luck” executed in 2011 by Shao Yan. For this work, he used a long and sharp brush, which he dipped in thick ink to execute the characters in cursive script. With a series of sharp strokes on the paper, changes of direction, arching and lifting the brush, Shao Yan brushed the first two characters, fei 飞 (“to fly”) and lai 来 (“to come”), from the upper part toward the lower part of the work. At this point, he dipped the brush in a small amount of ink, lifted it up, quickly drawing in the air a semi-circular arc and scattering dots of ink in order to give life to the character hao 好 (“Good”). To execute the character yun 运 (“Luck”) he used the paibi 排笔 (a brush composed of small brushes joined together) tilted at a 45-degree angle and loaded with ink. The form of the characters evokes the image of a boat carried by the wind: the oblique part of the character \"yun\" 云 recalls the prow of the boat hit by the splashing waves, the vertical strokes of \"yun\" 云, \"lai\" 来 and \"hao\" 好 symbolize the mast of the boat and the ink splashes represent the sails rolled up by the wind."@en ;
    ns1:hasBackgroundColour <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444> ;
