<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/447> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Full River Red (满江红)"@en,
        "满江红"@zh,
        "Mǎnjiānghóng"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1480 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5727902> ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q334398> ;
    ns2:P571 "1140"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "The poem entitled \"Full River Red\" (Man jiang hong 满 江红) was composed by Yue Fei 岳飞 (1103-1142), an officer of the Northern Song army. In 1127, when the Northern Song Dynasty came to an end as the Jurchen-Jin conquered northern China and the Song retreated south in the face of their attacks, he opposed the retreat. He continued, however, to serve the emperor of the Southern Song (1127-1279), engaging continuous battles with the Jurchen. He experienced success in his campaign against the Jin in 1140, but the Southern Song Gaozong Emperor (1107-1187) sought to make peace with the Jin, and ordered Yue Fei to withdraw, dashing in one moment all his achievements of ten years. Yue Fei composed this poem  during one of his victorious campaign against the Jin. In this poem, he describes his strong efforts to recover the lost territory, to fight against the barbarians and to persuade the emperor to support his army."@en ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """My hair bristles in my helmet.\r
Standing by the balcony as the rain shower stops,\r
I look up to the sky and loudly let Heaven know\r
The strength of my passions.\r
My accomplishments over thirty years are mere dust. \r
I traveled eight thousand li with the clouds and the moon\r
Never taking time to rest,\r
For a young man’s hair grows white from despair. \r
The humiliation of the Jingkang period\r
Has not yet been wiped away.\r
The indignation I feel as subject\r
Has not yet been allayed.\r
Let me drive off in a chariot\r
To destroy these base at Helan Mountain.\r
My ambition as a warrior\r
Is to satisfy my hunger with the flesh of the barbarians,\r
Then, while enjoying a rest,\r
Slake my thirst with the blood of the tribesmen. \r
Give me the chance to try again \r
To recover our mountains and rivers \r
Then report to the emperor. \r
(Translator: Buckley 1993, 169-70)\r
"""@en,
        """Sotto l’elmo, la mia ira,\r
sporto sulla balaustra,\r
il picchiettio della pioggia si estingue.\r
Gli occhi al cielo,\r
un grido lancinante,\r
e l’acuta forza del mio impeto.\r
Trent’anni di gloria in polvere.\r
Ottomila miglia di cammino,\r
senza mai sostare,\r
e la luna e le nuvole a vegliare.\r
Il giovane capo è incanutito,\r
è vano dolersi svilito.\r
Vivida l’umiliazione della sconfitta,\r
giammai estinta l’indignazione degli ufficiali.\r
I lunghi carri affrettiamo\r
contro le fortezze montuose dei nemici!\r
Affamati, sfamiamoci\r
con la carne dei barbari!\r
Assetati, dissetiamoci\r
col sangue dei barbari!\r
Monti e fiumi riconquistiamo\r
che al Celeste Impero restituiamo.\r
(Translators: A. Iezzi, M. Merenda)"""@it,
        """怒发冲冠，\r
凭阑处、潇潇雨歇。\r
抬望眼，仰天长啸，壮怀激烈。\r
三十功名尘与土，八千里路云和月。\r
莫等闲，白了少年头，空悲切。\r
靖康耻，犹未雪；臣子恨，何时灭？\r
驾长车，踏破贺兰山缺。\r
壮志饥餐胡虏肉，笑谈渴饮匈奴血。\r
待从头，收拾旧山河，朝天阙。"""@zh,
        """Nùfàchōngguān, \r
pínglán chù, xiāoxiāo yǔ xiē.\r
Tái wàng yǎn, yǎngtiān cháng xiào, zhuànghuái jīliè. \r
Sānshí gōngmíng chén yǔ tǔ, bāqiān lǐ lù yún hé yuè. \r
Mò děngxián, báile shàonián tóu, kōng bēiqiè! \r
Jìngkāng chǐ, yóu wèi xuě.\r
 Chénzǐ hèn, hé shí miè! \r
Jià zhǎng chē, tàpò hèlánshān quē.\r
 Zhuàngzhì jī cān hú lǔ ròu, xiàotán kě yǐn xiōngnú xuè. \r
Dài cóngtóu, shōushí jiù shānhé, cháo tiānquē."""@zh-Latn .

