<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/551> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Sound Series (声音系列)"@en,
        "声音系列"@zh,
        "Shēngyīn xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/249> ;
    ns2:P580 "2000"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """In this series, the formal aspect of calligraphy is only partially considered and the way the space is handled looks quite natural and loose. There is an aesthetic articulation of elements and an overlapping of the characters in the composition (which remembers the performative works but also the more formal ones) but it is not stiff or symmetric. It seems that the artist wants to combine together something formal and something more spontaneous. The title "Sound Series" (Shengyin xilie 声音系列) recalls the sound of the character being repeated over and over. The size and the transparency of the characters are suggesting the intensity of the voice to pronounce the characters or simply the overlapping repetition of the same sound. The reading and the quality of the reading could also be part of the creative process and eventually, of the way one interacts with the artwork. The way the composition is articulated is conceptually and/or visually conceived to suggest how a repeated sound can be represented as it is diffused. There could be many levels. The execution (in painterly terms) is also interesting with its attempt to represent sound through the various subtle tones of black and grey and levels of density in the way ink is used, as in the traditional "ink and wash painting technique" (shuimo 水墨画). The composition is articulate as follows: a layer of larger characters from below getting smaller in the higher part and a second layer of big characters above getting smaller on the way down while overlapping the first layer. There is a diagonal movement suggesting both a certain spontaneity and a sense of tension and again suggesting an informal level of symmetry.\r
In this series there are a lot of onomatopoeic Chinese characters both in traditional form and in simplified form, such as for example: "ai 唉" (expressing response); "ha 哈" (sound of laughing); "o 哦" (expressing doubt/realization); "ming 鳴" (sound of animals); "ca 嚓" (scraping sound); "pei 呸" (expressing disdain/disapproval); "wa 哇" (sound of crying or vomiting); "a 呵" (interjection of surprise), etc."""@en .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/553> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Dictionary Series (说文解字系列)"@en,
        "说文解字系列"@zh,
        "Shuō wén jiě zì xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/249> ;
    ns2:P580 "2000"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """This series is characterized by the combination of a conceptual approach (as the title “Dictionary Series”, Shuo wen jie zi 说文解字 itself suggests ) with a sophisticated painterly execution. The characters are partly dissolving in the background and they are repeated and overlapping in a composition that reminds also of the representation of a natural landscape or a natural phenomenon. The works emanate a tension in the composition, expressed through the diagonal movement. Conceptually we assume that there has been an inquiry in reference to a dictionary from which certain characters have been selected, studied and re-interpreted with a combination of ink painting techniques. The format is also interesting, not being a typical vertical scroll but one that tends to the square space. The square geometry is also typical of the Chinese and Asian artistic and cultural traditions, but it is less common when it comes to calligraphy as it implies a relevant change in the choice of the articulation of positive and negative space, in the layout of the composition and in the quality of the act of writing. \r
The size and shape of a calligraphic work can also suggest more information about the position and the quality of the gestures during the creative process. The materials (quality of paper and choice of brushes) also may suggest further conceptual elements to understand how the action of writing is approached. Here the brush seems to be only one of relatively medium dimensions and the writing style appears to be spontaneous and organized around the diagonal composition. The addition of the red stamps in the middle is another traditional element found in calligraphy practice.\r
Each vertical-hang scroll is characterised by characters of the same radical, such as “zhi 志" (will); “yi 意" (desire); “bei 悲" (sorrow); "chou 愁" (worry); "yuan 愿" (hope); "yuan 怨" (resentment); "e 恶" (evil)";  "si 思" (think);  "xiang 想" (consider); "nu 怒" (anger), etc., all containing the radical xin "心" (heart). Another vertical-hang scroll is characterised by characters all containing the radical "yan 言" (word), such as "yi 议" (discuss); "shuo 说" (say); "su 诉" (inform); "yu 语" (language); "ci 词" (word), etc. There may be also terms and contents of various types, such as the five elements of Chinese philosophy ( jin-mu-shui-huo-tu 金木水火土), or traditional folk sayings such as "riyue 日月" (sun and moon/time); "fengyu 风雨" (wind and rain/trials and hardships), "quanma 犬马" (dog or horse, which is a term used by courtiers in front of a sovereign to offer their humble service), etc.\r
Instead of writing poems, songs and aphorisms, Qiu Zhijie decides to copy characters from a dictionary, constantly changing the content of his writing. The words on the sheet of paper, even if they do not form an actual statement, suddenly find meaning because the artist discovers that they belong to the same semantic category, linked to the same radical. For example, "xian 仙" (immortal/celestial being) and "Fo 佛" (Buddha) are both linked to the radical "ren 人" (human being), as for the words "jiliang 伎俩" (trick) and "yulu 伛偻" (humpbacked).  \r
The artist uses a type of Japanese paper called "zhangzi zhi 障子纸", composed of a protective layer made of stems, reeds and shrubs that resists repeated washing, writing and cleaning the paper several times to create a "layered" effect. While looking at the work, the viewer's eye can abandon the idea of linearity and wander freely at will."""@en .

