        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q283> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/cursive_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/567.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Quotations from Ouyang Xiu in Mad Cursive B (欧阳修辞抄狂草 A)" ;
    ns1:description "This CWU is part of the artwork \"Quotations from Ouyang Xiu in Mad Cursive B (Ouyang Xiu ci chao kuangcao B 欧阳修辞抄狂草 B) executed by Wei Ligang in 2016. The characters are realized in mad cursive script (cao shu 草书) as an homage to Ouyang Xiu 欧阳修 (1007-1072), one of the eight great masters of Tang (618-907) and Song Dynasty (960–1279), excellent intellectual, statesman, magistrate, calligrapher, poet and novelist of the Song Dynasty, whose epic life and achievements are among the most celebrated in Chinese history. Wei Ligang embeds his work in this lineage with this large scroll where he let his spontaneous but well-learned gestures flow on five vertical sequences of characters deconstructed into pure lyrical rhythm and juxtaposed through subtle tonal nuances, carefully mastered through the dilution of the ink and the sensibility of the movement. If the piece clearly evocates the spontaneity of the wild-grass style, nevertheless it also shows a deliberate accuracy and attention to showing a broad variety of strokes, an alternation of clean bold marks and more overlapping delicate ones, together with an eloquent mastery of tone. "@en ;
    ns1:directionality "Traditional" ;
    ns1:hasBackgroundColour <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444> ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicLine true ;
    ns1:hasCharacterForm <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q178528> ;
