<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/601> a ns3:Q43229 ;
    ns2:P2561 "Department of Interpreting and Translation (DIT)" ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q13367> ;
    ns2:P571 "2012"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P856 <https://dit.unibo.it/it> ;
    ns1:description """The Department of Interpreting and Translation (DIT) was born in October 2012, taking on the legacy of the former SITLeC Department and the former Advanced School of Modern Languages ​​for Interpreters and Translators (SSLMIT).\r
The DIT places strong emphasis on research applied to the theoretical, practical and educational aspects of translation and interpreting, also with the support of new technologies, as well as on literary, genre and historical studies. The research strategies are therefore closely linked to the didactic and third mission aspects. This interaction and mutual valorisation have been considered fundamental characteristics for the Department since its creation.\r
From the point of view of teaching, the DIT offers a first level degree course and two master's degree courses , all with limited enrollment, and is home to a research doctorate . It has over 10 equipped laboratories, both for teaching simultaneous interpretation and assisted and multimedia translation. The quality of the educational offer is demonstrated by the excellent attractiveness , with an average percentage of students registered for the selection tests between 300% and 650% of the available places.\r
The DIT and its study programs are accredited by the most prestigious international networks , including the worldwide network of university institutions for the training of interpreters and translators (CIUTI), the European Master's in Translation (EMT) network, the International Master's in Conference Interpreting (EMCI) network and the International Doctorate in Translation Studies (ID-TS) network.\r
Finally, on the third mission front, the Department has a strong vocation for continuous training and open teaching, in which it invests its skills. In particular, skills in translation, terminology and language technologies are at the center of numerous Lifelong learning events and targeted internships with companies. The teaching skills of language 1 and 2 are instead the subject of meetings with school teachers. The DIT also shows a marked interest in consolidating relationships with local stakeholders , in particular through projects and collaborations that make it possible to disseminate knowledge to the public in areas such astheater and audio description, children's literature and gender issues and issues related to translation and mediation by girls and boys who are children of migrants."""@en ;
    ns1:hasOrganisationType <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2467461> .

