    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q37681> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle """CWU Landscape Painting Painted in Water (千里江山)\r
 """ ;
    ns1:description """This CWU is part of the artwork realized by Li Xinmo during the performance "Landscape Painting Painted in Water (画在水中的山水)". It consists of the four characters "Qianli jiangshan 千里江山" (One Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains) written with red vermillion ink in traditional form. These characters are inspired by a long scroll of blue and green landscapes painted by Wang Ximeng 王希孟 (1096 – 1119) a painter of the Song Dynasty entitled: “One Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains" (Qianli jianshan tu千里江山圖) , a very famous landscape painting which depicts the wonderful landscape of China. The stark contrast of the red ink on green algae slowly dissipates into the pond’s water, rendering the characters illegible.\r
\r
This painting is probably the finest example of Chinese blue-green landscape painting. It is the only surviving piece by the young genius Wang Ximeng, who painted it under Emperor Huizong’s guidance when he was 18 years old. Little is known about Wang Ximeng himself. It is said that he passed away probably around the age of 20. \r
The entire piece, painted in the hand scroll format, deployed scattered perspectives and the three visual angles (height, depth, level-distance), which are commonly used in Chinese landscape paintings. When viewing this piece, the viewer’s line of sight is directed along the contours of the mountains and rivers with a panoramic view of thousands of miles across. This painting uses scarce mineral pigments with bright colors to depict the peaceful and prosperous country. In ancient Chinese landscape paintings, especially court paintings, the natural landscape is often a metaphor for the Emperor’s longevity and the dynasty’s everlasting prosperity. "One Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains", as its name implies, depicts the vast country landscape. It is the ideal world with perfect order and harmony according to the Emperor’s will, and it is also the Daoist fairyland in the heart of the Daoist Emperor Huizong."""@en ;
    ns1:directionality "Unidentifiable" ;
