        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3257809>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q372973>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q373058>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42519>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q47071> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/regular_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/78.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Gas (气)" ;
    ns1:description "This Calli-Writing Unit consists of the large character Qi 氣 (Gas) written in traditional form in 3D graffiti style. This character is the tag of the artist: the choice of this character is related to the importance that this word/concept has in Chinese culture.  Literally meaning \"vapor\", \"air\", or \"breath\", the word Qi is often translated as \"vital energy\", \"vital force\", \"material energy\", or simply as \"energy\" because it is believed to be a vital force forming part of any living entity. In the traditional Chinese concept, qi is recognized as da qi 大氣 (great energy or energy of the macrocosm) and xi qi 吸氣 (breath). Originally, the da qi would have descended, giving birth to the earth; so the rain is understood as the \"qi of the earth\", which, becoming rarefied, would have risen, giving birth to the heaven. The human being, who is between heaven and earth, has both manifestations of da qi: the heavier one forms the body, the lighter one forms the heart. The idea of qi is also connected to another fundamental traditional Chinese concept, that of xing 行 (phase, process). The concept of the wu xing 五行 (five elements, more precisely defined as \"the five processes\") describes nature as the set of the five elements that compose it (fire, water, metal, wood and earth) and as a consequence of the relationships that occur between them. Referring to these two fundamental concepts of Chinese culture (qi and xing), the artist highlights, in the choice of his very name, how his will is to instill oriental \"breath\" in his art. The piece has a thick white outline. Every stroke has various layers of colors from dark to light blue, from dark to light purple, from dark to light brown and from dark to light green. The character has two backgrounds: the first is a grey \"bubble-cloud\", the second is a black wall. This character seems to reproduce a calligraphic seal of contemporary times: like a traditional seal, it is the sign of the artist, and it is characterized by angular strokes."@en ;
    ns1:directionality "Unidentifiable" ;
