        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1741>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q46> ;
    ns1:description "Sainkho Namtchylak is a singer originally from Tuva, an autonomous republic in the Russian Federation just north of Mongolia. She is known for her Tuvan throat singing[1] or Khöömei.Namtchylak is an experimental singer, born in 1957 in a secluded village in the south of Tuva. She is proficient in overtone singing; her music encompasses avant-jazz, electronica, modern composition and Tuvan influences. In Tuva, numerous cultural influences collide: the Turkic roots and culture it shares with Central Asian states, the Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region, Bashkortostan and Tatarstan; the strong Mongolic cultural influence and traditions it shares with Mongolia, Inner Mongolia, Buryatia and Kalmykia; the cultural influences from the various Siberian nomadic ethnic groups such as Samoyeds, Yeniseians, Evenks and from the Russian Old Believers, the migrant and resettled populations from Ukraine, Tatarstan and other minority groups west of the Urals. All of these, to extents, impact on Namtchylak's voice, although the Siberian influences dominate: her thesis produced while studying voice, first at the University of Kyzyl, then in the Gnesins Institute in Moscow during the 1980s focussed on Lamaistic and cult musics of minority groups across Siberia, and her music frequently shows tendencies towards Tungus-style imitative singing."@en ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Not present"@en .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/905> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "A Song of Dagger-dancing to a Girl-pupil of Lady Gongsun    (《观公孙大娘弟子舞剑器行》)"@en,
        "观公孙大娘弟子舞剑器行"@zh,
        "Guān Gōngsūn Dàniáng dìzǐ wǔ jiàn qì xíng"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q33772> ;
    ns2:P571 "0764"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """"A Song of Dagger-dancing to a Girl-pupil of Lady Gongsun", also known as "Watching Gongsun Daniang’s Students perform Sword Dance" (Guān gōngsūn dàniáng dìzǐ wǔ jiàn qì xíng 观公孙大娘弟子舞剑器行), is a poem written by Du Fu 杜甫 (712–770). This poem was inspired by a performance Du Fu witnessed  in 767, given by a group of disciples of Gongsun Daniang 公孙大娘, a famous sword dancer during the Tang dynasty. \r
The poet reminisces about first seeing the sword-whirling Lady Gongsun in his youth, when he was 27 years old (in the year 739).  In the preface to the poem, Du Fu mentions the calligrapher Zhang Xu 张旭 (active ca. 700-750), whose skill in cursive script significantly improved after observing Gongsun Daniang's performances of sword dance. \r
Du Fu’s poem was widely disseminated through handwritten copies, printed versions, and illustrated prints. It established a strong correlation between (sword) dance, calligraphy (particularly the cursive script), and performance art from at least the eighth century onward. \r
\r
"""@en ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q18899893> ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """(preface)\r
On the 19th of the Tenth-month in the second year of Dali, I saw, in the house of the Kueifu official Yuante, a girl named Li from Lingying dancing with a dagger. I admired her skill and asked who was her teacher. She named Lady Gongsun. I remembered that in the third year of Kaiyuan at Yancheng, when I was a little boy, I saw Lady Gongsun dance. She was the only one in the Imperial Theatre who could dance with this weapon. Now she is aged and unknown, and even her pupil has passed the heyday of beauty. I wrote this poem to express my wistfulness. The work of Zhang Xu, a native from the Wu district, as good at cursive calligraphyas good at cursive calligraphy. He often mentioned Mrs. Gongsun dancing with swords in Ye County. From then on, his cursive writing improved and he became bold and grateful. From this may be judged the art of Gongsun. \r
\r
(poem)\r
There lived years ago the beautiful Gongsun, \r
Who, dancing with her dagger, drew from all four quarters \r
An audience like mountains lost among themselves. \r
Heaven and earth moved back and forth, following her motions, \r
Which were bright as when the Archer shot the nine suns down the sky \r
And rapid as angels before the wings of dragons. \r
She began like a thunderbolt, venting its anger, \r
And ended like the shining calm of rivers and the sea.... \r
But vanished are those red lips and those pearly sleeves; \r
And none but this one pupil bears the perfume of her fame, \r
This beauty from Lingying, at the Town of the White God, \r
Dancing still and singing in the old blithe way. \r
And while we reply to each other's questions, \r
We sigh together, saddened by changes that have come. \r
There were eight thousand ladies in the late Emperor's court, \r
But none could dance the dagger-dance like Lady Gongsun. \r
Fifty years have passed, like the turning of a palm; \r
Wind and dust, filling the world, obscure the Imperial House. \r
Instead of the Pear-Garden Players, who have blown by like a mist, \r
There are one or two girl-musicians now-trying to charm the cold Sun. \r
There are man-size trees by the Emperor's Golden Tomb \r
I seem to hear dead grasses rattling on the cliffs of Qutang. \r
The song is done, the slow string and quick pipe have ceased. \r
At the height of joy, sorrow comes with the eastern moon rising. \r
And I, a poor old man, not knowing where to go, \r
Must harden my feet on the lone hills, toward sickness and despair. \r
(http://www.shigeku.com/xlib/lingshidao/hanshi/dufu.htm)"""@en,
        """(preface)\r
大历二年十月十九日，夔府别驾元持宅，见临颍李十二娘舞剑器，壮其蔚跂，问其所师，曰：“余公孙大娘弟子也。” 开元五载，余尚童稚，记于郾城观公孙氏，舞剑器浑脱，浏漓顿挫，独出冠时，自高头宜春梨园二伎坊内人洎外供奉舞女，晓是舞者，圣文神武皇帝初，公孙一人而已。玉貌锦衣，况余白首，今兹弟子，亦非盛颜。既辨其由来，知波澜莫二，抚事慷慨，聊为《剑器行》。\r
昔者吴人张旭，善草书帖，数常于邺县见公孙大娘舞西河剑器，自此草书长进，豪荡感激，即公孙可知矣。\r
\r
(poem)\r
昔有佳人公孙氏，一舞剑器动四方。\r
观者如山色沮丧，天地为之久低昂。\r
霍如羿射九日落，矫如群帝骖龙翔。\r
来如雷霆收震怒，罢如江海凝清光。\r
绛唇珠袖两寂寞，晚有弟子传芬芳。\r
临颍美人在白帝，妙舞此曲神扬扬。\r
与余问答既有以，感时抚事增惋伤。\r
先帝侍女八千人，公孙剑器初第一。\r
五十年间似反掌，风尘倾动昏王室。\r
梨园弟子散如烟，女乐余姿映寒日。\r
金粟堆前木已拱，瞿唐石城草萧瑟。"""@zh,
        """(preface)\r
大曆二年十月十九日，夔府別駕元持宅，見臨潁李十二孃舞劍器，壯其蔚跂，問其所師，曰：“餘公孫大娘弟子也。” 開元五載，餘尚童稚，記於郾城觀公孫氏，舞劍器渾脫，瀏漓頓挫，獨出冠時，自高頭宜春梨園二伎坊內人洎外供奉舞女，曉是舞者，聖文神武皇帝初，公孫一人而已。玉貌錦衣，況餘白首，今茲弟子，亦非盛顏。既辨其由來，知波瀾莫二，撫事慷慨，聊爲《劍器行》。\r
昔者吳人張旭，善草書帖，數常於鄴縣見公孫大娘舞西河劍器，自此草書長進，豪蕩感激，即公孫可知矣。\r
\r
(poem)\r
昔有佳人公孫氏，一舞劍器動四方。\r
觀者如山色沮喪，天地為之久低昂。\r
霍如羿射九日落，矯如群帝驂龍翔。\r
來如雷霆收震怒，罷如江海凝清光。\r
絳唇珠袖兩寂寞，晚有弟子傳芬芳。\r
臨潁美人在白帝，妙舞此曲神揚揚。\r
與余問答既有以，感時撫事增惋傷。\r
先帝侍女八千人，公孫劍器初第一。\r
五十年間似反掌，風塵傾動昏王室。\r
梨園弟子散如煙，女樂余姿映寒日。\r
金粟堆前木已拱，瞿唐石城草蕭瑟。\r
玳筵急管曲復終，樂極哀來月東出。\r
老夫不知其所往，足繭荒山轉愁疾。\r
"""@zh,
        """(preface) \r
Dà lì èr nián shí yuè shíjiǔ rì, kuí fǔ bié jià yuán chí zhái, jiàn lín yǐng lǐ shí'èr niáng wǔ jiàn qì, zhuàng qí wèi qí, wèn qí suǒ shī, yuē:“Yú gōngsūn dàniáng dìzǐ yě.” Kāiyuán wǔ zǎi, yú shàng tóng zhì, jì yú yǎn chéng guān gōngsūn shì, wǔ jiàn qì hún tuō, liú lí dùncuò, dú chū guān shí, zì gāo tóu yíchūn líyuán èr jì fāng nèi rén jì wài gòngfèng wǔnǚ, xiǎo shì wǔ zhě, shèng wén shén c wǔ huángdì chū, gōngsūn yīrén éryǐ. Yù mào jǐnyī, kuàng yú bái shǒu, jīn zī dìzǐ, yì fēi shèng yán. Jì biàn qí yóulái, zhī bōlán mò èr, fǔ shì kāngkǎi, liáo wèi “jiàn qì xíng”. Xī zhě wú rén zhāng xù, shàn cǎoshū tiē, shù cháng yú yè xiàn jiàn gōngsūn dàniáng wǔ xīhé jiàn qì, zì cǐ cǎoshū zhǎngjìn, háo dàng gǎnjī, jí gōngsūn kězhī yǐ. \r
\r
(Poem) \r
Xī yǒu jiārén gōngsūn shì, yī wǔ jiàn qì dòng sìfāng. \r
Guān zhě rúshān sè jǔsàng, tiāndì wéi zhī jiǔ dī áng. \r
Huò rú yì shè jiǔ rìluò, jiǎo rú qún dì cān lóng xiáng. \r
Lái rú léitíng shōu zhènnù, bà rú jiānghǎi níng qīngguāng. \r
Jiàng chún zhū xiù liǎng jìmò, wǎn yǒu dìzǐ chuán fēnfāng. \r
Lín yǐng měirén zài bái dì, miào wǔ cǐ qū shén yángyáng. \r
Yǔ yú wèndá jì yǒu yǐ, gǎn shí fǔ shì zēng wàn shāng. \r
Xiāndì shìnǚ bāqiān rén, gōngsūn jiàn qì chū dì yī. \r
Wǔshí niánjiān shì fǎn zhǎng, fēngchén qīngdòng hūn wángshì. \r
Líyuán dìzǐ sàn rú yān, nǚ lè yú zī yìng hán rì. \r
Jīn sù duī qián mù yǐ gǒng, qú táng shíchéng cǎo xiāosè. \r
Dài yán jí guǎn qū fù zhōng, lè jí āi lái yuè dōng chū. \r
Lǎofū bùzhī qí suǒ wǎng, zú jiǎn huāngshān zhuǎn chóu jí."""@zh-Latn .

