dànzhuāng nóng mǒ zǒng xiāngyí."""@zh-Latn ;
    ns1:vectorisedOrDigitised true .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/918> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Drinking by the Lake: Clear Sky at First, Then Rain (饮湖上初晴后雨)"@en,
        "饮湖上初晴后雨"@zh,
        "Yǐn húshàng chū qíng hòu yǔ"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1480 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q60070514> ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q36020> ;
    ns2:P571 "1073"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """The poem “Drinking by the Lake: Clear Sky at First, Then Rain” (Yin hu shang chu qing hou yu 饮湖上初晴后雨) is a celebrated seven-character quatrain by Su Shi 苏轼 (1037-1101), better knwon as Su Dongpo 苏东坡, an eminent poet and calligrapher of the Song dynasty (960 -1279). The poem praises the picturesque beauty of West Lake, one of the most famous scenic spots in China, through a subtle analogy to the rare beauty of Xi Shi 西施, one of the "Four Great Beauties" (Si da meinü 四大美女) of ancient China.  \r
Su Shi wrote two related poems with the same title. Both of them praise the beauty of West Lake and were written during the poet’s tenure as Prefect of Hangzhou. The first poem is about the poet welcoming his guests in the morning sun and drinking with them in the evening rain, indulging in the rainy scenery of West Lake. The second poem - the one quoted here, offers a more comprehensive description of the diversity and beauty of West Lake's scenery. """@en ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q18869077> ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """The brimming waves delight the eye on sunny days;\r
The dimming hills give a rare view in rainy haze.\r
The West Lake looks like the fair lady at her best;\r
Whether she is richly adorned or plainly dressed.\r
(translation by Xu Yuanchong)"""@en,
        """水光潋灧晴方好，\r
山色空蒙雨亦奇。\r
欲把西湖比西子，\r
淡妆浓抹总相宜。"""@zh,
        """水光瀲灧晴方好，\r
山色空濛雨亦奇。\r
欲把西湖比西子，\r
淡粧濃抹總相宜。"""@zh,
        """Shuǐ guāng liàn yàn qíng fāng hǎo,\r
shān sè kōng méng yǔ yì qí.\r
Yù bǎ xīhú bǐ xī zi,\r
dànzhuāng nóng mǒ zǒng xiāngyí"""@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/93> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Byōdō-in Temple (平等院)"@en,
        " 平等院 "@zh,
        "Píngděng yuàn"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8070552> ;
    ns1:description "In this poem, composed by Zhao Puchu 赵朴初 (1907-2000), the poet  describes the ecstatic sensation while contemplating the sublime beauty of the most beautiful building in the temple, the \"Phoenix Hall\" (Fenghuang Tang 凤凰堂), built about a thousand years before. He compares its solemn beauty to the literary peaks reached during the period of full literary grandeur in the Tang dynasty (618-907). In the poet’s vision, Buddhism overloaded those peaks, because the \"divine breath added something that goes beyond what human nature requires\" (shengongyijiang 神工意匠)."@en ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """Gasp in awe,\r
Like never before,\r
In front of the Phoenix Hall,\r
One look\r
And I am back to a thousand years ago.\r
Buddhism\r
Alone\r
Equates the Tang’s glow,\r
And passes on\r
The sublime beauty of the divine.\r
(Translator: A. Iezzi)"""@en,
        """Sospirare,\r
come mai nella vita,\r
di fronte al Padiglione della Fenice,\r
ritrovarsi d’improvviso\r
mille anni orsono.\r
Il Buddhismo\r
solo\r
equipara i Tang nel bagliore,\r
istillandovi postilla\r
la bellezza sublime del divino.\r
(Translator: A. Iezzi)"""@it,
        """叹观止，\r
凤凰堂，\r
置身恍在千年上。\r
岂只是象教庄严继盛唐?\r
更别有\r
神工意匠。"""@zh,
        """Tàn guānzhǐ, \r
fènghuáng táng, \r
zhìshēn huǎng zài qiānnián shàng. \r
Qǐ zhǐshì xiàng jiào zhuāngyán jì shèng Táng? \r
Gèng bié yǒu \r
shén gōng yìjiàng."""@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/947> a ns3:Q1400264 ;
    ns2:P2561 "Chantry Dance Company" ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q846683> ;
    ns2:P453 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/943> ;
    ns2:P571 "2012-05"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    ns1:description """Chantry Dance was formed in May 2012 by co-Artistic Directors and choreographers Paul Chantry and Rae Piper. The company's first performance was in 2012 on the main stage at Sadler's Wells as part of the Chinese Calligraphy, Music and Dance Concert, and televised to mainland China. In 2013 Chantry Dance re-located from London to Grantham, East Midlands. From this base, the company has created a varied body of work which has included themic and abstract short pieces, full-length narrative ballets, touring shows, site-specific works, outdoor shows and films. The Chantry Dance Company explores how contemporary dance bears witness to the joys, passions and pains of humankind. The Company highlights dance as a liberating art, bringing freedom of body and mind to performers, participants and audience, and promote the understanding of and participation in Contemporary Dance. The company reveals the meaningful impact that dance can have - how it can effect joy, reflection, hope, and change.\r
The company is composed of five dancers in total: 2 British, 1 Italian, 1 Spanish, 1 Portuguese, respectevely Rae Piper, Louis McMiller, Alessia Lugoboni, Melanie Lopez, Catarina Carvalho.""" ;
    ns1:hasArtisticCollectiveType <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2393314> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/95> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Rouged Lips (点绛唇 )"@en,
        "点绛唇 "@zh,
        "Diǎn jiàng chún"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q716950> ;
    ns1:description "This poem was composed by Wang Yucheng 王禹偁 (954-1001), a famous poet/scholar of the Song dynasty (960-1279), during his exile in the South of the Empire. In this poem, he expresses his inner emotions through a rich imaginary that allows him to skillfully establish similarities with nature. Empathy with nature, pain for an unjust exile, pervasive loneliness, and idyllic scenes are the central themes of the poem. These themes are recurrent in Chinese poetry of all times."@en ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """Laden with frowning cloud and steeped in tearful rain,\r
The southern shores still beautiful remain.\r
In riverside village flanked with fishermen’s fair,\r
A lonely wreath of slender smoke wafts in the air.\r
Afar a row of wild geese fly,\r
Weaving a letter in the sky.\r
What have I done in days gone by?\r
Gazing from the balustrade,\r
Could I weave my way as far as they?\r
(Translator: Xu Yuanchong)"""@en,
        """Pioggia di risentimento, nuvole d’ansia,\r
e la beltà imperitura del paesaggio.\r
Sulle rive del fiume Azzurro\r
mercanti pescatori,\r
e di fumo\r
un filamento\r
dai fumaiuoli.\r
All’orizzonte oche selvatiche,\r
sedule a un lungo vagare,\r
di lontano librarsi in volo\r
come punti cuciti a ricamare.\r
Un momento in una vita\r
di labor contrita\r
rimaner fissi a rimirare.\r
Chi comprender un uomo può\r
nel suo sentire\r
gravato\r
su una balaustra\r
lo sguardo\r
a di lontano gettar?\r
(Translator: A. Iezzi)"""@it,
        """雨恨云愁，\r
江南依旧称佳丽。\r
水村渔市，\r
一缕孤烟细。\r
天际征鸿，\r
遥认行如缀。\r
平生事，\r
此时凝睇，\r
谁会凭栏意!"""@zh,
        """Yǔ hèn yún chóu,\r
jiāngnán yījiù chēng jiālì. \r
Shuǐcūn yú shì, \r
yī lǚ gū yān xì.\r
Tiānjì zhēng hóng, \r
yáo rèn háng rú zhuì. \r
Píngshēng shì, \r
cǐ shí níng dì, \r
shéi huì pínglán yì!"""@zh-Latn .

