        <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/cursive_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1009.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 2  The Bamboo Path (《画竹》，《 画竹寄友人》)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU is part of the content written by Wang Dongling with white acrylic on transparent propylene panels, which were installed together with 201 bamboo poles to create the installation titled “The Bamboo Path (Zhu jing 竹径)”. \r
On the transparent panels, Wang used his distinctive Chaos Script (luanshu 乱书) to write poems about bamboo, as well as prefaces and afterwords (tiba 提跋) on paintings featuring and praising bamboo, written by famous poets and artists of past dynasties. Some examples are: "Spontaneous Verses at a Mountain Retreat in Late Spring" by Zhaolin 卢照邻 (ca. 634–ca. 684 or 686), "At Yuqian, Monk’s Green Bamboo Pavilion" by Su Shi 苏轼  (1037—1101), "Ink Bamboo for a Friend" by Ni Zan 倪瓒 (1301–1374), "Painting Bamboo" by Xu Wenchang 徐文长 (1521–1593), "Ink Bamboo" by Jin Nong 金农 (1687–1763), "Bamboo and Rocks" by Zheng Xie 郑燮 (1693—1765), and "Bamboo in the Rain" by Huang Yi 黄易 (1744–1802).\r
"""@en ;
