    ns1:description """"Four Books and Five Classics (Sishu Wujing 四书五经)" created by Chen Guangwu in 2008, involved a unique artistic process. Chen first wrote the contents of the books with ink on paper. Then, he macerated all the xuan paper with the text in a basin of water, making paper pulp, made of the fibers of paper, this process is tipically used to recycle paper or other materials. The process of making paper pulp involves breaking down the raw materials into small fibers, which are then mixed with water to form a slurry. \r
The process of making paper pulp begins with breaking down raw materials into small fibers, which are then mixed with water to create a slurry. This slurry undergoes further processing to eliminate water and impurities, resulting in a uniform mixture. Usually, the pulp is spread out and dried to form new sheets of paper, but in Chen's approach, he manipulates the black slurp to resemble a paper sculpture in the shape of the original books. The paper obvioulsy differs significantly with textures, thicknesses, and qualities of paper that differ significantly from the originals. Through  these meticulous techniques, Chen dissolve the texts into sculptures of paper pulps, exploring themes of cultural transformation and loss, and he offers a unique perspective on a culture he believes is eroding.\r
The Four Books (四书) and the Five Classics (五经) refer to the Confucian classics, essential texts in Confucianism that cover a wide range of topics such as ethics, governance, and philosophy. The Four Books are Chinese classic texts selected by Zhu Xi in the Song dynasty, as an introduction to Confucianism. They include the Great Learning (Da xue 大学), The Analects of Confucius (Lunyu 论语), The Doctrine of the Mean (Zhongyong 中庸), and the Mencius (Mengzi 孟子).\r
The Five Classics is a corpus of five ancient Chinese books used by Confucianism as the basis of studies. According to tradition, they were compiled or edited by Confucius himself. They are: Classic of Changes (Yijing 易经), Classic of Poetry (Shijing 诗经), Book of Rites (Liji 礼记), Book of Documents (Shujing 书经) and Spring and Autumn Annals (Chunqiu 春秋)."""@en ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q860861> ;
    ns1:hasSeal false ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q614467> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1130> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/788> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Continuously Spreading Event and Balls of Characters Combinations (连续扩散时间：字球组合)"@en,
        "连续扩散时间：字球组合"@zh,
        "Liánxù kuòsàn shíjiān: Zì qiú zǔhé"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1640 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1071> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P580 "1992-08-20"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "1992-08"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    ns1:description """The exhibition "Continuously Spreading Event and Combinations: Balls of Characters", curated by Fan Di'an, took place at the Art Gallery of Beijing International Art Palace (Beijing Guoji Yiyuan Meishuguan 北京国际艺苑美术馆) on August 20, 1992.\r
In this exhibition Wang Nanming introduced the concept of Balls of Characters to the public for the first time. Alongside his artwork, Wang Nanming presented a video providing insights into the origins of the piece and the techniques used in its installation to allow viewers to explore the artistic process and the underlying message.\r
Following this exhibition, Wang Nanming continued to  experiment the spheres with various arrangements. The artist realized several innovative compositions. For example, he placed them on significant landmarks like the ruins of the Old Summer Palace, adding layers of meaning to his artwork by juxtaposing ancient history with modern artistic expression. Wang Nanming also elaborated and incorporated his "Balls of Characters" into his artistic vision realizing cascading installations and functional furniture to furnish entire rooms."""@en ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1128> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1147> a ns1:TraditionalArtwork ;
    ns2:P1343 "Iezzi, A. (2019) The Kwanyin Clan Modern Literati Graffiti Writers. An Aesthetic and Text Analysis of Their Main Artworks, Annali di Ca’ Foscari. Serie orientale, n. 55, pp. 395-448: https://edizionicafoscari.unive.it/media/pdf/article/annali-di-ca-foscari-serie-orientale/2019/55/art-10.14277-AnnOr-2385-3042-2019-01-013_XWgnTc2.pdf"@en ;
    ns2:P1476 "Travelers Among Mountains and Streams (溪山行旅图)"@en,
        "溪山行旅图"@zh,
        "Xī shānxíng lǚ tú"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1278559> ;
    ns2:P571 "1000"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "\"Travelers among Mountains and Streams\" is a large hanging scroll (ink and light color on silk, 206.3 x 103.3 cm, National Palace Museum, Taipei). The Author of Travelers Among Mountains and Streams is Fan Kuan 范寬 (ca. 950-1032), one of the most important Chinese landscape painters during the Song Dynasty. This painting is considered one of the finest monumental Chinese landscape paintings. It also became a model for later Chinese painters. Fan Kuan based his painting on the Daoist principle of becoming one with nature. The classic Chinese perspective of three planes is evident - near, middle (represented by water and mist), and far. Unlike earlier examples of Chinese landscape art, the grandeur of nature is the main theme, rather than merely providing a backdrop. A packhorse train can barely be seen emerging from a wood at the base of a towering precipice. The painting's style encompasses archaic conventions dating back to the Tang dynasty. The clusters of vegetation at the top of the tall mountain here are actually distant forests clinging to precarious perches. Running along the central axis of the scroll, the central mountain dominates the scene in a classic example of Northern Song monumental landscape painting. The rooftops of a building complex stand out in the right middle-ground. By the cluster of rocks in the right foreground is a path on which a mule train makes its way. A cascade as slender as silk falls from the heights above, culminating in the stream rushing down in eddies towards the foreground. From near to far, Fan Kuan has described with realistic detail the solemn grandeur of a majestic landscape. Fan Kuan rendered the mountains and slopes with jagged outline strokes and filled them with brush dabs like raindrops – techniques that highlight the monumental and eternal features of the mountains. To the right of the mule train, among the leaves, is the signature of Fan Kuan, a final touch by an artist to epitomize the insignificance of humans (including himself) compared to Nature. The signature was discovered by Li Lincan (李霖燦), a scholar at the National Palace Museum in Taipei, on August 5, 1958."@en ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q191163> ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q15907822> .

