    ns2:P170 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q626360> ;
    ns2:P571 "1113"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "\"A Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains\" was painted by Wang Ximeng 王希孟 (1096-1119), one of the most renowned court painters of the Northern Song period (960-1126). This painting is one of the largest in Chinese history, and the only Wang’s surviving work. The painter used blue and green mineral colours for decoration, so this genre of painting is known as ‘blue and green landscape’ (qinglü shanshui 青绿山水). This technique was a recognised form of painting as early as the seventh century. The painting, finished by Wang when he was only 18 in 1113, has been described as one of the greatest works of Chinese art. It is in the permanent collection of the Palace Museum in Beijing. The scroll's size is 51.5 by 1191.5 cm. The scenery in the painting was later identified as Mount Lu and Poyang Lake in Jiujiang. Wang Ximeng used typical technique of multiple perspectives, painting mountainous landscapes naturally separated into six sections by bridges and water. Mountains were described as being in the style of Southern China; it can indicate that Wang Ximeng was from that region."@en ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q191163> ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q112971367> .

