    ns1:description """This performance is realized by the female choreographer Liu Qi 刘琦,  and consists of like a poem of movements that originate from the different styles of Chinese calligraphy: from slow and delicate movements to sinuous, fast, and expressive gestures; and from the oracle bones script style to the wild cursive, in an uninterrupted, changing, and iridescent flow.   Since its premiere in 2005, “Upon Calligraphy” has been invited to perform and tour continuously and has become one of the most popular repertoires in the corps de ballet. In 2011, the choreography expanded and today the piece is known as “Beyond Calligraphy” (Linchi wu mo 临池舞墨). The debut took place at the Friendship Theatre of Guangzhou on March 3, 2011, and then in Beijing on March 13 and 14, 2011. Liu Qi has further developed the work from a forty-three-minute work to approximately seventy minutes in length. The first part of the piece retains the dance structure of the calligraphy representations always called “Upon Calligraphy”, while a new epilogue is added, called “Ink Wash Landscape” (Shan shui 山水) which pays homage to the spiritual essence of ink wash painting.  The two-act production suggests a journey from mechanical, assertive, and staccato choreography in the first half, to a multimedia second half. The music, composed by Dickson Dee (Li Chin Sung 李勁松, b. 1969),  matches the dancers’ movement qualities with clanging sounds, squeaks, and rattles in the first act, followed by more harmonious and soothing acoustics after intermission. Costumes feature monochromatic ensembles, with the cast of thirteen dancers in either black, white, brown, or grey outfits. These colours recall both calligraphy (black of ink and white of paper) and also the bronze or grey stone supports, typical of jiaguwen or inscriptions on steles. The production’s projections reinforce the contrasts of the two-part structure of “Beyond Calligraphy”, juxtaposing robotic and acrobatic interactions during the first half with more sensuous, slow-moving montages in part two. The first part of the performance begins with Chinese characters engraved on oracle bones, projected scrolling onto the background. The performance continues with characters in small seal script, clerical script, regular script, and cursive script. The dancers perform very technical and acrobatic movements that reproduce the character traits, such as bent knees, flexed feet and arms that form perpendicular angles.\r
\r
In the second half of the performance, entitled “Ink Wash Landscape”, the projections instead present natural landscapes such as mountains, trees, and water. Sometimes these images merge with the dancers' bodies, making their torsos appear to be imprinted with Chinese characters or camouflaged with flowering trees. This second act tests the dancers’ ability to move as slowly as possible. The dancers wear long white dresses, thus enhancing the meditative quality of this section.  After having told the story and the evolution of writing through the body and movement, in this second part of the performance dancers let themselves be immersed in nature; for them, it means letting go completely after appreciating the charm of the rhythm. In the dance of Shanshui, “the real highlight is the movements expressed by the dancers, silently showing the body lines that naturally grow in the mountains and rivers, symbiotically dipping between heaven and earth”.  The “roots” of the dancers are their feet which pierce the ground beneath them and the branches reaching towards the sky are their arms, thus opening their body to breathe and absorb the essence of nature. There are no “technicisms” in the movements, it's just a feeling that passes through the body. Borrowing San Yu’s (Chang Yu 常玉, 1901-1966)   paintings as an example, the tension of lines that he expressed when he introduced Chinese brushwork into Western oil paintings clearly emerges. With this final, one could almost think of a “temporal circle” that opens with the oracle bones inscriptions, which arise from the observation of natural phenomena, until arriving, after having experimented with different forms of writing, at the highest point that it means for man reconnect with nature itself.\r
\r
Over the past twelve years, more than 100 performances have been performed in Denmark, Poland, the USA, Canada, the Netherlands, Russia, Italy, Germany, Belgium, Switzerland, and other countries. One example is at the Edinburgh Fringe Festival on 12 August 2017."""@en ;
