    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1327.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """"Qian"  is an artwork created by Tang Kaizhi in 2008. The title of the work is inspired to the Thousand Character Classic (Qianziwen 千字文) and it is one of the artworks included in the "New Tang Book" (Xin Tang Shu 新唐书), developed during the artist's doctoral studies. \r
Measuring 280 x 139 cm, the piece is made with white xuan paper and accumulated ink, a technique involving  the repeated layering of ink to write the single character "thousand" (qian 千) for a thousand time throughout the surface. The piece is named "Thousand Character Classic" not for its educational content, as the title would suggest, but for its visual tension using the structure of the character "thousand". In commentary on the piece, Tang Kaizhi noted: “What I want to convey is that my work is not a form of contemporary ink painting. In other words, I am not a noble ink artist. I once aspired to be such an artist, like the master Huang Binhong whom I admire, but I no longer think this way. I believe it is not important what category my work falls into. Through the long process of repeatedly creating with the same element, I have greatly improved and refined my technique and concepts. Whether these works are 'modern calligraphy' or 'abstract art', they might be either or neither.  Contemporary art encompasses diverse characteristics, and the contemporary art scene is open and free. Artists' creations and thoughts are constantly changing and enriching. I hope my works are unprecedented and original, though I know this is impossible. They will inevitably be studied, categorized, and evaluated.”\r
This artwork was exhibited at the second “Writing Non-Writing” exhibition in Hangzhou in 2010. """ ;
