<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1339> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Hidden Ink Calligraphy (隐墨书)"@en,
        "隐墨书"@zh,
        "Yǐn mò shū"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/801> ;
    ns2:P580 "2011"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1339.jpg> ;
    ns1:description " In the realm of Chinese contemporary art, Wang Yong is reknowned for his \"Hidden Ink\" (Yin mo 隐墨). In 2011, he named his works \"Hidden Ink,\" using  'chan (禅)' as a methodology and applying 'chan' approaches to contemplate and address issues. In his works, characters are rendered in a light, elegant, and hazy style, reflecting the artist’s exploration of the aesthetic possibilities of ink and script. According to Gao Tianmin: \"For Wang Yong, the concept of \"Yin\" (隐, concealment or obscurity) carries a certain philosophical significance—one that exists between Eastern and Western thought, exploring and contemplating the essence of phenomena through the intertextuality and complementarity of phenomenology and Daoism. However, what he engages with is not the physical object itself but rather the cultural and artistic existence of the “object”." ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1339_HR.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2038> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1357> a ns3:Q215627 ;
    ns2:P103 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P106 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q121594>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3303330> ;
    ns2:P19 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q21887022> ;
    ns2:P21 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6581072> ;
    ns2:P2561 "Cai Mengxia 蔡梦霞" ;
    ns2:P27 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P569 "1972"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P800 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1358> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1357.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Cai Mengxia is a contemporary Chinese calligrapher. Cai Mengxia is currently an associate professor at the School of Chinese Painting, Central Academy of Fine Arts in Beijing. She is a member of the Women's Work Committee of the China Calligraphers Association.  Cai Mengxia was born in 1972 in Nanning, Guangxi. In 1989, she began studying calligraphy under Chen Guobin and Zhang Yuxiang. Thanks to their guidance, in 1993 she participated in the "Fifth National Exhibition of Calligraphy and Seal Engraving for Young Artists", where she emerged as one of the winners (the only woman). This event made her a key figure, and the only woman involved in the so-called "Guangxi Phenomenon," associated with this exhibition and named because four of the ten winners came from the Guangxi region. She was also the only woman to be part of the so-called "School of Academics," a movement inaugurated in 1994. The school held its first group exhibition in Korea in 1997 and then in China in 1998, first in Beijing and later in Hangzhou, thus marking its official establishment. Cai Mengxia obtained a bachelor's degree in calligraphy from the China Academy of Art  (CAA) in Hangzhou in 1997, and a master's degree from the China Academy of Fine Arts (CAFA) in Beijing in 2005, under the supervision of Professor Wang Yong. In 2008, also at CAFA, she received her Ph.D. with Professors Xue Yongnian and Wang Yong as her advisors. Cai Mengxia received the first doctorate in calligraphy ever awarded to a woman in the People's Republic of China, marking a significant milestone in the history of Chinese calligraphy.  From 2008 to 2010, she conducted postdoctoral research at the Academy of Arts & Design at Tsinghua University, collaborating with Professor Du Dakai. \r
Her artistic achievements include first prize at the 5th National Calligraphy and Seal Engraving Exhibition, and participation in the 4th and 7th editions of the same, as well as in the first three "Popular Calligraphy Style" and "Popular Seal Style" Invitational Exhibitions. Cai is also the author of "Wei Jin shufa shijue xingshi yanjiu" (Research on the Visual Forms of Wei and Jin Dynasty Calligraphy), further contributing to academic and artistic dialogues in her field.""" ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Academic"@en ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1357.jpg> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/136> a ns3:Q215627 ;
    ns2:P103 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P106 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3303330>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q483501> ;
    ns2:P19 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q425492> ;
    ns2:P21 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6581097> ;
    ns2:P2561 "Su Yuanzhang 苏元章 " ;
    ns2:P27 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P569 "1924-11-02"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P570 "2002-10-19"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P742 "Su Yuan 苏原" ;
    ns2:P800 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/133> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns1:description """Su Yuanzhang, also known as Su Yuan 苏原, was a Chinese calligrapher born in 1924 in Luxian County, Sichuan Province. He graduated from the Law Department of Chongqing University in 1950. Immersed in a rich family tradition from an early age, Su Yuanzhang developed a passion for calligraphy and studied the masterpieces of Ouyang Xun, Wang Xizhi, and Zhao Mengfu, as well as the steles and calligraphic inscriptions from the Han and Wei dynasties.\r
Specializing in the running script (xingshu 行书), Su Yuanzhang is celebrated for his ability to blend traditional aesthetics with innovative techniques. His works are characterized by a disciplined form and an elegant, unrestrained grace. Since 1985, his calligraphy has been featured in numerous prestigious exhibitions. His works have been included in the "Modern Calligraphy Works Anthology" (Xiandai shufa zuopin xuanji 现代书法作品选集). Su Yuanzhang was a member of the China Calligraphers Association, an executive council member of the Modern Calligraphy and Painting Society, and served as the chief editor of the Culture and Life Department at China Youth Daily.""" ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Familial"@en,
        "Self-Taught"@en .

