    ns2:P2048 "32 cm" ;
    ns2:P2049 "163 cm" ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42519> ;
    ns2:P571 "2007"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P608 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1749> ;
    ns1:description "The digital artwork \"Mount Gushan 1\" is is a six-meter-long scroll inspired by the scenery around Mount Gushan (Solitary Hill) located in West Lake in Hangzhou, China.  In this work Wang Tiande merges calligraphy, photography, and the use of destructive fire in a unique artistic approach. He arranges piles of ash from burned printed copies of renowned calligraphic texts, forming compositions that resemble traditional mountain landscapes. Although the calligraphy itself is not visible, its presence is subtly referenced in the labels provided by the artist. As Zheng Shengtian has observed, \"Every time Tiande burns paper, 'ashes of words' are left on the floor of his studio. Now, he piles up these ashes in the shape of mountains. Then, by using a camera and computer, he recreates remarkably life-like landscapes. At first glance, this might seem playful, but it actually embodies profound philosophical meanings. The ashes of traditional culture, reshaped by the artist, are infused with new life and vitality.\"" ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q125191>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q191163>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q860372> ;
