    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1387.jpeg> ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q614467> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1394> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Blackism (黑色主义)"@en,
        "黑色主义系列"@zh,
        "Hēisè zhǔyì xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/84> ;
    ns2:P580 "1990"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "1990"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1394.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Blackism (Heise zhuyi 黑色主义) is one of Luo Qi’s most famous series and a prime example of Chinese "abstract calligraphic art." It marks the culmination of his experimental research on calligraphic lines, begun in 1986.\r
Created in 1990, Blackism was among Luo Qi’s first series to receive international recognition. It aligns with the "modern calligraphy" movement that emerged in China from the mid-1980s.\r
Within this movement, one key artistic direction sought to merge traditional calligraphic aesthetics with the abstract concept of graphic signs inspired by Western art. Luo Qi’s Blackism is one of successful examples of this synthesis. It resonates with the ideas of "anti-calligraphy" (fanshufa 反书法) and "non-calligraphy" (feishufa 非书法), which emerged in the early 1990s after three major exhibitions: the Shanghai Modern Calligraphy Exhibition (1991) and the First (1993) and Second (1995) Calligraphism Exhibitions in Zhengzhou and Weihai. \r
These exhibitions redefined the boundaries between calligraphy and abstract art. In such works, Chinese characters are deconstructed, detached from their linguistic function. They were reinterpreted as pure visual expressions, emphasizing the aesthetic beauty of the calligraphic line and the expressive energy of the gesture.""" ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1394_HR.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1395> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1409> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/100>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/277>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/84> ;
    ns2:P1476 "First Exhibition of Calligraphyism (第一次书法主义展)  "@en,
        "第一次书法主义展"@zh,
        "Dì yī cì shūfǎzhǔyì zhǎn "@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1640 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/84> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q106321016>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q30340> ;
    ns2:P580 "1993-08-18"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "1993-08-25"^^xsd:date ;
    ns1:description "The First Exhibition of Calligraphyism (di yi ci shufazhuyi zhan 第一次书法主义展) was an avant-garde exhibition held in 1993 at the Art Museum of Zhengzhou (Henan Zhengzhou meishuguan 河南郑州美术馆). It was conceptualized by Luo Qi in 1990 during a visit to Shanghai, before his departure for the United States.  The exhibition took shape with the support of Bai Di 白砥, editor of the magazine Shufa Yanjiu, and the organizational help of Xing Shizhen and Xu Encun from Henan Normal University. Luo Qi wrote two texts for the exhibition: Introduction to the Doctrine of Writing Action (shufazhuyi daoyan 书写主义导言) and Manifesto of Calligraphyism (shufazhuyi xuanyan 书法主义宣言), which became title and guiding philosophy of the exhibition. The exhibition showcased a radical shift towards abstract calligraphy, where the traditional linguistic and structural components of Chinese characters were abandoned. The calligraphic strokes became free-form expressive marks, breaking the compositional, stylistic, and technical constraints inherited from tradition. Many artists participated to the exhibitions such as Luo Qi 洛齐, Bai Di 白砥, Xing Shizhen 邢世真, Zhang Qiang 张强, Shao Yan 邵岩, Li Qiang 李强, Ma Xiao 马啸, Xu Encun 徐恩存 and others.  Notable pieces includeed were Xing Shizhen’s Untitled (Wu ti 无题), and  Luo Qi's \"Why Are You Worried?\" (Weihe youlü 为何忧虑), a large ink-on-paper piece. However, the exhibition faced significant controversy and was shut down only a week after its opening in Zhengzhou, due to objections to the concept of \"calligraphyism\". Despite this, the exhibition gained wide media attention, and by 1994, major critics and scholars were discussing \"calligraphyism\" in journals like Shufa Bao." ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1412> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1429> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P144 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/729> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1096367>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q220797>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q468777>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q748> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11474>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/regular_script> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU Double Hook and Simple Heart  (不生不灭不增不减)" ;
    ns1:description "This CWU consists of the  eight characters created in a monumental scale by Hua Jun in his calligraphic work Double Hook and Simple Heart (Shuanggou Suxin 双钩素心). Using the traditional double-hook technique, he inscribed 8 characters from the Heart Sutra (Xīnjīng 心经) on eco-friendly covering film at Hangzhou’s Tianziling landfill. The phrase written by the artist in large characters, with simple, majestic strokes is \"Neither born nor perishing, neither increasing nor decreasing\" (Bù shēng bù miè, bù zēng bù jiǎn 不生不灭 不增不减), which derives from the key phrase in the Heart Sutra: \"Shariputra! All phenomena are empty, non-born, non-destructed, non-dirty, non-clean, non-increased, non-decreased\" (Shèlì zǐ! Shì zhū fǎ kōng xiāng bù shēng bù miè, bù gòu bù jìng, bù zēng bù jiǎn 舍利子！是诸法空相不生不灭，不垢不净，不增不减), from which he removed \"non-dirty, non-clean\". The eight characters are written in black ink with a large brush, using simplified characters (jianti zi 简体字) in Regular Script (kaishu 楷书). The characters are arranged in four vertical columns of hollow strokes, each column containing two characters following the traditional Chinese calligraphic direction—arranged in vertical columns from top to bottom, right to left. Each individual character spanned approximately 200 square meters, though their exact dimensions varied, and together they covered an expansive total area of around 3,000 square meters (Hua 2016: 115). These eight characters stand solid stand solid as mountains, inscribed upon the earth, upon a place where forgotten waste is buried. However, the writer is waiting for new filling and growth, awaiting the blooming of spring and the harvest of autumn, the shifting of stars, and the rotation of the heavens (Xu 2011: 9). " ;
    ns1:directionality "Traditional" ;
    ns1:hasBackgroundColour <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444> ;
    ns1:hasBigCharactersCalligraphy true ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicLine true ;
    ns1:hasCharacterForm <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q185614> ;
    ns1:hasColophon false ;
    ns1:hasSignificance "Meaningful writing" ;
    ns1:isUnitOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1428>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2000> ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q614467> ;
    ns1:numberOfCharacters 8 ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation "\"Neither born nor perishing, neither increasing nor decreasing.\""@en,
        "不生不灭不增不减"@zh,
        "Bù shēng bù miè bù zēng bù jiǎn"@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1435> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8686> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Black Series (黑色系列)"@en,
        "黑色系列"@zh,
        "Hēisè xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/788> ;
    ns2:P580 "1985"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "1990"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1435.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "\"Black Series\" is a series of the late 1980s including abstract modern calligraphy produced in China by Wang Nanming. In his view, as well as being an elegant traditional system of writing and the basis of an evocative form of modern art, calligraphy could be regarded as representing the darker side of Chinese society (Barrass 2002, 251-252). He felt that over the centuries its purpose had been perverted in that it had been deployed as a mechanism for social control. Not only had the complexity of its scripts limited the spread of education and so blocked routes to privilege, but a cult of respect for calligraphic skills had been encouraged which ensured that the young deferred to their elders and the illiterate to the educated. This feeling of oppression has been captured in the 'Black Series', where the painted black spaces prevail on those kept plain white." ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1435.png> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1437> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/145> a ns3:Q43229 ;
    ns2:P2561 "Amateur Architecture Studio" ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4970> ;
    ns2:P527 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/143>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/146> ;
    ns2:P571 "1997"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "Amateur Architecture Studio (founded in 1997 by Wang Shu and Lu Wenyu), based in Hangzhou, works with local traditions of craftsmanship, set against modern architectural grandeur in Chinese megacities. Their projects are shown at international exhibitions such as the Venice Biennale of Architecture (“Tiles Garden”, 2006, “Decay of a Dome”, 2010). Wang Shu has been granted many international awards such as the Pritzker Prize, and, together with Lu Wenyu, the Schelling Architektur Preis. They teach at the China Academy of Art in Hangzhou." .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1450> a ns3:Q4502142 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q210287> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Tian Xiang (天象)"@en,
        "天象"@zh,
        "Tiānxiàng"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/231> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1>,
