    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1450.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "Tian Xiang (天象) is an installation of 24 uniquely shaped stones, in different shapes and sizes ranging in size from approximately 150 cm to 330 cm. The 24 stones are engraved with the Chinese twenty-four solar terms (jieqi 节气), which divide the solar year into 24 two-week periods, such as jingzhe 惊蛰, or “insects awaken”, marking the first stirring of hibernating insects. Each stone reflects a unique aspect of the infinite universe, crafted from massive natural rocks sourced from the depths of the boundless sea near Shandong province, historically known as Lu 鲁, the birthplace of Confucianism. For this reason, the rocks are referred to as “ru rocks” (ru shi 儒石, meaning Rocks of Confucianism). The calligraphy wraps around each side of the ru rocks, revealing only one or a few strokes from any given angle. It is only through observing the entire installation that viewers realize each stone represents a distinct solar term, symbolizing the vastness of the universe. “The characters carved into the ru rocks are smooth on the surface but firm in essence, embodying timeless Oriental elegance, in a pursue of a perfect balance of stiffness and fluidity” (Gu 2018, 5). The uncoloured strokes are delicately carved into the rocks’ natural texture with gentle transitions, reflecting the artist’s intention to integrate Chinese views ofthe universe and daily life into these naturally shaped rocks. This work expresses a profound respect for nature, standing in contrast to the excessive self-expression prevalent in contemporary art, design and architecture. As light changes with the shifting seasons and time of day, it emphasizes the significance of each solar term." ;
