<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/150> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/148> ;
    ns2:P1476 "From Ink to Apparel III"@en,
        "读衣 III"@zh,
        "Dú yī III"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1640 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/814> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1867> ;
    ns2:P580 "2018-10-20"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2018-11-05"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P664 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/814> ;
    ns2:P856 <https://en.tongyangtze.com/crossitem?id=6>,
        <https://tongyangtze.com/crossitem?id=6> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/150.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The exhibition “From Ink to Apparel III” is part of the interdisciplinary project “From Ink to Apparel,” initiated by the Taiwanese calligrapher Tong Yang-tze in 2016. “From Ink to Apparel III” took place from 10 October to 5 November 2018 in Taipei, Taiwan. The purpose of this exhibition is to promote Chinese culture in Taiwan through calligraphy art. Curator Tong Yang-Tze invites diverse and talented fashion designers of Chinese ethnicity to participate, create, and accumulate design context inspired by the calligraphy lines. “From Ink to Apparel III” stays true to its history of crossover collaborations and its exploration into calligraphy’s role in contemporary art. The exhibition theme is “wú” and features three designers from Taiwan, two from Beijing and one from UK: CHOU Yu-Ying, CHEN Shao-Yen, TSAI Yi-Fen, LI Ying-Jun, ZHOU You, and PAN Bernice.T heir exhibits will showcase thirty collections that illustrate the unique style and energy of each designer.\r
“Wu 無" (emptiness) is the theme of this exhibition, with fourteen fonts which are variations of the cursive script, running script, regular script, clerical script, and seal script. The forms of these characters are distinct, but designers must interpret them visually to form structures, materials, and prints from the fluidity of calligraphy lines and the spaces amongst them."""@en ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2585> ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/150.jpg> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1513> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1057925>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3974773> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q9186> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1088>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/small_seal_script>,
        <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/talismanic_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1513.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 1 Northeast (东北)" ;
    ns1:description """This Calli-Writing Unit features a large-scale tag in Chinese characters reading "Dongbei 東北," executed using only two colors: black and blue. "Dongbei" translates to "northeast" in English, referring to the northeastern region of China, which comprises the provinces of Liaoning, Jilin, and Heilongjiang. The artist selected this tag as a nod to his origins in one of these provinces.\r
The characters allude to the zhuanshu 篆书 (seal) style of Daoist talismanic script (fulu 符箓 or fuwen 符文), characterized by its twisted, irregular, and elongated strokes that sometimes resemble Chinese characters. Dongbay accentuates the strokes of "Dongbei" by incorporating small blue circles that evoke talismanic stars, reinforcing the mystical and spiritual symbolism of Daoist talismans.\r
The vertical arrangement of the blue circles recalls the structure of bamboo strips (jiace 简册), a traditional medium for writing in ancient China. By integrating these blue circles, the artist bridges ancient cultural heritage with modern aesthetics.\r
At the same time, this CWU appears to evoke imagery of "war or combat robots." The extended vertical strokes suggest powerful, grounded legs, while the arms create a visual contrast between futuristic technological elements and the mystical qualities of talismanic symbols. This juxtaposition underscores a tension between the esoteric and the mechanical, enriching the artwork's conceptual depth."""@en ;
