<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1515> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q345844> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1860>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q9186> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1088>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/bird_insect_script>,
        <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/small_seal_script>,
        <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/talismanic_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1515.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 2 Northeast (黄祸+ YP)" ;
    ns1:description """This Calli-Writing Unit features the characters "Huanghuo 黄祸" (Yellow Peril), inscribed on the right side of the large tag in small, heavily reworked forms that resemble xiaozhuan 小篆, or small seal script. The squared shapes of the characters subtly reference the niaochongshu 鸟虫书, or birds-and-worms script, further enriching the visual complexity.\r
This CWU draws upon the zhuanshu 篆书 or seal script style of Daoist talismanic writing (fulu 符箓 or fuwen 符文), characterized by its twisted, irregular, and elongated strokes that sometimes evoke Chinese characters. \r
Dongbay enhances the characters in "Huanghuo 黄祸" by adding decorative swirls and squiggles to the strokes, capturing the essence of talismanic forms. These ornamental flourishes contribute to a distinctive "Chinese graffiti style" for the New Era, offering an alternative to the traditional motifs of arrows, tribal designs, and spades often associated with graffiti's wild style.\r
At the same time, the artwork suggests the imagery of "war or combat robots," particularly through the depiction of the character huo 祸, whose extended vertical strokes evoke powerful, grounded legs. The "arms" of the character emphasize a contrast between technological elements and the esoteric, magical qualities tied to talismans. This interplay between futuristic and mystical themes imbues the artwork with a layered meaning, blending traditional Chinese symbolism with modern, technological motifs.\r
The lettering of the tag "YP" evokes “pixação graffiti”, a form of graffiti that originated in Brazil, particularly in São Paulo, characterized by a distinctive, cryptic style of lettering. It often features tall, thin, angular letters painted in black or other dark colors on buildings, walls, and other public spaces, typically in hard-to-reach places. """@en ;
