<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1525> a ns3:Q17514 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Violet (紫罗兰)"@en,
        "紫罗兰"@zh,
        "紫羅蘭"@zh,
        "Zǐluólán"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1523> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1454272> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P2048 "5 m"@en ;
    ns2:P2049 "2 m"@en ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q16572> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3257809>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q429220> ;
    ns2:P571 "2020-12-05"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q10920885>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1454272>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q428124> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1525.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The artwork “Violet (Ziluolan 紫羅蘭)” by Dohak625 exemplifies the artist's distinctive re-interpretation of calligraphy within the realm of anti-style graffiti. Created in 2020 in Guangzhou, this piece is one of his earlier works and is composed of the three Chinese characters in the title, referencing the tragic love story of Zhou Shoujuan (1895–1968), a Chinese writer. Zhou, born in an era when marriages were arranged without personal choice, was unable to be with the woman he truly loved. Her English name was Violet, which translates to Chinese as “Ziluolan 紫羅蘭”. Moved by this story, Dohak625 adopted “Ziluolan 紫羅蘭” as one of his primary tags, drawing a parallel between the personal tragedy of Zhou’s love and his own artistic expression.\r
The artwork marks a shift in Dohak625’s approach to color and form. While his usual process of color application tends to be more spontaneous, in “Violet (Ziluolan 紫羅蘭)”, he adopts a more deliberate and serious method of coloring. The piece, placed on the edge of a cliff, presents an intriguing juxtaposition of the vibrant violet hue against the starkness of the landscape, suggesting a deeper connection between the color and its natural inspiration. The deliberate use of purple, reminiscent of violet flowers, reflects the artist's intention to evoke nature through spray paint, an unusual yet thoughtful choice in his oeuvre.\r
The irregular, jagged shapes of the characters in the artwork align with the artist's anti-style aesthetic, evoking wild efflorescence. This artworks also conform to a contemporary trend in Chinese calligraphy known as “ugly calligraphy” (choushu 丑书), which emerged in the 1980s and is characterized by disharmony, asymmetry, and inconsistency.\r
The central stroke of the character “lan 蘭 (orchid)” extends beyond the wall, reaching toward the ground, a symbolic gesture connecting the artwork to the earth. This extension mirrors the relationship between a flower and the soil, as if anchoring the  “Violet” character to its natural roots.\r
By situating the word  “violet” and its floral elements in a demolition site amidst urban decay, Dohak625 seeks to restore the presence of nature in an otherwise degraded space. This piece operates as more than an act of personal expression; it serves as a critique of urban environments, a key tenet of graffiti culture. In this work, Dohak625’s focus extends beyond reclaiming urban spaces for human expression, to reclaiming them for nature. The graphic design of the characters suggests a sense of belonging, with the vertical strokes anchoring the characters to the earth, while the upper strokes form a protective, shelter-like structure, symbolically safeguarding nature within an industrialized, urban landscape."""@en ;
