<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1531> a ns3:Q17514 ;
    ns2:P1476 "The heavenly wind blows upon me (天风吹我)"@en,
        "天风吹我"@zh,
        "Tiān fēngchuī wǒ"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1523> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q177749>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q183670> ;
    ns2:P2048 "60 cm"@en ;
    ns2:P2049 "60 cm"@en ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q16572> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q429220> ;
    ns2:P571 "2021-07-30"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q30006487> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1531.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The artwork  “The Heavenly Wind Blows Upon Me (Tian feng chui wo 天风吹我)” was created by Dohak625 on July 30, 2021, in Guangzhou, using pink chalk on the ground. The phrase  “Tian feng chui wo 天風吹我”, written in non-simplified characters, is derived from a poem by Gong Zizhen 龚自珍 (1792–1841) from the Qing Dynasty, titled “Xiang Yue · Sky Wind Blows Me (《湘月·天风吹我》)”.\r
On the day the artwork was created, Dohak625 inscribed the first line of the poem onto the pavement as a tribute to Gong Zizhen's remarkable self-assurance and artistic legacy.\r
According to Dohak625 (interview with M. R. Bisceglia 2024) the nuances of Chinese poetry are often challenging to convey to non-native speakers. Simplified, these words might translate to  “I was here” in English, but they hold a deeper, more profound meaning. Rather than a mere statement of presence, the phrase evokes the image of a deity descending from the heavens, proclaiming,  “Here I am.”\r
While the artist does not liken themselves to a deity, they express deep admiration for Gong Zizhen's work, particularly this line, which left a lasting impression. The artist reflects on the historical context of the poem, noting that humility has been a defining characteristic of Chinese culture for millennia. However, Gong Zizhen's bold declaration,  “The sky wind blows me,” conveys his belief in his exceptional talent. He perceives himself as a figure of divine significance, as the term  “befell” or  “advent” is reserved for deities, unlike ordinary people who are simply  “born.” This statement reveals the poet's immense confidence, even arrogance, which the artist finds both admirable and striking.\r
From a purely stylistic perspective, he mixed his personal jagged/anti-style with the contemporary trend in Chinese calligraphy known as “ugly calligraphy” (choushu 丑书), which is characterized by disharmony, asymmetry, and inconsistency.\r
The use of pink chalk in Dohak625’s artwork likely carries both symbolic and practical meanings. Pink, often associated with softness, warmth, and compassion, stands in contrast to the poem's themes of divine presence and grandeur. This contrast may signify the artist’s effort to balance humility with boldness, reflecting the tension between the poem’s confident tone and the traditional Chinese cultural value of modesty. Furthermore, pink can symbolize life, renewal, and creativity, aligning with the artist's tribute to Gong Zizhen, a historical figure who challenged conventional modesty by celebrating his own brilliance.\r
Chalk, a temporary and easily erased medium, emphasizes the fleeting nature of the artwork. Its impermanence could represent the transient essence of human existence or life itself—themes that resonate with classical Chinese poetry. The chalk’s vulnerability to wind or rain may suggest the impermanence of art or the broader impact of life. By choosing chalk, Dohak625 taps into associations with childhood, innocence, and playfulness. This accessible medium suggests a commentary on the universality of art and the joy of creativity, transforming a simple material into something profound. Using the ground as a canvas brings the art into a public space, inviting interaction and engagement from passersby.\r
Moreover, the light, delicate quality of pink chalk contrasts with the weighty significance of Gong Zizhen’s poetry, creating an interplay between the contemporary, casual setting and the depth of the historical literary reference. This contrast bridges the gap between ancient and modern, the ephemeral and the eternal, reflecting the artist’s desire to create a dialogue between these two realms. Ultimately, the use of pink chalk serves as a personal and interpretive tool for Dohak625, emphasizing both the fleeting nature of art and its lasting resonance across time."""@en ;
