<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1538> a ns3:Q17514 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Just live (活着就行)"@en,
        "活着就行"@zh,
        "Huózhe jiùxíng"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1523> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q104482419>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q190273>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4105337>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q468777>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7798302> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1074584>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3133>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q843607> ;
    ns2:P571 "2021"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1538.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The artwork “Just live (Huo zhe jiu xing 活着就行)” was created in 2021. These four characters of the title "Huo zhe jiu xing 活着就行" mean "Just live" or "To breathe is to thrive." The phrase carries a philosophical tone, suggesting that mere existence is a form of thriving. It emphasizes the intrinsic value of life, highlighting resilience and survival, and implies that, despite challenges, the key is to keep going.\r
From a stylistic perspective, the four characters “Huo zhe jiu xing 活着就行” consistently adhere to Dohak's jagged/anti-style and the contemporary trend in Chinese calligraphy known as “ugly calligraphy” (choushu 丑书), which is characterized by disharmony, asymmetry, and inconsistency, this time painted in green (with a black outline). Each character are displayed on a single shutter from left to right. This type of writing evokes the big character calligraphy (dazi shufa 大字书法). The use of green and the jagged strokes reminiscent of leaves may serve as a “green” message, providing a splash of color against the otherwise gray and dreary shutters of the metropolis. Each character resembles a plant, contrasting not only with the gray of the shutter in the background but also with the squared characters of the shop signs. This highlights an interesting contrast between art and the urban environment, utilizing a style that not only captures attention but also conveys an ecological message. The choice of green for the characters, along with the jagged strokes that resemble leaves, emphasizes the idea of rebirth and connection to nature, creating a strong visual impact against the gray of the shutters. \r
Moreover, the contrast between the fluid, organic characters and the squared shop signs underscores a tension between the rigidity of commerce and the naturalness of life. This type of artistic intervention may also stimulate reflection on sustainability and the importance of preserving green spaces in cities.\r
Another important aspect of this "piece composition" is that each individual character is perfectly and harmoniously contained within a square, akin to the practice of writing within the "hanzigezi 汉字格子" grid, that is a grid composed of intersecting horizontal and vertical lines that create squares, traditionally utilized for practicing Chinese writing and calligraphy.\r
In this artwork, the artist enjoys creating graffiti on roll-up doors for two main reasons: i) First, these doors are only visible when the shop is closed, minimizing their impact on the environment. Compared to graffiti in other locations, the reduced visibility can increase the chances of the artwork lasting longer. ii) Second, the size of roll-up doors is particularly suitable for Chinese graffiti, especially when multiple doors appear in succession. On this occasion, the artist encountered four consecutive roll-up doors and decided it would be perfect to write a motto. Thus, they inscribed four Chinese characters meaning “Huo zhe jiu xing 活着就行” (Just live), which reflects one of their personal values. iii) Additionally, the artist enjoys designing signs for shop names on these roll-up doors. This approach can lead environmental protection workers, who are tasked with covering graffiti, to mistakenly believe the artwork was created by the shop owner, potentially protecting it from removal. The artist considers himself a non-traditional graffiti creator. While he recognizes that graffiti is often viewed as vandalism, their understanding of this concept differs; they are not solely focused on destruction. When creating graffiti on a roll-up door, they take into account the perspective of the shop owner, hoping to foster a sense of imagination and purpose in their work."""@en ;
