<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1548> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q100899503> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3133> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/oracle_bone_script>,
        <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/small_seal_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1548.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 1 Elvis (猫王)" ;
    ns1:description """This Calli-Writing Unit consists of two characters, representing the artist's tag, “Mao Wang 猫王,” written in Chinese characters. These characters are painted with a black outline and a white fill. The name “Mao Wang 猫王” translates literally to “Cat King” in English, which playfully alludes to Elvis Presley, who is often referred to as “The King” of Rock ‘n’ Roll. By adapting this title into Chinese, the artist introduces a humorous cultural reference. The character “Mao 猫” means “cat,” adding a whimsical element to the name, which may reflect the artist’s intention to blend cultural symbols in a light-hearted and engaging way (Huang 2009).\r
The two characters, “Mao Wang 猫王,” are superimposed onto a “non-fully aligned hanzigezi 汉字格子 grid." A hanzigezi 汉字格子 grid is traditionally used for practicing Chinese writing and calligraphy. By deviating from this grid, with strokes extending beyond its boundaries, the artist appears to challenge the constraints imposed by the grid, symbolizing a desire for creative freedom.\r
The character "mao 猫" (cat) is rendered in small seal script (xiaozhuan 小篆), known for its childlike, primitive, and naive quality, as well as its pictographic forms. By incorporating a “head” into the design of the radical "quan 犭"  that means “dog”, the artist personifies the tag. Furthermore, the radical "cao 艸/䒑" (grass) is aligned with the space designated for the “head,” with one of its two tufts overturned, creating a balanced composition both visually and semantically, reflecting a tension between human influence and nature. The green background, evocative of a field, alongside the radical "tian 田" (field) in the bottom right, which is represented as a pictographic wheel, enhances the visual and semantic readability of the artwork.\r
This piece is designed to capture attention through its irony and subtle political commentary, particularly concerning themes such as Nature versus Control. The natural and animal elements in the first character, "Mao 猫," extend beyond the grid against a green background, in contrast to the character "Wang 王" (king), which is confined within a perfect grid and set against the gray of the natural wall. This contrast symbolizes power, control, censorship, and containment, juxtaposed against the organic elements of nature."""@en ;
