<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1554> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5826> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Forest of Stone Steles: Retranslation & Rewriting of Tang Poetry (碑林－唐诗后著)"@en,
        "碑林－唐诗后著"@zh,
        "Bēilín - Tángshī Hòuzhù"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/231> ;
    ns2:P580 "1993"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2005"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1554.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "In 1993, Gu Wenda began \"Forest of Stone Steles – Retranslation & Rewriting Tang Poetry\", a project in which he carved 50 stone steles featuring retranslated Tang poems. These poems underwent a process of translation—first from Chinese to English, then back from English to the Chinese translation based on the English sound. The project explores themes of cultural exchange, assimilation, and misunderstanding, highlighting how cultures influence and transform one another. Gu Wenda’s works reflects the complexities of global cultural interaction and suggests that new cultural forms can emerge from the recognition of these misunderstandings. Through the act of \"retranslation\" and \"rewriting,\" Gu Wenda challenges the boundaries of both language and visual representation, offering viewers an opportunity to experience the profound aesthetic and cultural depth of Chinese literature in a new context." ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1554.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1915> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1568> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q170406>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q184421>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1952416>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q638> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11472>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q65050532>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1568.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 1 Writing Music – Silent Melodies: \"The Barber of Seville\" (signs)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU consists of the musical symbols transcribed in the front side of Silent Melodies: "The Barber of Seville" from the series  Writing Music – Silent Melodies.  Luo Qi translates the melody of "The Barber of Seville" by Gioachino Rossini (1792-1868) into a concatenation of calligraphic signs inspired by the most ancient Chinese musical notation system, the “Dunhuang Musical Notation System” (Dunhuang qupu/Dunhuang yuepu 敦煌曲谱/敦煌乐谱), made up of ancient symbols/simple characters. The musical symbols written entirely in black ink, and transcribed one below the other from right to left, to form columns (31 in total), exactly as if they were traditional characters within a written text. Reworking it through his personal and original interpretation, he transforms the opera’s music into a “visual and visible score,” strongly rhythmic and dynamic, with an inextricably calligraphic texture, as the other work of the series “Writing Music – Silent Melodies” (2018-2023) it belongs to. \r
To the right and left of the transcription, three red seals of the artist, one on the right and two on the left, represent the signature of the artist himself. Finally, on the left end, there is the colophon of the work with the indication of the year and month of execution “Er 0 er san jiu yue 二0二三九月” (October 2023), followed by the artist’s name “Luo Qi 洛齐” and the place of creation “Lisiben 里斯本” (Lisbon).\r
""" ;
