ránhòu tiān tī shí zhàn xiāng gōu lián.\r
"""@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1578> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Book from the Ground (地书)"@en,
        "地书"@zh,
        "Dìshū"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1096> ;
    ns2:P580 "2003"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """Book from the Ground is an on-going project started by Xu Bing in 2003. It represents an ongoing exploration of communication and language, in which the artist adopts symbols and pictograms from the public sphere to create a book exclusively written in this visual language. \r
The uniqueness of Book from the Ground lies in its accessibility. Due to the universal nature of its visual symbols, the book can be understood by any reader belonging to contemporary society, regardless of one’s cultural or linguistic background. For this reason, there is no need for any translation and it can be published anywhere.\r
Xu Bing realized several installations for the Book from the Ground, in which he suggests the continuous nature of the project as a never-ending exploration of visual communication. \r
Xu Bing Studio also created a character database software that corresponds to the language of the book. Users can enter words either in Chinese or in Chinese, and subsequently, the program translates them into Xu Bing's lexicon of signs. It serves as an intermediary form of communication and exchange between the two languages. As personal computers and the internet become increasingly integrated into daily life, the lexicon of digital icons grows accordingly, and the symbolic language of Book from the Ground has been further updated, augmented, and complexified. \r
In response to his own Book from the Sky (1988) whose language is completely illegible, the symbols of Book from the Ground are in contrast legible to all, and therefore, it may be considered an expression of Xu Bing’s long-standing vision of a universal language.""" ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1581> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1582> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P144 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/96> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1049294>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q41511> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3133>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 1 The Genetics of Reading Image (signs)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU consists of a set of signs "translating" the text of the famous Orchid Pavillion Preface (Lanting ji xu 兰亭集序), written by Wang Xizhi in 353 AD. The content of the CWU is a visual translation with pictographic (emoji) adaptation that convey this famous  text . This Preface is one of the most well-known pieces of Chinese calligraphy and describes a gathering of scholars at the Orchid Pavilion in Shaoxing, Zhejiang, for a spring festival.\r
The emoji-based translation presents the descriptions of time, place, nature, and the poetic spirit of the gathering. Starting with the introduction of the date and of the event, with the calendar 9th year of the Yonghe era, which corresponds to 353 AD. The text mentions the gathering during early spring when the vegetation is starting to grow.  The scene, then, depicts a group of people and a pavilion, representing the gathering of scholars at the Orchid Pavilion near the mountains of modern-day Shaoxing.  The scholars are seated along a stream where they write poetry while allowing cups of wine to float on the water. The content presents then the natural setting and the activities of the scholars. natural setting is presented as lush bamboo forests, flowing streams, and the tranquil surroundings of the pavilion. The mountains, bamboo, and various plants symbolize the serene environment where this gathering took place. The literati gathering is about enjoying nature, sharing drinks, and composing poems. The image shows people sitting by the stream with cups of wine, representing the famous drinking game where cups of wine were floated down the stream. Scholars sit along the stream and engage in writing poetry, reflecting the peaceful atmosphere and their enjoyment of nature. These scene are illustrated with symbols of the eye (viewing), waves (water), and a cup. \r
""" ;
