    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1608.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 1 Ode of Eight Thousand Verses: Fortune n. 1 (福)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU consists of the character "Fu 福" (Good Fortune), written in more than 20 different forms. To shape these forms,  Luo Qi  took inspiration from the very popular "Baifutu 百福图" (One hundred handwritings of the character fu), a design composed of 100 different handwritings of the character fu 福 in the style of ancient and modern seal character, expressing good wishes. This diagram is also linked to the forms of the so-called "Daoist talismanic script" (fulu 符箓 or fuwen 符文), usually reported on talismans. These forms make the character not linguistically (and immediately) recognizable, giving it magical power.  The artist decided to write this character also because from 2010 it has been the source of inspiration of an important part of his artistic production focusing on various reworking of this character. Additionally, the character "fu" refers to an essential religious text of Western tradition, the Gospel, which in Chinese is called "Fuyinjing 福音经" (lit. ‘Classic of Fortunate Music’ or ‘Classic of Happy Sound’). This text is the main topic of another series created by Luo Qi in 2018 entitled "Fujing chaoben 福经抄本" (The transcription of the Gospel) inspired by European Medieval incunabula. Through this character, the artist link together different series of his artistic production, different religious (Taoism and Christianity), popular and elite art, magical script and religious texts.\r
""" ;
