    ns2:P571 "2023-12-11"^^xsd:date ;
    ns1:description """This artwork was created by IRON on December 11, 2023, in Yinchuan, Ningxia, China. Measuring 5 meters in width and 3 meters in height, it was executed using spray cans, reflecting a vibrant blend of traditional Chinese calligraphy and modern graffiti techniques. The piece is anchored by bold Chinese characters, rendered in a dynamic yellow and orange palette with outlines of red and black, which create a powerful visual impact.\r
It is interesting how, in addition to a range of symbols and puppets, the character "wei 味," meaning "flavor" or "taste," features within its "kou 口 radical" (meaning "mouth") a depiction of the symbol "tao."\r
The tao 道 originates from Chinese philosophy and is a fundamental concept in Daoism (Taoism). It is often translated as "the Way" or "the Path." The concept of tao represents the underlying natural order of the universe, guiding the flow of all things. It is both a principle and a force that shapes the nature of existence and reality. Tao encompasses the balance and harmony found in the cycles of nature, the interconnectedness of all beings, and the guiding principles for how one should live in accordance with these natural rhythms.\r
As a symbol (taijitu 太极图),is often represented by the yin-yang icon in Daoism. The yin-yang symbol consists of a circle divided into two swirling halves—one black (yin) and one white (yang)—with a small dot of the opposite color within each half. It visually expresses the duality and interconnectedness of opposites in the universe, reflecting the principles of balance, harmony, and continuous change, which are central to the concept of tao.\r
The yin-yang symbol is closely linked to the idea of tao because it captures the dynamic interplay of forces in the natural world, highlighting how opposites (such as light and dark, male and female, active and passive) are interdependent and continuously transform into one another. This balance and constant flow are key aspects of the tao, which is viewed as the underlying force that governs the natural order and guides the cycles of life.\r
In the context of Daoism, living in harmony with the tao means aligning oneself with the natural order and flow of life, acting in a way that is spontaneous, intuitive, and in balance with the universe. The idea emphasizes simplicity, humility, and non-interference (wu wei), suggesting that by following the tao, one can achieve peace, wisdom, and fulfillment.\r
In this case, the symbol depicted does not strictly follow the circular shape but instead adapts to the form that IRON has chosen to give to his charactering.\r
Among the symbolic elements present, a stylized hand with an eye in its palm stands out prominently. This image is often linked to themes of perception, insight, and protection. In this context, it may signify an elevated awareness or sensitivity, symbolizing how one experiences the world through taste and other senses. The hand with an eye might also suggest a form of introspective observation, where the act of perceiving is as important as the object of perception itself.\r
The surrounding motifs, such as stars and the sun, add a cosmic dimension to the artwork, potentially symbolizing guidance, inspiration, or the life-giving energy that sustains both physical and cultural nourishment. These celestial elements may also evoke the idea of time's passage, drawing a parallel to the way flavors develop and evolve with changing seasons or through the continuity of tradition.\r
Overall, this artwork is an exploration of sensory perception, cultural identity, and philosophical reflection. It seamlessly integrates traditional Chinese elements with contemporary graffiti styles, embodying IRON's commitment to merging the past and present in a dialogue that speaks to both personal and universal experiences."""@en ;
