    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1669.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 2 Restored to Ashes (恢烬) + WOD, 2023" ;
    ns1:description """This Calli-Writing Unit features the characters "Huijin 恢烬," depicted in traditional Chinese characters (Changgui fantizi 常规繁体字) using an ignorant/anti-style. \r
The CWU is defined by a bold initial outline in red, followed by a secondary white outline, and culminating in a black fill, all of which contribute to its dynamic visual impact.\r
This CWU encapsulates a profound exploration of themes related to meaning, loss, and transformation. \r
"Huijin 恢燼," the artist's tag, conveys the idea of "restored to ashes" or "returning to dust." This duality reflects Beer1ne's personal journey, as the tag 恢烬 emerged during a tumultuous emotional period when he resonated with the symbolism of "ash," representing both decay and the remnants of a once-vibrant existence.\r
The origin of this concept dates back to the writer’s senior year in high school, during a period marked by intensive preparation for college entrance exams and emotional difficulty. At the time, the writer developed a strong addiction to smoking and would often find himself watching the ashes drift away while smoking by the window. This imagery of "ashes" inspired a reflective perspective.\r
The notion of ashes was soon juxtaposed with lines of classical poetry about wild grass: “野火烧不尽，春风吹又生” ("Wildfire cannot destroy it; when the spring wind blows, it lives again"). This verse, rich in symbolism, conveys resilience and renewal in nature’s cycles. The burning wild grass, reduced to ashes, symbolizes dissolution; however, these ashes integrate into the earth, nurturing it and allowing the wild grass to sprout once more. The imagery evokes an ecological perspective on the relationship between destruction and regeneration.\r
Simultaneously, the writer felt that "Huijin 恢燼" (translated as "ash") occupies a liminal space, existing at the boundary of presence and disappearance. During this time, he was actively engaged in graffiti and philosophical exploration, navigating a deeply introspective mental space. To him, ashes felt like "travelers in nature," resonating with his self-perception as a "wanderer in the subjective world."\r
This interpretation of "Huijin 恢燼" reflects one of several possible understandings. It is a concept the writer continues to contemplate and expand upon, suggesting that the metaphor of ashes may evolve over time.\r
Through this perspective, the artwork becomes a poignant metaphor for desolation intertwined with the potential for renewal. Just as ash can settle into the earth or drift with the wind, Huijin embodies a cycle of rebirth, suggesting that from the remnants of what once was, something new may arise. The piece captures a sense of melancholia while also expressing an openness to change, inviting viewers to reflect on their own experiences of loss and transformation.\r
In essence, Beer1ne’s work represents an improvisational response to the materials at hand, leading to a creative exploration of identity and existence. The incorporation of both traditional calligraphy and contemporary graffiti styles not only highlights the artist’s versatility but also emphasizes the ongoing dialogue between past and present, inviting contemplation on the nature of meaning in a world constantly in flux."""@en ;
