<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1703> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1412498>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q9186> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/small_seal_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1703.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 1 Real (意实) + @beer1ne " ;
    ns1:description """The “CWU 1 Real (意实) + @beer1ne” prominently features the characters "Yishi 意实" in the Qin Dynasty seal script style. The CWU is characterized by a bold red outline, followed by a secondary white outline and completed with a black fill, creating a visually impactful effect. In one collaborative piece, the artist incorporated the tags "Real" and "Restored to Ashes" (意实-恢烬), a unique instance due to the scale of the piece and the collaboration requirement, resulting in an expansive conceptual meaning.\r
"Yishi 意实" emerged as a tag during the artist’s high school years, specifically during an intensive art training period in an art studio. Initially, his graffiti leaned toward wildstyle, yet after prolonged practice in this style, he encountered creative limitations. Seeking a fresh, non-traditional approach, he shifted to writing in Chinese. Although he previously used "pijiu 啤酒" (the translation of "beer") as a Chinese tag, he eventually created "yishi 意实" to represent a more authentic and meaningful identity.\r
The origins of "Yishi 意实" trace back to this period of exploration, when the artist was interested in a stream-of-consciousness style. His formal art training included traditional Western figurative painting, which he embraced enthusiastically. Inspired by the idea of integrating Eastern and Western elements, he considered the relationship between "yi 意" (concept or idea) and "shi 实" (reality or substance). In traditional painting, Chinese styles emphasize the conceptual, while Western art often focuses on realism. This thematic duality of "idea and reality" in art—where ideas represent the soul, the ethereal, and the mysterious, while reality represents the physical, stable, and clear—was central to his creative direction. Naming his work "Yishi" symbolized his pursuit of a personal style that could establish his unique presence within the evolving timeline of his graffiti journey."""@en ;
