    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1728.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 2 Writing Music – Silent Melodies: \"The Barber of Seville\" (塞维利亚的理发师)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU consists of the text included on the back of the work entitled Silent Melodies: "The Barber of Seville" from the series Writing Music – Silent Melodies.  The text is composed of the Chinese characters “Saiweiliya de lifashi 塞维利亚的理发师” - the Chinese translation of “The Barber of Seville”, written using a style inspired by oracle bone script (jiaguwen), seal script (zhuanshu) and clerical script (lishu). A different character is transcribed on each sheet. The reading direction is from right to left. On the last sheet, next to the last character, the colophon is reported again in two vertical columns that are read from top to bottom and from right to left: in the first column the artist writes “Luoxini de zuopin 罗西尼的作品” (Work by Rossini), while in the second column the name of the author “Luo Qi 洛齐” , the year of execution of the work “Er 0 er san 二0二三” (2023) and the place of creation “Lisiben 里斯本” (Lisbon).\r
On the hard cover, in the golden rectangle, there is a calligraphic inscription in two columns: in the first column on the right, there are the characters “Luoxini 罗西尼” (Rossini) at the top, and “Luo Qi shu 洛齐书” (Calligraphy by Luo Qi) at the bottom, while in the second column “Saiweiliya de lifashi 塞维利亚的理发师” (The Barber of Seville) and “Er 0 er san 二0二三” (2023), the year of execution of the work.\r
To write the two colophons and the heading in the golden rectangle, the artist uses an entirely personal style that stems from the reworking of two distinct calligraphic forms: the running script (xingshu) and a particular form of clerical script (lishu) used in the Han era (206 BC - 220 AD).\r
""" ;
