    ns2:P1476 "Living Word Series (鸟飞了系列)"@en,
        "鸟飞了系列"@zh,
        "Niǎo fēi le xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1096> ;
    ns2:P580 "2001"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """This series of installations (2001, 2002, and 2003) is composed of hundreds of plexiglass cutouts of the various forms of the character niao 鸟 (bird). The forms range  from the most ancient “oracle bone script” (jiaguwen 甲骨文) to the modern standard, with each form rendered in a different color of the rainbow. The installation begins with a platform placed on the ground, displaying the dictionary definition of the word niao 鸟 (bird). From there, the character "takes flight",  rising into the air of the installation spaces, and breaking free from the confines of linguistic and literal definition. As the forms rise, the character undergoes a transformation from its modern simplified form to non-simplified traditional forms, written in various calligraphic styles, and finally into the ancient Chinese pictograph for “bird”. This journey is not just a physical ascent but a metaphorical journey through time and space, retracing the entire history of Chinese writing from its roots. The dynamic arrangement emphasizes both the linguistic and artistic evolution of the character, exploring its structure, definition, and transformation across time.  Its rainbow colors create a magical and fairy-tale quality. This installation is a flying 'flock of birds' and at the same time an 'animated writing': the linguistic sign frees itself from the preceptive constraints of writing to transform itself into something else, into something more alive and authentic. This transformation is made possible by the unique formal evolution of Chinese writing. Its pictographic origins allow it to recover its original form and, in doing so to come alive once again. \r
The last version of this installation was made in 2021-22 for the Shanghai Library East Collection and it is composed of more than 800 "bird" characters written in different styles of Square World Calligraphy—an innovative form of writing designed by Xu Bing, fashioning English letters in a manner that imitates Chinese square characters. The characters hover from a book placed on a big table with the English definition of the word "bird". Differently from the other installations, the characters are all white. """ ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1269> .

