    ns2:P571 "2011"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q378462> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1831.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Taking inspiration from a particular form of seal script called “bird seal script” (niao zhuan 鸟篆), in this artwork, Pu Lieping transcribes the two characters of the title "Zhi-xing 知行" (Knowing and doing) transforming the strokes of each character into heads of birds, bodies of fishes, profiles of horses, swimming turtles, flower stems and corollas, depicting an imaginative natural scenario that seems to have nothing to do with Chinese characters.  The “bird seal script” is a kind of artistic script in common use during the late Warring States period (475-221 BC) characterized by avian elaborations decorating each character. In this script the lines are gently and intricately curved and decorated with bird design, the strokes are replaced by bird shapes and the writing possesses a unique decorative style.  \r
The title of the artwork is reference to the expression "zhi-xing he yi 知行合一" (unity of knowledge and action), usually abbreviated to the two words  "zhi-xing 知行". This is a concept in Chinese philosophy created by Wang Yangming (1472-1529), who is astatesman, general, Neo–Confucian philosopher, and calligrapher during the Ming dynasty (1368-1644). It means that knowledge and action should go together and help each other. This idea says that only by behaving well can someone become wise and it is a denial of the possibility of weakness of will. """ ;
