<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1881> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q60> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Tracing the Origin (溯源)"@en,
        "溯源"@zh,
        "Sùyuán"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1879> ;
    ns2:P580 "2000"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2016"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P973 <https://calligrapher-workshop.artandarchaeology.princeton.edu/abstracts#p22> ;
    ns1:description """The series "Tracing the Origin" is composed of installations made of copper wires and pins that reproduce an asemic "script" revoking the forms of Chinese calligraphy but totally unreadable. The artist generally uses white or black walls, or etched copper as support. She also uses copper wires or sand and adhesive on board, such as in "Tracing the Origin XVII" (2012) commissioned by Socrates Sculpture Park in New York. \r
In this series, Cui Fei forcefully conveys both the relevance of nature, using natural materials, and her cultural and national origins, recalling Chinese calligraphy and adopting metallurgical techniques that suggest the Chinese Bronze Age. \r
The series "Tracing the Origin" together with another series titled "Manuscript of the Nature" (1999-2010) and the work "Not Yet Titled" (2009) where she shapes asemic writings, are reminiscent of similar experiments made by her fellow émigrés, such as Xu Bing, Wenda Gu, and Qiu Zhijie, who work to subvert existing Chinese traditions through linguistic distortion.  In all these series, using natural materials, Cui also points to the natural “roots” of Chinese linguistic and artistic traditions.""" ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1882> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1895> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q60> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Neon Calligraphy Series (霓虹灯书法系列)"@en,
        "霓虹灯书法系列"@zh,
        "Níhóngdēng shūfǎ xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/231> ;
    ns2:P580 "2004"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2007"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1895.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "The “Neon Calligraphy Series” consists of site-specific neon light installations, typically  commissioned by and for institutions (University of Pittsburgh, 2004), corporations (Sotheby’s, 2005), and brands (Ferragamo, 2004). Each installation is made of neon light tubes, plexiglass and transformers, which are used to write Chinese characters, written in the artist’s own regular style, and Latin letters. The artist uses red and yellow in the artworks of the series, the same colors of the Chinese flag, which are also employed in other works, such as those of the \"Heavenly Lantern Series\". Gu Wenda transforms English names in a poetic way through a creative process he calls “Complex English–Chinese Translation”, already employed for the “Forest of Stone Steles”. Starting from the English name of the institution, corporation, or brand in the upper register, the artist transliterates each syllable’s sound into Chinese characters. Then, in the lower register, the artist produces new English translations for each single Chinese character. This process highlights the challenges of linguistic and cultural translation in the era of globalization. Chinese writing and calligraphy function as a conceptual device: the linguistic contacts make them shift from the local Chinese frame to a global field.  " ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1895.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1893> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1908> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q46> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Jie: Chinese Knot Series (中国结系列) "@en,
        "中国结系列"@zh,
        "Zhōngguó jié xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/585> ;
    ns2:P580 "2022-06"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    ns2:P582 "2022-07"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1908.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The series entitled “Jie: Chinese Knot” (Zhongguo jie 中国结) is divided into six moments: \r
1) “Chinese Knot - Chapter One: Become Little Plum Blossom” (Jie - di yi zhang: Bian cheng xiaohua mei 结 - 第一章 : 变成小花梅), where the artist completely paints her body first using a base of white makeup and, later, covers herself with red plum blossoms that she has drawn herself.\r
2) “Chinese Knot - Chapter Two: Know Her Name, Remember Her Name” (Jie - di er zhang: Zhixiao ta xingming, laoji ta xingming  结 - 第二章 : 知晓她姓名, 牢记她姓名) where the artist, with her body completely painted in red plum blossoms, approaches long bright red drapes hanging from the ceiling of the open space used for the performance, tying them around her neck.\r
3) Chinese Knot - Chapter Three : She/Her (Jie - Di san zhang: Ta 结 - 第三章 : 她)  where Echo uses her body as a canvas, painting it white and writing Chinese characters in red ink. These characters represent both historical and contemporary attitudes towards women in China. Specifically, she writes derogatory words, many of which contain the character "nü" (female), highlighting the misogynistic culture embedded in these terms.\r
4) “Chinese Knot - Chapter Four: The Non-interchangeable Body” (Jie - di si zhang: Buke hu huan de shenti 结 - 第四章 : 不可互换的身体) where the artist, together with her husband and photographer Jamie Baker, creates a timelapse of her earlier body paint preparation.\r
5) “Chinese Knot - Chapter Five: Red Planet” (Jie - di wu zhang: Hongse xingqiu 结 - 第五章 : 红色星球) where Echo Morgan paints her body completely red and, wearing a large red Chinese knot attached to her neck, positions herself in front of the long bright red drapes.\r
6) “Chinese Knot - Chapter Six: Viennese Theater” (Jie - di liu zhang: Weiyena juyuan 结 - 第六章 : 维也纳剧院), in which Echo, after painting her body with Chinese characters that have misogynistic meanings, wraps herself again in the long bright red drapes, this time also voicing words aloud while her body is completely immobilized. """ ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1908.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/623> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1910> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/585> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Festival Perform"@en ;
    ns2:P1640 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q41672875> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1435049> ;
    ns2:P580 "2022-08-19"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2022-08-19"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P856 <https://www.festivalperform.org/> ;
    ns1:description "The Festival Perform, led by the French artistic director Sarah Trouche, celebrates performance in all its forms, focusing on contemporary art, dance, and digital art. Held annually, the festival aims to unite generations and communities through creative experiences. Trouche's vision for performance is to create a space for shared emotions and unique moments between the artists and audiences." ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/627> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1912> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/698> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Bald Girls Exhibition (秃頭戈女)"@en,
        "秃頭戈女"@zh,
        "Tūtóu gē nǚ"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P580 "2012"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2012"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """This exhibition was held at the Iberia Contemporary Art Space in Beijing 798 in 2012.  Bald Girls, was a three-woman show that boldly addressed feminism and gender identity in China. The three artists—Li Xinmo, Xiao Lu, and Lan Jiny—declared it to be China's first feminist art exhibition.\r
The exhibition even featured a short story by Herta Müller, the 2009 Nobel Prize winner in literature, who expressed her concerns about the state of feminism in China. The name “Bald Girls” is also based on the homonymous famous play “La Cantatrice chauve”, translated from French as “The Bald Soprano” or “The Bald Prima Donna” written in the 1930s by Eugène Ionesco (1909-1994), a French playwright of the Theatre of the Absurd. Replacing the “song” with the “dagger-axe”, the exhibition takes the form of a metaphor for the lived reality of women in China aiming that “in China female art is still something new” and “artists have to turn to arms instead of entertainment”.  Starting from 2012, Bald Girls has evolved from an exhibition into a base for promoting the development of cutting-edge feminist art, founding the Bald Girls website and journal to serve as an outlet and a medium for the voice of female artists in China. \r
During the opening ceremony, Xiao Lu invites her collaborators to shave their heads recalling the image of the three bald women visible on the exhibition poster.""" ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/733> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1925> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/184> ;
    ns2:P1476 "4th Kochi-Muziris Biennale: Possibilities for a Non-Alienated Life"@en ;
    ns2:P1640 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4172136> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1800> ;
    ns2:P580 "2018-12-12"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2019-03-29"^^xsd:date ;
    ns1:description """The exhibition Possibilities for a Non-Alienated Life at the Kochi-Muziris Biennale (December 12, 2018 – March 29, 2019) explores themes of body, sensuality, and relationships, with works such as those by Marlene Dumas. Curator Anita Dube, inspired by Guy Debord's theories on the dominance of images, focused the Biennale on the Global South, including marginalized craft and folkloric practices, such as those by Bapi Das, an auto-rickshaw driver who embroiders scenes from his daily life.\r
In this context, Song Dong's Writing Diary with Water, also on display in Kochi, explores the impermanence of the world through an interactive installation. Paintbrushes immersed in water invite visitors to draw marks on a translucent wall, but the traces fade over time, symbolizing the fleeting nature of everything. The work encourages reflection on alienation and the passage of time, with mirrored floors emphasizing the idea that reality is temporary, and every trace disappears when the person interacting with the piece leaves. Both exhibitions, while addressing different themes, share a focus on the inevitability of change and impermanence.""" ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1921> .

