<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1948> a ns3:Q215627 ;
    ns2:P103 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P106 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3303330>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q483501> ;
    ns2:P19 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q59209> ;
    ns2:P21 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6581097> ;
    ns2:P2561 "Huang Yeda 黄业达" ;
    ns2:P27 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P569 "1990-05-14"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P742 "Huang Ji (黄几)" ;
    ns2:P800 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2057>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2064> ;
    ns2:P812 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6498317>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q919348> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q16572>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4970> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1948.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Huang Yeda (黄业达), also known as Huang Ji (黄几), was born in 1990 in Yangjiang, Guangdong. He graduated from the Guangzhou Academy of Fine Arts in 2016 with a master's degree from the School of Chinese Painting. Since 2020, he has been pursuing a PhD at the China Academy of Art’s School of Chinese Painting and Calligraphy, under the mentorship of Professor Wang Dongling. He is currently an adjunct teacher at the Guangzhou Academy of Fine Arts, and lives between Hangzhou and Guangzhou.\r
Huang Yeda’s artistic practice is rooted in cross-media conceptual approaches, emphasizing the excavation of traditional cultural contexts and critical reflections on established narratives. His work often explores the interplay between the ordinary and the extraordinary, examining the relationship between diverse cultures and the human condition. Engaging with contemporary calligraphy, new ink painting, video, and installation, his work investigates the intersections of visual form and narrative structure, reinterpreting classical aesthetics within a modern framework.""" ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Academic"@en ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1948.jpg> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1953> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P144 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7071> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3235751> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11472>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/cursive_script>,
        <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/wild_cursive_script> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU The Music of Ink at the British Museum - Cursive Writing Poetry (草书歌行)" ;
    ns1:description "This CWU is part of the artwork created during the musicalligraphy performance entitled \"The Music of Ink at the British Museum - Cursive Writing Poetry (草书歌行)\", held during the event \"The Music of Ink at the British Museum\" on June 18, 2005. Its content is a famous poem from the Tang dynasty written by Li Bai (701 – 762), titled “Cursive writing poetry” (Cao shu ge xing 草书歌行).  The poem is written in wild cursive script (kuangcao), using the feibai (flying white) technique. The poem, with its romantic tone and unique imagination, vividly recreates a scene of the calligrapher Huaisu drunkenly and passionately waving his brush, writing in a wild and exuberant manner. It carefully and wonderfully portrays Huaisu's bold and expressive personality traits. The entire poem vividly captures Huaisu’s free-spirited, passionate, and exuberant character. It holds significant reference value for later studies on Huaisu’s cursive script art. In this poem, Li Bai describes calligraphy as music. As Zeng Laide explained: \"In Chinese, the title of the poem is Cao shu ge xing. 'Ge xing' means music; ‘cao shu’ refers to a type of calligraphy, specifically the grass script style. Thus, the poem and the calligraphy bring together the classical, the contemporary, and the creative\" (Wang 2012, pp. 69-70)." ;
    ns1:directionality "Traditional" ;
    ns1:hasBackgroundColour <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444> ;
    ns1:hasBigCharactersCalligraphy false ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicLine true ;
    ns1:hasCharacterForm <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q178528> ;
    ns1:hasSignificance "Meaningful writing" ;
    ns1:isUnitOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1943>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1951> ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q614467> ;
    ns1:numberOfCharacters 218 ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """So talented, the young monk named Huaisu \r
His grass script is unique upon this earth.\r
His inkpools shoot out fish from the darkest north\r
His brush-tips kill off hares in the Zhongshan mountains.\r
In the autumn months when the weather turns cool\r
His drinking friends and writers fill the hall.\r
Cream silk, pale hemp, piled up in the side-room \r
Ink glistening on the special Xuanzhou inkstone.\r
Our master, drunk, steadies himself on the rope bench \r
In a moment be'll sweep through a thousand sheets:\r
The soughing, howling of gales blowing, rain pelting \r
The boundless flurry of petals falling, snowflakes flying.\r
He stands to face the wall, his hand moves fast \r
A line of characters large as grain-barrels.\r
Bewitched as though the spirits are a-calling \r
In an instance I see a snake and dragon fee \r
Coil, flinch and dodge the lightning strokes \r
This calligraphy is the Chu-Han battleground.\r
Our master's script is proudly placed on all the doors \r
Of all the homes in all of Hunan.\r
o and Zhang Boying, old masters who had earned their names \r
And Zhang Dian, now dead, cannot be counted.\r
Of all the thousand things, the most important \r
Is not to follow the ancients, but to follow nature's way.\r
There is no need to call for Madam Gongsun \r
To inspire us with her sword dance."""@en,
        """少年上人号怀素, 草书天下称独步。\r
墨池飞出北溟鱼，笔锋杀尽中山兔。\r
八月九月天气凉，酒徒词客满高堂。\r
笺麻素绢排数箱，宣州石砚墨色光。\r
吾师醉后倚绳床，须臾扫尽数千张。\r
飘风骤雨惊飒飒，落花飞雪何茫茫！\r
起来向壁不停手，一行数字大如斗。\r
怳怳如闻神鬼惊，时时只见龙蛇走。\r
左盘右蹙如惊电，状同楚汉相攻战。\r
湖南七郡凡几家，家家屏障书题遍。\r
王逸少、张伯英，古来几许浪得名。\r
张颠老死不足数，我师此义不师古。\r
古来万事贵天生，何必要公孙大娘浑脱舞。"""@zh,
        """Shàonián shàng rén hào huái sù, cǎoshū tiānxià chēng dúbù. \r
Mò chí fēi chū běi míng yú, bǐfēng shā jǐn zhōngshān tù. \r
Bā yuè jiǔ yuè tiānqì liáng, jiǔtú cí kè mǎn gāotáng. \r
Jiān má sù juàn pái shù xiāng, xuān zhōu shí yàn mòsè guāng. \r
Wú shī zuì hòu yǐ shéng chuáng, xūyú sǎo jìnshù qiānzhang. \r
Piāo fēng zhòu yǔ jīng sàsà, luòhuā fēi xuě hé mángmáng! \r
Qǐlái xiàng bì bù tíngshǒu, yīxíng shǔ zì dà rú dòu. \r
Huǎng huǎng rú wén shén guǐ jīng, shíshí zhǐ jiàn lóng shé zǒu. \r
Zuǒ pán yòu cù rú jīng diàn, zhuàng tóng chǔ hàn xiāng gōng zhàn. \r
Húnán qī jùn fán jǐ jiā, jiā jiā píngzhàng shū tí biàn. \r
Wáng yì shǎo, zhāngbóyīng, gǔlái jǐxǔ làng dé míng. \r
Zhāng diān lǎosǐ bùzú shù, wǒ shī cǐ yì bù shī gǔ. \r
Gǔlái wànshì guì tiānshēng, hébì yào gōngsūn dàniáng hún tuō wǔ."""@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1955> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1949> ;
    ns2:P1476 "The Music of Ink"@en ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q84> ;
    ns2:P580 "2005-06-18"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2005-06-18"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P664 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6373> ;
    ns1:description "“The Music of Ink”, a completely experimental day held at the British Museum in London. The event brought together contemporary artists from Beijing, Dublin, and London: literary artists Yang Lian 楊煉 (b. 1955) and Romesh Gunesekera (b.1954); visual artists Qu Lei Lei 曲磊磊 (b. 1951) and Denis Brown (b. 1968); and performing artists Zeng Laide and Rohan de Saram (1939-2024). The artists were invited to explore their works in pairs in any way and form, investigating the creative links between the classical and contemporary, particularly regarding China. " ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1943> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/200> a ns3:Q184485 ;
    ns2:P1132 1 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Writing Time with Water (水写时间一个多小时)"@en,
        "水写时间一个多小时"@zh,
        "Shuǐ xiě shíjiān yīgè duō xiǎoshí"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P175 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/184> ;
    ns2:P179 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/202> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q108458>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11471>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q220797>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q25235>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3030513>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4916901> ;
    ns2:P186 ns3:Q22657,
        ns3:Q283 ;
    ns2:P2047 "PT1H"^^xsd:duration ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q84> ;
    ns2:P585 "2000"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """In this performance, Song Dong uses a brush dipped in water to write on the ground of a London street the exact time of the different moments of its execution (hours, minutes, seconds) in order to stop time from dissolving. The performance lasts for about one hour, during which the water written numbers instantly evaporate. Within this hurried setting, Song Dongs modest gesture compels us to focus on the present by exposing our unthinking consumption of time. Writing Time with Water is a compelling example of Song Dongs interest in context and ritual. The performance stems from the artist's series "Writing Diary with Water". \r
\r
"""@en ;
    ns1:hasPerformingArtType <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q213156> ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool ns3:Q614467 .

