    ns2:P608 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1999> ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q607543> ;
    ns1:description """The artwork "Cursive Momentum (Caoshu shi 草书势)" by Lu Dadong is a collage of 12 frames of a video in which the artist using a long strip of paper creates different forms that look like calligraphic brushstrokes. These brushstrokes revoke cursive script, and the use of cursive script as both the subject and title of the work emphasizes the rhythmic qualities of calligraphic lines. Blending traditional Chinese cursive calligraphy with modern abstract aesthetics and technology, "Cursive Momentum" captures the dynamic motion of calligraphy writing, recording the physical act of brush movement through video technology. This approach highlights the expressive rhythm of the strokes—rising and falling, flowing and pausing. The work integrates the fundamental expressive elements of traditional calligraphy, such as variations in speed and pressure, while also reflecting the abstract linear aesthetics found in Western modernist art, particularly the rhythmic line theories of Wassily Kandinsky. By starting from the foundation of traditional calligraphy and incorporating both Eastern and Western understandings of line and movement, Lu Dadong uses high-tech methods to reinforce the theme of the work, creating a fusion of classical and contemporary artistic language (Wang Jianing 2014: 31-2). \r
Cursive Momentum falls within the category of “non-written forms” in modern calligraphy, where traditional brushwork is replaced by contemporary digital, multimedia, and technological techniques. It is a mixed-media conceptual work that utilizes digital imagery, computer art, and audiovisual elements instead of the conventional execution of ink-on-paper. """ ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11060274>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q22669857> ;
    ns1:hasColophon false ;
    ns1:hasSeal false ;
    ns1:vectorisedOrDigitised true .

