    ns2:P1476 "Writing Time with Water Series (水写时间一个多小时系列)"@en,
        "水写时间一个多小时系列"@zh,
        "Shuǐ xiě shíjiān yīgè duō xiǎoshí xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/184> ;
    ns2:P580 "1995"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2010"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """In this series, Song Dong uses a brush dipped in water to write on street pavements the exact time of the different moments of its execution (hours, minutes, seconds) in order to stop time from dissolving. During these performances the water written numbers instantly evaporate. Within the city hurried settings chosen by Song Dong, the artist's modest gesture compels us to focus on the present by exposing our unthinking consumption of time. "Writing Time with Water" is a compelling example of Song Dong's interest in context and ritual. These performances stem from the artist's series "Writing Diary with Water". \r
The series took place in different cities of the world: Beijing (1995), London (2000), Sidney (2002), Brisbane (2002-2003), New York (2005), and Chambelltown (2010). \r
Another performance inspired by the same themes of this series is "Recording a Millennium in Water" (Shui\r
xie qian nian 水写千年, 1999-2000). This performance lasts 24 hours from 8 p.m. of December 31st, 1999 to 8 p.m. of January 1st, 2000. When each time zone of the earth reached midnight of the new millennium, Song  Dong  wrote  the exact time on a stone with a brush dipped with water. \r
"""@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/200> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2024> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1731>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q183> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Stone  Path Series (石径系列)"@en,
        "石径系列"@zh,
        "Shí jìng xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1096> ;
    ns2:P580 "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """The Stone Path Series (石径系列) by Xu Bing (徐冰) is a sculptural/installation artwork created for the first time in 2008, and realized again in 2018, exhibited at the Staatliche Kunstsammlungen Dresden (SKD), Germany. The series consists of carved stone pieces, particularly river rocks (鹅卵石), with dimensions varying across different pieces, such as 52 x 42 x 26 cm, 61 x 41 x 27 cm, and similar sizes.\r
 The choice of river rocks, natural yet altered by human intervention, suggests themes of transformation, permanence, and the dialogue between natural landscapes and human cultural expression.\r
\r
Rielaborating Stone Path and Square Word Calligraphy, Xu bing furtherly realized also two other installations:\r
- Xu Bing’s Writings at Helin Temple (2015), installed at Forsyth Plaza in NYC, merges Square Word Calligraphy with the surrounding nature, architecture, and art, creating a space for reflection by blending traditional and modern elements.\r
- Poem Stone Chairs (2019), placed at Beijing Daxing International Airport, feature poems carved on stone chairs in his signature style. """ ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2030> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2079> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4970> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Equality of Things - Fruit (齐物-果实)"@en,
        "齐物-果实"@zh,
        "Qíwù - Guǒshí"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2076> ;
    ns2:P580 "2012"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2019"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2079.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "The series “Equality of Things - Fruit” was born from the photographic project “Material Immaterial - Fruit” (物非物-果实) in 2010 and later reinterpreted using calligraphy starting from 2012 by Chu Chu. In this work, all traces of photography disappear and are replaced by calligraphy. Through the execution of the characters arranged vertically, in more or less tight rows and in cursive style, the artist succeeds in delineating the subject of the original photograph, different fruits in a state of decomposition.  The opposition that arises from observing first the photographic work and then the calligraphic work makes clearer the sense of materiality and immateriality of matter recalled by the artist in the \"Material Immaterial\" series of works. In the series \"Equality of things - Fruit\", as the title suggests, the calligraphic artist wrote proposes parts of the chapter from the \"Zhuangzi\" entitled “Discourse on the Equality of All Things”  (Qiwulun 齐物论). This text, through parables and allegories, explains how reality is dense with relativism and the human tendency to separate the world into categories (e.g., right vs. wrong). Through this text, Chu Chu reflects on the way we observe things and approach them." ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/2079_HR.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2077> .

