<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2092> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/231> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Translating Visuality - Gu Wenda: Forest of Stone Steles, Retranslation & Rewriting of Tang Poetry (文化翻譯: 古文達《碑林 - 唐詩後著》)"@en,
        "文化翻譯: 古文達《碑林 - 唐詩後著》"@zh,
        "Wénhuà fānyì: Gǔ Wéndá 《Bēilín - Táng shī hòu zhù》"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1640 <https://www.artda.cn/pinglunjiacezhanren-c-8767.html> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q15174> ;
    ns2:P580 "2005"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2005"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "The exhibition \"Translating Visuality - Gu Wenda: Forest of Stone Steles, Retranslation & Rewriting of Tang Poetry\" showcases the project Forest of Stone Steles, Retranslation & Rewriting of Tang Poetry (1993-2005) by Gu Wenda. Since its completion, the Forest of Stone Steles was exhibited in several locations in Australia, America and Hong Kong. The exhibition held at OCT Contemporary Art Terminal of He Xiang Ning Art Museum in Shenzhen (2005) was the first to display the entire series of works. The exhibition takes the theme of 'Cultural Translation' as its subject, and intends to explore the cultural meaning of this monumental works." ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1915> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2093> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4970>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q502371> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Brilliant Star (璀璨的星)"@en,
        "璀璨的星"@zh,
        "Cuǐcàn de xīng"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2076> ;
    ns2:P580 "2016"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2093.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "This series consists of photographs taken by Chu Chu at West Lake in Hangzhou, layered with colorful cursive-style writing in post-production. Each piece was then re-photographed again and further overlaid with an additional layer of handwritten, colorful text. The series aims to illustrate the different stages of life and the passage of time throughout the year, marked by the changing seasons with budding of flowers, growth of plants and fruit production. " ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/2093_HR.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2094> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2105> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q60> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Manuscript of Nature (自然的手稿)"@en,
        "自然的手稿"@zh,
        "Zìrán de shǒugǎo"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1879> ;
    ns2:P580 "1999"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P973 <https://calligrapher-workshop.artandarchaeology.princeton.edu/abstracts#p22> ;
    ns1:description """Cui Fei’s “Manuscript of Nature” series (1999-present) assembles organic material on boards. The resulting panels recall calligraphic formats, yet they remain uninscribed. Leaves, thorns, beans, sprouting seeds, and tendrils are typically arranged in vertical columns to evoke strokes and dots. These can be “read” from top to bottom, from right to left, as happens in traditional calligraphy, but the chance of being effectively able to “read” these works is slim at best (Chávez 2010). The echo of conventional calligraphic structure is emphasized by white gaps which act as pauses, and shorter side blocks which recall colophon-scale addenda in traditional calligraphy.\r
Thorns are central within the series (Letter IV, 2009; Manuscript of Nature VIII, 2010; Manuscript of Nature VIII_III, 2016), they are used in thousands to create constellations of serial, character-like units pinned onto panels in strict rows (Chávez 2010). In other works, Cui Fei uses beans and seeds (Manuscript of Nature VI, 2007; Manuscript of Nature VI_III, 2022) fixed on paper to form dotted syntaxes. Their spacing evokes Braille, but these works stay within visual poetics rather than literal legibility. A related strategy appears in Letter V (2009), where sprouting seeds generate curved, looping lines are reminiscent of cursive calligraphic stroke. The same approach is used in the works of the “Tracing the Origin” series with the only difference that in this second series, copper wires are artificially bent into calligraphy-like forms by the artist, while here is authored by a natural process (Goodman 2011, 104). The natural process of changes and transformation is a topic further explored in other artworks of the “Manuscript of Nature” series, and in particular using driftwood and rust, such as in “Leaves” (2014) and in “The Journey of Transformation” (2015).""" ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2106> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2124> a ns1:TraditionalArtwork ;
    ns2:P1476 " The Book of the Way and Its Virtue (道德经; 23 scrools)"@en,
        "道德经"@zh,
        "Dào dé jīng"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/748> ;
    ns2:P571 "2021"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "The 23 scrools forming the Dao De Jing have been used in occasion of the performance \"Calligraphy generated by body data\" realized by Wang Dongling. This piece consists of an installation featuring 23 massive scrolls descending form above and depicting verses by the \"Dao De Jing\" realized by Wang Dongling with black ink on white paper. Each scroll measuring 9 meters in length and suspended at a height of 2.5 meters above the ground. The scrolls are arranged in order to create a visual reminiscent of an ancient forest of stone steles fallen from the sky. The monumental presence of the installation with words of ancient wisdom imbues the space with a sense of vastness and weight. " ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20437094>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q22669850> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2147> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4970> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Writing Landscapes (书山水)"@en,
        "书山水"@zh,
        "Shū shānshuǐ"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2076> ;
    ns2:P580 "2015"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2147.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """In this series (2015-ongoing), Chu Chu reinterprets the visual language of traditional Chinese landscape paintings (shanshui 山水). She transforms calligraphic brushstrokes and Chinese characters written in cursive script into different pictorial landscape of mountains and streams. The compositions evoke the structural rhythm of  landscape painting while remaining grounded in the gestural dynamics of calligraphy.\r
The series includes both fan-shaped compositions and extended scroll formats. The pieces draw inspiration from classical Chinese painting and calligraphic treatises, while the landscapes inherit the scroll-like compositional logic. Among the principal references are the Yuan dynasty scroll "Dwelling in the Fuchun Mountains" (Fuchun shan ju tu 富春山居图)  attributed to Huang Gongwang 黄公望 (1269–1354); "Summer Mountains" (Xiashan tu 夏山图) of the Five Dynasties period, associated with Dong Yuan 董源 (932–962); "Endless Streams and Mountains" (Xishan wujin tu 溪山无尽图) of the Qing dynasty by Gong Xian 龚贤 (1619-1689); "Searching All the Strange Mountains for Sketches" (Sou jin qi shan da caogao 搜尽奇山打草稿), a work connected to the landscape theory and practice of Shi Tao 石涛 (1642-1707).""" ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/2147_HR.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2146> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2154> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2076>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/748> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Writing Non-Writing: The Third Hangzhou International Modern Calligraphy Exhibition (2015)"@en ;
    ns2:P1640 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/748> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4970>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q66454843> ;
    ns2:P580 "2015-05-08"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2015-05-19"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P856 <https://en.caa.edu.cn/news/369.html> ;
    ns1:description """"Writing Non-Writing: The Third Hangzhou International Modern Calligraphy Exhibition" was held at the Art Museum of the China Academy of Art in Hangzhou from May 8 to May 19, 2015. The exhibition featured the works of over 100 artists from 20 countries.\r
The exhibition was divided into four parts: “Writing Non-Writing: The Third Hangzhou International Modern Calligraphy Exhibition”, “Writing Non-Writing: Documentary Exhibition”, “Writing Non-Writing: Calligraphy of Wang Dongling”, and “Writing Non-Writing Forum: The Context of Modern Calligraphy.”""" ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2146> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2183> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1131> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Mengbo 2012 (梦波贰零壹贰)"@en,
        "梦波贰零壹贰"@zh,
        "Mèngbō èr líng yī èr"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7812188>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P580 "2011-10-29"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2011-11-12"^^xsd:date ;
    ns1:description """"Mengbo 2012" was Feng Mengbo's solo exhibition held in 2011 at the Today Art Museum in Beijing. The exhibited artworks included "Mortal Kombat (True Fighter)", "Q2012", a dance-based interactive game, and "Eye Charts". \r
"Mortal Kombat (True Fighter)" (Zhen ren kuai da zhi qian zhuan: hua quan xiu tui 真人快打之前传：花拳绣腿) is an interactive video installation in which Feng Mengbo digitizes himself, his family, and friends into arcade-style fighting game characters. Each figure is animated from a sequence of thousands of still photographs, merging nostalgic 8-bit aesthetics with martial arts parody and self-portraiture. Using Holovision technology, the three-dimensional moving images are projected onto a stage set designed to resemble the rural-urban outskirts of Beijing. \r
"Q2012" is an evolution of the artist's earlier work "Q2008", built on the Quake III Arena game engine. It transforms the interactive mechanics of a first-person shooter into a form of digital action painting, where audience members use a dance pad to navigate and create visual traces within a reactive virtual environment.\r
"Eye Charts" (Shilibiao 视力表) consists of two large panels featuring optometric-style charts composed of characters using two newly-designed computer fonts, named "Feng's Bitmap" (Dianzhenfeng 点阵冯) and "Feng's Vector"(Shiliangfeng 矢量冯).\r
The exhibition shows the artist's transition from new media experimentation toward a hybrid form that fuses digital interactivity, gaming engines, and traditional visual strategies, highlighting Feng’s use of game mechanics, performative interfaces (such as dance pads), and conceptual typography to explore themes of authorship, memory, and digital temporality.\r
""" ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2182>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2184> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2204> a ns3:Q4502142 ;
