    ns2:P571 "1951"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P608 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2222> ;
    ns2:P941 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2295> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2221.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """This dress named " 'Quiproquo' Cocktail Dress" (Ji wei jiu qun 鸡尾酒裙) was created in 1951 by Christian Dior. It follows the classic silhouette of a Dior cocktail dress, embodying the house’s refined and understated style. However, what distinguishes it is the calligraphic print featuring the text Letter about a Stomachache (Du Tong Tie 肚痛帖) by the Tang dynasty calligrapher Zhang Xu (张旭, c. 675–750 CE). Rather than appearing as a single, legible inscription, the calligraphy is multiplied across the surface of the dress, turning the script into a rhythmic decorative pattern that emphasizes visual impact over textual readability.\r
The original manuscript of Zhang Xu has been lost, but a stone-carved copy from 1058 CE (Song dynasty) is now housed in the Beilin Museum in Xi’an. Although commonly referred to as a poem, "Du Tong Tie" is more accurately a brief letter written in cursive script, composed in a moment of acute physical pain. Zhang Xu was suffering from a sudden stomachache and, in just a few lines, conveys his condition, the uncertainty as to whether it was caused by heat or cold, and his decision to take a rhubarb-based medicine to relieve the pain. The cursive script is concise and expressive, made up of only six lines and around thirty characters. While the content seems simple—perhaps even banal—its power lies in the expressive mastery of the brushwork. Zhang Xu’s use of cursive script captures intense emotion and physical discomfort through the rhythm, energy, and variation of his lines. Each stroke conveys a raw, agitated state, giving visual form to suffering. Printed on white fabric, the fluid, vigorous ink strokes create a dramatic visual effect. The result is both elegant and evocative, projecting a sense of exoticism to Western eyes. The title Quiproquo aptly reflects the cultural misinterpretation embedded in the use of Chinese calligraphy on a Western fashion piece. Once transformed into a decorative motif, the original meaning of the calligraphy is partially lost, creating a visual and conceptual “misunderstanding” between East and West.\r
It is clear that the designer was likely unaware of the original content and cultural background of the calligraphy by the so-called “master of cursive”. The choice seems to have been driven purely by aesthetic intuition—the expressive strokes of the calligraphy evoked abstract expressionism, aligning naturally with Dior’s visual identity and taste.\r
A similar example of a dress created with calligraphic print is the one designed by Coco Chanel in 1956, titled "Ensemble". Chanel’s dress, preserved at the Metropolitan Museum of Art in New York, is made of white surah silk and decorated with black Chinese characters. In this case, the Chinese characters, rotated and deconstructed, lose their semantic readability in favor of an aesthetic function, illustrating a process of stylization that evokes cursive script (caoshu), appreciated for its fluidity and artistic value.\r
Both the “Quiproquo” Cocktail Dress by Christian Dior and the “Ensemble” dress designed by Coco Chanel in 1956 were showed in the exhibition "China: Through the Looking Glass", held at the Metropolitan Museum of Art from May 7 to September 7, 2015.""" ;
