    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 1/2 (心、人、佛、观...)" ;
    ns1:description """The Chinese characters on Zhang Huan's body are written by some of his friends during the performance entitled "1/2". Some of the characters are simplified characters and some orthers are traditional ones. The style of the characters recalls regular and running scripts. \r
There are some characters that often recur, for example xin 心 ('heart') and ren 人 ('person'). The character xin can refer to either one's "heart" and "mind". Literally, xin (心) refers to the physical heart, though it also refers to the "mind" as the ancient Chinese believed the heart was the center of human cognition. However, emotion and reason were not considered as separate, but rather as coextensive; xin is as much cognitive as emotional, being simultaneously associated with thought and feeling. For these reasons, it is also often translated as "heart-mind". In this case, the use of the character xin 心 ('heart') is particularly significant because it represents the mind/heart unit that has been separated during the performance. The use of the character ren 人 is also significant because it remarks that the individual/person is divided between body and mind. The characters xin 心 and ren 人 seem to chase each other on the artist's body, without ever being reunited.\r
Other important words visible on Zhang Huan's forehead are Fo 佛 (Buddha) and  guan 覌 (sight, view, appaerance, to look at, to observe). The character Fo 佛 is transcribed in correspondence of the brain (on the arstist's head) and it recalls the importance that the mind and meditation have for Buddhism and for the Buddhist vision of life. The character guan 覌  is written between the artist's eyes because for Buddhism the real eyes are represented precisely by the mind.\r
In addition to Chinese characters, there are also Latin letters ("A, B, C" on the right arm of the artist) and punctuation marks (e.g. two question marks on his breast). """@en ;
