<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2295> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Letter about a Stomachache (肚痛帖)"@en,
        "肚痛帖"@zh,
        "Dù Tòng Tiè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1480 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5727902> ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q717343> ;
    ns2:P571 "0700"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """“Letter about a Stomachache” (Du Tong Tie 肚痛帖) is a short piece attributed to the renowned Chinese calligrapher Zhang Xu (ca. 700–750 CE). It is not a poem in the strict sense but rather a very brief personal note, written in cursive script during a moment of acute physical pain caused by a sudden stomachache. The text, consisting of about 30 characters in 6 lines, conveys in a concise and direct way the author's suffering, his uncertainty about the cause of the pain (whether due to cold or heat), and his decision to take a rhubarb-based decoction, known for being effective in either case.\r
The original manuscript has been lost, but a stone-carved copy made in 1058 CE during the Song dynasty is preserved today at the Beilin Museum in Xi’an. Scholars believe the original composition dates back to the 8th century, during the height of Zhang Xu’s career.""" ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """Suddenly stricken with unbearable stomach pain,\r
Unsure whether it was caused by heat or cold.\r
I thought of taking a decoction of rhubarb,\r
Beneficial in either case—hot or cold alike.\r
But what should I do?\r
This isn’t a clinical setting."""@en,
        """忽肚痛不可堪,                                       \r
不知是冷热所致，                                                    \r
欲服大黄汤，                                                                 \r
冷热俱有益。                                                                   \r
如何为计,\r
非临床”。"""@zh,
        """忽肚痛不可堪，\r
不知是冷熱所致，\r
欲服大黃湯，\r
冷熱俱有益。\r
如何為計，\r
非臨床”。"""@zh,
        """Hū dù tòng bù kě kān,\r
bù zhī shì lěng rè suǒ zhì,\r
yù fú dà huáng tāng,\r
lěng rè jù yǒu yì.\r
Rú hé wéi jì,\r
fēi lín chuáng."""@zh-Latn .

