<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2418> a ns3:Q17514 ;
    ns2:P1476 "I am very sad today (今天很伤心)"@en,
        "今天很伤心"@zh,
        "Jīntiān hěn shāngxīn"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P179 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2419> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q169251> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q84> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444> ;
    ns2:P571 "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """A representative example from the series “Chinese Emotional Graffiti – Sadness” is the work titled “I am very sad today (今天很伤心)”. The piece was documented in London and published online in 2024, suggesting that it was likely created in the same year. The graffito consists of the phrase "Jintian hen shangxin 今天很伤心 (I am very sad today)," rendered in white characters against a deep burgundy-red background, accompanied by the image of a crying face.\r
The visual contrast between the stark white script and the saturated red surface intensifies the emotional tension of the message, transforming a simple declarative sentence into an affective statement that resonates with viewers on an immediate, human level. The inclusion of the crying face reinforces this direct emotional communication, bridging textual and visual expression in a way characteristic of Chinese Emotional Graffiti.\r
It is particularly noteworthy that this work appeared in London rather than in China. Its geographical displacement raises questions about emotional universality and cultural translation: sadness here functions as a global affect, transcending linguistic boundaries while still retaining a distinctly Chinese graphic identity. The presence of Chinese characters in a Western urban environment also invites reflection on the diasporic condition and the ways in which emotions—especially those of melancholy or alienation—can be inscribed into public space as quiet gestures of vulnerability and connection."""@en ;
