<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2426> a ns3:Q17514 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Where Is Youth Going? (青年去往何处? )"@en,
        "青年去往何处?"@zh,
        "Qīngnián qù wǎng hé chù?"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P179 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2423> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q12101178>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q190007> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P571 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q81068910> ;
    ns1:description """“Where Is Youth Going? (青年去往何处?)” stands as a central work within the series "Chinese Emotional Graffiti – Discomfort of Youth". Painted in red spray paint on the crumbling wall of a ruined house at a street corner, the piece divides its inscription across two sections: "Qingnian 青年 (youth)" appears on the left wall, while  "qu wang he chu去往何处 (where is it going?)" occupies the right, visually emphasizing a sense of rupture and disjunction.\r
Across the wall, the question unfolds as both a personal outcry and a collective lament. It encapsulates the pervasive disorientation of a generation whose transition into adulthood was fractured by the COVID-19 pandemic. Visually, the characters convey a dynamic instability: their strokes alternate between assertive, calligraphic precision and erratic, dissolving forms, embodying the inner turbulence of contemporary youth. The surface bears visible layers of paint and traces of erasure, evoking the passage of time and the emotional residue left by isolation and uncertainty. Dripping marks and uneven textures suggest a gradual loss of solidity, as though the very notion of “youth” were disintegrating before the viewer’s eyes.\r
Conceptually, the work transforms a question of existential gravity into a public act of vulnerability. It gives form to the shared confusion and introspection of those who came of age during a global crisis, caught between nostalgia for an irretrievable past and anxiety toward an undefined future. More than a mere record of the pandemic’s emotional aftermath, the piece becomes an act of reclamation: a conversion of despair into dialogue.\r
By inscribing this question within public space, the graffiti refuses concealment and instead demands confrontation. It compels passersby to reflect on their own trajectories, revealing that the discomfort of youth is not a private affliction but a shared condition. In this way, "Where Is Youth Going?" exemplifies the ethos of Chinese Emotional Graffiti: merging linguistic expressiveness with urban immediacy to articulate the unspoken emotional currents of a generation in search of meaning."""@en ;
