<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2437> a ns3:Q17514 ;
    ns2:P1476 "What is love? (爱是什么?)"@en,
        "爱是什么?"@zh,
        "Ài shì shénme?"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P179 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2436> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q316> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q870> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2044> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q39338> ;
    ns2:P571 "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P6375 "Florence station"@en ;
    ns1:description """The piece "What is love? (爱是什么?)" appeared on a train near Florence, sprayed in vivid orange on the gray metal of the carriage. The choice of orange may have been deliberate, echoing the train’s lower stripe while ensuring the message stood out sharply against the silver background. The writing is executed in a simple, angular style, typical of Chinese Emotional Graffiti, where clarity of message takes precedence over aesthetic flourish.\r
This question touches a timeless concern of humanity. In contemporary society, where relationships can feel fleeting or fragile, it resonates anew. The phrase invites viewers to consider love in a broader sense, extending beyond romantic attachment to include friendship, compassion, and self-care. It encourages reflection on the many forms love can take, challenging idealized or conventional narratives.\r
Although the original post documenting this graffiti is no longer accessible, it reportedly attracted significant engagement. Like other examples of Chinese Emotional Graffiti, it served as a catalyst for public dialogue, with viewers responding to the question and sharing their own reflections. This interaction underscores a key feature of the movement: the ability to turn a single, universally relatable doubt or reflection into a shared, communal experience."""@en ;
