<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2444> a ns3:Q17514 ;
    ns2:P1476 "I can’t take it anymore (受的好累)"@en,
        "受的好累"@zh,
        "Shòu de hǎo lèi"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P179 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2441> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q9690> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P571 "2021"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q81068910> ;
    ns1:description """The graffiti I Can’t Take It Anymore (受的好累) was published online in 2021, suggesting that it was created in the same year. The composition consists of four Chinese characters: "shou de hao 受的好", rendered in red spray paint, followed by "lei 累 (fatigue)", executed in black. The chromatic contrast between red and black is visually and symbolically significant: the red conveys intensity, emotional agitation, and perhaps internalized pain, while the black character  "lei 累 (fatigue)" isolates and emphasizes the core sentiment of exhaustion.\r
This work encapsulates a profound sense of moral and psychological depletion. It reflects a state of emotional fatigue deeply connected to the sociocultural context of the Covid-19 pandemic, a period marked by confinement, the deprivation of individual freedoms, and collective trauma. In this context, the graffiti becomes more than a personal confession, it is a universal outcry, a silent plea for relief from unbearable psychological strain.\r
By inscribing this short yet emotionally charged phrase in public space, the writer transforms a private sentiment into a shared social message. The minimal composition, dominated by textual expression rather than imagery, aligns with the principles of Chinese Emotional Graffiti, in which language itself becomes the primary vehicle of affect. The act of writing thus functions simultaneously as catharsis and resistance, an effort to reclaim emotional agency amid the paralysis of global crisis.\r
Ultimately, "I Can’t Take It Anymore (受的好累)" stands as a poignant visual testament to the collective fatigue of a generation confronting uncertainty, isolation, and loss. Its simplicity belies a deep existential resonance, articulating the exhaustion of enduring a world in which suffering has become both normalized and inescapable."""@en ;
