<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2456> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8646> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Les Poèmes du Mandarin for Hermès"@en ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/578> ;
    ns2:P580 "2009"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2456.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The “Les Poèmes du Mandarin” tableware by Hermès is a limited-edition collection created for and inspired by the Krug Room at the Mandarin Oriental Hong Kong, representing a unique collaboration between the hotel, Krug champagne, and Hermès. The project originated in 2006, when the design studio Marc & Chantal, while finalizing the Krug Room, approached Hermès to create a bespoke tableware design rather than using existing stock.\r
The collection links Chinese calligraphy to the enjoyment of champagne and conviviality. The calligraphy is by artist Fung Ming Chip. He first executed the calligraphy freehand on traditional rice paper, later digitally adapted by Fu Qiumeng Fine Art to fit the tableware design. Among the various styles, Fung even invented scripts resembling rising bubbles in a glass of Krug. The inscriptions feature full poems, extracts, or individual characters from classical Tang and Song Dynasty poets—including Li Bai 李白 (701–762), Du Fu 杜甫 (712–770), and Cheng Hao 程颢 (1032–1085)—with themes of friendship, happiness, and the sharing of food and drink. The calligraphy is rendered in subtle shades of grey with delicate platinum accents to evoke the effect of freshly applied, wet ink, and punctuated with small red seals carved by the artist. The resulting collection comprises 18 pieces blending Chinese and Western tableware styles, each reflecting varying scales, compositions, and content.\r
Hermès’ ateliers, or Compagnie des Arts de la Table, ensured the meticulous execution of the calligraphy on ceramic, harmonizing traditional artistic techniques with contemporary functional design. \r
The collection was first installed in the Krug Room on 20 October 2008 and subsequently launched for public sale in Hermès stores worldwide in 2009, cementing Fung Ming Chip’s reputation as an artist bridging classical Chinese art and modern design.""" ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/2456_HR.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/730> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2463> a ns3:Q17514 ;
    ns2:P1476 "In Hong Kong, if you are sick, you don't see a doctor; you look at your wallet (在香港地,有病不是看醫生,是看看自己個銀包)"@en,
        "係香港地, 有病唔系睇医生, 系睇吓自己个銀包"@zh,
        "在香港地,有病不是看醫生,是看看自己個銀包"@zh,
        "Zài xiānggǎng de, yǒu bìng bùshì kàn yīshēng, shì kàn kàn zìjǐ gè yín bāo"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P179 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2462> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q15978337>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q31207> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8646> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P571 "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """The graffito “In Hong Kong, if you are sick, you do not see a doctor; you look at your wallet” (在香港地，有病唔係睇醫生，係睇吓自己個銀包) was executed in Hong Kong and circulated online in 2024. The inscription is written in traditional Chinese characters and employs Cantonese vernacular syntax, reinforcing its local specificity and immediacy.\r
Formally, the text is rendered in black, likely applied with a marker, on a tiled wall, with each character carefully positioned within the boundaries of a single tile. This strict alignment produces a grid-like visual order that contrasts with the critical and emotionally charged content of the message, a strategy frequently observed in text-based urban interventions.\r
The graffito constitutes a form of social protest addressing the high cost of healthcare in Hong Kong. Rather than advocating against specific political institutions, the work articulates an everyday economic anxiety, emphasizing how access to medical care is mediated by financial capacity. In doing so, it exemplifies how Chinese Emotional Graffiti operates not only as a vehicle for political dissent but also as an expressive register of social and economic grievance.\r
The phrase underscores a bitter irony: illness does not prompt a medical consultation but instead forces an assessment of one’s financial means. This succinct and colloquial formulation transforms a private concern into a public statement, using urban space as a site for collective recognition of precarity. Through its direct language and accessible placement, the graffito foregrounds the emotional dimension of economic pressure, situating personal vulnerability within broader structural conditions."""@en ;
