<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2468> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1384> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Ready-to-Wear 2013 Collection"@en ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2306> ;
    ns2:P580 "2013-02-10"^^xsd:date ;
    ns1:description """The Vivienne Tam Ready-to-Wear 2013 collection was presented at New York Fashion Week on February 10th, coinciding with the Chinese New Year, which added symbolic resonance to the show. The collection is notable for its fusion of Eastern and Western influences, combining contemporary fashion aesthetics with cultural and political references.\r
Tam incorporated Western pop culture elements, such as a pop-art print of Barack Obama wearing sunglasses alongside QR codes, and traditional Chinese motifs, including stylized Chinese characters and Maoist slogans like “long live” (wan sui 万岁). The color palette was dominated by bold red, black, and white, with touches of blue, while the silhouettes ranged from 1960s-inspired sheath dresses to modern reinterpretations of military jackets. Luxurious fabrics such as silk and leather were enriched with details like accordion pleats, leather appliqués, and intricate textures, giving the garments depth and visual interest.\r
The beauty and styling reinforced the edgy, contemporary feel of the collection, with bold dark eyeliner, deep burgundy lipstick, and sleek high ponytails creating a punk-inspired aesthetic. Beyond fashion, Tam used the collection as a platform for social engagement, collaborating with an organization that converts electronic waste into microloans for women in need.""" ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2305> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2470> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8686> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Yuanyuan Armchair Series (缘圆椅系列)"@en,
        "缘圆椅系列"@zh,
        "Yuányuán yǐ xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2348> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2470.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The Yuanyuan Armchair series by Minaxdo is a sophisticated exploration of the intersection between traditional Chinese calligraphy and contemporary furniture design. The collection is directly inspired by the techniques and gestural qualities of calligraphy: the movements, turns, hooks, inclinations, and directions of brushstrokes are reflected in the modeling of each piece. Rooted in the essence of classical script, the armchairs feature smooth and elegant lines that evoke the fluid rhythm of running and cursive script, translating ephemeral ink gestures into enduring material form.\r
In ancient China, scholars placed great importance on fenggu 风骨—the spiritual strength and integrity of artistic expression. Traditional calligraphy, while appearing graceful and flowing, contains an inner vigor, soft in appearance yet firm in spirit. The Yuanyuan Chair captures this duality: its form resembles a spontaneous cursive composition, while its design and craftsmanship rigorously adhere to structural and mechanical principles. Each handcrafted joint, executed through mortise-and-tenon construction, corresponds metaphorically to a momentary “pause” in the brush movement, imparting both solidity and dynamism to the piece.\r
The name Yuanyuan 缘圆, meaning “round-perfect edge” or “harmonious connection,” alludes to the continuity and fluidity of calligraphic motion, embodied in the ergonomically designed teak and rosewood armrests. Through this synthesis of traditional artistic philosophy, calligraphic movement, and contemporary design practice, the series exemplifies the translation of gestural energy into functional, elegant, and conceptually rich furniture.""" ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/2470.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/124> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2480> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1156970>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q177634>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q380962> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3133>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q943> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/cursive_script> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 1  Shanghai 2010 Expo Logo (世)" ;
    ns1:description "This CWU is part of the logo of the Shanghai 2010 Expo. It consists of the Chinese character \"shi 世\", meaning “world” or “generation,” which has been stylized as three figures holding hands to symbolize human unity and cooperation. The design of the character is calligraphic, with flowing and sinuous lines inspired by running script, allowing the emblem to merge semantic meaning with visual form in a single, coherent image. According to the \"Shuowen jiezi 说文解字\", the earliest surviving Chinese dictionary completed by Xu Shen 许慎 in 100 CE, the original form of the character (㔺) is a modification of the character sa 卅 (thirty), initially composed of the character shi 十 repeated three times, with the lower ends of the two outer vertical strokes converging to form a tree-like branching structure. The character shi 世 also signifies “generation”, reflecting the idea that every thirty years one generation produces the next, just as each branch of the character seems to emanate a successive one. The green S-shaped stroke that terminates the image, replicating the final part of the character in its pictographic form, is transformed into the number “2” in “2010” within the logo. These strokes are executed in a brush-like style, highly evocative of calligraphy, further emphasizing the integration of traditional Chinese writing with modern graphic design." ;
    ns1:directionality "Unidentifiable" ;
