<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2486> a ns3:Q17514 ;
    ns2:P1476 "There is Hope (有希望 )"@en,
        "有希望 "@zh,
        "Yǒu xīwàng"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P179 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2484> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q190507> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q207849>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P571 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """This graffiti reading “You xiwang 有希望” (“There is hope”) employs large-scale red Chinese characters painted directly onto an urban wall using a paint roller rather than spray paint. This technical and visual choice explicitly recalls the aesthetic and ideological legacy of dazibao (big-character calligraphy). Historically associated with Mao-era political slogans and practices of mass mobilization, dazibao was designed to be immediately legible, confrontational, and accessible to the public, transforming written language into a powerful instrument of collective persuasion.\r
The use of red, deeply embedded in the visual grammar of revolutionary China, further reinforces this historical resonance. Red functions not only as a chromatic reference to socialism, revolution, and state authority, but also as an affective device that intensifies the emotional charge of the message, demanding attention within the everyday urban environment.\r
The graffiti is additionally framed by a blue outline, a detail that invites multiple interpretations. On a symbolic level, blue may connote trust, calm, stability, or endurance, contrasting with the urgency and intensity of red. Within Chinese visual culture, blue can also evoke notions of continuity, protection, or transcendence, subtly tempering the confrontational force of the red characters. Alternatively, the blue frame may function as a visual boundary, separating the message from the surrounding urban decay and granting it a sense of enclosure, preservation, or intentionality, almost as if isolating hope itself within a fragile perimeter.\r
The chromatic pairing of red and blue is unlikely to be arbitrary. Red traditionally signifies fortune, vitality, and happiness, while blue may suggest rationality, distance, or emotional restraint. Together, they produce a visual tension that mirrors the broader sociopolitical climate: a moment marked by uncertainty, exhaustion, and suspended futures. In such a context, the declaration that “there is hope” reads less as naïve optimism than as an urgent, almost defiant emotional assertion.\r
Recontextualized within contemporary Chinese Emotional Graffiti, the large-character inscription undergoes a significant semantic shift. Detached from its original propagandistic framework, the phrase no longer operates as a directive imposed from above, but as an open-ended emotional statement addressed horizontally to the public. The monumental scale and stark simplicity of the characters echo Mao-era visual strategies, yet their contemporary placement transforms them into a site of reflection, memory, and emotional negotiation.\r
By appropriating the formal language of Maoist slogans while fundamentally altering their communicative intent, the work collapses the boundary between political history and personal affect. In doing so, it reclaims dazibao as an expressive medium capable of articulating hope, endurance, and psychological affirmation, demonstrating how historical visual codes continue to be reactivated and re-signified within present-day urban writing practices."""@en ;
