        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q18699055> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11472> ;
    ns2:P2048 "24 cm" ;
    ns2:P2049 "18 cm" ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q107381157>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3133>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42519>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q428124>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q943> ;
    ns2:P571 "1996"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P88 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1061469> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2517.jpg> ;
    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/130> ;
    ns1:description """In 1996, the renowned French estate Château Mouton Rothschild invited Chinese calligrapher and contemporary artist Gu Gan to design the label for its vintage —a historic first for a Chinese artist. Since 1945, this famous French winery (after an initial experiment in 1924) has had its labels designed by famous artists, including Miró, Chagall, Braque, Picasso, Warhol, Kandinsky, etc. These artistic labels became the visual hallmark of the winery. \r
Gu Gan's work - the first one by a Chinese artist, titled “Coeur à Coeur” (Heart to Heart), features five ideograms of the character xin 心 (heart), each rendered with subtle variations in stroke and color. For Gu Gan this work conveys the notion that "even if one's body is locked in a particular room, city or country, one's heart and mind can never be so contained" (Barrass 2002, 190). This powerful yet subtle composition is intended as an act of faith in universal harmony. Through the repetition of the character xin 心 (heart), the composition transforms the fluidity and rhythm of calligraphy into a visual meditation on harmony, emotion, and universality. The label exemplifies Gu Gan’s signature style: delicate yet dynamic, spontaneous yet structured, capturing both the energy and serenity of the written form. The work also includes a colophon in which the artist’s name appears in archaic characters (古干), preceded by the characters “Zhongguo 中国" (China) and followed by the title of the work (心), likewise rendered in the same archaic form. The original artwork made in Chinese ink on paper is then  reproduced in high-quality techniques, in particular lithography for special prints, while the bottle labels themselves are printed. \r
Gu Gan's work represents the upper part of the label. Immediately below is the wording ‘Encre de Chine par Gu Gan’ (Chinese ink by Gu Gan - on the left) and ‘Coeur à Coeur’ (Heart to Heart - on the right), and further below is the rest of the standard wine label with the coat of arms, the vintage and the name of the wine and the winery in classical serif typeface. The interplay of flowing, expressive brushwork with precise printed typography creates a subtle tension between spontaneity and formality, reflecting the dialogue between Eastern calligraphy and Western writing tradition. \r
This collaboration marked a significant cultural milestone, highlighting the dialogue between contemporary Chinese art and Western wine tradition, and showcasing Gu Gan’s ability to convey poetic meaning through the elegance of the brush.""" ;
