    ns2:P571 "1985"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/276.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "“Bright snow”, executed in 1985 by Shao Yan, is an artwork composed by two characters and a lower-left little inscription. The wo characters, qing  晴 (“bright”), whose edges look like carved wood signs, and xue 雪 (“snow”), fat and thick with a firm central part, represent a surprising contrast just like the sunlight that spreads with roar over the silent snow. For both characters, Shao Yan adopts the “flying white” (feibai飞白) technique, which means using the half-dry brush, in order to represent the wild landscape of Northern China struck by violent snowstorms. The little inscription is written with brushstrokes full of ink and it has an angular shape, balancing the entire calligraphy. This work is one of the first experimental works by Shao Yan: as the artist was influenced by the “few characters” Japanese calligraphic movement, the structure of each character embodies the meaning of the character itself."@en ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q22669850> ;
