<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/300> a ns1:TraditionalArtwork ;
    ns2:P1476 "This Baiju Valley (此白驹谷)"@en,
        "此白驹谷"@zh,
        "Cǐ bái jū gǔ"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11643938> ;
    ns2:P571 "0513"^^xsd:gYear,
        "0515"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """The inscription of this rubbing is entitled "This Baiju Valley" (Ci Baiju gu 此白驹谷). According to the data, this inscription was inscribed by Zheng Daozhao 郑道昭 (? - 515) during his tenure as the prefect of Qingzhou. The full text of the inscription is "Zhongyue xiansheng ying yang zheng daozhao you pan zhii shangu ye ci bai ju gu 中岳先生荧阳郑道昭游槃之山谷也此白驹谷" ("The Valley where Mr. Zhongyue Yingyang Zheng Daozhao visited Pan is also Baiju Valley") and "This Baiju Valley" has a total of 19 characters. The entire inscription is one foot square in diameter. It is a precious treasure of calligraphy art in Weifang.\r
The technique of rubbing consists in applying a sheet of paper with longer and more resisting fibers to the rocky surface of a cliff, stele or even small seal stone, to then ink the sheet, and take a life-size copy of the rubbed surface. An inversion in the perception of relief and shade takes place when intaglio lines become white, and relief parts, black. Obviously, while deep intaglio is totally white and high relief, black, there is a whole realm of in-betweenness where the middle grounds, low relief and smooth concavities can only be expressed in shades of grey. Rubbing is thus extremely interpretative and is a form of indirect perception, comparable to photography.\r
"""@en ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1640824>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7375860> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/304> a ns1:TraditionalArtwork ;
    ns2:P1476 "Peaceful and Happy Buddha (安樂佛)"@en,
        "安樂佛"@zh,
        "Ānlè fú"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q48886885> ;
    ns2:P571 "0550"^^xsd:gYear,
        "0577"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """This artwork is a fragment of a rubbing inscription entitled "Stele of An Gong (An Gong zhi bei 安公之牌)" realized by the monk Seng' An Daoyi 安道壹 during the Northern Qi Dynasty (550–577).\r
The "Stele of An Gong" is one of the total 23 inscriptions found on the Northern cliff at Mount Hongding, in the north bank of Dongping Lake (Shangdong Province). \r
The structure of Seng An’s characters, the calligraphic strokes and their technical execution in stone, as well as the monumental dimensions of the inscriptions are all elements which find no comparison in contemporaneous calligraphic production, under the Northern Qi Dynasty (550-577). The content of his epigraphy does not match contemporaneous mainstream religious texts or imagery. The sites that Seng' An marked are located in a peripheral area with respects to the imperial capital city of the time, Ye 鄴, in mountainous fringes neighbouring important pre-existing sacred sites such as Mount Tai 泰山. Unlike any known early epigrapher, Seng' An Daoyi carved directly on the raw, undressed stone surface, merging the shape of the text with the pre-existing shapes of nature. The characters follow or avoid folds and cracks, and they are animated by the movement of the water.\r
Seng An Daoyi extracted meaning from the sūtras, in the form of fragmentary passages, names and isolated sounds, creating his own esoteric dictionary. We also find complete inventions such as the mountain Buddha names, personal statements, and liberties taken in translation and in transcription. In a movement ‘from text to language’, Seng' An Daoyi extracted meaning from chosen sections of the sūtras, repeating this selection of fragmentary passages from one site to another, and mimicking the logical structure of sūtras in word games of his own. Bridging text to the mountain, Seng' An Daoyi builds up a chain of transformations, where, through the stele format, language is revisited, transcended, deconstructed and re-assembled. Going further into the language, and taking advantage of a supplementary level of language offered by the Chinese script, Seng' An Daoyi uses the format of names and the obvious anthropomorphic aspects of identification to bring calligraphy beyond the very structure of Chinese characters.\r
The technique of rubbing consists in applying a sheet of paper with longer and more resisting fibers to the rocky surface of a cliff, stele or even small seal stone, to then ink the sheet, and take a life-size copy of the rubbed surface. An inversion in the perception of relief and shade takes place when intaglio lines become white, and relief parts, black. Obviously, while deep intaglio is totally white and high relief, black, there is a whole realm of in-betweenness where the middle grounds, low relief and smooth concavities can only be expressed in shades of grey. Rubbing is thus extremely interpretative and is a form of indirect perception, comparable to photography."""@en ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1640824>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7375860> .

