    ns2:P571 "1995"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "“Peach blossoms fall in a chaotic rain of red dust” is an “artwork with more characters” (duozishu chuangzuo多字数创作) executed by Shao Yan in 1995. It  earned him the first prize at the Sixth National Exhibition of Young Calligraphers and Seal Engravers. The work, considered by the author himself the most representative work of modern calligraphy he had ever created until 2011, when he brushed out “Flying Good Luck” (see the artwork in the archive), is characterised by a huge variation in the thickness of the lines and by words that seem to radiate from the centre of the work and to continue beyond the paper’s edge. The words are executed in an original cursive form, achieved by frequently changing the speed and direction of the brush until the characters become almost unreadable, in order to emphasise the harmony and movement of the lines. Shao Yan drew inspiration from the poem “Invitation to Wine” (Jiangjinjiu 将进酒), written by the poet Li He 李贺 (790-816) in which the author describes the day of youth about to twilight through the image of peach blossoms falling like red rain and invites people to spend their last happy years of youth in the company of wine and entertainment.The artwork is executed not only with a usual brush but also with \"doubi 斗笔\", a large brush with the tip mounted on the final part of the bucket-shaped handle. The artwork is executed in a vertical format to call to the viewer's mind the poetic image of the falling peach blossoms and it is composed by horizontal lines and dots that cover the work and recall the lines of a score embellished with musical notes. This creates a perfect union between the spatial structure of Chinese characters and the temporal rhythm, which reminds of the crackling red rain. With this artwork, Shao Yan does not want to reproduce the meaningful forms of the characters but wants to convey to the observer the feeling evoked by the poetic verses."@en ;
