        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418> ;
    ns2:P2048 "50 cm (each)" ;
    ns2:P2049 "50 cm (each)" ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P571 "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "“The Thirty-Six Stratagems of using ink” is an artwork composed of 36 squares executed in 2008 by Shao Yan using a new tool: a syringe with a small ink reservoir. The title of this work is inspired by the literary work called “The Thirty-Six Stratagems” (Sanshiliuji 三十六计), an essay used to illustrate series of stratagems, used in politics, war and civil interaction. Using this reference, Shao Yan wants to \"fight death\" through the artistic action. When in 2007 the artist was hospitalized to undergo several heart surgeries, he realized that the brush was a tool too far from his heart, so he thought that the syringe could link him better with the experience of life and death he had lived, and began experimenting with it. This artwork is, in fact, Shao Yan's first experiment using a syringe of ink instead of a brush, the so called “ink shooting” (shemo 射墨) to write lines without meaning. The thick ink, squirting out of the syringe toward the paper, reminds of blood and the artist's life is the force that drives the tool. The entire artistic process is characterized by the force of the syringe, the force of gravity, the speed of the jet and the direction that create numerous ink marks of multiple shapes. Through this early experiment with the syringe, Shao Yan was amazed by the fan-like shapes that the ink, hurled at high speed like a projectile, formed on the paper, and this expedient enabled him to produce different shapes of lines and marks."@en ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q22669850>,
