    ns2:P571 "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/352.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """“Desolation” is an enormous artwork executed in 2008 by Shao Yan who was now increasingly interested in social topics and involved in deep thoughts about the meaning of life. On 12 May 2008, Sichuan Province in southwestern China was devastated by a magnitude-7.9 earthquake which caused the collapse of buildings, schools and roads and the death of at least 70,000 people. Shocked by the news, Shao Yan decided to carry out “Desolation” (You 幽), to express, through the deconstruction of "you", the reflection and fear provocked by the tragic event occured. Speaking of this work, he said that the paper reminded him of the earthquake-stricken land of southern China and that he was given the mission to save the world, grab the devil by the throat, save the Earth, even secure human beings, make the mountains and rivers peaceful and enchanting places. \r
Shao Yan uses a paibi 排笔 brush (composed of small brushes joined together) and a long-tipped brush. The concave bottom of the character "you", that should be intact and smooth, appears instead as a shaking groove. The bottom left corner tilts out of the work while the top right corner leaps in mid-air, filling the work with an enormous sense of instability like mountains collapsing as the earth quakes. The central part of the work is executed with a dry brush that creates the shapes of the cracks. Both '幺' elements of the character tilt and succumb together with the entire structure. The work is criss-crossed by dots and ink splashes that look like a crowd scattering in different directions but, there is no possibility to escape because it is impossible to elude natural disasters."""@en ;
