<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/486> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q37200>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q84>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q90> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Plaster Geometry Series (石膏几何体系列)"@en,
        "石膏几何体系列"@zh,
        "Shígāo jǐhétǐ xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/100> ;
    ns2:P580 "1998"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2018"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "This series is made by Zhang Qiang from 1998 to 2018. Zhang Qiang, together with his female counterparts, creates some geometric shapes of plaster on which he leaves \"traces\" of ink. Later, he uses these geometric shapes to create installations (both indoors and outdoors). Some of these installations have also been created outside Cina, such as in Paris in 2007 or in front of the Pyramids of Giza in 2008. One of Zhang Qiang's latest installations took place in London in 2018, where he collaborated with the artist Zhang Kaini 张凯尼. In this occasion, some geometric plaster shapes were exhibited in front of St. Paul's Cathedral in London."@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/482> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/488> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Confucianism, Daoism, Buddhism, Shintoism, Zen Buddhism Series (儒、道教、佛教、神道教、禅宗的系列)"@en,
        "儒、道教、佛教、神道教、禅宗的系列"@zh,
        "Rú, dàojiào, fójiào, shén dàojiào, chánzōng de xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/249> ;
    ns2:P580 "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """In this series, Qiu Zhijie carries out a conceptual work: he creates an archive of images representing some esoteric symbols and scriptures of various philosophical and religious influences. The prints are all of the same format (60X60 cm). The texts are copies of important documents, philosophical and religious writings also made in the Latin alphabet (Old Germanic, Medieval English, Aramaic, etc.). \r
Qiu Zhijie collected and classified many different forms of writing, both public and personal. For each form of scripture, he carved a layer of concrete. After the plate is dry the inscriptions were printed on paper by rubbing with ink, and a new layer of concrete was poured, upon which a new set of characters was then inscribed. Each new layer obliterated the previous one, burying the marks forever and replacing them with a new form of scripture; the only witness that remains is the single print, done with the traditional rubbing process that entails placing a sheet of paper on top of the stele. """ ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/489> .

