<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/555> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Light Writing Calligraphy Series (光写书法系列)"@en,
        "光写书法系列"@zh,
        "Guāng xiě shūfǎ xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/249> ;
    ns2:P580 "2005"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2010"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """This series expressed the artist’s will to re-imagine calligraphic practice through the exploration of different media, here precisely a well-know photographic and performative technique and different contexts. By using the light painting process, Qiu Zhijie maintains the performative elements of the calligraphy practice (the gesture, the immediacy and the text) while expanding the artwork to the new media field. By choosing photography he incorporates a different temporality into the work, introducing a site-specific element (the choice of the location in the public space, which is no more the studio and the night time, which is necessary for light painting) and co-opting a process where light substitutes ink and brush. The timing of the natural gesture of writing is recorded and fixed by and extra-long exposure time of the camera. The final result is a work that, while remaining clearly attached to the idea of calligraphy, can be reproduced in a set and includes a number of different definitions: it is a calligraphic piece, it is a new media piece and it is somehow also a public space intervention, which reminds tags and graffiti although in a fully ephemeral way. The image created can then be distributed through a traditional exhibition, as a photograph, but also as a post in social media, diversifying the virality and accessibility and the way in which the artwork is meant to be presented. The kind of audiences who can access the work is also differentiated compared to the experience of a traditional ink on paper calligraphy piece. The technique of light painting is not new but, due to the increasing quality and reduced size of digital cameras and devices, it has become an intelligent, relatively simple tactical medium to create text-based artworks incorporating public space differently. The light painting techniques most probably used for this artwork are the following two: \r
1) Light painting with on camera light source\r
This technique is achieved using a tripod. You keep your camera in a fixed position, then move your lighting source so it can be seen on camera. By shooting a long exposure, you will see the effects of the light source moving. You can use sparklers, a torch or glow sticks which can be bought quite cheaply. On the more expensive end of the spectrum is specialist light painting LED light sticks. This "on camera" light painting technique will create a smoother image than the Kinetic technique. Swirling, spiralling lines of light being favourites. It also allows you to introduce other elements into your images, such as people or buildings.       \r
2) Light painting with the light source off camera                                                      As above, this technique uses a tripod. However, rather than showing the light source directly by showing it’s movements, this technique is all about showing the light created but not the source.This is less about lines of light and more about lighting up areas of an otherwise dark scene.To achieve this effect, you would project light into the scene you are photographing – but you would not directly see the source of the light in the image, only the light it produces. Weaving light in and out of trees for instance.\r
Qiu Zhijie decides to use both Chinese characters and Latin letters to realized the series, then photographing the results, such as in:\r
1) "Light Writing Calligraphy - The Shape of Time" (Guang xie shufa - Shijian de xingzhuang 光写书法 - 时间的形状) realized in 2006. In these two photographs, Qiu Zhijie writes the sentence "The Shape of Time" both in Chinese characters and Latin letters;\r
2) "Light Writing Calligraphy - 94 go over" (Guang xie shufa - 94 ge guo qu 94个过去) realized in 2006. In these ten photographs, Qiu Zhijie writes the two characters "guo qu 过去" (Go Over). The artist takes the photographs in a sort of very dark garage, placing a row of clocks that indicate the passing of time;\r
3) "Light Writing Calligraphy - All Times when Old are Good" (Guang xie shufa - Yiqie shiqu de dou shi meihao de 光写书法 一切逝去的都是美好的) realized in 2009. In this photograph Qiu Zhijie writes with Latin letters the sentence "All Times when Old are Good";\r
4) "Light Writing Calligraphy - The Twenty-four Poems on Poetry" (Guang xie shu fa - 24 shi pin 光写书法 -  24诗品) realized in 2010, composed by the three photography entitled "Chenzhuo 沉着" (Calm), "Jingshen 精神" (Vigorous) and "Jingjian 劲健 (Strong and Energetic). In these photographs Qiu Zhijie writes the three Chinese characters "Chenzhuo 沉着" (Calm), "Jingshen 精神" (Vigorous) and "Jingjian 劲健 (Strong and Energetic), recalling three poems of the literary work "Twenty-four Poems on Poetry" (Ershisi shipin 二十四诗品) by Sikong Tu 司空图 (837-908), a poet of Tang Dynasty (618-907). Sikong Tu's teaches that poets must perfect themselves internally in order to achieve perfection in what they write.\r
5) "Light Writing Calligraphy - Untersberg Mountain Series" (Guang xie shufa - Weng de si bo ge shan xilie 光写书法 - 翁德斯伯格山系列) realized in 2007. The Untersberg is the northernmost massif of the Berchtesgaden Alps, a prominent spur straddling the border between Berchtesgaden, Germany and Salzburg, Austria. The series is composed by the twelve photography entitled "Anshi 暗示" (Hint), "Daolu 道路" (Way), "Huiyi 回忆" (Memory), "Jiqing 激情" (Passion), "Kaipi 开辟" (Opening), "Mima 密码" (Cipher), "Shuji 书籍" (Book/Work), "Tanbai 坦白" (Confession), "Tianci 天赐" (Bestowed by Heaven), "Yizhi 意志" (Will), "Yugan 预感" (Premonition) and "Zhenxiang 真相" (Truth).\r
6) "Light Writing Calligraphy - Changed" (Guang xie shufa - Bianle 光写书法 - 变了) realized in 2006 composed by the five photography entitled: "Bianle - Beijing Lugouqiao 变了- 北京卢沟桥" (Changed - Beijing Marco Polo Bridge); Bianle - Yan'an baota shan 变了- 延安宝塔山 (Changed - Yan'an Pagoda Mountain); "Bianle - Yushu Gesaer Wang 变了-玉树格萨尔王" (Changed - Yushu King Gesaer); "Bianle-Aomen 变了- 澳门" (Changed - Macau) and "Bianle - Beijing gongyuan dadao 变了-北京公园大道" (Changed - Beijing Park Avenue). \r
7) "Light Writing Calligraphy: Commemorating Anderson Series" (光写书法：纪念安徒生系列 Guang xie shufa: Jinian Antusheng xilie) realized in 2006 in memory of the Danish writer and poet Hans Christian Anders (1805 - 1875). These series is composed of nine photography entitled: "Commemorating Anderson - I Have Seen Tears in the Emperors Eyes" (Jinian Antusheng - Wo zai huang shang de yanlei li kan dao le yanlei 纪念安徒生 - 我在皇上的眼泪里看到了眼泪), "Commemorating Anderson - I Love Your Heart More than Your Crown" (Jinian Antusheng: Wo ai nin de xinling shengguo nin de huangguan 纪念安徒生 - 我爱您的心灵胜过您的皇冠), "Commemorating Anderson - My Song Sounds Best in the Green Wood" (Jinian Antusheng - Wo de gesheng zai shulin li ting qilai zui mei 纪念安徒生 - 我的歌声在树林里听起来最美), "Commemorating Anderson - Even in a Emperor the Heart is on the left side" (Jinian Antusheng - Jiusuan shige huang shang ta de xinzang ye shi zai zuobian de 纪念安徒生 - 就算是个皇上他的心脏也是在左边的), "Commemorating Anderson - I Can not Built my Nest in the Palace" (Jinian Antusheng: Wo bu neng ba wo de chao jian zai gongdian li 纪念安徒生 - 我不能把我的巢建在宫殿里), "Commemorating Anderson - The Storm Shift the Signs" (Jinian Antusheng - Fengbao ba zhaopai huanle 纪念安徒生 - 风暴把招牌换了), "Commemorating Anderson - Who Was the Luckiest" (Jinian Antusheng - Shei shi zui xingyun de ren 纪念安徒生- 谁是最幸运的人), "Commemorating Anderson - Under the Willow Tree" (Jinian Antusheng - Liushu xia de meng 纪念安徒生 - 柳树下的梦), "Commemorating Anderson - Something" (Jinian Antusheng 纪念安徒生 - 一点成绩);\r
8) "Light Writing Calligraphy - Twenty-Four Seasons" (Guang shufa - 24 jieqi 光书法 - 24 节气). In Chinese the twenty-four markers of the seasons are known as jieqi 节气 . The traditional Chinese calendar is a lunisolar one. Although the twenty-four seasonal markers are based on the solar calendar, the months are lunar months. Translated into the standard astronomical convention of ecliptic longitude, chunfen 春分(the Spring Equinox) is positioned at zero degrees, and the next marker qingming 清明 (the Clear and Bright) at fifteen degrees. Thus, the subsequent jieqi 节气 are separated by the sun's entry into a sign of the tropical zodiac (fifteen degrees). This system is almost identical with the Gregorian calendar. For example, dongzhi 冬至 (the Winter Solstice) always falls around the 22th of December. This calendar was first utilized four thousand years ago during the Xia dynasty (ca. 2205 B.C. C 1766 B.C.), so it is also called the Xia calendar (xiali 夏历). Starting from the summer of 2005, on the day of each jieqi 节气 , Qiu Zhijie writes the names of the twenty-four terms at different locations. The arrangement and content of this series of twenty-four photographs are pre-determined by the sequence of the twenty-four seasonal markers and the location changes according to his presence. \r
9) "Light Writing Calligraphy - 'Heart Sutra' Series" (Guang xie shufa: “Xinjing” xilie) composed of fifty-five photographs with sentences and words extracted from the famous Buddhist text;\r
10) "Light Writing Calligraphy - Fable" (Guang xie shufa - Yuyan 光写书法 - 寓言), realized in 2005. It consists of three different photography in which Qiu Zhijie writes the word "yuyan 寓言" in different places;\r
11) "Lighwriting Calligraphy - Forever Night" (Guang xie shufa - Ye weiyang 光写书法 - 夜未央) realized in 2005. It consists of two photographs in which Qiu Zhijie writes the three characters "ye weiyang 夜未央" in different places.\r
12) "Light Writing Calligraphy - Deep in the Reality" (Xianshi shen chu 光写书法 - 现实深处) realized in 2005. It consists of four photographs in which Qiu Zhijie writes the four characters "Xianshi shen chu 现实深处" (Deep in the Reality) in different places. \r
13) "Light Writing Calligraphy - Where" (Guang xie shufa - Where 光写书法 - Where) realized in 2005. In these thirty- five photographs, Qiu Zhijie writes in Latin letters some words and sentence like "Past", "Future", "Listen to Me", Dream", "Be Careful", "I am Fine", "Let it be", "Home", etc.\r
14) "Light Writing Calligraphy - Dartington" (Guang xie shufa - Da ting dun 光写书法 - 达汀顿) realized in 2005. It consists of three photographs entitled "Shici 诗词" (Poem), "Gui qu 归去" (Back Home) and "Zhiwu 植物 (Plant)" in which Qiu Zhijie writes the three words and sentences in different places. """@en ;
