<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/559> a ns3:Q4502142 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Wei State of Myriad Beings (魏州万生园)"@en,
        "魏州万生园"@zh,
        "Wèi zhōu wàn shēng yuán"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/557> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1274479>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3393049>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q65560351> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11472>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q207849> ;
    ns2:P2048 "97 cm" ;
    ns2:P2049 "121 cm" ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q71404162>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q943> ;
    ns2:P571 "2016"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1074987> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/559.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """In this work Wei Ligang combines figurative elements and Chinese characters using a mixed technique acrylic and ink on a typical light brown xuan paper with rough texture which underlines the paper’s fibers. The classical references to the concept of “myriad of beings” within the historical frame of the warring states is here re-interpreted and re-imagined in a free floating composition. \r
The concept of “myriad things” 萬物 (wanwu), which later gains paradigmatic importance in Chinese philosophy, was first introduced in the Laozi and developed in the Zhuangzi. Under the collective heading of “myriad”, “myriad things” encompasses all of the variegated existing entities in the empirical world, while also taking into account the individual particularities of each entity without calling forth any singular or set of qualities, or “essence”, that is shared by all individuals. As a concept that safeguards individual particularities, the use of “myriad things” in the Zhuangzi serves to counterargue against the essentialist tendency to treat “humans 人 (ren)” as a collective of moral agents with a singular and identifiable moral essence. \r
The figurative elements and characters carry out a clear calligraphic brushwork touch, which is rendered in a hybrid informal aesthetic register. It can evocate both various styles of illustration, (even comics) and pop culture while maintaining its classical references. The free composition, the color palette and the acrylic/ink technique are the basis elements which help the transition from a traditional to a contemporary language. Particularly the use of acrylic medium in association with ink defines Wei Ligang’s attempt to bridge the gap between strict traditional calligraphy’s rules and modern and post-modernist approach. Acrylic per se allows corrections, retouches and a different time-span of ri-elaboration of the work, while ink confront the artist with its unavoidable immanence and the impossibility to erase or change the marks once they are traced. Perhaps we can say that ink functions at the crossroad between the ‘carpe diem’ of the moment and the event, but it can also record every mark as a trace of each single gesture, as an accurate memory and documentation of what happens from the beginning to the end. On the other side, with acrylic (and also oil) the final work can be planned, changed and re-worked until the final result and it would not be possible (unless using x-rays and complex technologies) to see all the changes and phases. With ink any unwilling gesture should be adapted on the moment, which means one needs to rely on an excellent mastering of his own technique and on the capacity to improvise and adjust in real time whenever a mark does not come out as expected. With acrylic the mindset is different as it is possible to erase or cover a section and start again.  The size of the work is also more typical of a modern or contemporary piece while not being totally strange to certain formats of traditional Chinese landscape painting. \r
Displaying the influences of both Western abstraction and the Chinese calligraphic tradition, Wei uses the impulsive gestures of expressionism (in ink that ranges in density from solid black to pale gray) but his characters remain rooted in the square structures that form the basis of calligraphy. Wei trained as a mathematician, and many of his works are based on “Wei Squares,” a formula drawn from the square framework printed on the practice paper used by students of calligraphy."""@en ;
