        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q207849> ;
    ns2:P2048 "283.5 cm" ;
    ns2:P2049 "145.5 cm" ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q68223248> ;
    ns2:P571 "2010"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/561.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "In this work, Wei Ligang creates an apparently simple image that in fact incorporates a variety of elements: from American abstract expressionism, and the lyrical abstraction and informal art of movements like Tachism ( like Cobra and Gutai). We can detect the “clins d’oeil” at artists such as Jiro Yoshihara, R. Motherwell, F.Kline, A.Tapies. In this work, ink strokes recall a deconstructed interpretation of the character “pine tree” (songying 松影) exemplifying an attitude detached from a strict technical execution and rather developing in a more loose, natural and expressionist way. Another important reference here is that of the “few characters” Japanese calligraphic movement, whose influence has been that of progressively reducing the number of characters in a painting until reaching the format of one single character occupying the space. The mixing of acrylic and ink on a traditional vertical format with a classical poetic-evocative title is a typical example of Wei Ligang’s methodology to create a new form of calligraphy which exists in an intermediate zone of formal and conceptual tension between the past and the present. To compare this approach to an existing western theoretical frame, we could say that it belongs to the realm of post-modernism, according to which the parameters of pure stylistic expression and hierarchic  coherence between the choice of materials, techniques and outcome (typical of the modernist approach) are deliberately abandoned in favor of a more unusual mix of elements that dismantles those same notions of purity and coherence. This creates a further interesting level of ambiguity and complexity ,as we can say that, if Wei Ligang’s works visually seem to reproduce the aesthetic of modernist abstract art, the conceptual root behind his process is much more similar to the post-modern one. "@en ;
