<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/565> a ns3:Q4502142 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Quotations from Ouyang Xiu in Mad Cursive B (欧阳修辞抄狂草B)"@en,
        "欧阳修辞抄狂草B"@zh,
        "Ōuyáng xiū cí chāo kuángcǎo B"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/557> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q25388391>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q25496059>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q334323>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q607543>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q80113> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11472>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418> ;
    ns2:P2048 "551.5 cm" ;
    ns2:P2049 "215 cm" ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P571 "2016"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q334323> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/565.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """In this large vertical scroll, Wei Ligang remains close to tradition by choosing both a classical reference and a traditional technique and support: ink on xuan paper. The work recombines fragments of poetry by Ouyang Xiu (1007–1072) and other writers of exceptional literary beauty, transforming them into a dream-like sequence of vivid images and sensory experiences. Wei writes in “mad cursive” (cao shu 草書) script, conjoining several characters in each passage, a choice that reads as a clear homage to Ouyang Xiu, one of the Eight Great Masters of the Tang and Song dynasties.\r
Ouyang Xiu was a pivotal figure of the Song dynasty—an outstanding intellectual, statesman, magistrate, calligrapher, poet, and novelist—whose life and achievements remain among the most celebrated in Chinese cultural history. The calligraphic resonance invoked here may be linked to the period of his exile in Anhui province, when, living in the countryside, he called himself "Zuiweng 醉翁" (the Old Drunkard), built a pavilion of that name, and authored the celebrated essay associated with it. This moment, imbued with Daoist ideals and the celebration of wine and friendship, has long been connected to the wild cursive calligraphic tradition, a lineage into which Wei Ligang consciously situates his work.\r
Across five vertical sequences, Wei Ligang allows spontaneous yet deeply learned gestures to flow, deconstructing characters into pure lyrical rhythm and juxtaposing them through subtle tonal modulations achieved by masterful ink dilution and sensitive control of movement. The brushwork varies rhythmically between outward expansion and knotted compression in response to the complexity of the forms.\r
While the piece clearly evokes the spontaneity of the wild-grass style, it also reveals a deliberate precision and an acute awareness of stroke variety: bold, clean marks alternate with overlapping, delicate passages, displaying an eloquent mastery of tone. Through this work, Wei Ligang establishes a dialogue with the literati tradition, positioning himself as a virtuoso who both demonstrates and questions his calligraphic knowledge. Even in the absence of definitive proof, works such as this may be understood as attempts to transform and interrogate calligraphic practice by simultaneously subverting and celebrating its classical elements, walking a fine line between heresy and orthodoxy, lightness and depth.\r
Finally, the large scale of the scroll may be read as a compromise between traditional Eastern formats and Western artistic sensibilities, recalling both monumental calligraphic scrolls and carvings, as well as the gestural expansiveness of Western Abstract Expressionism."""@en ;
