<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/569> a ns3:Q4502142 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Ten Thousand Birds Startled by the Dawn Drums (万点城鸟惊曙鼓) "@en,
        "万点城鸟惊曙鼓"@zh,
        "Wàn diǎn chéng niǎo jīng shǔ gǔ"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/557> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q170406>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q18699055>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3393049>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3502310> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q207849>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4259259> ;
    ns2:P2048 "120 cm" ;
    ns2:P2049 "120 cm" ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q208045>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P571 "2011"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2620036> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/569.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """In this work Wei Ligang, at least in part, moves away from any calligraphic register recognizable at first sight, to explore a different kind of composition which looks as if it was informed both by the modernist geometrical abstraction and also by some other figurative imagery and primitive decorative motives. In reality, this operation is part of nearly thirty years of artistic exploration, during which Wei Ligang has been creating a universal abstract language based on the lines and spatial structures of Chinese characters. This language attempts to accommodate the complexity of a variety of western and eastern elements. In his so-called "Wei's Magic Block" he deconstructs Chinese characters and incorporates image elements, tracing back to the origin of pictographs. The choice of the large round canvas and the mixed media technique (ink and acrylic) establishes another parallel between the eastern tradition (in which the round support is a classic choice but mainly on paper and silk) and the work of several western lyrical and geometrical abstract artists. The choice of another eastern classical element, the palette, black on golden-brown, together with the homogeneous thickness of the line contribute to highlight the hybridity of this work, which is then further re-elaborate with reversed colors on xuan paper mounted in the form of a large screen (as in the work "Fluttering Butterflies and Buzzing Bees, a Pleasure Boat and Orchids at Dawn", Die luan feng xuan lan xiao hua ge 蝶乱蜂喧 兰晓画舸, 2016).\r
This continuous formal re-elaboration of the Chinese calligraphic tradition comes forward as the trade mark of Wei LiGang’s artistic research. As further references to formal and compositional solutions, they can be found in some specific works by Picasso (1881-1973): the series of drawings executed with a continuous, uninterrupted line in a single gesture and the consequent series of drawings with light, in which, precisely to bear witness to this type of apparently spontaneous technique, but in reality possible only thanks to a particular experience of manner, the Albanian photographer Gjon Mili (1904-1984) invents a photographic procedure with very long exposure times and synchronization of flash and dim light, which allows Picasso to exhibit "drawing in the air" recording the final gestures and figures as lines of light. We could also find a visual reference for the shapes and the character of the Nazca Lines, a collection of giant geoglyphs—designs or motifs etched into the ground—located in the Peruvian coastal plain about 250 miles (400 kilometers) south of Lima, Peru. There are three basic types of Nazca Lines: straight lines, geometric designs and pictorial representations. The Nazca are large environmental artworks only visible from the sky, despite the obvious differences in terms of context, size and technicality. """@en ;
