        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42519> ;
    ns2:P571 "2013"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P6375 "Beijing, Chaoyang, Dongba."@en ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q119380> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/596.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """This intervention with industrial tape (that remained there for a couple of months) is an ironic statement about the politic of the "China Dream" issued by the Xi Jinping administration. This work was made for the 2nd chapter of the autonomously organized event of the "Space Regeneration Project", curated by Tianjin-based curator Ren Han in 2013 in an abandoned foundation/museum in Dongba area, within Chaoyang District (between Caochangdi and Heiqiao) in Beijing. The place, a modernist building of around 1000 sq. metres was built in 2008, to become a museum and a foundation for the plastic and performative arts; it was never finished and it was left abandoned a couple of years later. The Space Recuperation Project aimed at re-vitalize for one or two days with ephemeral or permanent artistic actions and interventions some of these buildings, already embodying the huge contradiction of the Chinese Real Estate and Cultural policies. In line with the curatorial statement (which supported two days of informal actions in abandoned or demolished new buildings), this work is meant to witness and evocate the absurdity of gentrification, real estate and financial speculation when articulated by the speed of authoritarian hyper-capitalism. \r
The artist uses red duct tape to write the two Chinese characters zuo meng 做梦 (Dream!) on a wall of an abandoned building. Duct tape is a very typical industrial material that can be found in any construction site and industrial environment; the choice of red is obviously linked to the Chinese tradition of writing with red ink (and even blood) to convey and something urgent and aggressive. The sentence is meant to be the Chinese equivalent of a command with the verb in the imperative mode (Dream!). Since the new slogan had changed from Hu Jingtao’s "Harmonious society" to Xi Jingping’s "Chinese Dream", this action was thought to send a provocative message about the contradiction between the continuity of the unresolved precarity between the government change. The duct tape calligraphic writing was applied in one day and then left there to gradually detach from the wall, while a photographic documentation of the process was collected. The work is an emblematic example of a tactical intervention: it has been done in unauthorized public space, using simple and conceptually accurate materials and conceived in two phases: a physical  one to be executed quickly and gain visibility in the area; a digitally one, as viral documentation on the internet. Red duct tape in the form of writing possesses the directness and energy of the political dazibaos, but also in itself, recalls a powerful materiality, symbolically evocating industrial reality and construction sites, which is intercepted and re-appropriated in this work. In this case the ephemerality of the process is still evocating the philosophical concept of change and immanence, but within the clearly defined socio-political context of exposing the brutality, the destruction and the exploitation carried on by a super-fast, unethical form of development. The work remained on the wall for 3 days before the tape started to detach and it was removed after 5 days. """@en ;
