    ns1:madeUsingTool <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q614467> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/668> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8686> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Dance Remains Series (舞迹系列)"@en,
        "舞迹系列"@zh,
        "Wǔ jì xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/645> ;
    ns2:P580 "2018"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """This series was created by the artist Jessie Yingying Gong in 2018, during the six months of the artist residency at the "Swatch Art Peace Hotel" in Shanghai. “Dance Remains” is a series of long exposure photographs the artist made of public dancers (both men and women) in various parks around Shanghai in the evening, after asking them to attach LED lights on their bodies while dancing. The artist captured around 80 photographs and in eight of them, the result appears as if dancers are writing an abstract secret language with the movement of their bodies, resembling some characters written in Nüshu 女书, such as "xin 心 (heart)"; "bei 背" (sadness); "shui 水" (water) and "xiao 小" (small).\r
This for the artist evokes the times when the female population gathered together to sing songs and do embroidery in the Nüshu village. By combining these photographs with the flowy Nüshu characters, she intends to form a poetic visual compilation of photography and writing."""@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/670> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/679> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8686> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Away with the Flow Series (顺气自然)"@en,
        "顺气自然系列"@zh,
        "Shùn qì zìrán xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/645> ;
    ns2:P580 "2019"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "In this series, Jessie Yingying Gong explores the subject of public exercise, this time spotlighting Taiji. This traditional form of Chinese martial arts incorporates routine and movement, breathing and mindfulness, along with the ideological balancing of the forces of yin and yang. In this binary, movement generates \"yang 阳\"; tranquility ultimately generates \"yin 阴\". The concept of Qi, the flow of energy, is essential to Taiji practice. It is the language used to pinpoint how the internal arts feels – an imagined communication between the mind and the body. In \"Away with the Flow\", the artist wants to visualize and manifest the bodily sensations in Taiji through a variety of media, involving photography, calligraphy, installation, visual poetry, video, and live performance."@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/681> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/681> a ns3:Q4502142 ;
