    ns2:P571 "2019"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """This artwork is composed of a set of 175 calligraphy drawings visualizing the movement and body language of Taiji practice. Jessie Yingying Gong has drawn lines with a brush dipped in yellow ink, drawing two arms and two legs of a little man that reproduces all the main movements of Taiji.\r
Jessie Yingying Gong also created a video with one of these drawing entiled "Away with the Flow - Animation video" (Shun qi ziran - shufa donghua 顺⽓⾃然 - 动画) a video of fiftyfive seconds creating one "Taiji Calligraphy" dancing animation. """@en ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q22669857> ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q614467> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/685> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8686> ;
    ns2:P1476 "New Semiotics Series (新符号学系列)"@en,
        "新符号学系列"@zh,
        "Xīn fúhào xué xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/645> ;
    ns2:P582 "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "In this series, starting in 2017 and ongoing, Jessie Yingying Gong compares  two ancient styles of writing: \"Linear B\", that was a syllabic script used for writing in Mycenaean Greek, the earliest attested form of Greek and \"oracle bone script\", used anciently in China. The letters and characters of an alphabet began as depictions of daily objects and at first, signified those alone. In time they came to represent syllables or sounds. In the Chinese written language, this is particularly evident, as the original memory of meaning is still visible. A study into the Greek history showed that it also exists in very early manifestations of the western alphabet. Linear B is a form of ancient Greek dating from the Mycenaean civilization. This half pictographic, half syllabic language surprisingly bears much similarity with the ancient Chinese oracle bone script. Studying the ancient languages and their semiotics, looking at how images transform into symbols that represent words and concepts, this project is to explore this resemblance of linguistic history - how each of them and their meanings evolved through time, and how two very different cultures began their rich literary traditions from a similar elementary starting point. The contrasts and similarities is brought to life with lively neon signs and playful clay figurines depicting and representing those ancient languages. These letters and symbols are something foreign and alien, yet they are the foundation of something very familiar and recognisable. The contrasts and similarities of linguistic history is brought to life with lively neon signs and playful clay figurines. Jessie Yingying Gong idealized Chinese characters in oracle bone script, such as: \"xue 学\" (study); \"xi 喜\" (happiness); \"meng 梦\" (dream); \"yi 艺\" (arts and crafts), etc."@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/687> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/692> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q727> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Enfolding Series"@en ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/645> ;
    ns2:P580 "2019"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """This series is divided into two parts:\r
- Part 1. In this part, the series conceptualized by Jessie Yingying Gong consists of the combination of Chinese characters which became “folded” into Chinese Nüshu and Japanese Onnade respectively.  “Women’s Writing”, Nüshu in Chinese, is a writing system developed by and only passed on among the female population of small villages in South Hunan Province. Derived from square Chinese characters and local symbols, Nüshu takes on a whimsical shape, looking almost like dancing figures. “Women’s Hand”, Onnade in Japanese, is a simplified writing style developed by women from Chinese cursive script, (later known as Hiragana), while Kanji was dominantly practiced by men at the time, and used to write official documents. The lenticular installation realized by Jessie Yingying Gong, demonstrates how one Chinese character became “folded” into Chinese Nüshu (Women’s Writing) and Japanese Onnade (Women’s Hand). respectively. According to the artist, it is the union between the Nushu and the Onnade that creates the Chinese character (or Kanji), underlining how the Chinese character is the element of universal connection and communication between the two worlds.\r
- Part 2. In this part, Jessie Yingying Gong conceptualized different lenticulars which demonstrate how Chinese characters became “folded” into the Japanese Onnade. In Japan, women developed a simplified writing style from Chinese cursive script, known as Onnade, meaning “Women’s Hand” (later known as Hiragana).\r
For both part 1 and part 2 the characters are designed on the computer first, by cutting each character (of the pair) into vertical strips, then stitching the strips one next to the other. By folding the sheet several times, an optical effect is obtained in which the two characters appear to join each other. \r
Onnade and Nüshu appeared and manifested themselves in distinct ways. Eventually Onnade gained the upper hand and became the accepted form of writing together with Kanji, while Chinese Nüshu is now a dying practice considered cultural heritage. Yet together they tell the story of our shared history and memory — how women in different countries gave themselves a voice in a restricted and patriarchal environment, and how our communities and societies are connected through this resourceful act of defiance."""@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/694> .

