        <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/cursive_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/757.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 1 The Bamboo Path (《诗经》,《 离骚》)" ;
    ns1:description "This CWU belongs to the installation \"The Bamboo Path\" (Zhu jing 竹径). The artist used cursive and clerical scripts on the bamboo poles to write poems from the \"Classic of Poetry\" (Shijing 诗经), some lines from the famous \"Encountering Sorrow“ (Li sao 离骚) and other historical lyrics about the bamboo from past dynasties. Instead of ink, Wang Dongling adopted a writing fluid made up of  kneading oil  (nieyou 捏油 ), a special oil used to protect Moso bamboo, mixing with acrylic paint. He wrote on 201 bamboo poles with a height of 5 meters and a diameter of 15 centimeters.  He employed clerical script exclusively on one pole, clearly alluding to the Qin (221-206 BCE) and Han (206 BCE–220 CE) dynasties. During these two dynasties, strips of bamboo or wood  written in clerical script and represented the main media for writing documents before the introduction of paper. "@en ;
    ns1:directionality "Traditional" ;
