    ns2:P2561 "Lin Hwai-min 林懷民 " ;
    ns2:P27 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1867> ;
    ns2:P361 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/811> ;
    ns2:P569 "1947-02-19"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P800 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1266> ;
    ns2:P812 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q186035> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1867> ;
    ns1:description "Lin Hwai-min attended Chun Wen Elementary School in Chiayi for one year before transferring to Taichung First High School. He next moved to Weido, a private Catholic Brothers’ boy school. His father encouraged him to take on law studies at the National Chengchi University but after a year, he switched majors to journalism. Graduating with a degree in journalism, Lin was drafted into the army with an office job in communications in Taipei. In the 1960s, when modern dance was gaining popularity in Taiwan, Lin enrolled in a dance studio. Later on, Lin attended workshops by renowned dancers such as Chungliang Al Huang and Yen Lo-Wong. In 1969, Lin left for the United States on a scholarship to study journalism at the University of Missouri. At Missouri, he received a fellowship to the University of Iowa’s International Writing Program to study under renowned poet, Paul Engle. At UI, he participated actively in theatre and fine arts. He also attended a Modern Dance course taught by Marcia Thayer. Lin would later on refer to her strong passion and dedication to the art of dance as an important influence. It is from this increased involvement in dance and fine arts that he sharpened his interest and enrolled in a summer course at Martha Graham Center of Contemporary Dance in New York, where he would later on spend most of his time upon receiving his master's degree from Iowa to hone his artistic skills and knowledge. When Lin returned to Taiwan in the 1970s, he started writing and choreographing as well as teaching at the National Chengchi University and the Chinese Cultural University. He founded \"Cloud Gate Dance Theatre of Taiwan\" in 1973 and retired at the end of 2019, appointing CHENG Tsung-lung the company’s new Artistic Director. Celebrated by Time magazine as one of Asia’s Heroes, he has led this company to international prominence through his thrilling creations that transform Asian aesthetics into contemporary celebration of motion. LIN has created over 90 choreographic pieces, many of which have left an indelible imprint on the audience’s memories, such as LEGACY, NINE SONGS, MOON WATER, RICE, SONGS OF THE WANDERERS, and “Cursive: A Trilogy”—CURSIVE, PINE SMOKE, and WILD CURSIVE. In 2019, his FORMOSA was selected by the Guardian as one of the Top 20 “Best Dances of the 21st Century.” In 1983, he created the Department of Dance at Taipei National University of the Arts and since 2000, he has been serving as the Artistic Director of the Novel Dance Series for the Novel Hall dance venue in Taipei. Lin often assimilates old forms of dance with contemporary style and interpretation. He has composed many original works as well as modified and adapted classical works from Peking opera. He incorporates different art forms into his pieces and marries Western and Eastern aesthetics and style into a distinct fusion signature of the Cloud Gate Company. He is famous for his ability to seamlessly integrate different styles such as ballet, traditional Chinese opera, Buddhist meditation and martial arts like tai chi as well as Taiwanese aborigines ritual dance into his choreographic works. He immerses himself in the study of Asian culture, such as studying court dance in Seoul in 1975 as well as spending quality time throughout his career in Bali to observe local rituals and ceremonies. Furthermore, most of his works would include themes that relate closely to Taiwan. At the beginning of his career, he was deeply concerned with political changes in his home country and he was also aware of the rise of Taiwanese consciousness. He felt responsible to compose pieces to reflect Taiwan’s history as this also intimately relate to his own past and upbringing. He draws inspiration from diverse sources of artistic styles to create unique contemporary dance movements that reflected Taiwan society's complexity. The dynamic Chinese and Japanese experiences of his family (and Taiwan's colonial past), his western education in the United States as well as his vast knowledge on Asian and Taiwanese Aboriginal traditional cultures and rituals all contributed significantly in helping him form his artistic vision of Taiwan."@en ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Academic"@en ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q528265> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/838> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Encountering Sorrow (离骚)"@en,
        "离骚"@zh,
        "Lí Sāo"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1480 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5727902> ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q313330> ;
    ns2:P571 "0300"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "This is an ancient Chinese poem from the anthology \"Chu Ci\" (楚辞) traditionally attributed to Qu Yuan 屈原 (c. 340 BCE – 278 BCE). \"Li Sao\" dates from the 3rd century BCE, during the Chinese Warring States period (475-221 BCE). It is the purported biography of its author. In \"Li Sao\", in fact, the poet despairs that he has been plotted against by evil factions at court with his resulting rejection by his lord and then recounts a series of shamanistic spirit journeys to various mythological realms, engaging or attempting to engage with a variety of divine or spiritual beings, with the theme of the righteous minister unfairly rejected sometimes becoming exaggerated in the long history of later literary criticism and allegorical interpretation."@en ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q679294> ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """Of the god-king Gao-yang I am the far offspring,\r
My late honored sire bore the name of Bo-yong.\r
The she-ti stars aimed to the year's first month;\r
Geng-yin was the day that I came down.\r
\r
He scanned and delved into my first measure,\r
From the portents my sire gave these noble names:\r
The name that he gave me was Upright Standard;\r
And my title of honor was Godly Poise.\r
\r
Such bounty I had of beauty within,\r
And to this was added fair countenance.\r
I wore mantles of river rush and remote angelica,\r
Strung autumn orchids to hang from my sash.\r
\r
I knelt with robes open, thus stated my case,\r
Having grasped so clearly what is central and right.\r
I teamed jade white dragons, rode the Bird that Hides Sky,\r
Waiting on winds to fleetly fare upward.\r
\r
At dawn I loosed wheel-block there by Cang-wu,\r
And by twilight I reached the Gardens of Air.\r
I wished to bide a while by the windows of gods,\r
But swift was the sun and it soon would be dusk.\r
\r
I bade sun-driver Xi-he, to pause in her pace,\r
To stand off from Yan-zi and not to draw night.\r
On and on stretched my road, long it was and far,\r
I would go high and go low in this search that I made.\r
\r
I watered my horses in the Pools of Xian,\r
And twisted the reins on the tree Fu-sang,\r
I snapped a branch of the Ruo Tree to block out the sun,\r
I roamed freely the while and lingered there.\r
\r
Those men of faction had ill-gotten pleasures,\r
Their paths went in shadow, narrow, unsafe.\r
Not for myself came this dread of doom—\r
I feared my king's chariot soon would be tipped.\r
\r
In haste I went dashing in front and behind,\r
Till I came to the tracks of our kings before.\r
Lord Iris did not fathom my nature within,\r
He believed ill words, he glowered in rage.\r
\r
I knew well my bluntness had brought me these woes,\r
Yet I bore through them, I could not forswear.\r
I pointed to Heaven to serve as my warrant,\r
It was all for the cause of the Holy One.\r
\r
It is now done forever!\r
In all the kingdom there is no man, no man who knows me,\r
Then why should I care for that city, my home?\r
Since no one will join me in making good rule,\r
I will go off to seek where Peng and Xian dwell."""@en,
        """帝高陽之苗裔兮，\r
朕皇考曰伯庸。\r
攝提貞于孟陬兮，\r
惟庚寅吾以降。\r
\r
皇覽揆余初度兮，\r
肇錫余以嘉名。\r
名余曰正則兮，\r
字余曰靈均。\r
\r
紛吾既有此內美兮，\r
又重之以脩能。\r
扈江離與辟芷兮，\r
紐秋蘭以為佩。\r
跪敷衽以陳辭兮，\r
耿吾既得此中正。\r
駟玉虯以乘鷖兮，\r
溘埃風余上徵。\r
\r
朝發軔於蒼梧兮，\r
夕余至乎縣圃。\r
欲少留此靈瑣兮，\r
日忽忽其將暮。\r
\r
吾令羲和弭節兮，\r
望崦嵫而勿迫。\r
路曼曼其脩遠兮，\r
吾將上下而求索。\r
\r
飲余馬於咸池兮，\r
總余轡乎扶桑。\r
折若木以拂日兮，\r
聊逍遙以相羊。\r
惟黨人之偷樂兮，\r
路幽昧以險隘。\r
豈余身之憚殃兮，\r
恐皇輿之敗績。\r
\r
忽奔走以先後兮，\r
及前王之踵武。\r
荃不察余之忠情兮，\r
反信讒而齌怒。\r
\r
余固知謇謇之為患兮，\r
忍而不能舍也。\r
指九天以為正兮，\r
夫唯靈脩之故也。\r
\r
已矣哉，\r
國無人莫我知兮，\r
又何懷乎故都？\r
既莫足與為美政兮，\r
吾將從彭咸之所居。"""@zh,
        """Dì gāoyáng zhī miáoyì xī,\r
zhèn huáng kǎo yuē bó yōng.\r
Shè tí zhēn yú mèng zōu xī,\r
wéi gēng yín wú yǐjiàng.\r
\r
Huáng lǎn kuí yú chūdù xī,\r
zhào xī yú yǐ jiā míng.\r
Míng yú yuē zhèngzé xī,\r
zì yú yuē líng jūn.\r
\r
Fēn wú jì yǒu cǐ nèi měi xī,\r
yòu zhòng zhī yǐ xiū néng.\r
Hù jiāng lí yǔ pì zhǐ xī,\r
niǔ qiū lán yǐwéi pèi.\r
Guì fū rèn yǐ chén cí xī,\r
gěng wú jìdé cǐ zhōngzhèng.\r
Sì yù qiú yǐ chéng yī xī,\r
kè āi fēng yú shàng zhǐ.\r
\r
Zhāo fārèn yú cāngwú xī,\r
xī yú zhì hū xiàn pǔ.\r
Yù shǎo liú cǐ líng suǒ xī,\r
rì hūhū qí jiāng mù.\r
\r
Wú lìng xī hé mǐ jié xī,\r
wàng yān zī ér wù pò.\r
Lù màn màn qí xiū yuǎn xī,\r
wú jiāng shàngxià ér qiúsuǒ.\r
\r
Yǐn yú mǎ yú xián chí xī,\r
zǒng yú pèi hū fúsāng.\r
Zhé ruòmù yǐ fú rì xī,\r
liáo xiāoyáo yǐ xiāng yáng.\r
Wéi dǎng rén zhī tōu lè xī,\r
lù yōu mèi yǐ xiǎn'ài.\r
Qǐ yú shēn zhī dàn yāng xī,\r
kǒng huáng yú zhī bàijī.\r
\r
Hū bēnzǒu yǐ xiānhòu xī,\r
jí qián wáng zhī zhǒng wǔ.\r
Quán bù chá yúzhīzhōng qíng xī,\r
fǎn xìn chán ér jì nù.\r
\r
Yú gù zhī jiǎn jiǎn zhī wéihuàn xī,\r
rěn ér bùnéng shě yě.\r
Zhǐ jiǔtiān yǐwéi zhèng xī,\r
fū wéi líng xiū zhī gù yě.\r
\r
Yǐ yǐ zāi,\r
guó wúrén mò wǒ zhī xī,\r
yòu hé huái hū gù dōu?\r
Jì mò zú yǔ wéi měi zhèng xī,\r
wú jiāng cóng péngxiánzhī suǒ jū."""@zh-Latn .

