    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/regular_running_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/94.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 2 Ars divina  (《点绛唇》) " ;
    ns1:description "This Calli-Writing Unit is a calligraphic inscription in the top-right corner of the artwork.  It seems to be a Chinese traditional calligraphy written in a style between \"regular script\" (kaishu 楷书) and \"running or semi-cursive script\" (xingshu 行书), but it was not created using the classic tools of Chinese calligraphy: the writers employed spray cans instead of brushes; the ink is replaced by black spray paint; the support is a white wall, instead of paper. The text quoted in the calligraphy is the second stanza of an ancient poem entitled \"Rouged Lips\" (Dian Jiangchun 点绛唇). It was composed by Wang Yucheng 王禹偁 (954-1001), a famous poet/scholar of the Song dynasty (960-1279), during his exile in the South of the Empire, after being dismissed from his government position due to strong criticism against the policies of the time. In the poem, he expresses his sadness and despair, with nature becoming the mirror and voice of his deepest feelings: empathy with nature, pain for the unjust exile, pervasive loneliness and idyllic scenes are the central themes of the poem. These are recurrent themes in China’s lyric poetry of all times, and through this work they are revived in a fragment of contemporary urban art."@en ;
    ns1:directionality "Traditional" ;
    ns1:hasBackgroundColour <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42519> ;
