    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1128.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "Influenced by the arguments put forth by the French philosopher and critic Jacques Derrida, who advocated for the complete deconstruction and reassembly of conventional structures, Wang Nanming swiftly recognized that his habit of crumpling sheets of paper into tight balls whenever dissatisfied with his work during calligraphy practice symbolized the myriad contradictions inherent in calligraphy itself. Viewing these discarded paper balls as emblematic of the negative aspects of Chinese culture, Wang found in them a compelling visual and conceptual form. His act of crumpling and discarding sheets of work became a performative gesture of refusal. Since 1992, Wang Nanming began transforming his Balls of Characters (Ziqiu 字球) into Combinations: Balls of Characters (Ziqiu zuhe 字球组合), with which he has been creating various objects such as chairs, sofas, and tables. This artistic evolution served as his protest against what he perceived as the superficiality of mass culture in contemporary China."@en ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q128115>,
