    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/cursive_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1185.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 2 Olympic Beijing (lines)" ;
    ns1:description """This Calli-Writing Unit is the inscription located in the upper right-hand corner of the graffiti artwork "Olympic Beijing". It is a long calligraphy composed of 36 columns of "pseudo-Chinese-characters" written in a highly stenographic cursive style (caoshu 草书), where the strokes of the characters sometimes merge, impairing their recognizability. The written text is therefore unreadable because these "pseudo-Chinese-characters" have no actual meaning. According to EricTin: "It's akin to how people today might see large sections of text but lack the patience to decipher their meaning" (interview 2024). The text is arranged in vertical columns to be "read" from right to left and from top to bottom, as is customary in classical Chinese, and is enhanced by a pronounced dripping effect that simulates ink drippings on paper.\r
The cursive calligraphic style, the variation in stroke width, the vertical arrangement of the text (from top to bottom), and the reading direction (from right to left) adhere to traditional calligraphic conventions and evoke the strict rules of traditional Chinese calligraphy. However, the widespread dripping effect underscores the graffiti aesthetic. """@en ;
