    ns1:description "This series of contemporary dance performances is composed of: “Cursive” (Xingcao 行草, 2001), “Pine Smoke · Cursive II” (Song yan · Xingcao er 松煙 · 行草贰, 2003) and “Wild Cursive” (Kuangcao 狂草, 2005). All of these performances are realized by the Cloud Gate Dance Theatre of Taiwan (Yunmen wuji 云门舞集) under Lin Hwai-min 林懷民 (b.1947), the artistic director of the company."@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/819> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1271> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Landscript ( 文字写生) "@en,
        "文字写生"@zh,
        "Wénzì xiěshēng"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1096> ;
    ns2:P580 "1999"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2013"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "In this series, Xu Bing used Chinese characters as pictorial elements to arrange landscape paintings. In this way, characters for “stone” (shi 石) make up images of rocks; the character for “tree” (mu 木) makes up trees; the character for “grass” (cao 艸) grass, for “leaves” (ye 叶) leaves, for “clouds” (yun 云) clouds, and so on. The characters varying from regular and cursive scripts to simplified forms as well as such archaic forms as bronze and oracle-bone scripts are the ones he uses the most because of their pictographic value. In this series, started in 1999 when the artist went to the Himalayas sketching natural “scenes” with Chinese characters, Xu Bing transforms visual images of landscapes into linguistic forms, inviting the viewers to reassess the distinctiveness of Chinese culture hidden within traditional landscape paintings, and offers a unique approach to “read a scene”.  Chinese characters, becoming pictorial elements, regain their value as semantic elements."@en ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1828> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1304> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "New Poems Series (新诗系列)"@en,
        "新诗系列"@zh,
        "Xīnshī xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1260> ;
    ns2:P580 "1988"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "1989"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1304.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The New Poem Series (新诗系列), begun by Qiu Zhenzhong in 1980, marks a radical shift in Chinese calligraphy. The artist transcribes free verse in vernacular language (xinshi 新诗), following a horizontal layout from left to right — a completely new orientation for Chinese script. In this format, the characters lose their legibility, becoming pure visual rhythm.\r
The series explores the visual power of Chinese characters, especially family names. Inspired by the Hundred Family Surnames, each character becomes a symbol of individuals, families, or collective memory. Without the rules of grammar or syntax, Qiu gains full freedom to arrange the characters as pure visual forms. """ ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1304_HR.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1305> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/132> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "The Hard Road to Sichuan/Hard Roads in Shu (蜀道难)"@en,
        "蜀道难 "@zh,
        "Shǔ dào nán"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1480 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5727902> ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7071> ;
    ns2:P571 "0742"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "“The Hard Road to Sichuan/Hard Roads in Shu” is a representative poem of Li Bai (701-762), a great poet of the Tang Dynasty. The whole poem is a continous and elaborate narration of the difficulties in the access to western Sichuan called Shu. The poem exhibits rich connotations of the expression about the jeopardy and fright of the Shu Road.  The poetic form of this poetry belongs to the poetic genre of 'yuefu' (乐府)." ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q18897158> ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """   Oh, but it is high and very dangerous!  \r
Such traveling is harder than scaling the blue sky.  \r
...Until two rulers of this region  \r
Pushed their way through in the misty ages,  \r
Forty-eight thousand years had passed  \r
With nobody arriving across the Qin border.  \r
And the Great White Mountain, westward, still has only a bird's path  \r
Up to the summit of Emei Peak --  \r
Which was broken once by an earthquake and there were brave men lost,  \r
Just finishing the stone rungs of their ladder toward heaven.  \r
...High, as on a tall flag, six dragons drive the sun,  \r
While the river, far below, lashes its twisted course.  \r
Such height would be hard going for even a yellow crane,  \r
So pity the poor monkeys who have only paws to use.  \r
The Mountain of Green Clay is formed of many circles-  \r
 Each hundred steps, we have to turn nine turns among its mound --  \r
Panting, we brush Orion and pass the Well Star,  \r
Then, holding our chests with our hands and sinking to the ground with a groan,  \r
We wonder if this westward trail will never have an end.  \r
The formidable path ahead grows darker, darker still,  \r
With nothing heard but the call of birds hemmed in by the ancient forest,  \r
Male birds smoothly wheeling, following the females;  \r
And there come to us the melancholy voices of the cuckoos  \r
Out on the empty mountain, under the lonely moon....  \r
Such traveling is harder than scaling the blue sky.  \r
Even to hear of it turns the cheek pale,  \r
With the highest crag barely a foot below heaven.  \r
Dry pines hang, head down, from the face of the cliffs,  \r
And a thousand plunging cataracts outroar one another  \r
And send through ten thousand valleys a thunder of spinning stones.  \r
With all this danger upon danger,  \r
Why do people come here who live at a safe distance?  \r
...Though Dagger-Tower Pass be firm and grim,  \r
And while one man guards it  \r
Ten thousand cannot force it,  \r
What if he be not loyal,  \r
But a wolf toward his fellows?  \r
...There are ravenous tigers to fear in the day  \r
And venomous reptiles in the night  \r
With their teeth and their fangs ready  \r
To cut people down like hemp.  \r
Though the City of Silk be delectable, I would rather turn home quickly.  \r
Such traveling is harder than scaling the blue sky....  \r
But I still face westward with a dreary moan.\r
(Translated by Witter Bynner)"""@en,
        """ 噫吁戲 \r
 危乎高哉 \r
  蜀道之難難於上青天 \r
  蠶叢及魚鳧 \r
  開國何茫然 \r
  爾來四萬八千歲 \r
  始與秦塞通人煙 \r
  西當太白有鳥道 \r
  可以橫絕峨眉巔 \r
  地崩山摧壯士死 \r
  然後天梯石棧方鉤連 \r
  上有六龍回日之高標 \r
  下有衝波逆折之迴川 \r
  黃鶴之飛尚不得 \r
  猿猱欲度愁攀援 \r
  青泥何盤盤 \r
  百步九折縈巖巒 \r
  捫參歷井仰脅息 \r
  以手撫膺坐長歎 \r
  問君西遊何時還 \r
  畏途巉巖不可攀 \r
  但見悲鳥號古木 \r
  雄飛雌從繞林間 \r
  又聞子規啼 \r
  夜月愁空山 \r
  蜀道之難難於上青天 \r
  使人聽此凋朱顏 \r
  連峰去天不盈尺 \r
  枯松倒掛倚絕壁 \r
  飛湍瀑流爭喧豗\r
砯崖轉石萬壑雷 \r
  其險也如此 \r
  嗟爾遠道之人 \r
  胡為乎來哉 \r
  劍閣崢嶸而崔嵬 \r
  一夫當關 \r
  萬夫莫開 \r
  所守或匪親 \r
  化為狼與豺 \r
  朝避猛虎 \r
  夕避長蛇 \r
  磨牙吮血 \r
  殺人如麻 \r
  錦城雖云樂 \r
 不如早還家 \r
  蜀道之難難於上青天 \r
  側身西望常咨嗟 """@zh,
        """噫吁戏\r
危乎高哉\r
蜀道之难难于上青天\r
蚕丛及鱼凫\r
开国何茫然\r
尔来四万八千岁\r
始与秦塞通人烟\r
西当太白有鸟道\r
可以横绝峨眉巅\r
地崩山摧壮士死\r
然后天梯石栈方钩连\r
上有六龙回日之高标\r
下有冲波逆折之回川\r
黄鹤之飞尚不得\r
猿猱欲度愁攀援\r
青泥何盘盘\r
百步九折萦岩峦\r
扪参历井仰胁息\r
以手抚膺坐长叹\r
问君西游何时还\r
畏途巉岩不可攀\r
但见悲鸟号古木\r
雄飞雌从绕林间\r
又闻子规啼\r
夜月愁空山\r
蜀道之难难于上青天\r
使人听此凋朱颜\r
连峰去天不盈尺\r
枯松倒挂倚绝壁\r
飞湍瀑流争喧豗\r
砯崖转石万壑雷\r
其险也如此\r
嗟尔远道之人\r
胡为乎来哉\r
剑阁峥嵘而崔嵬\r
一夫当关\r
万夫莫开\r
所守或匪亲\r
化为狼与豺\r
朝避猛虎\r
夕避长蛇\r
磨牙吮血\r
杀人如麻\r
锦城虽云乐\r
不如早还家\r
蜀道之难难于上青天\r
侧身西望常咨嗟"""@zh,
        """Yī xū xì \r
wēi hū gāo zāi \r
shǔ dào zhī nán nányú shàng qīngtiān \r
cán cóng jí yú fú \r
kāiguó hé mángrán \r
ěr lái sì wàn bāqiān suì \r
shǐ yǔ qín sāi tōng rén yān \r
xī dāng tàibái yǒu niǎo dào \r
kěyǐ héng jué éméi diān \r
dì bēngshān cuī zhuàngshì sǐ \r
ránhòu tiāntī shí zhàn fāng gōu lián \r
shàng yǒu liù lóng huí rì zhī gāo biāo \r
xià yǒu chōng bō nì zhé zhī huí chuān \r
huánghèzhī fēi shàng bùdé \r
yuán náo yù dù chóu pānyuán \r
qīng ní hé pán pán \r
bǎi bù jiǔ zhé yíng yán luán \r
mén cān lì jǐng yǎng xié xī \r
yǐ shǒu fǔ yīng zuò zhǎngtàn \r
wèn jūn xīyóu héshí hái \r
wèitú chán yán bùkě pān \r
dàn jiàn bēi niǎo hào gǔmù \r
xióngfēi cí cóng rào línjiàn \r
yòu wén zǐguī tí \r
yè yuè chóu kōngshān \r
shǔ dào zhī nán nányú shàng qīngtiān \r
shǐ rén tīng cǐ diāo zhū yán l\r
iánfēng qù tiān bù yíng chǐ \r
kū sōng dàoguà yǐ juébì \r
fēi tuān pù liú zhēng xuān huī \r
pīng yá zhuǎn shí wàn hè léi \r
qí xiǎn yě rúcǐ \r
jiē ěr yuǎndào zhī rén \r
hú wèi hū lái zāi \r
jiàngé zhēng róng ér cuīwéi \r
yīfū dāng guān wàn fū mò kāi \r
suǒ shǒu huò fěi qīn \r
huà wéi láng yǔ chái \r
zhāo bì měnghǔ \r
xī bì chángshé \r
móyá shǔn xuè \r
shārén rú má \r
jǐnchéng suī yún lè \r
bùrú zǎo hái jiā \r
shǔ dào zhī nán nányú shàng qīng tiān \r
cèshēn xī wàng cháng zī jiē."""@zh-Latn .

