    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1391.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The artwork “Love Writing 98-18” is part of Luo Qi’s “Love Writing” series (1990–2002), which explores the intersection of calligraphy and painting. In this work, Luo Qi uses oil on canvas, a Western medium traditionally absent from Chinese calligraphy, to reinterpret calligraphic forms through a painterly, abstract language. Although the brushstrokes may resemble Chinese characters at first glance, but they are actually stylized graphic signs inspired by ancient scripts, particularly the ancient bronze script (jinwen 金文), which serve as the visual backbone of the composition. Their pictographic quality, combined with the use of bold, primary colors, gives the painting both symbolic depth and strong visual impact.  The pictographic feel of these signs, along with the visual and symbolic power of the composition, is marked by the use of primary colors and bold contrasts. \r
Drawing inspiration from Western abstraction, especially the structured compositions of Piet Mondrian (1872 – 1944) and the symbolic language of Paul Klee (1879 – 1940). Luo Qi does not aim for geometric perfection. Instead, he maintains the expressive quality of his gestures: the lines shift in weight and rhythm, and colors are layered with tactile texture and spontaneous rhythm. """ ;
