<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1393> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Love Writing (情书)"@en,
        "Lettere d'amore"@it,
        "情书"@zh,
        " Qíngshū"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/84> ;
    ns2:P580 "1990"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2002"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1393_1.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1393_2.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Love Writing (Qíngshū 情书, 1990-2002) is one of Luo Qi’s most emblematic and conceptually rich series, representing a pivotal development in his artistic career. Initiated in 1990, the series evolves through an increasingly complex dialogue between Chinese calligraphy and Western abstraction. It marks a significant departure from the conventions of classical calligraphy, leading to the formation of what Luo Qi himself later defined as "Calligraphyism" (Shūfǎ zhǔyì 书法主义).\r
The link with Chinese heritage is reasserted through the use of pictographic and seal script forms (e.g. Jiǎgǔwén 甲骨文 and Zhuànshū 篆书), transformed into abstract visual language. These archaic elements, reworked and reimagined, become a recurring motif across the series. As Lars (p. 42) notes, “The results were really remarkable, transforming these old scripts into a variety of richly coloured abstractions”. Indeed, from 1994 onward, Love Writing becomes a true fusion of Chinese and Western aesthetics (Iezzi 2019, 97–98), due to the employment of oil painting and the influence of major Western modernists, especially Mondrian and Klee, in the artist's compositional strategies and chromatic choices. The artist explores the expressive possibilities of both black and colored ink, treating them in painterly fashion to create macro-signs increasingly oriented toward abstraction and symbolic resonance. In this context, calligraphy becomes line, and painting becomes color—two elements integrated into what Luo Qi calls “calligraphy-and-painting” compositions.\r
Throughout the series, the evolution of Luo Qi’s visual language unfolds in four main phases (Iezzi 2019, 195–221), with key turning points in 1994, 1996, and 1998:\r
- Phase 1 (1990-1993): The series begins with Love Writing No. 1, later exhibited in the landmark show Brushed Voices: Calligraphy in Contemporary China (Miriam & Ira D. Wallach Art Gallery, Columbia University, New York, April 15 – June 6, 1998). This work, together with The Original Word (原句 Yuan ju, 1997) and Blackism No. 1 (黑色主义之一 Heise zhuyi zhi yi, 1990), encapsulates the artist’s early explorations of deconstructed calligraphic forms. Luo Qi blends brush techniques from various styles, fragments character shapes, and begins to introduce dynamic spatial arrangements. The works are executed in ink on paper, in the traditional vertical scroll format.\r
- Phase 2 (1994–1995): The influence of classical calligraphy remains pronounced. Strokes of black ink form structures reminiscent of archaic or cursive characters, though their proportions are distorted and their composition is intentionally disjointed. Luo moves away from the vertical column structure typical of traditional calligraphy.\r
He begins to introduce color within outlined shapes, creating harmonic relationships between tones. Yet the medium remains ink on paper, and the large-scale hanging scroll format is still employed.\r
- Phase 3 (1996-1997): This phase marks a radical shift: Luo Qi embraces oil on canvas, a Western medium traditionally absent from Chinese calligraphy. The reference to written characters largely disappears, replaced by abstract grids, intersecting lines, and bold color fields. Influences from Mondrian’s compositional rigor and Klee’s symbolic abstraction become increasingly visible. Unlike Mondrian’s pursuit of formal perfection, however, Luo Qi retains a strong human presence in the gesture and texture of his works. Lines vary in weight and direction, and colors are layered expressively rather than mechanically.\r
- Phase 4 (1998-2002): Luo Qi returns to a more structured visual rhythm, recalling the organization of calligraphy in columns. The compositions are more balanced, featuring grid-like arrangements, stylized symbols, and a playful graphic vocabulary akin to Klee and Miró. (compare the artwork "Love Writing 98-18" of this series). There is a marked tendency toward monochrome palettes and compositional clarity, signaling a synthesis of abstraction and calligraphic heritage.""" ;
