    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/140.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 1 2008 Beijing Paralympic Games Logo (之)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU consists of the Chinese character “zhi 之”—which can signify “birth,” “life,” “going,” or “attainment”—shaped as a stylized figure of an athlete in motion. The character is rendered in a Chinese calligraphic typeface inspired by running script (xingshu 行书), conveying a sense of fluidity and energy. Its design embodies the Paralympic motto “Spirit in Motion,” while also illustrating the philosophical principle of the integration of heart, body, and spirit, as well as the unity and harmony of "heaven, earth, and humankind" (Tiandiren 天地人), two central tenets of Chinese culture. The three colors of the logo—red, blue, and green—represent the sun, sky, and earth, respectively, reinforcing the symbolic connection between human beings and the natural cosmos.\r
According to the "Shuowen Jiezi" (说文解字), the earliest surviving Chinese dictionary, completed by Xu Shen (许慎) around 100 CE, the original form of the character “zhi 之” derives from “zhi 止” (foot) with a line added beneath it. This etymology emphasizes movement and the notion of “going forward,” which resonates with the mission of the Paralympics: transcending physical and personal limits. The character’s form appears to depict a human figure with a head (the red dot), arms and torso (the blue stroke), and a single stroke in place of two legs (the green stroke). This visual form evokes the physical conditions of some Paralympic athletes, while emphasizing their capacity to move, walk, and progress in continuous motion—symbolizing the extraordinary effort that persons with disabilities must exert both in athletic competition and in everyday life."""@en ;
