    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1437.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """"Black Series" (1990) is one of the abstract calligraphic works realized by Wang Nanming. The artist used black ink to almost covers the entire surface of the paper. The work was first exhibited at the 1991 Nanjing Invitational Exhibition of Works by Contemporary Calligraphers. It marks Wang's critical exploration of calligraphy as a cultural system. This inquiry continued in later works, including  "Balls of Characters"  (1992).\r
Wang regarded calligraphy not only as a refined art form, but also as an instrument of social control in Chinese history (Barrass 2002, 251-252). Its complexity restricted access to education and reinforced cultural authority and social hierarchy. For this reason, his works acquire a stronger provocative dimension. Before viewing the work, visitors were asked to sign their name on a sheet of paper with a brush is placed beside it. However, the warning instructions: “Do not sign using the brush" exposed the visitors to the tension between ritual habit and contemporary awareness, as traditional forms and tools appear anachronistic and obsolete. Wang suggests that traditional conventions are no longer viable in the present.""" ;
