<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1453> a ns3:Q4502142 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q217681> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Ink Shooting (射墨) - Artwork"@en,
        "射墨"@zh,
        "Shè mò"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/277> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q170406>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3502310> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11472>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P571 "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:derivesFrom <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/356> ;
    ns1:description """This artwork is part of the Shao Yan’s "Ink Shooting" performance realized in 2017.  The artwork, created through the projection of ink from syringes onto an extended sheet of paper, challenges conventional understandings of form, legibility, and medium. What emerges is not a composition of decipherable symbols, but a dynamic visual field marked by bursts, splatters, and directional traces—each a record of intentional movement and physical force.\r
Far from being a random abstraction, the image is charged with conceptual depth: it embodies a deliberate departure from the brush as instrument, and from character as the sole carrier of meaning. Shao Yan reframes calligraphy as a visual language of energy and impulse, one in which ink becomes a conduit for embodied expression. His technique, defined "ink shooting" positions the work within a lineage of Chinese calligraphy, while also radically expanding its scope. By removing recognizable characters, the work foregrounds the expressive residue of movement and the visceral relationship between artist and medium. The image on paper is the trace of a momentary yet intentional act, an inscription of force and presence that redefines the boundaries of what writing can be.""" ;
