    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/526> ;
    ns1:description """This performance by Wu Xixia is realized in 2024 in Guizhou. The whole conceptual work is based on the deconstruction of the Chinese word “tongjing 痛经” (dysmenorrhoea/menstrual pain). This word indicates the menstrual flow and the pain it causes. Separating the two characters, “tong 痛” means “pain, sadness, sorrow”, while the word “jing 经” refers to “scriptures” or “classics”, such as “Book of the Way and Virtue” (Dao De Jing), “Four Books and Five Classics” (Sishu wujing 四书五经), etc.  For the artist, separating these two characters means “deviating from the scriptures” and therefore is equivalent to suffering.  At both ends of the riverbank, the artist invites the public to pull up red cloth strips and apply tension to them to play “tug of war” at both ends of the bank. The artist is in the middle of the riverbank and writes the top ten criminal laws used to punish women in ancient China on the red cloth strips, such as: 1) "zhen xing 针刑" (needle torture), a type of torture involving the use of needles or pins to inflict pain on the victim, especially to penetrate the skin or sensitive areas of the body; 2) "yi zhang hong 一丈红" (one foot of red), a torture method also known as "red stick torture" that was practiced with a stick used to repeatedly hit a person, causing bruises and damage to the skin; 3) "mu lü 木驴" (wooden donkey), a form of torture involving the victim being forced to sit or lean on a sharp or awkwardly shaped wooden object, causing intense pain; 4) "you bi 幽闭" (confinement), a torture by solitary confinement, where the victim was locked in a narrow or dark space for a prolonged period, inflicting both physical and psychological suffering; 5) "luo xing 烙刑" (branding), a torture by branding, which involved burning the victim's skin with a hot iron, to brand them with permanent scars or to inflict intense pain; 6) "peng zhu 烹煮" (boiling), a torture method that involved cooking the victim alive in boiling water or other high-temperature liquids, causing severe burns and suffering; 7) "ku ti 刳剔" (beheading), a type of torture in which internal parts of the victim's body, such as organs, were removed or mutilated with the intent of inflicting suffering and slow death; 8) "jin zhu long 浸猪笼" (soaking in pig cage), a torture in which the victim was immersed in a narrow cage, often filled with water or other liquids, causing asphyxiation or physical torment; 9) "hong xiu xie 红绣鞋" (red embroidered shoes), a symbolic torture method, where the victim was forced to wear special shoes as a sign of humiliation or social punishment; and 10) "za xing 拶刑" (finger crushing), a type of torture in which the victim's fingers were crushed or injured with torture instruments, causing intense pain and permanent deformities.\r
After writing on the red cloth strips, Xixia uses them to wraps herself in. At the same time, the pulling force in the hands of the public continues to increase. The artist is bound and tightened in the red cloth strips, with the whole body immersed in the river water. The work mobilizes the cultural body and sensory body in the female body. This is a self-salvation with pain and suffocation as the purpose. The red cloth strips symbolize the umbilical cord and the menstruation (personified by the public) that “trapped” the body. Even in this case the “woman/self” is regarded as an “object” to be viewed, and the public is the “subject” of the gaze. The feeling of “pain” and “suffocation” can perceive the body, or “existence”. In suffocation, we gain the internal perception of the body."""@en ;
