<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1502> a ns3:Q215627 ;
    ns2:P103 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P106 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q15095148>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2914170> ;
    ns2:P1412 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1860> ;
    ns2:P21 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6581097> ;
    ns2:P2561 "Exas 灵丹" ;
    ns2:P27 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P361 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1494> ;
    ns2:P800 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1557> ;
    ns2:P856 <https://www.instagram.com/fat_spoon_x/> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns1:description """Exas, a writer from Beijing, began his journey into graffiti during high school around 2005. At the time, he was a "toy" – a graffiti term used to describe novices – and primarily imitated the work of European graffiti artists. However, it was not until 2012 that Exas decided to focus on using Chinese characters in his own work. This shift was inspired by his crewmate, Funk, who motivated him to explore Chinese characters that resembled his English name. As a result, Exas adopted the tag "Ling Dan 灵丹," which means "panacea." He explained that the first character of this name means "soul," and together with Funk, they presented themselves on the streets as "funk and soul."\r
Originally, Exas was part of the BJPZ graffiti crew for a period, where most of the members incorporated Chinese characters in their work. However, he noted that much of this work leaned more toward commercial design rather than traditional graffiti. But he was also part of several crews, as there are continuous switches and collaborations between writers and crews in China.\r
Transitioning from English to Chinese characters in his graffiti presented significant challenges for him. He explained that Chinese characters require more time to execute and make it harder to develop a distinctive style without directly imitating someone else’s work. \r
His lettering in Chinese reflected a traditional and simple aesthetic, characterized by straight lines and rounded edges, unintentionally resembling the Cai Yun font. He admitted that he hadn’t realized the resemblance, attributing it to the simplicity that made it easier to paint and suited his preference for bombing—graffiti in public spaces—over legal work. He emphasized that graffiti should integrate into daily urban life rather than be confined to art districts or shopping mall walls. Reflecting on the changes in Beijing’s graffiti scene, Exas expressed his disappointment, noting that the scene had largely faded as much of the work was being erased overnight. He recalled a time when Beijing attracted graffiti artists from around the world, even allowing graffiti on subways. In 2008, the government promoted graffiti as a form of urban decoration, but he acknowledged that this period had long since ended.\r
In 2020, during the Black Lives Matter protests following the death of George Floyd, Exas felt compelled to take action. Along with his crewmate, Elvis, he founded a graffiti crew called "Yellow Peril," with the intention of supporting graffiti in Asian languages. The two had long wanted to create such a crew, and the moment seemed right. Exas explained that, since he listened to rap music and engaged with Black culture, he wanted to show solidarity through his work. The name "Yellow Peril" had historically been a xenophobic term used to describe Asian immigrants but had been reclaimed during the 1960s civil rights movement by Asian American activists, who aligned with Black power movements like the Black Panthers.\r
Despite the name's activist roots, Exas acknowledged that its layered history made it difficult to communicate its meaning across different cultures. He noted that in China, many people misinterpreted "Yellow Peril" as an insult if they searched for it online, unaware of its activist connotations, leading to significant backlash, including hate mail. He also produced T-shirts featuring the slogan, but some of his European crewmates were uncomfortable wearing them, concerned that a white person wearing a "Yellow Peril" shirt might be misinterpreted as racist. As a result, Exas began limiting the use of the full phrase to private settings, using only the abbreviation "YP" in public. Following the surge of anti-Asian hate crimes in Western countries, the message behind Yellow Peril gained a greater sense of urgency. Exas expressed his desire to show that Chinese or Asian individuals should not feel afraid or ashamed and could instead take pride in their identity. Despite this, he concluded on a pessimistic note, expressing doubt that graffiti could enact significant change."""@en ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Present (not specified)"@en ;
    ns1:hasTag "Exas"@en,
        "灵丹"@zh,
        "Língdān"@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1507> a ns3:Q215627 ;
    ns2:P103 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q9186> ;
    ns2:P106 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q15095148> ;
