    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1552.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "This installation is part of the series \"United Nations\" by Gu Wenda. It was exhibited at the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art in 1999. The artist creates a tower entirely made of human hair collected from 325 barber shops and hair salons from 18 countries from every continent. The hair has been woven into what appears to be a text, but all of these texts are illegible. They are based on Chinese, English, Arabic and Hindi; there are also fragments of English language words but in fact none of them could be read, and texts actually don't make full sense. This is why this artwork is called \"Babel of the Millennium\", referencing to the biblical story of the Tower of Babel from the Genesis. This artwork is a contemporary interpretation of this story contextualized in a global world where we understand each other but only in fragments misunderstanding the  subtext and nuances of language. The utopia of achieving unity within humanity may never be realized in reality, but can become possible through art." ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20437094>,
