    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3257809>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q372973>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q373058>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q429220>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q943> ;
    ns2:P571 "2024-02"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q131772>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q215605> ;
    ns1:description """This artwork was completed in February 2024, during the Year of the Wood Dragon, which began on February 10, 2024, and will end on January 28, 2025. \r
The Chinese Dragon (loong/long 龙) is a mythical creature, and the only fictional animal among the twelve zodiac signs. It is composed of nine animals: the body of a snake, the horns of a deer, the head of an ox, the mouth of a crocodile, the claws of an eagle, and the scales of a fish. In Chinese culture, the dragon holds a highly esteemed position, symbolizing auspiciousness and imperial power since ancient times.\r
When the element of Wood is combined with the Dragon, it brings qualities like growth, creativity, and flexibility into the Dragon's already strong nature.\r
In this piece, the graffiti artist Exas once again employs a distinctive technique, transforming the two characters of his Chinese tag Lingdan 灵丹, which means "elixir" or "magic pill," into animated "puppets." Through this zoomorphic transformation, the characters themselves become dynamic and lifelike, as if animated with the spirit of the dragon. Zoomorphism, the process by which letters or characters are given animal-like forms, plays a central role in this artwork. Exas' interpretation of the characters takes inspiration from the concept of letterform illustration, where the shapes of letters or symbols are designed to resemble living beings, whether human or animal. This method effectively bridges language with visual art, allowing the written word to acquire physicality and presence.\r
What stands out in this animation is the striking contrast between warm and cool tones. The dragon's body is depicted in vibrant red, while its flame-like tentacles are rendered in shades of sky blue, creating a dramatic interplay of colors. Interestingly, the dragon’s tail takes on the appearance of a frozen tree, enhancing the contrast between fire and ice.\r
The forms themselves are rounded yet sharp, embodying both fluidity and precision, much like the dragon’s claws and teeth. As in traditional calligraphic compositions, Exas masterfully juxtaposes Yin and Yang elements: heat and cold, water and fire, blending opposing forces through chromatic contrast and symbolic imagery. This tension between elements ‒ sharp and soft, warm and cold ‒ imbues the artwork with a deeper, dualistic narrative, symbolizing balance in contrast."""@en ;
