<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1586> a ns3:Q4502142 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q490> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Chinese Calligraphy Meets Shamanic Music - Artwork"@en ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/592> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q622868> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11472>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P571 "2018-03-28"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P941 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/729> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1586.jpg> ;
    ns1:derivesFrom <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/846> ;
    ns1:description """This artwork by Silvio Ferragina was created during the performance "Calligraphy Meets Shamanic Music". Centered around the voice of Tuvan shamanic singer Sainko Namtchylak, the performance becomes an immersive ritual, where sound and sign intertwine in a spiritual and meditative space. At the center of the room, a white vertical scroll becomes the stage for Ferragina’s gesture. To the hypnotic cadence of Sainko’s voice, the artist transcribes select verses from the Heart Sutra, a foundational text of Mahayana Buddhism, using a range of traditional Chinese calligraphic styles: seal script (zhuanshu), cursive script (caoshu), and regular script (kaishu). These lines are: "Form is not different from emptiness. Emptiness is not different from form. Form is emptiness. Emptiness is form". These verses have previously been explored by the artist in an earlier performance with Alessandro Cerino in Hangzhou (2016), and are here reactivated in a new sensory and emotional context. During the act of writing, Silvio Ferragina employs a unique variation of the feibai (flying white) technique, using a dry brush to trace the characters, which he then soaks in ink and lets drip spontaneously onto the paper. This fusion of brushwork and dripping recalls elements of action painting, blurring the boundary between writing and abstraction, control and chance. Each stroke responds intuitively to the timbre, rhythm, and emotional depth of the music. The result is a visual meditation in motion—one in which the artist’s movements and the sonic environment are inextricably linked. The calligraphy does not merely illustrate the sutra; it embodies it. The artwork becomes a channel through which the philosophical concept of emptiness is not only represented but experienced—both by the artist and the audience.\r
"""@en ;
