<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/162> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q59100>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Brush Strokes Series (毛笔抚摩系列)"@en,
        "毛笔抚摩系列"@zh,
        "Máobǐ fǔmó xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/160> ;
    ns2:P580 "2002"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2007"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/162.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "In this series, Wu Wei uses a big dried brush to \"caress\" human bodies, almost naked . Instead of traditional calligraphy, he doesn't use ink and paper to write characters. The artist makes a critique at the \"comforting pleasantness\" of the traditional Chinese art: the traditional culture is embodied by the brush, which produces a softening and conforting pleasantness on the skin. Through the ostentation of the body and the use of the brush as a \"tool of pleasure\", Wu Wei wants to provoke a state of shock in order to shake the viewer from the social and existential torpor in which he lives. According to Wu Wei, Chinese art is still strongly anchored to old styles and traditional tools and methods, so the artist is not free to express himself. The only way to break with the tradition is to overturn traditional tools and methods in a \"sensual\" provocative way."@en ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/162.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/167>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/169> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/163> a ns3:Q184485 ;
    ns2:P1132 2 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Brush Array · Woman's Body (毛笔布阵 · 女人体)"@en,
        "毛笔布阵 · 女人体"@zh,
        "Máobǐ bù zhèn · nǚ réntǐ"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P175 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/160> ;
    ns2:P179 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/164> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q154136>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q15978337>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3519050>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q47455763> ;
    ns2:P186 ns3:Q23852,
        ns3:Q614467 ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q59100> ;
    ns2:P585 "2002"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P941 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/925> ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/165> ;
    ns1:description "In this demonstrative performance, Wu Wei throws a lot of  brushes on a naked woman body.  Wu Wei deprives Chinese calligraphy of his traditional tools, methods and meanings: he doesn't uses paper and ink, the brush is no longer a calligraphic tool and the human body is only a deposit of the \"throwed brushes\". The artist makes a critique at the \"conforting pleasantness\" of the traditional Chinese art: the traditional culture is embodied by the brush, which produces a softing and conforting pleasantness on the skin. Through the ostentation of the body and the use of the brush as a \"tool of pleasure\", Wu Wei wants to provoke a state of shock in order to shake the viewer from the social and existential torpor in which he lives. According to Wu Wei, Chinese art is still strongly anchored to old styles and traditional tools and methods, so the artist is not free to express himself. The only way to break with the tradition is to overturn traditional tools and methods in a \"sensual/sexual\" provocative way."@en ;
    ns1:hasPerformingArtType <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q213156> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/164> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q59100> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Brush Array Series (毛笔布阵系列)"@en,
        "毛笔布阵系列"@zh,
        "Máobǐ bù zhèn xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/160> ;
    ns2:P580 "2002"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2003"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/164.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """This series is composed of two parts: "Brush Array · Woman's Body" (毛笔布阵 · 女人体, 2002) and "Brush Array · Man's Body" (笔布阵 · 男人体, 2003). The artist makes a critique at the "comforting pleasantness" of the traditional Chinese art: the traditional culture is embodied by the brush, which produces a softening and comforting pleasantness on the skin. Through the ostentation of the body and the use of the brush as a "tool of pleasure", Wu Wei wants to provoke a state of shock in order to shake the viewer from the social and existential torpor in which he lives. According to Wu Wei, Chinese art is still strongly anchored to old styles and traditional tools and methods, so the artist is not free to express himself. The only way to break with the tradition is to overturn traditional tools and methods in a "sensual/sexual" provocative way.\r
In addition to these parts, Wu Wei creates another work entitled "Encounter with the Brush on a Grassland" (毛笔交遇 · 草地, 2002). In this work, brushes are embedded in the ground: they are similar to stakes of an imaginary fence. \r
"""@en ;
