        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P571 "2023-04-02"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q15907859> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1667.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The artwork "The tags "Real" and "Restored to Ashes" (意实-恢烬)" by the artist Beer1ne, created on April 2, 2023, in Wuhan, China, is an evocative piece measuring approximately 5.4 meters by 1.8 meters. Utilizing latex paint on a wall and employing both aerosol paint and a roller brush, the artist ingeniously combines traditional and modern elements in this work.\r
At the forefront of the piece are the characters "Yishi 意實," rendered in the Qin Dynasty seal script style, and "Huijin 恢烬", depicted in traditional Chinese characters using an ignorant style. This striking juxtaposition serves to engage viewers not only visually but also conceptually, as it hints at a dialogue between history and contemporary artistic expression. The artwork is characterized by a bold initial outline in red, followed by a secondary white outline, culminating in a black fill, all of which contribute to its dynamic visual impact.\r
In one collaborative piece, the artist incorporated the tags "Real" and "Restored to Ashes" (意实-恢烬), a unique instance due to the scale of the piece and the collaboration requirement, resulting in an expansive conceptual meaning.\r
"Yishi 意实" emerged as a tag during the artist’s high school years, specifically during an intensive art training period in an art studio. Initially, his graffiti leaned toward wildstyle, yet after prolonged practice in this style, he encountered creative limitations. Seeking a fresh, non-traditional approach, he shifted to writing in Chinese. Although he previously used "pijiu 啤酒" (the translation of "beer") as a Chinese tag, he eventually created "yishi 意实" to represent a more authentic and meaningful identity. The origins of "Yishi 意实" trace back to this period of exploration, when the artist was interested in a stream-of-consciousness style. His formal art training included traditional Western figurative painting, which he embraced enthusiastically. Inspired by the idea of integrating Eastern and Western elements, he considered the relationship between "yi 意" (concept or idea) and "shi 实" (reality or substance). In traditional painting, Chinese styles emphasize the conceptual, while Western art often focuses on realism. This thematic duality of "idea and reality" in art—where ideas represent the soul, the ethereal, and the mysterious, while reality represents the physical, stable, and clear—was central to his creative direction. Naming his work "Yishi" symbolized his pursuit of a personal style that could establish his unique presence within the evolving timeline of his graffiti journey.\r
The other tag "Huijin 恢燼" conveys the idea of being "restored to ashes" or "returning to dust." This duality reflects Beer1ne's personal journey, as the tag 恢烬 emerged during a tumultuous emotional period when he resonated with the symbolism of "ash," representing both decay and the remnants of a once-vibrant existence.\r
Through this lens, the artwork becomes a poignant metaphor for desolation interwoven with the potential for renewal. Just as ash can settle into the earth or drift with the wind, Huijin embodies a cycle of rebirth, suggesting that from the remnants of what was, something new may arise. The piece captures a sense of melancholia while also expressing an openness to change, inviting viewers to reflect on their own experiences of loss and transformation.\r
In a sense, Beer1ne’s work represents an improvisational response to the materials at hand, leading to a creative exploration of identity and existence. The incorporation of both traditional calligraphy and contemporary graffiti styles not only highlights the artist’s versatility but also emphasizes the ongoing dialogue between past and present, inviting contemplation on the nature of meaning in a world constantly in flux."""@en ;
