<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/170> a ns3:Q184485 ;
    ns2:P1132 1 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Encounter with the Brush · Hand (毛笔交遇 · 手)"@en,
        "毛笔交遇 · 手"@zh,
        "Máobǐ jiāo yù · shǒu"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P175 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/160> ;
    ns2:P179 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/171> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q154136>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q15978337>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3519050>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q47455763>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8396> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q15174> ;
    ns2:P585 "2002"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/173> ;
    ns1:description "In this performance, Wu Wei  caresses a big brush and holds it tightly in his hands. The brush is no longer the tool for calligraphy, but it becames the passive subject of the performance. In this case the brush became a \"tool of pleasure\" for the artist himself, who practises a kind of auto-eroticism.  The artist makes a critique at the \"pleasantness\" of the traditional Chinese art, that it is seen as a auto-conforting practise: the traditional culture is embodied by the brush, which produces a softing and conforting pleasantness on the skin. Through the use of the brush as a \"tool of pleasure\", Wu Wei wants to provoke a state of shock in order to shake the viewer from the social and existential torpor in which he lives. According to Wu Wei, Chinese art is still strongly anchored to old styles and traditional tools and methods, so the artist is not free to express himself. The only way to break with the tradition is to overturn traditional tools and methods in a \"sexual\" provocative way."@en ;
    ns1:hasPerformingArtType <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q213156> ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool ns3:Q23852,
        ns3:Q614467 .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1706> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Status (状态)"@en,
        "状态"@zh,
        "Zhuàngtài"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1260> ;
    ns2:P580 "1994"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1706.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The series "Status” includes various subseries of abstract works, each with distinct traits and stylistic feature. Qiu Zhenzhong  already produced Status works as early as 1994, then over the years the artist  produced Status I, II, III, IV (2015),  V (2015), VII (2003).\r
The pieces of this series consists of structures of abstract lines / calligraphic brushstrokes incorporated into painting.\r
""" ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1706.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1707> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/171> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q15174> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Encounter with the Brush Series (毛笔交遇系列)"@en,
        "毛笔交遇系列"@zh,
        " Máobǐ jiāo yù xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/160> ;
    ns2:P580 "2002"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2003"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/171.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "In this series, Wu Wei uses the brush in various ways: for example, he holds it in his hands, touches a tongue, a foot, a buttock, a pubis, a breast of a statue or petals of a flower; the tip of the brush can also be placed on a toy car, on a flag or inside a condom. The artist makes a critique at the \"conforting pleasantness\" of the traditional Chinese art: the traditional culture is embodied by the brush.  The brush became a \"tool of pleasure\" for the the artist himself or for a passive subject/object. In the first case, the artist practises a kind of auto-eroticism, in the second case, the subject/object is  sexually stimulated. According to Wu Wei, Chinese art is still strongly anchored to old styles and traditional tools and methods, so the artist is not free to express himself. The only way to break with the tradition is to overturn traditional tools and methods in a \"sensual/sexual\" provocative way."@en ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/171.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/170>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/173> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1711> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Word Series  (语词系列)"@en,
        "语词系列"@zh,
        "Yǔcí xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1260> ;
    ns2:P580 "1988"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "1989"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1711.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "Word Series (1988–1989) presents a sequence of compound words beginning with the morpheme “triangle” (sanjiao 三角), written horizontally and drawn from a Chinese dictionary. These words are semantically unrelated, forming a list without any narrative or logical connection. Qiu Zhenzhong sees this humorous fragmentation as a way to reflect on contemporary linguistics, philosophy, Freudian theory, and the tension between order and absurdity in daily life. The repetition of “triangle” creates a distinctive visual rhythm and spatial pattern, inviting viewers to see language as both a visual and psychological experience." ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1711_HR.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1307> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1712> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Signatures Series (众生系列)"@en,
        "众生系列"@zh,
        "Zhòngshēng xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1260> ;
    ns2:P580 "1988"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "1989"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1712.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "Signatures Series uses personal and ritual elements, like repeated signatures in \"Diary\" ( Riji 日记) or chanting in \"Grandmother\" (祖母). The series blends calligraphy, memory, and abstraction to reflect human connection and tradition. In Diary, he composes daily signatures into a work that captures subtle shifts in mood through variations in brushstroke. In Grandmother , the repeated invocation \"Homage to Amitābha Buddha\"  (Namo Amituofo 南无阿弥陀佛) becomes a sonic and visual memory. The rhythm of her indistinct, continuous chanting is echoed in the fluid and restrained pulse of the brushwork. This suggests a deep, intuitive engagement with tradition — one that bypasses scholarly precision in favor of lived, embodied experience." ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1712_HR.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1309> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1713> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Characters to Be Deciphered Series (代考文字系列)"@en,
        "代考文字系列"@zh,
        "Dài kǎo wénzì xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1260> ;
    ns2:P580 "1988"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "1989"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1713_1.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1713_2.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1713_3.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "\"Characters to Be Deciphered Series\"  explores the visual potential of Chinese characters. Qiu Zhenzong uses ancient, undeciphered carved on pre-Qin dynasty (pre-221 B.C.) coins to write what appears as lines and strokes on paper. Unlike traditional calligraphy, where semantic meaning often interferes with the spatial and rhythmic qualities of the work, these indecipherable signs avoid semantic interference  liberating the form from content. The calligraphic abstraction derived from a deliberate selection of unknown glyphs and allows the artist full imaginative engagement with line, form, and spatial composition.  Thus, the series shifts focus from textual legibility to visual and spatial freedom." ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1713_1_HR.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1713_2_HR.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/1713_3_HR.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1311> .

