        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q12681> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4970> ;
    ns1:description "Lu Dadong, born in 1973 in Yantai, Shandong, is a Chinese artist and calligrapher. He began his artistic education in 1991 at the China Academy of Art, where he majored in calligraphy and seal engraving, later pursuing a master's degree in seal engraving at the same institution. He is a member of the China Calligraphers Association,  and associate professor at the International College of the China Academy of Art. His work merges traditional Chinese calligraphy with modern digital and multimedia techniques. Lu Dadong is also the lead vocalist of the Hangzhou-based band Yuren (与人乐队), having released two albums and performing occasionally." ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Academic"@en ;
    ns1:isCuratorOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1739> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1742> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/788> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Ta Yin Drought - Wang Nanming solo exhibition (拓印干旱——王南溟作品展)"@en,
        "拓印干旱——王南溟作品展"@zh,
        "Tàyìn gānhàn——Wáng Nánmíng zuòpǐn zhǎn"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1640 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2042> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q113431337>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8686> ;
    ns2:P580 "2012-05-25"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2012-06-28"^^xsd:date ;
    ns1:description """"Ta Yin Drought - Wang Nanming solo exhibition" was held at the 99°art center Creative Center (99创意中心) of the Institute of Art at Shanghai University (上海大学美术学院) from the 25th May to 28th June 2012. In this exhibition, Wang Nanming showcases his project of conceptual artworks created between the late 1990s and 2009. The exhibitions addresses to ecological issues in China, presenting "Rubbing Drought" and "Taihu Water" and a combination of mounted works, video, and images. The mounted works display the ink rubbings, while the video documents the artist’s process of imprinting on-site, alongside media reports and images related to the realization of "Rubbing drought".\r
This exhibition was part of interactive events and was one of the key projects of the 2012 Shanghai New Ink Art Exhibition. This last exhibition was of great significance for advancing the understanding of contemporary ink art, as well as for enhancing the collaboration and interaction between the 99°art center and other art institutions.""" ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1432>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2298>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2356> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1749> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1081> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Made by Tiande II (天德制造II)"@en,
        "天德制造II"@zh,
        "Tiāndé zhìzào II"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1640 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1093> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1384>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5069646> ;
    ns2:P580 "2007-03-01"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2007-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P856 <http://www.chambersfineart.com/ch/zhan-lan/tian-de-zai-zhi-zao/bu-fen-zuo-pin?view=slider> ;
    ns1:description "The exhibition \"Made by Tiande II\" is the sequel to \"Made by Tiande\" held at Chambers Fine Art in 2004. This  exhibition was curated by Zheng Shengtian 郑胜天 and held in 2007 at the Chambers Fine Art in New York. Another title for the exhibition is Tiande zai zhizao (天德再制造). For the Digital Series of 2004, Wang Tiande created works where inscriptions in running script were partially obscured by layers of Chinese paper, with freely executed characters burned into the surface. For this exhibition, the artist has extended this technique by creating a six-meter-long scroll depicting the landscape around Mount Gushan in Hangzhou, China. This is accompanied by a series of photographs, each capturing the same imagery through different methods. As Zheng Shengtian notes, when Tiande burns the paper, the \"ashes of words\" are left behind on the studio floor. He gathers these ashes into the shape of mountains, then uses photography and digital tools to create realistic landscapes. While this process may seem playful at first, it carries deep philosophical meaning: the ashes of traditional culture, transformed by the artist, are given new vitality." ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1381>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1743>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1744> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1750> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1081> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Made by Tiande (天德制造)"@en,
        "天德制造"@zh,
        "Tiāndé zhìzào"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1640 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1093> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1384>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5069646> ;
    ns2:P580 "2004-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2004-10-23"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P856 <http://www.chambersfineart.com/ch/zhan-lan/tian-de-zhi-zao> ;
    ns1:description "The exhibition \" Made by Tiande\" held at the Chambers Fine Art in 2004 showcases two series by Wang Tiande: the Digital Series of 2002 and the Chinese Robe Series of 2003. In the Digital Series, calligraphic inscriptions in running script are subtly revealed through layers of Chinese paper, the surface of which is marked by abstract forms burned into the material. This creates a sense of transparency and texture. In contrast, the Chinese Robe Series features traditional robes as canvases for calligraphic gestures. The robes, symbolizing China's rich cultural heritage, are used to convey barely legible messages. These cryptic inscriptions reflect the fading influence of traditional culture, particularly in the context of China's rapid technological advancement in the twenty-first century. The robes, once symbols of cultural identity, now bear these fragile messages, highlighting the tension between the past and the present in contemporary China." ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1083>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1743>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1744>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1751> .

