    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4742013> ;
    ns1:derivesFrom <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1959> ;
    ns1:description "This artwork was created during the TENG Ensemble's digital performance entitled: \"Heaven, Earth, Mankind and Unity (天地人合)\" realized on September 2, 2021 by the TENG company and recorded in the concert hall of the Singapore Chinese Culture Centre. The female calligrapher Grace Chen Liang 陈亮 was inspired by the composition played by the TENG Ensamble, who draws inspiration from the legendary Chinese martial Pipa solo, “Ambush from Ten Sides” (Shi mian mai fu 十面埋伏), reproducing the “Wu” 武 or martial style gestures, recalling the Battle of Gaixia in 202 BC during which General Xiang Yu 項羽 (c. 232–202 BC ) was defeated by Liu Bang 劉邦 (256 – 195 BC). The energetic and tenacious nature inspired Chen Liang to brush the words “Endlessness” (Shengsheng bu xi 生生不息). At first glance, “Ambush” can be interpreted as a state in war, but from a broader perspective, it can also be the representation of the never-ending cycle of collisions between the old and the new. The cycle of growth and development and reincarnation aligns with the philosophy of Laozi’s Dao De Jing: “Yisheng er, er sheng san, san sheng wanwu 一生二， 二生三， 三生万物” (One begets Two (Yin and Yang), Two begets Three (Heaven, Earth and Mankind; or Yin, Yang and Qi), Three begets all things). It is with this ideology that the words “Sheng sheng bu xi 生生不息” (Endlessness) were befitting for this final movement."@en ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q22669850> ;
