<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1979> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Song of the Great Wind (大风歌)"@en,
        "大风歌"@zh,
        "Dàfēng gē"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1480 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5727902> ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7210> ;
    ns2:P571 "0206"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """"Song of the Great Wind" was composed by Liu Bang (256–195 BCE), the founder of the Han Dynasty (206 BCE–220 CE). The poem was likely composed around 206 BCE, after Liu Bang had emerged victorious in the Chu-Han Contention (206–202 BCE), a struggle for control of China against Xiang Yu, a powerful warlord. Liu Bang ultimately defeated Xiang Yu and established the Han Dynasty, after which he adopted the title Emperor Gaozu of Han.\r
The imagery of the "mighty wind" symbolizes Liu Bang's unstoppable force in overcoming his adversaries, and it represents the broader historical narrative of how he overcame obstacles to create a lasting and powerful empire.""" ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """大风起兮云飞扬,\r
威加海内兮归故乡,\r
安得猛士兮守四方。""",
        """The mighty wind blows, scattering the flying clouds,\r
After meriting the unification of the world, (I) returned home.\r
(Will) fierce warriors guard the four corners."""@en,
        """Dàfēng qǐ xī yún fēiyáng,\r
Wēi jiā hǎinèi xī guī gùxiāng,\r
Ān dé měngshì xī shǒu sìfāng."""@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1980> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Spring Blossoms On The Moonlit River (春江花月夜)"@en,
        "春江花月夜"@zh,
        "Chūnjiāng huā yuèyè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1480 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5727902> ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6804586> ;
    ns2:P571 "0700"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "\"Moonlit Night on the Spring River (春江花月夜)\" is a poem written by the Tang Dynasty poet Zhang Ruoxu 张若虚 and it is likely dated around the mid-Tang period, around the 8th century. The poem is a masterpiece of descriptive poetry that uses the imagery of the river and the moon to express universal emotions of solitude, nostalgia, and the passage of time. Zhang Ruoxu evokes vivid images of nature, particularly the scene of a bright moon illuminating a river in spring, creating an atmosphere of calm and reflection." ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """春江潮水连海平，                       \r
海上明月共潮生。                       \r
滟滟随波千万里，                       \r
何处春江无月明!                         \r
\r
江流宛转绕芳甸，                      \r
月照花林皆似霰;                         \r
空里流霜不觉飞，                       \r
汀上白沙看不见。                       \r
\r
江天一色无纤尘，                       \r
皎皎空中孤月轮。                       \r
江畔何人初见月？                       \r
江月何年初照人？                       \r
\r
人生代代无穷已，                       \r
江月年年望相似。                       \r
不知江月待何人，                       \r
但见长江送流水。                      \r
\r
白云一片去悠悠，                      \r
青枫浦上不胜愁。                       \r
谁家今夜扁舟子？                       \r
何处相思明月楼？                      \r
\r
可怜楼上月徘徊，                      \r
应照离人妆镜台。                      \r
玉户帘中卷不去，                       \r
捣衣砧上拂还来。\r
\r
此时相望不相闻，                      \r
愿逐月华流照君。                       \r
鸿雁长飞光不度，                     \r
鱼龙潜跃水成文。                       \r
\r
昨夜闲潭梦落花，                       \r
可怜春半不还家。                      \r
江水流春去欲尽，                       \r
江潭落月复西斜。                       \r
\r
斜月沉沉藏海雾，                       \r
碣石潇湘无限路。                       \r
不知乘月几人归，                       \r
落月摇情满江树。                       """,
        """In this moment, both gazes at the moon, but unable they are to hear each other,\r
Only wishing the brilliance of the moon is shining upon the dear husband.\r
Swans and geese flies long distances but cannot fly beyond the light,\r
Fishes and [marine] reptiles dives and jumps but only forms ripples in the water.\r
\r
Dreaming of flowers descending upon the tranquil pool last night,\r
Pitiful it is, half of spring has past yet there is still no return.\r
The running river water flowing by, along with spring,\r
Into the river pool, the descending moon goes, tilting west again.\r
\r
The setting moon hiding deeply into the ocean mist,\r
Jie Shi and Xiao Xiang, the distance between is infinite.\r
It is unknown how many people managed to return home riding the moonlight,\r
Only knowing the descending moon stirs immense emotions amongst the trees by the riverbank."""@en,
        """Chūn jiāng cháo shuǐ lián hǎi píng,\r
Hǎi shàng míng yuè gòng cháo shēng.\r
Yàn yàn suí bō qiān wàn lǐ,\r
Hé chù chūn jiāng wú yuè míng!\r
\r
Jiāng liú wǎn zhuǎn rào fāng diān,\r
Yuè zhào huā lín jiē shì sǎn;\r
Kōng lǐ liú shuāng bù jué fēi,\r
Tīng shàng bái shā kàn bù jiàn.\r
\r
Jiāng tiān yī sè wú xiān chén,\r
Jiǎo jiǎo kōng zhōng gū yuè lún.\r
Jiāng pàn hé rén chū jiàn yuè?\r
Jiāng yuè hé nián chū zhào rén?\r
\r
Rén shēng dài dài wú qióng yǐ,\r
Jiāng yuè nián nián wàng xiāng sì.\r
Bù zhī jiāng yuè dài hé rén,\r
Dàn jiàn cháng jiāng sòng liú shuǐ.\r
\r
Bái yún yī piàn qù yōu yōu,\r
Qīng fēng pǔ shàng bù shèng chóu.\r
Shuí jiā jīn yè piān zhōu zi?\r
Hé chù xiāng sī míng yuè lóu?\r
\r
Kě lián lóu shàng yuè pái huái,\r
Yīng zhào lí rén zhuāng jìng tái.\r
Yù hù lián zhōng juǎn bù qù,\r
Dǎo yī zhēn shàng fú hái lái.\r
\r
Cǐ shí xiāng wàng bù xiāng wén,\r
Yuàn zhú yuè huá liú zhào jūn.\r
Hóng yàn zhǎng fēi guāng bù dù,\r
Yú lóng qián yuè shuǐ chéng wén.\r
\r
Zuó yè xián tán mèng luò huā,\r
Kě lián chūn bàn bù huán jiā.\r
Jiāng shuǐ liú chūn qù yù jǐn,\r
Jiāng tán luò yuè fù xī xié.\r
\r
Xié yuè chén chén cáng hǎi wù,\r
Jié shí xiāo xiāng wú xiàn lù.\r
Bù zhī chéng yuè jǐ rén guī,\r
Luò yuè yáo qíng mǎn jiāng shù."""@zh-Latn .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1991> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "XL (一比特)"@en,
        "一比特"@zh,
        "Yī bǐtè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1131> ;
    ns2:P580 "2009"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2009"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """In 2009, Feng Mengbo explored the possibilities of traditional Chinese painting and calligraphy through new media in works such as “XL” (Yi bite 一比特) and “Not Too Late” (Bu tai wan 不太晚). The “XL” series consists of 25 wood panels, each covered with a silkscreen print on a layer of silver leaf, onto which calligraphic and/or pictorial elements are digitally transformed into pixel-like forms. The content of the panels includes model calligraphies by Tang dynasty masters such as Zhang Xu 张旭 (685-759) and Huaisu 怀素 (737-799), as well as seals and images of ancient sages and monks depicted in scenes of philosophical reflection or spiritual discourse. \r
The panels of this series come in three sizes, small (50 x 50 cm), medium (120 x 120 cm), and large (200 x 180 cm) and reflect the artist’s engagement with digital aesthetics through the visual language of 1-bit pixel art. Adopting the working methods of classical calligraphers and painters, the artist develops a visual syntax reduced to essentials, in which artistic expression is translated into  digital units. Despite this extreme reduction, the aesthetic beauty and form of the characters remain nearly intact, reflecting the sensibilities of the contemporary digital age.  The series highlights the evolution from traditional brush techniques to modern digital codes and systems and becomes a meditation on continuity and transformation of visual culture in the digital age.""" ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1425> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1994> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Drinking on West Lake First Shine After the Rain (饮湖上初晴后雨) "@en,
        "饮湖上初晴后雨"@zh,
        "Yǐn húshàng chū qíng hòu yǔ"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1480 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5727902> ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q36020> ;
    ns2:P571 "1071"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """"Drinking on the Lake on a Clear Day Followed by Rain" (饮湖上初晴后雨) by Su Shi 苏轼, is a reflection on the beauty of nature and the transient, yet harmonious, relationship between different states of the environment. It captures the changes of weather on West Lake (Xi Hu) in Hangzhou, which Su Shi famously enjoyed during his time as a deputy governor.\r
The poem is divided into two parts: \r
The first part of the poem conveys a moment of serenity, where the clear sky and the lake's sparkling surface create a stunning and tranquil scene. Su Shi then compares this with the rain that follows, which brings a unique and mystical atmosphere to the landscape. In his view, the misty rain enhances the beauty of the mountain and lake, making it a sight that is equally as remarkable as the clarity before.\r
In the second part, Su Shi compares the lake's beauty to that of a famous ancient beauty, Xi Shi 西施 whom he refers to as Lady Shi 西子. This allusion to Xi Shi, celebrated for her beauty in Chinese history, emphasizes how both the lake and the woman are lovely in their natural, unadorned state, as well as in their more dramatic, heightened forms, just as the lake can look stunning in both clear and rainy weather.""" ;
    ns1:transcriptionAndTranslation """Early morning sunlight welcomes guests, \r
daubs the tiered hills,\r
evening rain invites them to stay, \r
sends them to a domain of drunkenness.\r
Gentleman, do you not understand this fine intention?\r
Let’s have a toast to the King of the Water Immortals.\r
\r
When it is clear, the ripples gleam on this beautiful lake,\r
the colours of the mountain drizzled in mist are also wonderful.\r
Should I compare Western Lake with Lady Shi?\r
Lightly made up or heavily rouged, it is always fitting for her.     """@en,
        """朝曦迎客艳重冈，晚雨留人入醉乡。\r
此意自佳君不会，一杯当属水仙王。\r
\r
水光潋滟晴方好，山色空蒙雨亦奇。\r
欲把西湖比西子，淡妆浓抹总相宜。"""@zh,
        """Cháo xī yíng kè yàn zhòng gāng, wǎn yǔ liú rén rù zuìxiāng. \r
Cǐ yì zì jiā jūn bù huì, yībēi dāng shǔ shuǐxiān wáng. \r
\r
Shuǐ guāng liàn yàn qíng fāng hǎo, shān sè kōng méng yǔ yì qí. \r
Yù bǎ xīhú bǐ xīzǐ, dànzhuāng nóng mǒ zǒng xiāngyí.\r
"""@zh-Latn .

