    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q64>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns1:description "Qin Feng, born in Xinjiang in 1961, is a contemporary Chinese artist known for his innovative approach to ink painting. He graduated from Shandong University of Art and Design in 1985 and currently lives and works in Beijing. Qin Feng is also the founder of the Museum of Contemporary Art Beijing. Fusing traditional Chinese calligraphy with Western abstract expressionism, his work explores themes of cultural fusion, identity, and history. His art has been exhibited globally, including solo shows at the British Museum (London), the Metropolitan Museum of Art (New York), and Palazzo Reale (Milan). In the 1990s, Qin moved to Berlin after being invited by the German government to curate an exhibition promoting cultural exchange between China and Germany. There, he began blending Western modernism with the Chinese ink tradition in abstract compositions. While he uses traditional calligraphic materials, his brushwork carries the unrestrained energy of Abstract Expressionism." .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2005> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Civilization Landscape Series (文明景观)"@en,
        "文明景观"@zh,
        "Wénmíng jǐngguān"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2004> ;
    ns2:P580 "2004"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2015"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "Initiated in the early 2000s, Civilization Landscape Series explores the encounter between cultural traditions, historical memory, and contemporary global transformation through the language of abstraction and gestural ink painting. Inspired by his upbringing in Xinjiang, a multicultural crossroads along the Silk Roads, Qin Feng incorporates visual references to Chinese, Uighur, Arabic, and Russian scripts, evoking the coexistence and exchange of diverse cultural identities. Throughout the series, expressive brushwork and monumental compositions reinterpret the tradition of Chinese landscape painting while moving beyond direct representation. In several works, the artist further emphasizes the dialogue between East and West by using tea, symbolizing Asia, and coffee, symbolizing the Western world, as pigments or background washes, transforming everyday materials into carriers of cultural memory and exchange." ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2006>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2117> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2012> a ns3:Q4502142 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "The Character of Characters (汉字的性格)"@en,
        "汉字的性格"@zh,
        "Hànzì de xìnggé"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1096> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11425>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q34516>,
