        "天问"@zh,
        "Tiānwèn"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1948> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q31179856>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q468777> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q104614>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q287> ;
    ns2:P2048 "4 m" ;
    ns2:P2049 "6 m" ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P571 "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5694896> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2057.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "\"Inquiry of Heaven\" is an installation calligraphic artwork realized  by Huang Yeda in 2023. It stands as a profound meditation on the relationship between writing and the body, memory, and monumental presence. The installation is striking in its scale, measuring 4 meters in height and approximately 6 meters in width, comprising 70 wooden panels (each 8.5 cm thick) on which over 1,560 characters are inscribed with ink and cinnabar. These materials evoke the ancient form of bamboo slips (zhujian 竹简), one of the earliest writing mediums in Chinese history. Huang Yeda transforms this traditional format into an architectural and immersive dimension, where the text is not merely read but experienced as a spatial event. By enlarging the original dimensions of the bamboo slips, he amplifies the visual and symbolic impact of the work. The color scheme of black and red, inspired by the aesthetics of the Chu culture, evokes the mystique of ritual inscriptions, with cinnabar imparting a sacred quality to the piece. The text presented on the wooden surface is the famous poem \"Questions to Heaven\" (Tianwen 天问) by Qu Yuan (屈原), a timeless work that poses cosmological and metaphysical questions about the fate of humanity and the order of the universe. In this context, the writing becomes a threshold between past and present, a bridge linking the poet's ancestral inquiries to their contemporary resonance. Huang Yeda does not simply reproduce an ancient text but stretches and amplifies it, transforming it into a sensory and conceptual experience. " ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20437094>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q22669850> ;
    ns1:hasColophon false ;
    ns1:hasSeal false ;
    ns1:hasUnit <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2058> ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/2057_HR.jpg> ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q614467> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2059> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Questions to Heaven (《天问》)" ;
    ns2:P1480 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5727902> ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q313330> ;
    ns2:P571 "-0290"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "Questions to Heaven (Tianwen 天問, “Heavenly Questions”) is a long poem traditionally attributed to Qu Yuan 屈原 (c. 340-278 BCE), one of the most influential poets of the Warring States period and a central figure of the Chu ci (Songs of Chu) tradition. Probably composed during the fourth or third century BCE, the poem consists of approximately 172 questions arranged in a continuous sequence without explicit answers. Rather than presenting a coherent narrative, Tianwen unfolds as a series of inquiries addressing cosmology, creation myths, celestial phenomena, legendary rulers, divine beings, and historical events. Through its interrogative structure, the poem explores contradictions and uncertainties within ancient Chinese mythology and religious thought, making it one of the earliest and most important textual sources for the study of early Chinese cosmological and mythological traditions." ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5694896> .

