    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2099.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """This artwork is part of the series "Material Immaterial - Tree Whisper"  (2010-2019). During this project, Chu Chu collected various branches from different trees in the botanical garden near her studio in Hangzhou. She  captured large-format black-and-white photographs, on which she realized calligraphic inscriptions with gold, silver, white, and black ink. The artist used small characters that are arranged in order to shape the shadow of branches from a distance.  Characters are written in cursive style, and feature excerpts from  the Chinese literary tradition, such as the poems of Qu Yuan  (屈原, 339 B.C. - 278 A.D.), the Shijing (诗经, Classic of Poetry) and the “West Lake in My Dreams” (Xihu Mengxun 西湖梦寻) by Zhang Dai 张岱 (1597-1684). \r
In this work, Chu Chu portrays the branch of a tea tree. Upon printing its photograph in black and white, she wrote a poem from the Classic of Poetry, entitled "Peach Tree Soft and Tender" (Tao yao 桃夭), as to reproduce the shadow of the branches giving a three-dimensional effect to the image. """ ;
