<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2178> a ns1:TraditionalArtwork ;
    ns2:P1476 "Four Ancient Poems Calligraphy"@en,
        "古诗四帖"@zh,
        "Gǔshī sì tiē"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q717343> ;
    ns2:P571 "0750"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """"Four Ancient Poems Calligraphy” is a calligraphic work by Zhang Xu 张旭 (685-759). Along  with Huaisu 怀素 (737-799), he is one of the two greatest Tang dynasty calligraphers of wild cursive (kuangcao 狂草), the most bold and expressive calligraphic style. The two are often nicknamed "the crazy Zhang and the drunk Su" (dian Zhang zui Su 颠张醉素), reflecting their unrestrained artistic spirit.\r
In this calligraphic scrool of different colors, measuring 29.5 x 195 cm, Zhang Xu transcribes with black ink four classical poems of the Southern and Northern Dynasties (420-589): “Daoist Chant – Poem No. 6" (Daoshi buxu ci - qi liu 道士步虚词·其六) and "Daoist Chant – Poem No. 8" (Daoshi buxu ci - qi ba 道士步虚词·其八) by Yu Xin 庾信 (513-581); "Ode to Prince Jin" (Wangzi Jin zan 王子晋赞) and "Seeing an Old Man and a Few Youths under a Cliff" (Yan xia jian yi lao weng si wu shao nian zan 岩下见一老翁四五少年赞) by Xie Lingyun 谢灵运 (385—433). The four poems share several Daoist themes, evoking mystical imagery and philosophical reflections on nature, immortality, and transcendence. Zhang Xu's calligraphy in wild cursive style is characterized by sponaneous strokes and dynamic rhythm. Scholars generally attribute the piece to Zhang Xu's mature period (740-755). This piece is housed in the Liaoning Provincial Museum (Shenyang, China).\r
""" ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q22669850> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2182> a ns1:DecorativeAndAppliedArt ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Mengbo 2012: Feng’s Bitmap + Feng’s Vector (梦波2012: 点阵冯 + 矢量冯)"@en,
        "梦波2012: 点阵冯 + 矢量冯"@zh,
        "Mèngbō 2012: Diǎnzhènféng + Shǐliàngféng"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q14255687>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q355198>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4916901>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6776685> ;
    ns2:P287 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1131> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P571 "2011"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P608 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2183> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2182.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """"Mengbo 2012" consists of two sets of typeface created by Feng Mengbo in 2011, titled "Feng's Bitmap" (dianzhenfeng 点阵冯) and "Feng's Vector"(shiliangfeng 矢量冯). This is an aesthetic experiment of using software to set typefaces, Feng Mengbo created his font aided by a software program able togenerate a font bank after the modelling of a few characters that Feng made on the computer. Adopting digital tools to accelerate the aging process of characters of stone rubbings, the artist reduces the fonts to their most essential forms, placing them on the edge between readability and unreadability.  The reduction of characters is based on 'stone flowers' (shihua 石花). These 'stone flowers' are rubbings made from eroded or damaged steles that show white marks where the stones or inscriptions had been before, often rendering the characters illegible. Feng Mengbo chose to  revive the stone flowers to preserve them in an artistic digital remediation. \r
In "Feng's Bitmap" (dianzhenfeng 点阵冯) the font is a pixel-based reduction of characters, while in "Feng's Vector" (shiliangfeng 矢量冯) are reduced to gentle lines. This is why bitmap images are based on pixels, while vector images are based on mathematical formulas that represent lines and shapes. \r
"Feng's Bitmap" (dianzhenfeng 点阵冯) and "Feng's Vector"(shiliangfeng 矢量冯) have been released by Founder Electronics Co., Ltd. at the opening ceremony of the exhibition "Mengbo 2012" and they are free for personal use.\r
\r
 """ ;
    ns1:hasColophon false ;
    ns1:hasProductType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q17451> ;
    ns1:hasSeal false ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/2182.jpg> ;
    ns1:vectorisedOrDigitised true .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2185> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q14255687>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q162668>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q355198>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4916901>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6776685> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/regular_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2185.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 1 Eye Charts - Feng's Bitmap (signs)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU consists of the typefaces created by Feng Mengbo in the two panels of "Eye Charts" (2011). Each panel includes 90 characters varying gradually in sizes in the shape of optometric charts. The artist uses two newly-designed computer fonts: in the first panel the "Feng's Bitmap" (Dianzhenfeng 点阵冯) and in the second one the "Feng's Vector" (Shiliangfeng 矢量冯). In the first one the characters are pixel-based reduced, while in the second one they are reduced to gentle lines based on vectors and mathematical formulas. The reduction of characters is based on 'stone flowers' (shihua 石花). These 'stone flowers' are rubbings made from eroded or damaged steles that show white marks where the stones or inscriptions had been before, often rendering the characters illegible. Feng Mengbo chose to revive the stone flowers to preserve them in an artistic digital remediation. Adopting digital tools to accelerate the aging process of characters of stone rubbings, the artist reduces the fonts to their most essential forms, placing them on the edge between readability and unreadability. In a deliberate paradox, the larger the characters appear, the less legible they become; the smaller they are, the clearer they read. \r
""" ;
