<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2188> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1096> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Three installations by Xu Bing"@en ;
    ns2:P1640 <https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chazen_Museum_of_Art>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q119791940> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3666871>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q43788>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q838330> ;
    ns2:P580 "1991-11-30"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "1992-01-19"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P856 <https://search.library.wisc.edu/digital/AQMUF7KG3U3UQG8Q>,
        <https://www.xubing.com/en/exhibition/details/373?year=1991#373> ;
    ns1:description """The "Three installations by Xu Bing" exhibition was held at Elvehjem Museum of Art, now Chazen Museum of Art, located at the University of Wisconsin-Madison from 30 November 1991 to 19 January 1992. The exhibition included three installations by Xu Bing: "Five Series of Repetitions" (1986-87), "Book from the Sky" (1987-1991), and "Ghosts Pounding the Wall" (1990-91).\r
The "Book from the Sky" installation was firstly  exhibited in October 1988 at the National Art Museum of China (Namoc) in Beijing (Abe 1998, 174; Erickson 2001, 37). However, Xu Bing, dissatisfied with the initial presentation, later revised the installation and exhibited it abroad for the first time at Elvehjem Museum of Art in a modified and definitive form. The installation has since been exhibited in several museums and galleries around the world up to today.""" ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1478> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2209> a ns3:Q215627 ;
    ns2:P103 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P106 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q483501> ;
    ns2:P19 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q58324> ;
    ns2:P21 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6581097> ;
    ns2:P2561 "Wu Shanzhuan 吴山专" ;
    ns2:P27 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P569 "1960-10-25"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P800 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2211> ;
    ns2:P812 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q271588> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1055> ;
    ns1:description "Wu Shanzhuan (b. 1960) is a Chinese contemporary artist based in Hamburg, Germany. He is known for his conceptual and installation art that merges language, culture, and political critique. He studied printmaking at the Zhejiang Academy of Fine Arts (now China Academy of Art) and became active in China's avant-garde art scene in the late 1980s. Wu Shanzhuan is best known for his large-scale text-based works, where he appropriates the visual forms of traditional Chinese calligraphy but subverts their content by inserting Maoist slogans, commercial advertisements, or absurdist phrases. His art challenges authority and cultural identity, creating a tension between the formal beauty of calligraphy and the critical messages embedded within it. By transforming calligraphic traditions into platforms for social commentary, Wu Shanzhuan turns writing into a powerful tool for conceptual exploration and cultural reflection." ;
    ns1:hasWikidataResource <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q113147306> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/221> a ns3:Q184485 ;
    ns2:P1132 1 ;
    ns2:P1476 "1/2 "@en ;
    ns2:P175 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/86> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11042>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q170494>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q18511800>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2937981>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q315>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q37260>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q450>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q82821>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q844569> ;
    ns2:P186 ns3:Q127418,
        ns3:Q23852 ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P585 "1998"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/224> ;
    ns1:description """The performance is composed of three different moments. In the first part of the performance ("1/2 Meat"), Zhang Huan is naked and he 'wears' a carcass of raw bovine meat, reminiscent of the armors of ancient Chinese warriors. In the second part of the performance ("1/2 Text"), Zhang Huan invites friends to write phrases or words on his face and body with black ink, so that Zhang Huan's ethnicity is literally inscribed on his body. In the last part of the performance ("1/2 Meat + Text"), Zhang Huan wears again the carcass of the raw bovine meat, but this time his  body is completely covered by written characters. \r
The performance conveys different meanings and interpretations, based on three principal aspects of human life: individual, society and culture. \r
Zhang Huan expresses the contradiction of human nature: on one hand the civil and rational side and on the other hand the primitive and animalistic side. Consequently, Zhang Huan's body becomes a medium that carries and expresses this dualistic concept. According to the Chinese philosophical tradition, body and mind are blended together and they represent a single vital unit. As Chinese characters are a product of the human mind, the artist's body serves as a support for the text. Writing is fused with the body so that mind and body are blended together. The bloody carcass of the animal represents the scarnification of the body, which is "overlapped" to the text. It is an element of division between the human body and the text, so it breaks the fusion between mind and body. The title "1/2" expresses that in contemporary times the traditional union/harmony between body and mind has been divided into two separate entities .\r
The carcass of the animal also represents the society, which is stripped from the "chaotic flesh and blood" of culture, customs and traditions. Society is deprived of its "vital pulp", that is culture, showing how the human mind is losing its value in contemporary society. \r
Finally, this performance is a visual metaphor for the difficult transcultural experience upon which Zhang was about to embark. As an Asian body circulating within a Western culture, Zhang tries to make himself understood through his native language. He said: "The body is the only direct way through which I come to know society and society comes to know me. The body is the proof of identity. The body is language.\""""@en ;
    ns1:hasPerformingArtType <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q213156> ;
    ns1:hasUnit <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/226> ;
    ns1:madeUsingTool ns3:Q614467 .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2211> a ns3:Q4502142 ;
