<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2342> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4970> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Ode of Eight Thousand Verses (八千颂歌) "@en,
        "Ode degli ottomila versi "@it,
        " 八千颂歌 "@zh,
        " Bāqiān sònggē "@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/84> ;
    ns2:P580 "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2342.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """In "Ode of Eight Thousand Verses (八千颂歌)" series, Luo Qi combines two different writing systems shaped in calligraphic form: the Chinese characters and the Sanskrit alphabet. The graphs in black are reworkings of Sanskrit letters, while the graphs in white outlined in black are reworkings of Chinese characters. The Sanskrit parts are extracts from the Sutra of the Perfection of Wisdom in 8,000 Lines, also called Transcendental Knowledge Sutra (Prajñāpāramitā Sutra), which is a collection of Buddhist texts fundamental for the creation of Mahāyāna Buddhism. There are four Chinese characters: Fo 佛 (Buddha), Luo 洛 (the artist's surname), Shou 寿 (Longevity), a character linked to esoteric calligraphy of Taoist inspiration, and Fu 福 (Good Fortune), an auspicious symbol particularly important in China and for Luo Qi, and a reference to the word “Gospel,” in Chinese "Fuyinjing 福音经". These four characters link together Buddhism, Taoism, and Christianity, popular and elite art, phonetic sounds, puns and religious litanies.\r
The genesis of this series dates back  to 2006-2007, when Luo Qi created the first artworks inspired by the Sutra of the Perfection of Wisdom in 8,000 Lines. In 2016, he decided to mix Sanskrit letters with Chinese auspicious characters in ink on paper artworks. In 2017, he then created 30 folding books in black ink on paper hung as vertical scrolls entitled "Fu Luo shou 福洛寿" (Fortune – Luo – Longevity) that respresented the first nucleus of this series. In 2019,  in occasion of the exhibition "Luo Qi: Writing Music“ (Bologna, 25/03-08/04), they were renamed "Fu – Baqian songge 福· 八千颂歌” (Fortune – Ode of Eight Thousand Verses). In 2023, the name has been further simplified to "Baqian songge 八千颂歌” (Ode of Eight Thousand Verses), and part of them were displayed in the exhibition "Sounds of Ink: Luo Qi and 30 Years of Calligraphyism" (Bologna,  12/10-05/11).\r
The artist's attempt in this series is to use different linguistic signs that, juxtaposed, reiterated, mixed and outlined through expert calligraphic brushstrokes, create a visual rhythm that visually reproduces the musical rhythm of Buddhist litanies arisen from reading the transcribed sutras. The linguistic content of the work is so no longer important, but is only the means, or rather the material through which this “score” unfolds and addresses the eyes and ears of the spectators. The raw material of these works are therefore letters and characters, the means to transcribe them is calligraphy, but the result is a linguistically illegible and, at the same time, sonically perceptible plot. These works can be described as a form of "musical calligraphy", a form that we could define as "universal", since it simultaneously accords Buddhist, Daoist and Christian echoes, embracing Eastern and Western traditions. They are works made of signs that go beyond borders and unite worlds through their "visual sound". They are works that exemplify the quintessence of Luo Qi's "calligraphyism", an artistic form that uses writing, or rather writings, and calligraphic art to create new visual codes that are universally understandable.""" ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/2342_HR.jpg> ;
