<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/246> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4970>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q60> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Mythos of Lost Dynasties Series (遗失的王朝系列)"@en,
        "遗失的王朝系列"@zh,
        "Yíshī de wángcháo xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/231> ;
    ns2:P580 "1983"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "1998"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/246.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """In this series,  Gu Wenda invents a new form of writing, based on the ancient seal-script characters, but these new characters are meaningless. Exploring the loss of meaning of writing, Gu Wenda reduces writing to a mere sign.\r
Among the works from this series, one is "Synthesized Words" (Chang shen 畅神). The work is exposed in 1985 during a group exhibition entitled "Group Exhibition of the New Traditional Painting of Hubei Province" (Hubei guohua xinzuo yaoqing zhan 湖北国华新作邀请展) held at the Wushang Exhibition Center in the city of Wushang. On that occasion, the work was presented as part of this series entitled "Mythos of Lost Dynasties - Tranquility Comes from Meditation" (Yishi de wangchao  - Jingguan de shijie 遗失的王朝 - 静观的世界 ) composed of five oversize hanging scrolls. These five artworks then have become part of the series entitled "Pseudo-characters Series" (Xugou wenzi xilie 虚构文字系列)."""@en ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/246.jpg> ;
    ns1:includesArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/240> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2472> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q41670> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1860> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2472.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 2 Beijing 2008 Paralympic Games Logo (Beijing 2008)" ;
    ns1:description """This CWU consists of the inscription “Beijing 2008,” which forms part of the logo created for the Beijing 2008 Paralympic Games. It is composed of Latin letters and Arabic numerals, yet the typeface emulates the expressive qualities of Chinese calligraphic brushstrokes, integrating different writing traditions and cultures into a unified visual form. The use of black lettering on a white background further recalls the traditional aesthetics of Chinese calligraphy, specifically the contrast of black ink on white paper.\r
""" ;
