<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2477> a ns1:DecorativeAndAppliedArt ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8686> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Shanghai 2010 Expo Logo"@en ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q12767209>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q177634>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q380962> ;
    ns2:P287 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2478> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3133>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q943> ;
    ns2:P571 "2010"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P88 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q192452> ;
    ns1:description """The logo of the 2010 Expo, held in Shanghai under the theme “Better City, Better Life” (Chengshi, rang shenghuo geng meihao 城市，让生活更美好), emphasized improving the quality of life through urban development and is remembered as the most widely attended World Expo to date. The emblem, designed by Shao Honggeng 邵宏庚, is inspired by the Chinese character shi 世, meaning “world,” and is shaped to depict three figures—“I, you, and he/she”—holding hands, symbolizing the unity of humanity. The logo conveys the values of understanding, communication, and cooperation, central to the ethos of the Expo.\r
In addition to the character shi 世, the logo incorporates the number “2010”, attached to the first element as if it were a natural continuation. The lines are sinuous and flowing, recalling Chinese calligraphy and exemplifying a balance between globalized design and local cultural elements. The visual composition is based on the pictographic form of the character shi 世, represented as three stylized entities arranged in a cross and connected by a continuous line.\r
According to the "Shuowen jiezi 说文解字", the earliest surviving Chinese dictionary completed by Xu Shen 许慎 in 100 CE, the original form of the character (㔺) is a modification of the character sa 卅 (thirty), initially composed of the character shi 十 repeated three times, with the lower ends of the two outer vertical strokes converging to form a tree-like branching structure. The character shi 世 also signifies “generation”, reflecting the idea that every thirty years one generation produces the next, just as each branch of the character seems to emanate a successive one.\r
The green S-shaped stroke that terminates the image, replicating the final part of the character in its pictographic form, is transformed into the number “2” in “2010” within the logo. These strokes are executed in a brush-like style, highly evocative of calligraphy, further emphasizing the integration of traditional Chinese writing with modern graphic design.""" ;
