<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2496> a ns1:DecorativeAndAppliedArt ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "CAFA ART INFO Logo"@en ;
    ns2:P179 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1277> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1094542>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1666147> ;
    ns2:P287 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1096> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P856 <https://www.cafa.com.cn/en> ;
    ns2:P88 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q837873> ;
    ns2:P941 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1277> ;
    ns1:description """This logo belongs to the artistic organization CAFA ART INFO. This organization is  closely related to the Central Academy of Fine Arts (CAFA) in Beijing,  and aims to promote domestic and international artistic exchange, enhance the influence of Chinese contemporary art, and provide comprehensive information on art education, exhibitions, and research. Based on its activities and mission, it serves as an information platform and digital, academic, and practical extension of the CAFA. \r
In the logo, the phrase CAFA ART INFO is written using the “New English Calligraphy” (Xin Yingwen shufa 新英文书法) or “Square Word Calligraphy” (Yingwen fangkuaizi shufa 英文方块字书法) by Xu Bing. Each of the three words forming the phrase is arranged within an imaginary square, resembling Chinese characters. The letters are constructed as if they were brushstrokes of a character, following an order and method devised by Xu Bing according to precise rules. The strokes composing the letters evoke the ink brushwork of the original artwork, which was subsequently refined and vectorized into a stylized, contemporary logo. This approach produces a hybrid visual language, merging the structural logic of Chinese calligraphy with the legibility of Latin script.""" ;
