<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2544> a ns3:Q184485 ;
    ns2:P1476 "1988-2025 - Hair Painting"@en ;
    ns2:P175 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/585> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q18699055>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2692606>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4184> ;
    ns2:P186 ns3:Q11472,
        ns3:Q127418 ;
    ns2:P2047 "PT1H"^^xsd:duration ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q12501>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P585 "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P856 <https://www.echomorgan.com/#/echo-exhibitions/> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2544.jpg> ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2545> ;
    ns1:description """This performance constitutes an autobiographical and transgenerational project that examines the relationship between personal experience, family memory, and artistic practice. The work originates from two distinct yet related events: in 1988, the artist’s mother took her five-year-old daughter to the Great Wall of China shortly after her divorce, crossing the site during a sandstorm that significantly reduced visibility and made orientation difficult; in 2011, following her own divorce, the daughter—Echo Morgan—began developing a performance-based practice centered on the use of her own hair as a painting tool.\r
The Great Wall is approached as a concrete, historically charged location. In the account of 1988, the sandstorm is described not symbolically but as an environmental condition that physically obstructed movement and altered spatial perception. The experience of walking under limited visibility became a foundational element in the family’s shared memory.\r
In 2025, mother and daughter returned together to the same site. The visit took place in spring, under stable weather conditions, with apricot blossoms in bloom and diffused natural light. This return created a direct comparison between two historical moments in the same location, highlighting the differences in both environmental circumstances and biographical stages.\r
The project is realized through a live hair painting performance executed by Echo Morgan. The artist uses her own hair as a brush, dipping it into black ink and dragging it across the surface of a canvas. The action requires repeated movements of the head and upper body, producing broad, continuous marks.\r
The use of hair as a painting instrument removes the mediation of a conventional brush and establishes direct contact between body and surface. Black ink is selected for its density and chromatic uniformity, allowing variations in pressure, direction, and rhythm to remain clearly visible. The resulting marks document the duration of the action and the physical relationship between body, gravity, and material. In this sense, the canvas does not function as a figurative image but as a material record of a performative act.\r
The overall structure of the work emphasizes cyclical temporality: two generations of women visit the same site at different moments in their lives, both following marital separation. The return in 2025 does not replicate the experience of 1988 but recontextualizes it within a new biographical and artistic framework.\r
Through the repetition of the hair painting practice—initiated in 2011 and further developed in subsequent years—Echo Morgan transforms a personal event into an artistic methodology. The performance integrates individual memory, family history, and bodily gesture into a single operational process in which the body functions simultaneously as instrument, subject, and archive of lived experience.""" ;
