        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q283> ;
    ns2:P2048 "220 cm" ;
    ns2:P2049 "100 cm" ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P571 "2011"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description "“Chaos” (混沌)  is an artwork executed in 2011 by Shao Yan combining the technique of ink “washed” in water (shui de “chongxi” 水的 “冲洗”) with the use of a syringe. The artist added water to the ink in the syringe and then injected it on the paper allowing it to flow strong and free. The syringe emits a rustling sound that remembers the heart's pained cry when blood is sucked through the aorta artery.The work has a bitter taste because it is a reminder of the experience between life and death lived by the artist during various heart surgeries. Shao Yan decided to limit the work beauty by only executing solid lines. The initial buzzing sound emitted by the ink escaping from the syringe, gradually fades into silence. The sound could represent the flowing blood that, when stopping, puts an end to life."@en ;
