<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/454> a ns1:CalliWritingUnit ;
    ns2:P144 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/447> ;
    ns2:P1480 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q60070514> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q178561>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q198>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q26237350> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1348059>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935> ;
    ns2:P282 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/running_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/454.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 2 With Irresistible Force (《满 江红》)" ;
    ns1:description """This Calli-Writing Unit is the long calligraphy situated on the upper right side of the artwork. According to EricTin (interview 2016), the content of this calligraphy is inspired by the poem "Full River Red (Man jiang hong 满 江 红 )" composed by the General Yue Fei (1103-1142), who wrote it during one of the last battles against the northern barbarians, before the final withdrawal of his imperial army. \r
Yue Fei, an esteemed officer of the Northern Song army, staunchly opposed the retreat in the face of the Jurchen-Jin invasion of northern China in 1127. Despite the fall of the Northern Song Dynasty, Yue Fei continued his service under the Southern Song Dynasty (1127-1279), engaging in continuous battles against the Jurchen. His military success against the Jin in 1140 was overshadowed by the Southern Song Emperor's decision to seek peace, leading to Yue Fei's abrupt withdrawal and the undoing of his decade-long achievements. It was during one of these victorious campaigns against the Jin that Yue Fei composed the poem "Full River Red", expressing his determination to reclaim lost territories, fight against invaders, and rally imperial support for his cause. In this poem, in fact, Yue Fei expresses his anger and wrath towards the enemy, as well as his regret for the emperor’s decision to withdraw his troops on the verge of reconquering lost lands. \r
The calligraphy is  executed in running script (xingshu 行书), and it is characterized by a tangled and tense style that effectively conveys the tumultuous battles and intense passion described in the poem. \r
The connection between Yue Fei’s resolute behavior as depicted in his poem and the "post-up" movement depicted in the graffiti artwork centers around the four-character chengyu "shirupozhu" (势如破竹) positioned at the artwork's core. Both the posture of a basketball player in a post-up position and that of a military general confronting adversaries are marked by "irresistible force". In basketball, executing a post-up involves shielding one's defender with the body to facilitate receiving a pass or making a shot. Similarly, Yue Fei aimed to shield the territories he had reconquered with his army and persist in advancing to return them to the emperor.\r
This thematic convergence illustrates the enduring appeal of themes such as determination, resilience, and strategic positioning across different cultural and historical contexts, linking the artistry of graffiti with the valor of historical military figures like Yue Fei."""@en ;
