        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8201> ;
    ns2:P407 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1860>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7850> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P9302 <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/cursive_running_script> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/455.jpg> ;
    ns1:attributedTitle "CWU 3 With Irresistible Force (tags)" ;
    ns1:description """This Calli-Writing Unit is composed of the red inscription located on the lower left side of the artwork. This inscription is notable for its juxtaposition of Chinese characters and Latin letters, a characteristic often seen in other works by the Kwanyin Clan. Vertically, the characters "GuanYin" (观音) are written in cursive-running style (xingcaoshu 行草书), while horizontally, the names or tags of the crew members who contributed to the artwork (ERICTIN, NAT, YUMI, QUANER) are written from left to right, following the convention of graffiti pieces. Despite the differences in writing systems—Chinese characters versus Latin letters—the writing style of both characters and letters is identical. This uniformity creates a visual harmony, effectively merging Chinese and Western artistic traditions within a single artwork. The use of red in this inscription further evokes the tradition of red seals in traditional Chinese art for several reasons: \r
1. Red Color: Red is traditionally associated with auspiciousness, vitality, and importance in Chinese culture. Red seals are commonly used to mark significance, authenticity, or ownership.\r
2. Position: The placement of the red inscription, particularly with the signature of the artists, echoes the customary positioning of seals in Chinese art, often found in a corner or prominent location.\r
3. Content: The inclusion of the signature of the artists within the red inscription reinforces its function akin to a seal, indicating authorship and ownership of the artwork.\r
In essence, this graffiti inscription not only blends artistic techniques from different cultural backgrounds but also pays homage to traditional Chinese art practices through its use of red, position, and content, thereby enriching the artwork with layers of cultural significance and aesthetic appeal."""@en ;
