    ns2:P571 "2013-06"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    ns1:description """This installation was created in June 2013 by Zhang Qiang and Lia Wei. After performing a "Biface Graphy" (双面书法) performance, Zhang Qiang and Lia Wei hung for two/three hours the three "traced" long silk scrolls (each 50 meters long) on the top of the Jinshanling Great Wall. Through a long tube made of plastic, the rolls of silk are fixed with a rope and, once hung, there is an automatic system that allows them to open. As Zhang Qiang specified, the silk rolls were left hanging for only a few hours due to complaints from tourists, who considered them a sign of bad luck (white, in this case, is a color associated with funerals).\r
Some examples of this kind of installations are realized in: \r
1) November 2010 at the Xijiang Qianhu Miao Village (Xijiang Qianhu Miao zhai 西江千户苗寨), an ancient village in the Guizhou Province of China, in which the traced silk rolls are dropped off a high cliff; \r
2) December 2010 at the Bishan Confucian Temple in Chongqing (Chongqing bi shan wenmiao 重庆璧山文庙);\r
3) March 2012 at the Venice Arsenale in occasion of the "6th Laguna Art Exhibition" (Di liu jie la gu na yishu da zhan 第六届拉古娜艺术大展); \r
4) June 2012 at the Taihang Mountains in Linzhou, Henan in occasion of the "Second Linzhou Ink and Wash Biennale" (Di er jie Linzhou shuimo shuang nian zhan 第二届林州水墨双年展)."""@en ;
