<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/549> a ns3:Q4502142 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q14773> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Confrontation (对峙) - Artwork"@en,
        "对峙"@zh,
        "Duìzhì"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/526> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q115585781>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4115079>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q41798914> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q207849>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q855680> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P571 "2023-01-07"^^xsd:date ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/549.jpg> ;
    ns1:derivesFrom <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/545> ;
    ns1:description """This artwork is the result of the performance "Confrontation". It consists of the mannequin a red humanoid mannequin used by the artist Wu Xixia during the performance. The artist writes with a brush the white characters "human" (ren 人) and "art" (yishu 艺术) on the mannequin to give life to the inanimate object, announcing the beginning of the confrontation. To the sound of the drums, the artist "stabs" the mannequin with the brush, leaving traces of white and red ink on the object. Each time Wu Xixia hits the mannequin, laser beams are projected onto the artist's back, until both the mannequin and the artist's body are covered with marks. At that point, the cloth is replaced and the "fall" of both sides marks the end of the confrontation.\r
The humanoid mannequin is an inanimate object created by industrial civilization and modern technology, while the living person represents humanity, identity, role, creation, etc. The dynamics of the act of "punching each other" through this game of parts represents the confrontation between living and non-living beings. Both sides are kneeling towards each other, in an equal position, just to represent that at the end there is no winner.\r
As Wu Xixia helself explained: "The mannequin in "Confrontation" is made of plastic-plated stainless steel. I sawed off the legs of the ready-made vertical mannequin and turned it into a kneeling state. In fact, at first I thought about using a female body as a mannequin, but I think AI is a genderless non-creature, and I don't want to use looks to blur the concept. For me, the act of comparison is a kind of game. From the microscopic to the macroscopic point of view, the mannequin is a dead object, an inanimate body created by industrial civilization and modern technology, and the living person represents human beings, identities, roles, creations, etc. it is God. The behavior dynamic of "poking" with the tip of the pen represents the confrontation between the living body and the non-living body, and the "kneeling" pattern of both sides represents that there is no winner. Is the coexistence of dead and living things that give meaning to "human beings" controlled or lost in the development of science and technology? Is this exploration of materiality and non-materiality a superego or a complete me? The art itself explores the meaning of existence and the meaning of creation. At the end of the confrontation, 'I' poked 'it' with God's fingertips [...] I wrote the word 'person' on the body of the 'mannequin' on the front, and the word 'art' on the back , which is the product of the transfer from the imaginary world to the real world, even if it is always resisting expression and giving meaning. The appearance of the word "person" in the "mannequin" made of mirrored material is a sign of self-certification and self-affirmation. This is also my thought on the combination of Lacan's "symbolic world-imaginary world-real world" and the theory of the mirror [...]. Expression is also accompanied by "comparison" behavior. On the one hand I understand that art can be both refined and popular, and on the other hand I also understand that art needs unique expression, and this state often has binary oppositions. The backlash and tension between these dualities also drives us to become more well-rounded people"."""@en ;
