    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11736> ;
    ns2:P585 "2010"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/700_1.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/700_2.jpg> ;
    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/698> ;
    ns1:description """Li Xinmo is dressed in white, and she is sitting in the water in a small pool. She starts writing a love poem entitled "Love Poem Written in Water" using a classical lyric form (ci 词) using vermilion ink on a silk cloth. Traces of the characters, written in traditional form, spread outward in the water. In the end, all that remained on the white silk cloth are stained spots which resembled tears. As the artists specified: "It's a poem that I created before the performance. It was a writing of my feelings about a love that I had experienced, an intimate inner experience. I put a long strip of white yarn in the water and then wrote the poem in vermillion ink, the writing dispersing as I wrote. The poem eventually dissolved in the water. The process of this performance is also an interpretation of love, that it is fickle, sad, uncertain, and impossible to keep. With this work I may be saying goodbye to the love I have experienced before, and to the person I once was".\r
"Love" is a major theme in literature from the Romantic period, but here Li Xinmo uses contemporary art forms and a specifically Eastern wisdom in order to shed new light on this never-ending topic. The performance is about the feelings of contemporary people, but it has a distinctly Chinese Chan Buddhist feel to it. \r
This performance largely recalls that of Zhu Qingsheng "Flute Melodies, Flowing Water, Writing Calligraphy (箫声 · 流 水 · 书写, 1997), where the protagonist of the performance is always the impermeability and evanescence given by the passing of time.\r
"""@en ;
