    ns2:P186 ns3:Q127418,
        ns3:Q37681 ;
    ns2:P2047 "PT15M"^^xsd:duration ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P585 "2011"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q626360> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/712_1.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/712_2.jpg> ;
    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/698> ;
    ns1:description """In this performance, Li Xinmo sits quietly in a polluted pond, painting with red ink on a piece of white gauze floating on the surface. She writes few characters of natural mountains and rivers, and at the same time she painted these characters into the shape of a landscape, blurring them into a red colour in the water. The stark contrast of the red ink on green algae slowly dissipates into the pond’s water, rendering her painting illegible; the longer she goes on painting the harder her markings are to decipher, a slow, losing battle against the contaminated water’s relentlessness. This piece highlights how industrialisation and commercialisation of the natural environment is leading to its destruction, with the red ink like blood in the toxic water.\r
As the artist points out: "Black is the most common type of ink colour. However, I chose to use red ink, mainly to reinforce a kind of colour contrast and conflict; the contrast is very strong when it was drawn and written in red on green water. Red also conveys a more dangerous, cruel and disturbing feeling [...]. Water has always been an important theme in my artworks. Probably because I have always painted traditional Chinese ink paintings and have often used water as a metaphor in the Chinese philosophy of Lao Zhuang, as well as in ancient poetry, where there are many descriptions of water. This is why I have a special feeling for mountains and water. Water pollution is also a constant concern for me. So water is my connection with nature and a substance for seeing nature and understanding the world"."""@en ;
    ns1:hasChineseVisualElement <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/landscape_painting> ;
    ns1:hasPerformingArtType <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q213156> ;
