<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/739> a ns3:Q4502142 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Female Character Writing in a Patriarchal Society (父系社会的女字书写) - Artwork"@en,
        "父系社会的女字书写"@zh,
        "Fùxì shèhuì de nǚ zì shūxiě"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/698> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1072348>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q181339>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q308857>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q467>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q600571> ;
    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q127418>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P571 "2012-03"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    ns2:P608 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1912> ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1072348> ;
    ns1:derivesFrom <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/733> ;
    ns1:description "This artwork was produced during the performance \"Female Character Writing in a Patriarchal Society (父系社会的女字书写)\" held by Li Xinmo in March 2012, during the \"Bald Girls Exhibition\" (tutou ge nu 秃頭戈女) at the Iberia Contemporary Art Space of Beijing. It consists of some big traditional characters of the famous Chinese dictionary named \"Shuowen Jiezi 说文解字\" realized by Xu Shen (c. 58 – c. 148 CE). These characters are accompained with their explanations written in smaller size. These big characters with female radical have all a negative meaning, for example: \"hai 妎\" (jealous/demanding); \"fu 妇\" (woman/wife); \"wang 妄\" (presumptuous/rash); \"lan 婪\" (be greedy); \"fang 妨\" (harm), \"hun 婚\" (married), \"yin 婬\" (lascivious/licentious); \"ning 佞\" (sycophant/toady); \"du 妒\" (jealous/envy); \"qu 娶\" (take a wife); \"wei 委\" (throw away/cast aside); \"zi 姊\" (older sister) etc. Characters are realized with white ink on a black wall in regular script. In addition, they are realized in non-simplified form and from top to bottom and from right to left, recalling ancient scriptures. In China female discrimination has a very ancient origin. It starts from writing and, consequently, from calligraphy, an artistic practice exclusive to male in China."@en ;
