    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/920.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Brush Array is an ink artwork consisting of four sheets measuring 248 x 124 cm (each). Each sheet is composed of lines of black ink and water. \r
The title of this artwork (Brush Array) refers to the famous treatise on calligraphy "Diagram of the Battle Formation of the Brush" (Bi zhen tu 笔阵图) written by Wei Shuo 卫铄 (Madame Wei) during the Eastern Jin Dynasty (317–420). Her treatise discusses the methods and techniques for holding and using the brush and outlines seven basic strategies for writing characters with strokes.\r
Wang Dongling's Brush Array is inextricably linked to the different visual effects shaped by the brush and ink. Evolving from the artist’s earlier explorations of traditional calligraphic styles,  Wang developed several artworks focused on modern artistic concepts, eliminating references to any recognizable character form,  the artist use abstract lines with great dynamic tension to emphasise the pure expression of subjective images and emotions.\r
Wang Dongling explores the expressive potential of the brush and the ink on the paper in several series of different abstract artworks, such as Untitled, 1999; Dark (Heaven) and Yellow (Earth), 2005; Being Open and Empty, 2005; Ink Meditation 2005.  These works embody the spirit of traditional Chinese calligraphy and its essence, manifested through the abstract and expressive potential of the brush. Explicitly referencing the treatise "Diagram of the Battle Formation of the Brush", Wang's work reinterprets this legacy from a  modern perspective. Large areas of the composition are saturated with several bold, rich black ink brushstrokes that are allowed to seep outward, creating uneven, dilated edges. """@en ;
