    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1135.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """"Not Too Late" represents Feng Mengbo's first digital artwork engaging directly with calligraphy. It is structured as an interactive digital video based on video game logic and incorporates  aesthetic elements of Chinese calligraphy. In this video, the calligraphic gesture is  literally brought to life: it dissolves, reconfigures, dances, and explodes. The work’s emphasis on performative gesture and kinetic energy recalls Jackson Pollock’s action painting (artist interview, March 2024). \r
The soundtrack plays an important role in the structure of the video: it opens with traditional Chinese instruments and transitions into electronic music in the second part. The title "Not Too Late" is derived from the second musical track, which is composed by Hua Dong, member of the Chinese indie rock band New Pants (Xin kuzi yuedui 新裤子乐队). From a technological point of view, Feng Mengbo uses a customized version of the popular first-person shooter video game “Quake 3 Arena” (id Software Inc., ©1999) to simulate the movements of brushstrokes in a three-dimensional virtual space. \r
This artwork was first exhibited in "Decode: Digital Design Sensations", organized by the Victoria and Albert Museum and hosted at the CAFA Museum in Beijing in 2010. \r
From this video piece, the artist developed a series of ink and mixed-media paintings that capture isolated moments from the video and transfer them to canvas and paper. These works were presented for the first time in the "Not Too Late - Recent Works by Feng Mengbo"  exhibition at Chambers Fine Art Gallery in Beijing in 2012, and later in New York in 2013."""@en ;
