    ns2:P585 "2020"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1196332> ;
    ns1:description """This new dance theater piece took place at the “Ringling Historic Asolo Theater” (Sarasota, Florida) in January 2020.   This piece is a boundary-defying work of intercultural dance theater inspired by one of the most revered plays in Chinese literature: “The Peony Pavilion” (Mudan meng 牡丹亭), also named “The Return of Soul at the Peony Pavilion”, a romantic tragicomedy written by the great Chinese playwright Tang Xianzu 湯顯祖 (1550- 1616). It is traditionally staged as a Kunqu play. The play focuses on the love story between Du Liniang 杜丽娘 a female young noble and Liu Mengmei 柳梦梅. During a walk in her house garden, Du Liniang stops to rest and falls asleep. In her dream, a handsome young man named Liu Mengmei appears to her. She has never met this young man, but she falls in love with him at first sight. Awakened by a rain of petals, the dream vanishes: unable to find the enchantment of the dream in her life and reality, Du Liniang dies of melancholy. As it happens, her dreamed-of lover passes by the girl’s house. Seeing her picture on the wall, he declares: “This is the woman I saw in my dreams—this is the woman I will marry!”. Informed of her passing, he is despondent—but when her family brings him to her coffin, the girl suddenly wakes from death. The two immediately fall in love and live happily ever after. The effect of this work on Chinese history and culture can hardly be overstated. Epitomizing a deep strain of romantic mysticism within Daoist philosophy, “The Peony Pavilion” states the case for life as an essential mystery.  “The Peony Pavilion” is part of the collection “The Four Dreams of Linchuan” (Linchuan si meng 临川四梦), also called “The Four Dreams of the Jade Tea Hall”.  \r
The performance is realized by a total of four dancers: Yin Mei 殷梅, Zhang Huiwang 张慧望, Zhou Lijun 周丽君and Hui Guanglei 回广磊. It begins with Yin Mei in the centre stage standing on a red sheet placed along the midline and holding a bowl full of ink. A voiceover (by Yin Mei herself) recites: “In my dreams, I see what I cannot see and hear what I cannot hear”  , repeated three times. At the same time, another performer enters from the back of the stage, carrying a paper folding fan with her. She approaches Yin Mei moving very slowly, then exits the scene. Yin Mei remains alone in the centre of the stage and begins to drip the black ink in the bowl on the red carpet. A voice-over (this time a male one) continues to recite: “In my dreams, I see what I cannot see and hear what I cannot hear”, taking the viewer into another dimension. Two dancers dressed in white enter the stage, starting to run towards a scroll of paper placed on a panel to the left of the stage. The two dancers write Chinese characters very quickly with black felt-tip pens, various signs, lines, and scribbles, including a date, running back and forth, as in a sort of competition to see who “writes faster”. The men race to a vertical scrim to madly scribble or smear paint in the wild style pioneered by the calligrapher Huaisu known as the “Crazy Monk”   or like an Asian Jackson Pollock. At the same time, Yin Mei continues to struggle on the red sheet placed in the centre of her and to trace movements with her body, starting to spread ink all over the sheet with her body using sponges soaked in ink. One of the two dancers and Yin Mei leave the stage, while the other dancer remains. After a few seconds, the three are all on the scene. Zhou Lijun begins to caress the white sheet on which the two dancers had written, full of signs and scribbles, “feeling” that kind of creation just made; the other two dancers, on the other hand, roll and dance on the sides of the creation created shortly before by Yin Mei, which lights up becoming almost incandescent. One of the two dancers “climbs” on Yin Mei’s calligraphy, also starting to “feel” those traces of ink. As she struggles with strong, snappy movements, Yin Mei begins to rewind the red roll of paper, walking bent over. The other dancer leaves the stage, then returns several times and throws dust (or earth/clay?) at Yin Mei. Then the dancer slowly follows her, throwing the powder on her head while Yin Mei continues to rewind the roll, to then both leave the scene.\r
Then a male dancer and a female dancer enter, he dressed in white and she in black and they position themselves very close, facing each other. The male off-screen voice starts again, and the two dancers begin to make very slow movements in the centre of the stage, where the roll used to be and which has now left only a luminous trail. The dancer is left alone, and Yin Mei enters the stage with a microphone where she reads some letters she wrote to her mother when she was young. The letters are particularly wrenching: “Mom, I’m going to the military training […]; I prepare for suffering […] to distinguish myself”. Simultaneously, the same words are also enunciated in Chinese by a voice-over. Yin Mei leaves the stage, the dancer dressed in black remains and the other two dancers dressed in white enter, who begin to unroll a roll of white paper, then making it swing diagonally and turning in the centre of the stage. Yin Mei and the other dancer enter the stage, while the other two dancers hold the scroll, holding it up towards the audience. The two females begin to rub it on all sides and draw calligraphic lines with sponges soaked in ink, as if they wanted to “feel” it: Yin Mei and the other female dancer write on both sides of the roll, alternating in movements. Once the calligraphy is finished, the scroll is taken away and Yin Mei begins to speak again, this time off-screen, addressing her mother and describing the artist she will become, calling herself “The Eve of a Chinese New Year”. The male dancer and the female dancer remain on stage and begin to dance together. A little later from two, they become three, starting to make linear and sinuous movements in which the bodies intertwine with each other. Only the female dancer, Zhou Lijun 周丽君, remains on the scene, who is joined by Yin Mei, who begins to wrap a piece of blue cloth around the mouth of her collaborator. As she does this, her voice-over continues her story to her mother, telling her to be safe and to have everything she needs: two pairs of socks, a jacket, a pair of boots, a bed to sleep on. Zhou Lijun gradually manages to get rid of the fabric with which Yin Mei “tied her to himself”.\r
A half-naked male dancer enters the scene and the two dancers begin to calligraph on his body, until it becomes covered in ink. The dancer then begins to dance alone in the center of the stage while another male dancer, behind him, begins to cover the hanging white sheet that had been previously calligraphed with red paint. This dancer starts to take a running start, as if he wants to climb onto the panel and then hang on it. He then begins to make movements remaining in contact with the sheet, rubbing his body against it. Another dancer hands him a vat full of ink, where he will dip both feet. Maintaining perfect balance inside the bowl, he starts jumping with feet together, moving forward and splashing black ink out of the bowl. The result is a thick black line smeared on the white floor. Another male dancer joins him, rotating the body of the dancer who always remains with his feet inside the basin. Yin Mei enters the scene with a white mask on her face and the two dancers start making her dance, carrying her up and accompanying her movements. While Yin Mei remains alone on the stage, two other dancers enter and bring folded sheets of paper with them which they place at the sides of the stage. The two then lie down on it, while from the back two female dancers carry a sheet of paper spread out like a fan soaked in red ink. A female dancer positions herself in the centre, and the two male dancers stand up, also fanning the sheets. The three dancers begin to dance together, opening and closing their fans almost simultaneously. The three then stop and kneel in front of the papers. Yin Mei enters the stage with both black and red ink, which she begins to sprinkle on the fans of the other dancers, then throws a basin full of black ink at them. The four, now completely covered in ink, perform a final choreography, in which bodies, ink and splashes of color form a single whole.\r
"""@en ;
