<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1265> a ns3:Q184485 ;
    ns2:P1132 1 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Cursive / Wild Grass (草书)"@en,
        "草书"@zh,
        "Cǎo shū"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P175 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1642> ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3326454>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q607543> ;
    ns2:P186 ns3:Q11472,
        ns3:Q127418 ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1384> ;
    ns2:P585 "2006"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1642> ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1641> ;
    ns1:description "More than a real choreography, “Cursive (Wild Grass)” is a mixed media solo installation/performance work.  It was held for the first time on 25 February 2006 at the Vision Festival of New York.  The performer is Yin Mei herself, accompanied by the music of the American jazz multi-instrumentalist Joe McPhee (b.1939).  Yin Mei is dressed in black and lying on a white paper sheet. She starts to move, snaking and rolling around on the paper. Then she drenches her body in ink, starting to move as before but leaving traces of ink on the paper through her body movements. With her hands and feet, she performs circular and spiral movements, creating both linear and spotty smears of ink, becoming a calligraphic brush. This type of performance is very reminiscent of the movements and gestures made by the artist and performer Echo Morgan in “Anatomy of Posidonia” (2022) (see the archive) or also in “Painting until it becomes Marble - Love Never Dies” (2019). Both these female artists have their bodies pervaded by ink or colours, they both lie down on the sheet and move, leaving “traces” of themselves on the sheet. What changes is the calligraphic reference: in “Cursive/Wild Grass” there is certainly a more direct reference to calligraphy (already visible from the title itself), while in Echo Morgan’s performances the intention to revoke calligraphy is more veiled. In both artistic concepts, however, what prevails is the expression of themselves through tradition, through something that speaks of themselves and their Chinese cultural identity. And for both, the only means to implement this type of expression is the body, transformed it into a brush."@en ;
