    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1268.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The "Running Script - GPS Calligraphy Project" is a video that describes the project Feng Mengbo carried on between 2005 and 2006, in which he merges modern GPS technology—utilizing waypoint marking, path mapping, voice navigation, and real-time GPS tracking—to translate Chinese calligraphy into large-scale geospatial art. The video consists of two main parts: creating Chinese character shapes through GPS tracking on the roads of Beijing (Part 1) and on the sea near Brisbane, Australia (Part 2).\r
Part 1: Driving in Beijing: This phase involved studying and planning Chinese characters by marking waypoints and mapping the path, which served as a blueprint for the driving route. The waypoints and path were programmed into a GPS navigation system, which the driver meticulously followed. During the execution, the artist used a separate GPS device to record and collect data on the vehicle's path. Several Chinese characters in Regular Script were created, including shì 世 (world).\r
Part 2 Motorboating in Brisbane: In this phase, the execution shifted from urban streets to open water. Instead of a car, a motorboat was used to trace the characters. The pre-mapped path was followed via GPS navigation, and the artist once again collected data on the movement. On the water, the artist created characters in Running Script, a more fluid and expressive calligraphic style.\r
The transition from Regular Script in Beijing to Running Script in Brisbane was a deliberate adaptation to the specific environment. The rigid, angular structure of Regular Script suited the straight roads and intersections of Beijing, while the flowing and dynamic nature of Running Script was more compatible with the movement of water. The video, The Invisible Words: A GPS Calligraphy Project, was exhibited at the Asia Multimedia Arts Festival (Yataiduo Meiti Yishujie 亚太多媒体艺术节, MAAP) in Australia in 2006.""" ;
