<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/138> a ns1:DecorativeAndAppliedArt ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Logo of the Paralympic Games Beijing 2008"@en ;
    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1094542>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1206287>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q25439032> ;
    ns2:P287 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/139> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1088>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3133>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3142> ;
    ns2:P571 "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/138.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The logo of the 2008 Paralympic Games, dubbed “Sky, Earth and Human Beings” (Tiandiren 天地人), was designed by Paul Liu, a professor at the Central Academy of Fine Arts in Beijing. The logo represents a stylized figure of an athlete in motion, symbolizing the tremendous efforts that a disabled person must exert both in sports and in daily life. This figure is the result of the transformation of the Chinese character “zhi 之”—which can mean “birth,” “life,” “go,” or “attainment”—into a modern, dynamic visual element. The character is rendered in a Chinese calligraphic font, drawing on the aesthetic qualities of semi-cursive script to convey fluidity and energy.\r
The design reflects the unity and harmony of “sky, earth, and human beings”, integrating Chinese characters, calligraphy, and the spirit of the Paralympics. In this way, the logo embodies the Paralympic motto “Spirit in Motion”, while also illustrating the philosophical principle of the integration of heart, body, and spirit—a central tenet of Chinese culture. The three colors of the logo—red, blue, and green—represent the sun, sky, and earth, respectively, reinforcing the symbolic connection between human beings and the natural cosmos.\r
According to the "Shuowen Jiezi" (说文解字), the earliest surviving Chinese dictionary, completed by Xu Shen (许慎) around 100 CE, the original form of the character “zhi 之” derives from “zhi 止” (foot) with a line added beneath it. This etymology emphasizes movement and the notion of “going forward,” which resonates with the mission of the Paralympics: transcending physical and personal limits. The character’s form appears to depict a human figure with a head (the red dot), arms and torso (the blue stroke), and a single stroke in place of two legs (the green stroke). This visual form evokes the physical conditions of some Paralympic athletes, while emphasizing their capacity to move, walk, and progress in continuous motion. \r
The designer enhances the aesthetic and visual impact through the skillful combination and arrangement of dots and lines, ensuring that the shape of the athlete harmonizes with the seal of the Beijing Olympic Games. The inscription “Beijing 2008”, like that of the Olympic logo, uses black calligraphic strokes, while “Paralympic Games” is written in black using a Western-style digital font, reflecting a thoughtful integration of Eastern and Western visual systems.\r
Thus, the logo can be seen as an example of glocalization, where local elements of Chinese tradition—characters, calligraphy, and philosophy—are combined with the global visibility and universal spirit of the Paralympic Games, achieving a perfect synthesis of tradition, modernity, and international significance.\r
\r
This logo is directly linked to the official logo of the Beijing 2008 Olympic Games, designed by Guo Chunning, commonly referred to as “Dancing Beijing.” Selected through an international design competition and officially unveiled on August 3, 2003, the emblem represents a sophisticated synthesis of traditional Chinese calligraphy, cultural symbolism, and the dynamic spirit of the Olympic Games.\r
The logo features a red seal containing a stylized character "Jing 京" (capital), which has been transformed into the abstract shape of a dancing figure or athlete in motion, evoking energy, vitality, and the forward momentum of competition. The fluid, brush-like strokes not only recall the gestural qualities of Chinese calligraphy, particularly cursive script, but also convey a sense of harmony, rhythm, and cultural continuity. The design can further be interpreted as resembling a dancer or a dragon, both potent symbols of welcome, auspiciousness, and national identity in Chinese culture.\r
The emblem’s dominant red color symbolizes prosperity, good fortune, and celebration, while the integrated typography—“Beijing 2008”—and the five interlocking Olympic rings anchor the composition within the international visual language of the Olympic movement. By harmonizing indigenous cultural elements with global iconography, the logo achieves a visual and conceptual dialogue between local identity and worldwide recognition, reflecting the ethos of the Olympic Games as a unifying, cross-cultural event.\r
Overall, “Dancing Beijing” exemplifies the capacity of contemporary emblematic design to translate historical, calligraphic, and symbolic references into a modern visual identity, capable of conveying both movement and meaning to a global audience."""@en ;
