    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1395.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """In  Blackism No. 1, Luo Qi departs from traditional calligraphy and focuses on the brushstrokes rather than the characters' meaning. He replaces the conventional pointed brush with a wide, flat brush producing bold forms. Luo Qi creates a piece with dynamic, abstract forms that echo the styles of Western abstract artists like Franz Kline and Robert Motherwell. The work reveals the combined influence of Western modernism, Japanese calligraphy, and contemporary Chinese art. It captures a decisive turning point in Chinese calligraphic practice in the late 1980s and early 1990s.\r
Blackism No. 1 belongs to the Chinese Avant-Garde movement that emerged in the mid-to-late 1980s. Although the artwork originates from a traditional Chinese character, Luo Qi deliberately disrupts the conventional stroke order, rendering the character unreadable. This transformation shifts the work from "calligraphic" to "abstract".  The disappearance of the legible sign leads to an inevitable loss of meaning. """ ;
