    ns2:P580 "1998-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "1998-06-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P664 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q49088> ;
    ns1:description """"Brushed Voices: Calligraphy in Contemporary China" represents the first important exhibition of contemporary Chinese calligraphy outside China. It was held from April 14 to June 6, 1998 at the Miriam & Ira D. Wallach Art Gallery of the Columbia University.  In the exhibition, curated by Zhang Yiguo, 64 works by more than 20 leading calligraphers from the People's Republic of China were displayed. The exhibited artists included Bai Di, Han Tianheng, He Yinghui, Liang Yang, Liu Bingsen, Liu Tianwei, Liu Wenhua, Liu Zhengcheng, Luo Qi, Ouyang Zhongshi, Shao Yan, Shen Peng, Sun Boxiang, Sun Xiaoyun, Wang Dongling, Wang Xuezhong, Wang Yong, Wang Youyi, Zhang Daoxing, Zhang Qiang, and Zhou Huijun.\r
According to Zhang Yiguo, the exhibited artworks exemplies the two main trends of contemporary calligraphy: "The first is marked by the revitalization of traditional methods. [...] The second trend is the so-called modern calligraphy (xiandai shufa), which departs radically from traditional methods." (Zhang 1998, 5). This second trend can be divided into two main currents: "paintinglike calligraphy" and "non-character calligraphy" (ibid, 19-20). Representative artists of the first trend are Wang Youyi, Liu Wenhua, Sun Boxiang, Liu Zhengcheng, and Shen Peng. Rpresentative artists of the second trend are Wang Yong, Zhang Daoxing, Shao Yan, Luo Qi, Wang Dongling and Zhang Qiang.  """ ;
