    ns1:description """"Double Hook and Simple Heart"(Shuanggou suxin 双钩素心) is an artwork realized by Hua Jun in 2010. The artist merges traditional Chinese calligraphy with contemporary concerns about waste and sustainability. His work engages directly with environmental themes. In the early spring of 2010, during the Qingming Festival, Hua Jun visited the Tianzilin landfill in Hangzhou, where a hill had been formed from 18 years of accumulated city waste. On this site, he applied the traditional double-hook (shuanggou 双钩) technique to write eight characters  from the Heart Sutra (Xinjing 心经): bu sheng bu mie, bu zeng bu jian 不生不灭.，不增不减 ('Neither born nor perishing, neither increasing nor decreasing') on eco-friendly covering film. Guided by his mentor Wang Dongling, Hua Jun integrated the landfill into his artistic process. The contrast between calligraphy and garbage sharpens his questioning of "human traces" and the relationship between cultural heritage and environmental degradation. The title of this artwork reflects its method and meaning. "Double-hook"(Shuanggou 双钩) refers to a traditional calligraphic method used to outline characters with precise, slender lines before filling them in. Often employed to  replicate ancient calligraphy, the technique is here left unfinished. Hua Jun outlines the eight large characters but deliberately leaves them empty. This absence suggests impermanence and ephemerality. The second part of the title, "Simple Heart" or "Pure Heart" (Suxin 素心) evokes Buddhist ideals: sincerity, simplicity, and spiritual clarity. The artist uses calligraphy on biodegradable material at a garbage site further reinforces the contrast between accumulation (waste) and emptiness (pure heart). The Heart Sutra’s core message, i.e. existence and impermanence, resonates through the work. The realization of this work was documented in a 7-minute and 12-second video, exhibited at "Shu Fei Shu — 2010 Hangzhou International Modern Calligraphy Art Exhibition" in 2010.\r
Building on this approach,  Hua Jun created "Garbage Diary (Laji Riji 垃圾日记)" in 2012. This series of paintings (68 cm x 138 cm) was made from garbage and discarded materials. Through these works, he critiques the environmental consequences due to the rapid industrialization, mass consuption and urbanization in China. Repurposing trash as medium, he challenges notions of permanence and value. Also in 2012, he performed “Non-Born, Non-Destructed, Non-Pure, Non-Impure, Non-Increased, Non-Decreased (bu sheng bu mie, bu gou bu jing, bu zeng bu jian 不生不灭，不垢不净，不增不减). The artist used ashes from waste as his material. By touching and moving through these remains, he showed key ideas from Buddhist thought. Ashes are both what is left after destruction and a sign of purification.""" ;
