        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1432_2.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "\"Rubbing Drought\" is a conceptual work by Wang Nanming. It addresses social and ecological issues in China. In February 2009, parts of central China suffered a severe drought. Xiliu Lake in Zhengzhou, once the city's main water reservoir, dried up. Its bed cracked under the heat. Wang used rice paper and the traditional tayin 拓印 technique (rubbing) to capture those fissures. Using cotton pads wrapped in cloth, called \"rubbing pad\" or \"rubbing pouch\" (tabao 拓包), he imprinted  the cracks. He thus turned the drought’s physical marks into a visual record. The work consists of a variable number sheets, each measuring 140 x 79 cm. It belongs to Wang's Ecological Trilogy (shengtai zuopin sanbu qu 生态作品三部曲). It was exhibited during \"Ta Yin Drought: Wang Nanming Solo Exhibition\" (2012)." ;
