    ns2:P19 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q16572> ;
    ns2:P21 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q6581097> ;
    ns2:P2561 "Elvis 猫王 " ;
    ns2:P27 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P361 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1494> ;
    ns2:P800 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1517> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q16572> ;
    ns1:description """Elvis, known as "The Unabated Vandal" (No Apologies 3, 188) has made a name for himself in the urban art scene through his relentless and raw style. His graffiti can best be described as anarchic and rebellious, often drawing comparisons to the intensity of "being in a mosh pit while the Dead Kennedys perform I Fought the Law" (No Apologies 3, 168) . His work stands out for its unapologetic crudeness, a stark contrast to the gentrified streets where it often appears, turning these sanitized areas into canvases for his audacious tags.\r
Elvis's approach to graffiti is defined by its quantity and audacity. His tags dominate the urban landscape, especially in China, where he doesn't concern himself with creating aesthetically pleasing pieces. Instead, his goal is to leave as many marks as possible, wherever he can. His persistence and stamina are unmatched by most graffiti artists in the region, making him a ubiquitous presence in cities like Guangzhou.\r
Interestingly, Elvis’s alias,  Mao Wang 猫王 (The Hillbilly Cat), nods to the cultural collision between Chinese and Western influences. Guangzhou, his home province, has historically been one of the first Chinese cities exposed to American pop culture. Although Elvis acknowledges some subtle influence from Western culture, he insists that the origin of graffiti matters less to him than the fact that he continues to create without pause or compromise.\r
A distinctive aspect of Elvis's work is his experimentation with traditional Chinese calligraphy, particularly the ancient script known as jiaguwen 甲骨文 (oracle bone script). His style has been deeply influenced by classical Chinese calligraphic works, drawing inspiration from Yanghuáibiao jì 杨淮表记 (The Record of Yang Huai), from Sanshi pan 散氏盘 (Sanshi Disk), and the Guangwu jiang jun bei 广武将军碑 (Guangwu General Monument). These ancient forms have shaped his graffiti, lending his work a sense of depth and a nod to China's rich cultural heritage.\r
The use of jiaguwen (oracle bone script) and the personification or zoomorphism in his works also evokes the style of Keith Haring.\r
Elvis also extends his artistic expression into tattooing, where the connection between Chinese culture and traditional motifs is evident. He finds inspiration in the naive folk-art traditions and integrates these influences into his tattoo designs, aiming to bridge the ancient allure of Chinese culture with modern interpretations.\r
Elvis is a prominent member of the GB (Ge Bang, Pigeon Gang) crew, a collective that merges illegal graffiti with underground music and cultural events. The group consists of a diverse set of individuals, including graffiti artists, tattooists, photographers, skaters, and even finance professionals. Their low-profile presence adds to their mystique, with members rarely showing their faces publicly, creating a sense of an underground movement operating in the shadows.\r
When discussing the rapid gentrification of Chinese cities, Elvis is philosophical. He doesn't see his graffiti as reshaping the city but rather views the city as an evolving force that constantly challenges his work. In this sense, his pieces are personal expressions that struggle against the erasure brought on by urban development. He paints continuously to counteract this impermanence, knowing that graffiti is fleeting by nature.\r
Among his influences, Elvis frequently cites TIE (RIP) from San Francisco, whose spirit and rebellious energy continue to guide him. Despite his admiration for other graffiti artists, Elvis remains indifferent to the broader graffiti scene, focusing instead on his personal expression and the thrill of seeing his tags on the streets.\r
Elvis's work in Guangzhou and Hong Kong has connected him with like-minded local artists who share a dedication to graffiti as a serious art form. He admires the caution and commitment with which Hong Kong writers approach their craft, contributing to a dynamic exchange between the two cities’ urban art communities.\r
For Elvis, the journey of graffiti is as significant as the destination. He finds joy in the act of painting, often recounting memorable nights spent tagging the streets and then relaxing under a giant statue of Mao in Changsha with his friends. His dedication to graffiti is not just about leaving a mark on the world but about the camaraderie and shared experiences that come with it."""@en ;
