    ns2:P2561 "Dongbay 東北 " ;
    ns2:P27 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P361 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1494> ;
    ns2:P569 "1996"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P800 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1511> ;
    ns2:P812 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q219625> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q60>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8686>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1510.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Dongbei 東北 (Dongbay) writes graffiti tags such as ASKO, LEEX, and TOTEM. The tag ASKO is a playful reference, a shortened form of “Taba 她爸ASKO,” which sounds like “TABASCO” but means “Her Daddy ASKO.” The tag "Dongbei 東北 (Dongbay)" holds special significance in a Chinese context. It refers to Northeast China, a vast region whose people are often generalized as "North-easterners" without making distinctions about their specific origins, unlike other regions in China. Historically, this area carries complex connotations—its ruggedness and rough cultural identity contrast with traditional Chinese philosophies like Confucianism and Taoism. Yübo, having left the Northeast at an early age, finds humor and paradox in this identity and uses "Dongbei 東北 (Dongbay)" as both a personal code and a reflection of his life's experience.\r
Yübo has been part of several crews, including YDS, 2G, GB, Yellow Peril, LateNiteStars, and HEX. Though he currently lives in Beijing and spends much of his time there, he leads a nomadic lifestyle. His artistic journey took shape in New York City, where the competitive graffiti scene had a lasting impact on him. Although Beijing and Shanghai are central to his life now, Yübo finds the graffiti scene in these cities lacking. He prefers Guangzhou for its vibrant graffiti culture and hopes to visit more often.\r
Yübo started engaging with graffiti during high school, initially drawn to its rebellious nature. In a stressful academic environment, graffiti provided an outlet for resistance and escape. His real engagement with graffiti began in college, where he met other graffiti writers and started developing his style more seriously.\r
He describes his graffiti as a spontaneous, hit-and-run sport rather than an artistic pursuit, though he acknowledges the artistic elements that inevitably come with it. His style and process are shaped by personal habits, sketches, and experiments from other artistic fields, mostly improvising without the use of a formal sketchbook.\r
Graffiti, for Yübo, serves primarily as a source of enjoyment and personal meditation amid the anxieties of urban life. It also allows him to connect with friends who live on the margins of society, which he finds refreshing.\r
When asked about a potential "Chinese graffiti style," Yübo doesn’t fully embrace the idea. He points out that the graffiti scene in China is weak, with few devoted writers left. Western graffiti once had a significant influence on the Chinese scene, but many foreign writers have left due to visa, legal, or personal reasons. Chinese censorship and the quick removal of graffiti make it hard for writers to sustain their work. The risks of getting caught and jailed are high, and with fewer writers around to appreciate and recognize each other's contributions, graffiti has become less appealing. Many younger graffiti writers have shifted their activities to the internet, where they face fewer risks but gain more visibility.\r
Yübo feels fortunate to count several devoted Chinese graffiti artists as his friends, including Ghettosino (2G/YP/KDG), SBAM (RSG), Zatuo 杂投 ZATO (2G/YIA/YP), Lingdan 灵丹 (KTS/KDG/YP), WRECK (KTS/KDG/YP), JOINTS (2G/KDG), SMOK (2G), KISH (MTS), ZEIT (2G/YP), ROMPER (2G/KDG/GB), and ELVIS (GB/HEX).\r
Regarding Yellow Peril, Yübo mentions that there are over ten members, though he can only speak for the Beijing crew. Notable members include Lingdan 灵丹, Zatuo 杂投, and Qizi 漆仔, who were prominent in the city's graffiti scene before the formation of Yellow Peril. LIngdan 灵丹 founded the crew during the pandemic to unite graffiti writers who tag their names in Chinese. The crew has members spread across the world, from Europe to North and South America, many of whom don't speak or understand Chinese but continue to engage in this cultural practice."""@en ;
