    ns2:P186 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3055935>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q42948> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1088>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P571 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1511.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The artwork "Dongbei 東北"  was made in Beijing in 2022. In this masterpiece, Dongbay painted his large-scale tag in Chinese characters “Dongbei 東北” using only two colors: black and blue. The term Dongbei 東北, which means “northeast,” denotes the northeastern region of China that includes the provinces of Liaoning, Jilin, and Heilongjiang. The artist chose this tag because he comes from one of these provinces. Next to the large tag, whose characters are heavily reworked and shaped in squared forms as in small seal script (xiaozhuan 小篆), the characters Huanghuo 黄祸 (Yellow Peril, the name of his crew) are written on the right side in small characters, incorporating a subtle reference to the birds-and-worms script (niaochongshu 鸟虫书). The entire piece also alludes to the zhuanshu 篆书 (seal script) form of Daoist talismanic style in three ways: (i) by reproducing Daoist talismanic script (fulu 符箓 or fuwen 符文), characterized by symbols with twisted, irregular and elongated strokes that sometimes resemble Chinese characters, (ii) by decorating the strokes of the characters Dongbei 東北 with small blue circles that resemble talisman stars, and (iii) by adding decorative swirls to the strokes of Huanghuo 黄祸. The use of ornamental embellishments, such as squiggles and swirls, found in talismanic forms stand for a distinctly “Chinese graffiti style” of the New Era (2020-2024) that perhaps replaces the traditional three-dimensional arrows, tribal motifs, and spades typically associated with the wild style. The vertical alignment of the blue circles evokes the structure of bamboo strips (jiace 简册), traditionally used for writing in ancient China, serving as a visual reference to the orderly arrangement of these ancient writing materials. Simultaneously, the artwork seems to portray “war or combat robots”,  particularly through the depiction of the three characters (dong 東, bei 北, huo 祸) with powerful legs firmly anchored to the ground, as indicated by the extension of the vertical strokes, and with arms that enhance the contrast between technological elements and the esoteric, magical qualities associated with talismans. \r
The lettering of the tags "2G" and "YP" evokes “pixação graffiti", a form of graffiti that originated in Brazil, particularly in São Paulo, characterized by a distinctive, cryptic style of lettering. It often features tall, thin, angular letters painted in black or other dark colors on buildings, walls, and other public spaces, typically in hard-to-reach places. \r
Beyond the contrast between the themes of robotics and magic, this graffiti is characterized by other correlated elements: (1) the harmonious juxtaposition of Chinese characters 東北 in the center and  黄祸 on the left and Latin letters “2G”, the name of another crew Dongbay is part of, on the left, and "YP" for “Yellow Peril” on the right; (2) the integration of seal script with the graphic style of talismans; (3) the interplay between graffiti and traditional calligraphy; (4) the uncommon combination of black and blue, which are rarely paired together; and (5) the use of the compound word Dongbei 東北 (“northeast”) which indicates two different directions (north and east). Collectively, these contrasting elements create a sense of harmony, a “harmony within diversity,” reminiscent of the balance between yin and yang that permeates traditional calligraphy. """@en ;
