    ns2:P571 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P6375 "University Town, Guangzhou"@en ;
    ns1:description """The artwork “Elvis (Mao wang 猫王)” was created by the artist Elvis between 2021 and 2022 at Guangzhou University Town. In this piece, Elvis merges playful humor and cultural commentary by writing his tag, "Mao Wang 猫王," in Chinese characters using a black outline and a white fill-in. The name Mao Wang 猫王 translates to “Cat King” in English, a whimsical nod to "The King of Rock 'n' Roll," Elvis Presley. By blending the term mao 猫 (meaning “cat”) with wang 王 (meaning “king”), Elvis both adapts and localizes the legendary moniker, injecting a lighthearted twist into the tag.\r
The inscription sits on a "non-fully aligned hanzigezi 汉字格子 grid," a structure traditionally used for practicing Chinese calligraphy. By deliberately diverging from the grid while tracing the characters, the artist conveys a desire to break free from established rules and constraints, perhaps a metaphor for artistic and cultural liberation. The grid not only functions as a physical backdrop but also as a symbolic reference to traditional forms of control and regulation.\r
Elvis writes the character mao 猫 using small seal script (xiaozhuan 小篆), a style known for its childlike, primitive, and pictographic qualities. This stylistic choice evokes ancient Chinese calligraphy while imbuing the character with a playful, naive quality. In an interview, Elvis discussed how Oracle bone script (jiaguwen 甲骨文) influenced his character style, pointing to a deeper engagement with ancient writing systems (No Apologies 3, 2021).\r
A creative interpretation of radicals (key components in Chinese characters) is also present in the work. Elvis personifies the radical "quan 犭," which means “dog,” by integrating a “head” into the design. The figure is shaped to resemble a living creature with a head, legs, and a tail, though the head’s round shape feels more human than canine, subtly blending the animal and human worlds. Another notable element is the radical cao 艸 (grass), which is placed near the head of the figure, with one tuft of grass overturned. This balances the composition both visually and semantically, suggesting an underlying tension between nature and humanity.\r
The background of the artwork is green, reminiscent of a natural field. The radical tian 田 (meaning "field") appears in the bottom right corner and is stylized as a pictographic wheel, reinforcing the connection between the natural world and human intervention. This contrast between the green background and the gray wall upon which the grid and characters are placed speaks to themes of Nature versus Control, with natural elements overflowing beyond the grid while the character wang 王 (“king”) is contained within a rigid, orderly structure.\r
The character wang 王 itself is written in a style reminiscent of Jian-du 简牍, the script used on ancient bamboo or wooden slips (zhujian 竹简 and mudu 木牍, respectively). These characters, originally inscribed on Qin dynasty bamboo slips, often followed the style of Oracle bone and bronze inscriptions, linking Elvis’s work to both ancient and more contemporary forms of calligraphy. In this context, the wang 王 character symbolizes power, authority, and containment, a contrast to the free-flowing natural elements represented by mao 猫.\r
In the upper left corner of the piece, the characters “Huanghuo 黃禍” (Yellow Peril) appear in traditional script, with certain components written in small seal script. Their placement evokes a colophon, similar to the artist’s signature in traditional calligraphy, like in large-character calligraphic works (dazi大字).\r
This subtle addition invites the viewer to contemplate the broader implications of the work—potentially hinting at veiled political themes such as censorship, authority, and cultural identity. By combining humor, history, and social commentary, Elvis creates a multifaceted piece that engages viewers both visually and intellectually."""@en ;
