    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/154> ;
    ns1:description "Zhu Qingsheng and Zhang Weiliang had a small dwelling near a stream that flows on Mount Huairou (near Beijing). Wearing a cloak of straw (suoyi 蓑衣) and a bamboo hat (douli 斗笠) and bringing with them paintings, calligraphies, and musical instruments, they wanted to look like people returning to their native village. Then, with the aid of a bamboo flute (xiao 箫), calligraphy and a silk scroll, they reproduced a banquet of literati: Zhang Weiliang played the flute, which does not sing melodies but reproduced the sound of water flowing and breaking on the boulders; in the meanwhile, Zhu Qingsheng wrote a calligraphy poem on the silk scroll suspended on the stream, that soaked it. Only the parts of the scroll that lay on the rock kept traces of ink; on those that instead sank in the stream, Zhu Qingsheng wrote directly in the water that washed away the ink. As a result, the “absorbed” characters had been taken away and only the soaked silk scroll remains." ;
