    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q60> ;
    ns1:description "Cui Fei, born in Jinan, China in 1970, received her Bachelor of Fine Arts from the China Academy of Arts and her Master of Fine Arts from Indiana University of Pennsylvania before moving to New York in 1996. She was originally trained as a painter, but then she shifted to working with natural materials such as vine tendrils, branches, and thorns. Influenced by Chinese traditional culture and calligraphy, her installations often evoke the fluidity of cursive script while remaining deliberately unreadable, highlighting the inherent patterns in nature. Her work has been widely exhibited, including at the New Britain Museum of American Art, The Aldrich Contemporary Art Museum, and Museum of Chinese in America, the Princeton University Art Museum. She currently lives and works in New York." ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Present (not specified)"@en .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/189> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Writing Diary with Water Series / Water Diary Series (水写日记系列)"@en,
        "水写日记系列"@zh,
        "Shuǐ xiě rìjì xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/184> ;
    ns2:P580 "1995"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """In this series started in 1995, Song Dong uses a brush dipped in water to write a sort of invisible diary. Evaporating brushstrokes are methodically painted one on the top of the other, over and over again, on the same impassive surface. The text appears momentarily before vanishing and being replaced by the next fluid characters. The artist sets aside time each day to think about life and living, and to let such thoughts dissolve through the water that flows from his brush. The contemplative nature of this process transforms it into an act of meditation. His intent as an artist is fundamentally iconoclastic: to disrupt categories, media hierarchies and bureaucratic as well as aesthetic expectations. And yet these "political" goals are achieved more often than not through works of art that are uncommonly intimate. Diaries serve a number of purposes, from keeping track of appointments to the documentation of events over time. They can be used to trigger memories or to reflect on ideas, emotions and reactions. The defining character of a diary, however, must surely be its self-confessional and innately private nature: diaries contain information relating to an individual, information that is written down rather than shared. Nevertheless with conventional diarising there is an inherent chance of disclosure: the awareness that other people may at some time read what is initially written in secret. \r
The only record that testifies this series is a set of four photographs titled "Writing Diary with Water" made in 1995 that captures four different moments of one daily performance. \r
In 2018, as an extension of this series, Song Dong created an installation with the same title as part of the Kochi-Muziris Biennale, inviting visitors  to dip paintbrushes in the water and move them freely along the translucent wall that constitutes the installation. \r
This series also inspires other Song Dong's artworks, such as: 1) a performance video entitled “Water Records" (Shuiji 水记, 2010), in which Song Dong uses a brush soaked in water to paint images and write texts on hot stone; 2) “Traceless Steel” (Wu hen bei 无痕碑, 2016), a stele made of steel on which Song Dong writes with a brush soaked in water. \r
"""@en ;
