        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q5694896> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2064_1.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2064_2.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "In this video work, Huang Yeda reinterprets Qu Yuan's ancient poem \"Heavenly Questions\" (Tianwen 天问) through a dialogue between human calligraphy and artificial intelligence. The work unfolds on a digital screen where each sequence presents a selection of Qu Yuan’s questions alongside AI-generated responses. Three vertical columns of text sections appear simultaneously: the first reproduces Huang Yeda’s seal-script (zhuanshu) transcription of the original poem, digitally acquired from the installation \"Inquiry of Heaven\"; the second displays a computer-generated transcription in standard script (kaishu); the third presents the responses generated by artificial intelligence in the running-cursive style (xingcao) of the Northern Song master Mi Fu (1051 - 1107). As the video progresses, the characters emerge stroke by stroke in real time, visualising the process of algorithmic text generation and transforming it into a calligraphic performance. Through the continuous succession of questions and answers, the work creates an encounter between an ancient poetic voice and contemporary computational systems, situating classical calligraphy within the technological conditions of the digital age." ;
