    ns2:P571 "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P608 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1569> ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q201405>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q65050532> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2069.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """This artwork is one of the three large vertical scrolls made of transparent plastic of the series "Writing Music - Silent Melodies" (写乐·无声之歌) realized by Luo Qi in 2023. It is inspired by the famous Italian aria "Se l'aura spira" by Girolamo Frescobaldi (1583–1643).  Luo Qi transcribes this famous Italian aria using the oldest Chinese musical notation system, the "Dunhuang Musical Score System" (Dunhuang qupu/yuepu 敦煌曲谱/乐谱). This system, dating back to the 10th century, consists of twenty symbols derived from ancient characters, radicals or character components/parts, traditionally used for notating "pipa"(Chinese lute) tablature. The artist reshapes these symbols according to his personal and original interpretation, so that the musical composition is transformed into a “visual and visible musical score”. The writing flow follos the traditional format (from top to bottom and from right to left), while the materials are contemporary (acrylic on plastic). The use of plastic creates an osmotic interplay of transparencies and reflections and the use of black and white, although traditional, is reconfigured within the transcription of the signs. Furthermore, outlining the signs rather than filling them creates a dynamic interplay of empty and full spaces, typical of calligraphy but rendered in a new form.\r
This artwork was displayed for the first time during the exhibition "Sounds of Ink: Luo Qi and Thirty Years of Calligraphyism", held in 2023 at the International Museum and Library of Music in Bologna.  A similar version of the artwork, executed exclusively in white, was displayed in 2018 during the exhibitions "The Music of Ink - Calligraphies by Luo Qi and Silvio Ferragina in dialogue with the Chinese archive collection of the Braidense Library" (National Braidense Library, Milan - Italy); "The Music of Ink - Calligraphies by Luo Qi and Silvio Ferragina" (2018, Forlì Campus of the University of Bologna); and in 2019 during the exhibition "Luo Qi: Writing Music" (University Library of Bologna). """ ;
