    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2073.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "This artwork is one of eight large works in the series “Sound Images\"  (声音的图像) realized by Luo Qi in 2023. Its signs exemplify Luo Qi's fusion of textual form and visual abstraction, originated from the reshaping of ancient Chinese pictograms. This conceptual shift was  already explored extensively by Luo Qi in his earlier \"Love Writing\" series (Qingshu 情书, 1990-2002) and \"Bird\" and \"Fish\" series (Niao yu yu xilie “鸟”与\"鱼”系列, 2005–2009).  Eyes, hearts, fish, birds, stylized human and animal figures, flags, overflowing vessels, dwellings, arrows, triangle roofs, and crosses populate the composition. Together, they form a vivid kaleidoscope of imagined and yet-to-be-imagined narratives, immersed in a dynamic and pulsating visual rhythm. The arrangement of signs is free and fluid, while the palette alternates between red and black, generating a continuous rhythm punctuated by accents and pauses. The filling in of parts of the characters with red colour not only deepens the three-dimensional feeling of the writing but also adds to its pictorial quality. In fact, the artist draws inspiration particularly from pictograms, that are simple images representing what they signify. These signs demonstrate the artist’s engagement with ancient Chinese writing practices, and in particular with the use of oracle bone script. There are large characters, interspersed with smaller ones, reminiscent of the arrangement in manuscripts and old printed books , where the main content of the text is written in big characters and the commentary inserted in smaller script. The characters are organized in columns, reading from right to left and from top to bottom, like a classical piece of calligraphy. However, in some cases, characters are enclosed by a big rectangular frame that resembles an ancient seal. The characters within this frame are a prominent feature within the overall composition organized in vertical columns. Curved lines makes the shapes appear more welcoming, evoking the fluid lines found in Miró’s visual language. " ;
