    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2146.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """"Writing Landscapes - Drafting from Extensive Research on Ancient Model Scrolls" is one of the works included in "Writing Landscapes" (Shu shanshui 书山水), an ongoing series started in 2015 by Chu Chu. In this work, Chu Chu reinterprets the visual structure of traditional Chinese landscape painting (shanshui 山水) by transforming calligraphic brushstrokes and Chinese characters into a landscape configuration.  \r
The overall composition forms mountains, ridges, and flowing streams. It draws inspiration from "Precipitous Peak" (Sou jin qi feng da caogao 搜尽奇峰打草稿, literally "Searching All the Strange Mountains for Sketches"), a landscape painting by Shi Tao 石涛 (1642 – 1707).  The textual content of the characters used to generate the landscape derives from historical collections of calligraphic rubbings and model works known as 'model calligraphy from famous steles and rubbings of successive dynasties' (lidai ming bei tie 历代名碑帖). These textual sources are rendered through a range of scripts, from seal script to cursive. The composition therefore integrates multiple calligraphic styles and techniques within a single pictorial surface.""" ;
    ns1:hasArtworkType <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q191163>,
