<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2298> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q140712>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q30340> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Ecological Trilogy (生态作品三部曲)"@en,
        "生态作品三部曲"@zh,
        "Shēngtài zuòpǐn sānbùqǔ"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/788> ;
    ns2:P580 "2007"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2298_1.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2298_2.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """ Ecological Trilogy is an uncompleted series by Wang Nanming conceived between the late 1990s and 2009. It includes  "Water of Lake Tai" (Taihu shui 太湖水, 2007), "Rubbing Drought" (Ta yin ganhan 拓印干旱, 2009) and the unrealized “Sandstorm" (Shachenbao 沙尘暴, unrealized). Through this trilogy, Wang transforms environmental traces into visual artistic expression,  creating a visual testimony of China’s ecological crises.\r
"Water of Lake Tai" records water pollution at Lake Tai, while “Rubbing Drought" captures cracks in the dried  Xiliu Lake in Zhengzhou through traditional tayin rubbing.\r
"Sandstorm“ was conceived as a work in which an actual sandstorm would blow onto linen coated with glue, thereby fixing the sandstorm's physical traces onto the surface. Despite several attempts, this project remains incomplete, as the artist’s location in Shanghai and time constraints prevented him from working directly in regions affected by sandstorms. Wang still intends to realize it in situ, for example in Dunhuang (artist interview 2025).\r
Works from the series are held in the British Museum, Annie Wong Art Foundation, and National Art Museum of China.""" ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/2298_1_HR.JPG>,
