    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q217681> ;
    ns2:P585 "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/277> ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1453> ;
    ns1:description """This performance is held by Shao Yan in 2017  at the "Taishan Large-Scale Painting and Calligraphy Art Expo" - Shangdong province (泰山大型书画艺术品博览会). The artist uses several syringes full of ink injecting it to a long sheet of paper. This new technique is called Shemo 射墨 (Ink Shooting).\r
The video of this performance begins circulating on internet in 2018, filming Shao Yan during his calligraphic performance.  Helped by several assistants who held a long sheet of paper, the artist squirts streams of ink with four syringes by means of sudden movements, being acclamed by the astonished spectators. In a short time, the video becomes widely known and triggers different reactions: there are those who appreciate this innovative technique and those who do not like the novelty, wondering if it is still calligraphy or not. Shortly afterwards, the artist has been interviewed in his studio in Songzhuang, a district of Beijing, by Liu Pai 刘湃, a correspondent of the "China News", who wanted to know more about his art and about his opinion on the criticism he had received since the video was released. He emphasized the artist’s mission as one of “visually guiding the viewer,” asserting that even traditional cursive (caoshu) often defies immediate legibility. His work, he argues, is simply the next logical step: “If you don’t write Chinese characters, you have to go beyond writing Chinese characters. That’s what I’ve done” (Liu 2018). He proposed terms like Shao Yan’s Calligraphy (Shao Yan de shufa 邵岩的书法) or Shao Yan’s Ink Shooting (Shao Yan de shemo 邵岩的射墨), embracing the idea that this new technique stands as a personal and valid evolution of the medium.\r
By transforming the calligrapher’s gesture into a pure act of kinetic inscription, Shao Yan pushes the boundaries of what calligraphy can be. The resulting image is less about the legible character and more about the embodied energy, intention, and performative mark-making.\r
Since 2017, Shao Yan continues executing these performances in several  Chinese cities, animating the debate about the real nature of calligraphy. """@en ;
