<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/476> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q148> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Free Cloud Calligraphy Series (云自在书系列)"@en,
        "云自在书系列"@zh,
        "Yún zìzài shū xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/100>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/474> ;
    ns2:P580 "2020-06-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2021-05-29"^^xsd:date ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/476.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """This series goes from June 6, 2020 to May 29, 2021. It initially takes place inside the studios. In a second phase, the performances are made public during some exhibitions in Hangzhou and Shanghai and in some open spaces (beaches and other natural spaces). \r
Zhang Qiang creates the so-called "Blinded Calligraphy" (mang shu 盲书): looking away, the artist holds up an ink-soaked brush, creating traces of ink while the female counterpart Ding Feifei 丁斐斐 moves a sheet of paper or silk over his head, as if to simulate the movement of clouds in the sky. However, as Ding Feifei points out, Yunshu 云书 is a work that Ding Feifei conceived and created together with Zhang Qiang, while Mang shu 盲书 is a work that Zhang Qiang conceived independently, inviting women to work with him. In fact, all materials and techniques used are chosen by Ding Feifei, who thus becomes a full-fledged collaborator and creator of the experimentation.\r
The performances have a duration that varies from 10 minutes to half an hour (excluding the preparatory work). During the performances inside the studios, relaxing background music is usually played. The music consists on various tracks collected in NetEase Cloud Music (Wangyi yun yinyue 网易云音乐), a freemium music streaming service famous in China. From these performances a total of 100 works on paper or silk are created.\r
Different types of paper or silk are used, e.g. goose skin paper (yanpi zhi 雁皮纸), alum silk (fan juan 矾绢), Geng silk (geng zhuan耿绢) or artificial Japanese silk (Riben rengong juan 日本人工绢); different types of brush are also used, such as: zha bi 楂笔, dou bi 斗笔, baiyun bi 白云笔, yi wen bi 衣纹笔, ye jin bi 叶筋笔 and xie jing bi 写经笔; different types of ink such as  jiao mo 焦墨, ku mo 枯墨, se mo 涩墨, su mo 宿墨, zhong mo 重墨, zhong mo 中墨, dan mo 淡墨; the movements of the female counterpart Ding Feifei may also be different: throwing (pao 抛), tossing (shuai 甩), pulling (che 扯), shaking (doudong 抖动), etc.; the artworks realized may vary depending on the humidity present in the places of execution and the climatic weather (rainy, sunny or cloudy). """ ;
