    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/615_1.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/615_2.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/615_3.jpg> ;
    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/585> ;
    ns1:description """This performance took place in 2022 at the TJ Boulting Gallery of London during "Love, Celebration and the Road Ahead", a three-day festival (September, 29 - October, 1st ) to mark the launch of Hettie Judah's book entitled "How Not To Exclude Artist Mothers (and other parents)". In this book Hettie Judah explains that for too long, artists have been told that they can't have both motherhood and a successful career. In this polemical volume, critic and campaigner Hettie Judah argues that a paradigm shift is needed within the art world to take account of the needs of artist mothers (and other parents: artist fathers, parents who don't identify with the term "mother", and parents in other sectors of the art world). For this occasion, therefore, the artist Echo Morgan decides to paint her face in white with the Chinese character "ma 妈" (mother). The character "ma 妈" is written in blue/green ink, as well as her clothes are drenched in the same ink. The artist then wraps white lanterns with various writings around her body, combining Chinese characters with the English language, such as: "ziyou 自由" (freedom); "hai 海" (sea); "hao 好" (good); "popo 婆婆" (grandmother); "shengming 生命" (life); "tudi 土地" (soil); "tielian 铁链" (iron chain); "Can be a good mum and a good artist?"; "The Chinese word mum is combined woman 女  + horse 马"; "Flying dress"; "Mothers are the best in the world if you have one you are precious"; "Yellow face wife", "Sea Big Ocean like Mother Care", "Don’t get greedy. Only harvest for as long as you can hold your breath", etc. At some point, the surrounding space becomes dark and the lanterns light up with a yellow light.\r
Echo Morgan in her performances often uses Chinese characters or their decomposition to send messages related to the female world, social denunciation or to talk about her history, her family, as well as reflections on nature and climate change, such as:  "妇 (Woman) = 女 (Female) + 扫 (Broom), 2016; "Delete" (Shan 删), 2017 ; "Absent" (Quexi 缺席), 2017; "You Have My Blood in You (Ni shenshang liu zhe wo de xue 你身上留着我的血), 2017; "Demolition" (Chai 拆), 2018; "Sea" (Hai 海), 2019; "Human" (Ren 人), 2019; "Disaster (Zai 灾), 2020; "Love Wild Lawn" (Ai Ye Cao 爱野草), 2021."""@en ;
