    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/627_1.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/627_2.jpg> ;
    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/629> ;
    ns1:description """This performance took place during the "Festival Perform" of Vertheuil-en-Médoc (France) in August 19, 2022. This is the first official performance by Echo Morgan with her sons, who wear t-shirts with the images of "Tao" and "Zen". Face and body of the artist and her children are written with fluorescent paint protest words in French and Chinese, such as: "droit des femmes"; "pouvoir"; "égalité"; "droits des humains"; "droit queer"; "liberté"; "ren quan 人权" (human rights); "nü quan 女权" (women rights); "dongwu quan 动物权" (animal rights); "zhenxiang 真相" (real situation), etc. The Chinese character "quan 权" (power, right, authority) is composed of two parts: "mu 木" (wood) and "you 又" (again/repetition). It is a powerful and symbolic gesture calling for the balance between man made world and the nature. During this twenty minutes live performance, Echo Morgan with her sons, scomposed the two parts of the character  "quan 权". Echo Morgan composed the first part of the character "mu 木" (wood) by using local died plants, the giant reeds, and attaching her body on it; while her sons carry the other half of the character "you 又" (again/repetition). During the performance Echo Morgan and her two sons start to run, screaming sentences of protest and shouting about human rights, women's rights, animal rights, LGBT rights, etc. All this is accompanied by background voices broadcasting protest news from all over the world. At one point in the performance, Echo Morgan is hit by a jet of water, thus ending her run and starting to break the "mu 木" character she had built, freeing herself from the giant reed. As the artist herself explained, "using locally-sourced giant reed, I created a sculpture in the shape of this character to convey its profound meaning. Throughout the performance, an audio recording played in the background, recounting the history of the word 权 and blending in protest news from around the world calling for various rights such as human, women's, queer, and animal rights. This added social and emotional context to our actions, as we carried the weight of these important issues on our shoulders while running with the sculpture. Towards the end of the performance, I was symbolically attacked with water, representing the brutal treatment that many activists have faced over the years and across the globe. Through this powerful visual and auditory experience, I aimed to highlight the complex and interconnected nature of power, authority, and rights, urging the audience to reflect and take action. The wood-shaped sculpture symbolised the weight of these issues, while our running embodied the urgent need to address them in our society today. Overall, "权 - Rights, Power, and Authority" was a call for a more equitable and just society, using art as a powerful means of expression and activism.\r
This performance  conveys two main meanings:\r
1) The artist believes that art should be eco-sustainable. As Echo Morgan herself explains, one life changing her experience was meeting her childhood hero Betsy Damon. As one of the pioneer 1970s eco feminist warrior, Betsy made a promise to herself after a moving land art action - cast two hundred and fifty feet of a dry riverbed with handmade paper. She wrote: “The whole world is patterned by water, yet I know nothing about it.” At that moment, she decided that she will devote her whole life to learn and to protect water. She will only teach art that’s activism and make art that will dissolve in the eco system.\r
2) The artist speaks about human rights. After the pandemic, a brutal war began in Ukraine, as well as a bloody crackdown on protests in Iran. The artist reflects on all the people who are still struggling to survive, to have love and freedom. She's answering questions like: "How can art be the healing power for the society now?". """@en ;
