    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/698> ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/710> ;
    ns1:description "In this performance, Li Xinmo writes the content of the Fahua Sutra. She spent about a week before the performance to complete a piece of the text. The Swedish Museum invited her to participate in the exhibition \"The Story of Paper\" and at the opening of the exhibition she goes on to finish the rest of the text during the performance. The 30-meters long volume of the Fahua Sutra in her handwriting hanged in the museum space, and she began to write in an ancient ceremony. Ancient people who copied scriptures were called monks and men. She wants to write Scriptures as a replacement for woman, giving rise to a feminist perspective. In her opinion, Buddha is a symbol of the equality of all living beings, but also transcends gender. Reading and writing Buddhist sutras was her early lesson. The interpretation of the universe and everything in the sutras had an important influence on her thoughts. In her opinion, to become a Buddha is to transcend the existing social shaping, knowledge system and integrate with all things, thus connecting the energy field of the universe."@en ;
