        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q956> ;
    ns1:description """Huang Miaozi (1913-2012) is a towering figure in the Chinese art scene. His art blends old and new. Growing up in a Cantonese family in Hong Kong, at the time a British colony. He felt that he lightened the burden of being a British 'subject', by studying Chinese culture. He practiced calligraphy since childhood.\r
With the Japanese attack on China in 1932, the eighteen-year-old Huang decided to rush to Shanghai to enlist. After the Chinese government's decision to retaliate the attack, Huang remained in Shanghai, which he saw as the most thriving and cosmopolitan city in Asia, where the arts were also flourishing. Huang worked as a cartoonist and met  Yu Feng, his future wife, at the Cartoonists' Club.\r
As Japanese attacks intensified, Huang Miaozi followed the shift of the capital to Chongqing, which became China's vibrant cultural center and home to the leading artists and scholars of the time, including Guo Moruo. Besides these scholars, Huang met Zhou Enlai who took a liking to Huang fr his liberal views and sense of humour.\r
In the late 1940s Huang returned to Hong Kong. In 1949, shortly after the Communists had secured control of the mainland, he accepted their invitation to return to Beijing. For a while he worked in the new secretariat of the government headed by Zhou Enlai, but soon the opposition to the\r
the employment of non-party members in sensitive posts led him to leave government service and take up an editorial job at the People's Fine Arts Publishing House.\r
After the "Hundred Flowers Movement" of 1956-7, an anti-Rightists campaign put Huang in a difficult position. The Chongqing group of artists and writers was claimed to include nationalist spies and to plot against the communists. Labeled a "Rightist," Huang was despatched to northeastern China for "reform through labor." Huang was sent back to Beijing after a few months, probably thanks to a personal intervention by Zhon Enlai.\r
In Beijing, he wrote artist biographies at the Fine Arts Publishing House. His wife Yu Feng became the director of exhibitions at the newly opened China Art Gallery.The pleasures of the 60s were brief. Within a few years Huang and his wife had fallen victim of the Cultural Revolution. Both were imprisoned in 1968. The years of confinement turned Huang into a calligrapher. He tried to maintain his sanity by composing poems, paintings and calligraphy in his mind.\r
After seven years in prison, the couple was released in 1975 and  returned home. Huang, now sixty-two, decided to retire.\r
In the years of retirement, Huang was inspired by copies of calligraphic masters and worked in his own style for the first time. He used different styles to express his reflections on life and recent event, to enhance the power of each message: archaic characters for calm and reflective moods, powerful cursive characters to convey joy or anger.\r
Under the new climate of optimism with Deng Xiaoping's reforms, Huang was offered a spacious apartment in Beijing. In 1983, Huang was made a member of the Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference (CPPCC) along with other scholars and artists who had suffered during the Cultural Revolution. These benefits included visits to Japan as well.\r
Huang was artistically attracted to Japan, where calligraphy had already been shaped into modern forms. To him it seemed almost like abstract art created with Chinese ink and encompassed ideas and research that he was already exploring. But the more he saw of Japanese art, the more he felt it lacked both the strength and depth of content of its Chinese counterpart.\r
Huang's participation in the controversial Modernist Calligraphy Exhibition held in Beijing in 1985 was\r
a more important factor in the further development of his art. "What made Huang's work stand out from many other exhibitions was the lyricism of his characters and the interrelationships between them. He brought a vitality to the ancient characters of seal script by writing them with speed and fluency. Furthermore, he made his works more like paintings by breaking with the tradition of writing characters in regular columns and instead arranging them on paper in freer artistic arrangements (Barrass)."\r
\r
In 1988 Huang published his first calligraphy book. It contained more than seventy of his works, showing his mastery of the art of calligraphy, which has allowed him to create intriguing effects through the use of different ink densities and varying degrees of brush dryness. Equally important was his ability to combine an exploration of new forms of expression with a deep understanding of Chinese poetry and culture, often marked by a satirical edge. Not only did Huang demonstrated great skill in selecting appropriate quotations from his prodigious knowledge of the classics, but he also wrote poetry in the classical tradition. \r
In the late 1980s Huang found himself involved in politics. A few days after the oppression in Tiananmen Square at the end of May 1989, he spoke out strongly in support of the protesters and for this, following the suppression of the demonstrations, Huang and Yu Feng, resentful of being forced to leave the country, fled to Australia.\r
Whilst waiting for an opportunity to return home, Huang traveled to Germany in 1992, where he met his young friend Gu Gan. The two arstis were boh visiting fellows at the Heinrich Böll Institute.\r
At the end of that year, an exhibition of their works (Huang Miaozi - Gu Gan) was held at the Museum für Ostasiatische Kunst in Cologne. It was the first major modern Chinese calligraphy exhibition held in Europe or the United States. Huang's most memorable piece from this exhibition was  Mit diesen Händen ("With These Hands").\r
After Germany, Huang and Yu Feng traveled to Great Britain, Italy, France, Switzerland, the United States and South Korea. Finally, they settled in Brisbane in 1993. Huang and Yu Feng  appreciated the quality of life Australia offered and had no difficulty keeping abreast of the latest developments in China. \r
But they still hoped that the improved political climate at home would allow them to return one day. In 1993, they received a message from the Chinese authorities in which they hoped the couple would return to China. Returning in 1994, Huang and Yu Feng staged an exhibition in Beijing covering the full range of their works.\r
The exhibits of Huang's calligraphy included The Twittering Birds, The Flower in the Rough Ground, With these Hands, Great Changes, The Rain-filled Clouds, and The Gentlemanly Scholar, which together presented a clear statement of his views. The opening of the exhibition was a great event, which was attended by the major artists and art historians of the city.\r
The interest that Huang's work arouses and the pleasure it brings to so many people is attributable not only to the versatility of his technique, but also to his vast knowledge of the Chinese classics, his profound experience of life and politics, and of course his wicked sense of humor. His characteristic determination to make the best of things was heightened by the tragedies and vicissitudes of his own life. As some Chinese critics have observed, the enduring quality of his work and its "spiritual power" (ling qi 靈氣) is the most appropriate hallmark for a man than Huang's unwavering integrity."""@en ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Familial"@en,
        "Self-Taught"@en ;
