    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/775.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """This artwork includes ten monumental characters in cursive style displayed on four huge panels. The content is a transcription of a poem by Ruan Ji (210-263) on the grandeur of the Chinese panorama seen from the sky. \r
The artwork was exhibited for the first time in August 1987 at the National Art Museum of China in Beijing during his first personal exhibition entitled "Exhibition of Wang Dongling's Calligraphy" (Wang Dongling shufazhan 王冬龄书法展). This artwork was later featured in the "China: 5,000 Years, Innovation and Transformation in the Arts" exhibition opened at the Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum in 1998.\r
As described in Barrass (2002, p. 166), "Wang would have liked to paint this piece to the sound of Chinese music. but he felt it demanded something more stirring. He finally decided on Dvořák's New World Symphony, playing it at full volume on his recently acquired 'ghetto-blaster' as he took up his huge brush and plunged into the creation of his composition. Wang was amused by the thought that music composed by a European about the American landscape was inspiring a Chinese artist (who at that point had never travelled outside his own country) to create the largest piece of calligraphy ever executed in China." \r
In the visual result given by the ten monumental characters of View from Heaven, and in particular in the last character of the verse (dai 带), which is projected onto the entire size of the last panel, we notice the influence by one of the currents of Japanese calligraphic art, called Few-characters current (shao zi pai 少字派) and in particular by Inoue Yuichi (井上有一, 1916-1985) on the art of Wang Dongling, who began writing monumental characters in his studio in 1985.\r
"""@en ;
