    ns1:description """"Share the Peripateticism: Calligraphy Exhibition by Wang Dongling" was Wang Dongling's third solo exhibition at the National Art Museum of China (NAMOC). The exhibition opened on December 3, 2007, in Halls 1 and 8 of the museum. It was distinguished by two key aspects. First, the calligraphic works drew inspiration from great Chinese classical texts, including The Book of Changes, The Analects of Confucius, Dao De Jing (Laozi), Zhuangzi, and Tan Jing (坛经), as well as The Book of Songs, Li Sao, Tang poetry, and Song lyrics. Second, the exhibition integrated modern artistic concepts to convey the profound spirit of traditional Chinese calligraphy.\r
Hosted by the National Art Museum of China, the exhibition was organized with support from the Publicity Department of the CPC Zhejiang Provincial Committee, the Association of Chinese Calligraphers, the Zhejiang Federation of Literature and Art Circles, and the China Academy of Art. The exhibition was curated by Gao Shiming (高士明), with Xu Jiang (许江) as artistic director and Fan Di’an (范迪安) as academic director.\r
\r
"""@en ;
    ns1:exhibitedArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/920> .

<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/925> a ns3:Q7725634 ;
    ns2:P1476 "Diagram of the Battle Formation of the Brush (笔阵图) "@en,
        "笔阵图"@zh,
        "Bǐ zhèn tú"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P50 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2994742> ;
    ns1:description "Wei Shuo 卫铄 (272–349), later renowned as Madame Wei (Wei furen 卫夫人), is traditionally credited as the author of the treatise \"Diagram of the Battle Formation of the Brush\" (Bi zhen tu 笔阵图), In this work, Madame Wei studied the theory of flow and the principles of calligraphy, emphasizing the importance of the brush, ink, and inkstone for grinding and preparing the ink. She also illustrated seven methods of holding the brush and their effects. This treatise outlines her views on what constitutes good handwriting and is among the earliest works to analyze the essence of brushworks and brush strokes."@en .

