    ns2:P585 "2011-10-31"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P941 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1994> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/941.jpg> ;
    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/748> ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1788> ;
    ns1:description """This performance by Wang Dongling was held the 31st of October, 2011 at the Zhejiang Art Museum of Hangzhou. Accompanied by classical music, seven male and female dancers dressed in white dance gracefully to outline vigorous and powerful calligraphic characters, while Wang Dongling shapes large Chinese characters on a huge white scroll. The choreographies were created by the female choreographer Cui Wei 崔巍 (b. 1966).  The content of the calligraphy realized by Wang Dongling is one of the two poems entitled “Drinking on West Lake First Shine After the Rain” (Yin hu shang chu qing hou yu 饮湖上初晴后雨) by Su Shi 苏轼 (1037-1101, better known as Su Dongpo 苏东坡), an eminent poet of the Song dynasty (960-1279). The poem describes the beauty of West Lake in Hangzhou, China, after a rain. It evokes vivid images of nature, highlighting the scenic beauty of the lake and its surroundings and reflecting on the interaction between man and nature. Characters are written in cursive script (cao shu) and praise the beauty of West Lake (Xihu 西湖).  In the background, three large-scale calligraphic works previously created by Wang Dongling himself are also hung on the walls: “Wandering Beyond: III” (San xie ‘Xiao yao you’ zhi ben shuimo 三写 ‘逍遥游’ 纸本水墨, 2011); “Big Scale of the Heart Sutra” (Ju fu dacao ‘Xinjing’ 巨幅大草 ‘心经’, 2011) and “Impression After Visiting Pyramids in Egypt” (Guan aiji jinzita ganhuai zhi ben shuimo 观埃及金字塔感怀 纸本水墨, 2011). In this performance, the dialogue between the three arts (calligraphy, music and dance) is very rich:\r
1. Calligraphy created by Wang Dongling during the performance, dialogues with the other calligraphies already realized previously, creating a “traditional” calligraphic fusion between all of them in a contemporary context.\r
2. Traditional Chinese music accompanies the bodies of the dancers who perform their movements using contemporary dance techniques, while Wang Dongling performs a traditional but still contemporary calligraphic work.\r
3. Dancers dressed in white, like flying paper, dialogue with the black of the brush and the ink of Wang Dongling’s calligraphy following the rhythm of music.\r
"""@en ;
