    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/592> ;
    ns1:description """This theatrical performance took place during the Chinese New Year of 2011 at the Teatro dal Verme in Milan. The actors are the calligrapher Silvio Ferragina, along with contemporary dance choreographer Olivia Kwong accompanied by the sound of the flute played by musician and composer Alessandro Cerino. The performance consists of two different moments: 1) initially, Silvio and Olivia Kwong perform dance steps while both hold a large brush dipped in black ink. 2) In the second part, Silvio begins to write calligraphy on sheets hung on a tripod (fig. 378) creating the characters “Qianli xin xing shi yu zuxia 千里心行始于足下” (A Journey of a Thousand Miles Begins with a Single Step), which will later be hung on a wire at the back of the stage. Olivia’s movements mirror those of Silvio as he writes the characters, synchronizing with the rhythm of the notes played by Cerino. Together, the three create a harmonious combination of writing, music, and dance. Olivia wears a dress reminiscent of that used in the traditional Chinese jinghong dance (jinghong wu 惊鸿舞), characterized by “water sleeves,” or long flowing sleeves that evoke the movement of the brush. Cerino, with his flute, plays a melody that he describes as “fragmented”, characterized by a series of simple notes that are repeated at an increasingly faster tempo. The melody begins with a slow rhythm and evolves into a quicker pace, creating a sense of acceleration and intensification. The notes, varying in rhythm and intensity, can be combined in different ways, contributing to a dynamic and engaging atmosphere.  The calligraphic-musical composition, accompanied by Olivia’s dancing movements, is rooted in a “significant” aspect of the characters that form a proverb rich in Chinese cultural connotations. This proverb not only conveys a universal concept of progress and initiative but also carries a strong connection to traditional Chinese philosophy and wisdom.\r
"""@en ;
