    ns2:P180 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q622868> ;
    ns2:P186 ns3:Q11472,
        ns3:Q127418 ;
    ns2:P2047 "PT30M"^^xsd:duration ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4970> ;
    ns2:P585 "2016-10-20"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P608 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1781> ;
    ns2:P941 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/729> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1249.jpg> ;
    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/592> ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1256> ;
    ns1:description """This musicalligraphy performance took place in 2016, in occasion of the 12th exhibition on calligraphyism (Di 12 jie shufa zhuyi zhan第12届书法主义展).  The performance was realized under the direction of the Italian master calligrapher Silvio Ferragina and the Italian musician and composer Alessandro Cerino at the Zhejiang Conservatory of Music (Zhejiang yinyue xueyuan tushu guan 浙江音乐学院图书馆) on October 20. Silvio Ferragina uses the method of the musicalligraphy project to translate into music the four lines of the “Heart Sutra”, specifically “Se bu yi kong, kong bu yi se, se ji shi kong, kong ji shi se色不异空，空不异色，色即是空，空即是色” (Form is not different from emptiness, emptiness is not different from form; form is precisely this emptiness, emptiness is precisely this form). This translation then was performed during the performance by Alessandro Cerino, who was inspired by the calligraphy realized by Silvio.  These verses represent the fundamental essence of the Heart Sutra. The same characters, rendered in kaishu by Silvio Ferragina during the performance and written from top to bottom and from right to left, engage in a dialogue with the work title “Heart Sutra” that he created in 2016 in Milan to serve as a backdrop during the performance. This monumental piece, measuring 70x140 cm, consists of 10 vertical panels made with black ink on rice paper, featuring all 268 characters of the Heart Sutra written in zhuangshu. Looking at the work from afar in its entirety, one can notice how Silvio emphasizes the four large characters “se bu yi kong 色不异空” (form is not different from emptiness). While Cerino played his music on the contrabass flute, Silvio, inspired by the musical notes derived from calligraphy, engaged in a calligraphic performance during which he transcribed the same 16 characters of the chosen sutra. The musical dialogue is also created with the Chinese instruments positioned in the room, such as the drums that appear around the audience. \r
\r
The 23th of October, another similar event took place at the Liangzhu Center of Arts (Liang zhu wenhua yishu zhongxin良渚文化艺术中心), a magnificent art museum designed by the renowned Japanese architect Tadao Ando (b. 1941). In this second musicalligraphy performance, Silvio calligraphed only eight of the non-simplified characters of the Heart Sutra “Se bu yi kong, kong bu yi se色不异空，空不异色” (Form is not different from emptiness, emptiness is not different from form) also realized in kaishu script and written from right to left while Cerino plays the musical notes derived from the Chinese characters previously extracted from the “musicalligraphy project” system created by Silvio.\r
"""@en ;
    ns1:hasPerformingArtType <https://write-dataset.github.io/vocabs/T1> ;
    ns1:hasUnit <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1254> ;
