    ns1:description """"Women's Writing: A Blank Page" (Nüshu: Kongbai zhi ye 女书——空白之页) is an installation realized by Cai Mengxia in 1997 . It features four wooden panels linked with small rods, each displaying a different script:\r
The first panel (top right) features Nǚshū, an exclusive script from the Jiangyong area in Hunan Province, China, known for its use as a secretive form of communication among women. This script, rediscovered in recent years, serves as a poignant reminder of the timeless significance of women's voices in history. Adjacent to this, the second panel (top left) displays Japanese Kana, specifically the syllabic characters used historically by women in Japan, reflecting a similar theme of feminine linguistic expression. On the third panel (bottom right), Korean Hangul script is presented on white silk, adhering to ancient cultural preferences for white in Korean tradition. This choice of script and material highlights the historical context in which Korean women used Hangul as a mode of literary and personal expression. The fourth and final panel (bottom left) is deliberately left blank except for a single seal, symbolizing the potential for future narratives and the silent yet potent presence of women throughout history. This blank space invites viewers to consider the unspoken and unwritten stories of women, echoing the artist’s conceptual exploration of silence and potentiality in feminine expression.\r
Materials are thoughtfully chosen to enhance the cultural narrative of each script: yellow silk for the Japanese Kana reflects the ancient tradition of colored paper in Japan, coarse hemp for the Hunan Nǚshū underscores its rustic and folk origins, and white silk for the Korean Hangul respects historical aesthetic preferences (Chen Zhenlian 1998, 46).\r
This installation not only showcases the artist's deep understanding and respect for historical calligraphy forms used by women but also challenges viewers to reflect on the role of women in cultural heritage and the continuing evolution of their expressive forms. The artwork stands as a testament to the artist's commitment to exploring complex themes of gender, culture, and history through the nuanced lens of calligraphy.  It explores themes of "women's writing" and general perceptions of writing. The texts, presented in scripts unfamiliar to its intended Chinese audience, symbolize the exclusion from dominant linguistic codes. This sequence moves from a localized gender-specific script to other related scripts, culminating in a blank page that invites endless interpretation.\r
In 1995, Cai Mengxia also created another installation titled "Women's Script (Nüshu) - Classics" (Nǚ shū·jīngdiǎn 女书·经典), which further explores these themes.\r
This piece was exhibited at the graduation exhibition of the Chinese Academy of Art’s calligraphy undergraduate program and at the inaugural exhibition of the "School of Academics" held in Korea in 1997.""" ;
