    ns1:description """This performance realized by Echo Morgan took place on October 29, 2023,  at the International Museum and Library of Music in Bologna, as part of the collateral events related to the exhibition “Sounds of Ink: Luo Qi and 30 Years of Calligraphyism” (October 12 - November 5, 3023).  This performance represents a pivotal moment in the artistic practice of Echo Morgan, bringing together calligraphy, music, ecology, and ecofeminism within a transcultural and intergenerational dialogue.\r
Collaborating with the local guzheng female musician Zhang Yaqin 张雅钦 (b. 2000), the two performers created a new eco-feminist work. Initially, Echo spent almost two hours painting her body before the performance, shaping five different flowers, each symbolizing one of the five gardens demolished during the “Return Forests to Agriculture Movement” (RFFP) in May 2023  and writing the four characters “Shan he gu ren 山河故人” on her face.  These characters are the original title of Jia Zhangke’s 2015 film, internationally known as "Mountains May Depart". It is a drama exploring memory, affection, and the social transformations of contemporary China. Set across three distinct periods, the film follows the 25-year-old shopkeeper Tao and the choices that separate her from both the men in her life and her son, Daole/Dollar. Through the growing geographical and emotional distance between mother and son, Jia reflects on globalization, cultural displacement, and the fragility of family bonds.\r
Literally, the phrase "Shan he gu ren 山河故人" can be read as “mountains, rivers, and old friends”, evoking a sense of enduring landscapes set against the fragility of human relationships and time. Jia Zhangke’s choice of title reflects the film’s central concern with change, memory, and the passage of generations: the permanence of nature contrasts with the impermanence of human bonds and personal destinies. Echo inscribed these characters on her face by following the same iconographic style as the film’s official poster, thereby creating a visual and symbolic bridge between cinema and performance art. By appropriating Jia Zhangke’s cinematic language and re-situating it within her own body art practice, she reactivates the poetic resonance of "Shan he gu ren" in a new context marked by ecological urgency. \r
This evocation of landscape also resonates with her long-standing dialogue with classical Chinese poetry. As she explains, Echo has often found solace in the poetry of Su Shi (1037–1101), the poet-official repeatedly exiled during the Song dynasty, whose retreat into nature produced writings of enduring wisdom and healing power. For Echo, his voice becomes a refuge when reality is overwhelming and emotion resists articulation.\r
The music, composed and performed by Zhang Yaqin in complete improvisation, interwove an homage to Maddalena Casulana (1544–1599), the first female composer to publish music under her own name. This choice situates the performance within a genealogy of women’s creativity and underscores its ecofeminist orientation: a philosophy that connects environmental crisis with patriarchal systems of oppression, proposing instead forms of resistance that unite ecological and gender justice. Echo’s decision to engage with this female composer was also inspired by the fact that the museum preserves some of her original musical scores.\r
As the performance unfolded, Echo continued to paint plants and flowers across her body while a video she produced was projected in the background, recounting stories of displacement and ecological trauma. These narratives referenced the urban and environmental transformations of Sichuan: from the Tian Fu Lawn project of 1997, to the mass migration triggered by the Three Gorges Dam, to the more recent demolition of the San Sheng Flower Village. Through this layering of memory and storytelling, personal biography converged with collective histories of dislocation and loss.\r
At the end of the performance, Echo extended an invitation to the audience to contribute to the performance by writing in various languages on her body paint, as well as on the long cloth scroll where she had walked. Her son Tao added a smiling face, and the other son Zen writes “SAVE THE WORLD – NO MORE WAR” accompanied with a symbol of peace, joining in the creative action. People wrote words, such as: “SPERANZA” and “FIORE” (“hope” and “flower” in Italian); “paqe” (“peace”, in Albanian); “amour” (“love”, in French) or “Jia you 加油” (“come on”, in Chinese). \r
This live painting performance—described by Echo as a “film in action”—epitomizes her practice of "re-vision" in the Adrienne Rich's sense (Rich 1979): the feminist act of critically rereading and rewriting cultural traditions. In Echo’s case, this extends to Chinese writing and calligraphy, historically embedded within patriarchal structures. By inscribing, fragmenting, and recontextualizing characters on her own body, she transforms calligraphy into what has been described as an embodied female language. In this performance, painting, calligraphy, music, and collective participation merge into an aesthetic-political device that brings together personal memory, ecology, and the struggle against cultural oppression.\r
Ultimately, the performance reveals the power of art as a tool for testimony and transformation. On the one hand, it narrates the intimate and collective histories of a changing homeland; on the other, it opens a shared space of reflection, resilience, and hope, where ink and music converge as instruments of ecofeminist resistance."""@en ;
