    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1730.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """This artwork is one of the 12 paper folding books of the series "Writing Music - Silent Melodies" (写乐·无声之歌) realized by Luo Qi in 2018. It is inspired by the famous Italian opera “La Traviata” by Giuseppe Verdi,  one of Giuseppe Verdi's most famous operas, first performed in 1853. The story of "La Traviata" is based on the play "La Dame aux Camélias" by Alexandre Dumas, and it explores themes of love, sacrifice, and societal judgment. Luo Qi transcribes this famous Italian opera using the oldest Chinese musical notation system, the "Dunhuang Musical Score System" (Dunhuang qupu / yuepu 敦煌曲谱/乐谱). This system, dating back to the 10th century, consists of twenty symbols derived from ancient characters, radicals or character components/parts, traditionally used for notating the tablature for the pipa (Chinese lute). The artist reshapes these symbols according to his personal and original interpretation, in a way that the musical composition is transformed into a “visual and visible musical score,” strongly rhythmic and dynamic, with an inextricably calligraphic thread. In addition to the traditional black ink, the artist adds two other colors - green and brown, to create a dinamic coloristic rhythm to the whole composition. \r
This artwork was displayed for the first time during the exhibition "The Music of Ink - Calligraphies by Luo Qi and Silvio Ferragina in dialogue with the Chinese archive collection of the Braidense Library" held in 2018 at the National Braidense Library. It was also exhibited in other three exhibition: "The Music of Ink - Calligraphies by Luo Qi and Silvio Ferragina" (2018, Forlì Campus of the University of Bologna), "Luo Qi: Writing Music" (2019, University Library of Bologna), and "Sounds of Ink: Luo Qi and Thirty Years of Calligraphyism" (2023, International Museum of Music in Bologna). \r
""" ;
