    ns1:description "Wang Tiande's \"Ink Banquet\"(1996) is an installation that merges traditional artistic techniques with an unconventional medium. The work features a complete banquet setting—consisting of a round table, eight chairs, dishes, bottles, and brushes—entirely splashed with ink. Through the application of ink across these objects, Wang transforms functional items of daily life into an immersive, artistic experience. The ink-splashed surfaces evoke the aesthetics of traditional Chinese ink painting and calligraphy extending it into three-dimensional space. Measuring approximately 300 x 300 x 94 cm, the installation presents an evocative contrast between the rigid structure of furniture and the fluidity of ink, reflecting themes of transformation, cultural heritage, artistic experimentation and reinterpretation of familiar objects. His earlier two-dimensional works are transformed into everyday objects with unusual, hybrid forms. This installation was featured in the 1998 exhibition \"Inside Out: New Chinese Art\" (Xin Zhongguo yishu zhan - tuibian 新中国艺术展─蜕变) at Asia Society in New York and the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art, which focused on Chinese contemporary art." ;
