    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/196> ;
    ns1:description """In this hour-long performance, Song Dong is sitting cross-legged in a shallow portion of the Lhasa River in Tibet. He sistematically stamps the "sacred" river water with a large wood seal carved with the Chinese character for water (shui 水). Song Dong explores the impact of transcribed words that leave no semantic trace. According to the artist, the idea of this work came to him while attending an artists' symposium in Lhasa that examined the human impact on the natural world.  He learned from speaking with local residents of an annual ritual held on the fourth day of the fourth lunar month, when small statues of the Shakyamuni Buddha are immersed in the water. When the statue is in the water, the river bears the image of Buddha; when it is removed, the essence of Buddha remains. Song Dong adapted this idea by writing out a large regular-script version of the character shui 水 (water), and then having local craftsmen carve it into the wood. In his mind, stamping the river with this symbol, which was meant to represent the pure concept of water, became a way of ritually purifying it as it made its way from the Tibetan highlands to Beijing, where it arrives much polluted  owing to man's intrusions. In actuality the Lhasa River joins the Yarlung (or Brahmaputra) River, which flows into the Bay of Bengal, but in the artist's conception, it represented the head-waters of China's great rivers that, figurately at least, flowed to Beijing from whence political control flowed back to Tibet.\r
Song Dong carried out this performance twice: first, before a large audience; then, in front of a camera operated by his wife, the artist Yin Xiuzhen. To stage this second performance, Song and Yin rode bicycles to a spot about ten kilometers outside the center of Lhasa, where the camera would capture an image that included water, mountains and traces on man's presence. Song Dong stamped continuously for one hour, enough time to meditate on man's impact on the natural world, without knowing when Yin Xiuzhen would hold down the shutter release. Song Dong went on to explain that his posture, seated cross-legged in a shallow portion of the river, echoes that of the Buddha, while the replication of his image recalls the Thousand Buddhas motiv, a metaphor for the potential of all sentient beings to achieve Buddhahood. \r
This performance invokes Buddhist and Daoist concepts, which advocate harmonizing the self with natural principles. Song's intention may have been to link traditional religious practise with a greater awareness of and reverence for the natural environment. Thus, the act of stamping the Chinese word for "water" onto a Tibetan river, may be read as a response to China's efforts to assert its cultural hegemony over Tibet."""@en ;
