    ns2:P88 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q160236> ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2783918> ;
    ns1:description """"Art for the People for The Met" is a six-color lithograph and relief print composed of three components: the transcription of the phrase "Art for the people" using "Square Word Calligraphy", a system developed by Xu Bing that adapts the English alphabet to the structural forms of Chinese calligraphy. The phrase is also rendered in typewriter-like font. The third element consists of five red “seals” surrounding the English text. In traditional culture of Chinese  literati, seals functions as the artist's signature(s) and mark of authorship. In this work, however, they take the form of QR codes. Visitorns can scan them to access further  information on the website of the Metropolitan Museum of Art. The use of QR codes reflects on the role of digital technologies to identifies individuals in the age of the internet and social media. At the same time, it suggests the capacity of networked media to generate connections and amplifies the human shared knowledge. \r
This work was commissioned by the Metropolitan Museum of Art to celebrate its 150th Anniversary in 2020. The Met also acquired the first edition of the print portfolio.\r
Xu Bing used the phrase "Art for the people" in earlier works, in particular in 1999, when he wrote it in Square Word Calligraphy on a large banner placed at the entrance of the Museum of Modern Art in New York. The expression echoes one of Mao Zedong's central views on art, formulated in the famous "Talks at the Yan'an Conference on Literature and Art” (1942). At the same time, it reflects of the artist's personal conviction that the notion of art serving the people has a broader and universal relevance. In this sense, the work exemplifies how Xu Bing integrates his particular cultural background and life experience into the international context of contemporary art. The same banner was displayed again in 2001 at the entrance of the Victoria and Albert Museum in London.\r
 """ ;
