    ns2:P462 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23444>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q23445> ;
    ns2:P571 "2018-03-16"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P608 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1569>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/599> ;
    ns2:P941 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q207990>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q65050532> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2067.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """"Writing Music - Silent Melodies:  Turandot" is one of the 20 small square works in ink on paper of the series "Writing Music - Silent Melodies" realized by Luo Qi in 2018. In this work, he transcribes an aria from the Italian opera "Turandot" using the oldest Chinese musical notation system, the "Dunhuang Musical Score System" (Dunhuang qupu / yuepu 敦煌曲谱/乐谱).  "Turandot" is one of the Giacomo Puccini's most famous opera, first performed in 1926. The opera is set in China and follows the Prince Calaf, who falls in love with the cold-hearted Princess Turandot. The choice of this opera settled in China and composed by an Italian is another link between the two countries. The echo of the powerful Italian opera resonates in the sweet notes of the Chinese pipa. Just as happens for those who have not studied music and do not know how to “read” a pentagram, but can appreciate great musical compositions, so those who are not experts in ancient Chinese music will never know how to “read” these musical scores but can appreciate the intrinsic rhythm of the calligraphic signs. The implicit invitation to the viewer is to open your heart to listening to these “silent melodies", to let yourself be carried away and enveloped by the harmonic flow of this “musical calligraphy”.\r
This artwork was displayed for the first time during the exhibition "Luo Qi: Writing Music" held in 2019 at the University Library of Bologna, and then in 2023 during the exhibition "Sounds of Ink: Luo Qi and Thirty Years of Calligraphyism" held at the International Museum of Music in Bologna.""" ;
