    ns2:P571 "2013"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P608 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/2313> ;
    ns1:description """This dress was created by Vivienne Tam as part of the Fall-Winter 2013 "Ready-to-Wear" collection, presented at New York Fashion Week in 2013. At the center of the dress’s design appears the character "ai 愛" (love), written in non-simplified characters and symbolically placed at heart level. The choice of red for the “heart” radical (xin 心) aligns perfectly with traditional Chinese color symbolism, enhancing its visibility while the other components of the character "ai 愛" are rendered in white. Below it, a vertical sequence of Latin alphabet letters, also in white clearly spell out the word "CHANGE". The word is embedded into the garment, creating a visual tension between the two writing systems. Their coexistence is not merely decorative but establishes a true dialogue: Eastern calligraphy and Western typography reflect one another, contrast each other, and ultimately harmonize into a single visual syntax. \r
The dress contains a highly symbolic color palette — red, black, and white — that directly references the calligraphic tradition. Red, associated with vitality and good fortune, is the color of calligraphic seals; black is the color of ink; and white is the color of paper, and functions as neutral background and space for expression. These color are extended to the accessories as well, such as the black and red shoes, creating a formal continuity between the garment and the body. \r
Far from any superficial exoticism, Vivienne Tam’s poetics are grounded in a deep cultural negotiation, using writings — both Chinese characters and Latin letters— and languages— both Chinese and English, as media to explore the relationship between East and West. In this context, calligraphy becomes a generative matrix for design: not merely a visual trace of a culture, but a compositional and narrative principle capable of merging historical memory with aesthetic experimentation.""" ;
