    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/2589.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """This garment belongs to the iconic series of five garments designed by Polly Ho and Andy Wong, inspired by the calligraphic works of Tong Yang-tze. The collection was created on the occasion of the exhibition "From Ink To Apparel II" (Duyi 讀衣II), held in Taipei in 2017, which explored the relationship between contemporary fashion design and Chinese calligraphy.\r
Within this project, the garments seek to translate the fluidity and dynamism of calligraphic movement into a wearable form. \r
The garment is constructed through an ensemble of strips made from three different types of silk fabrics, whose silhouette derives from the expressive brushstrokes of the renowned calligrapher. These elements generate a sense of rhythm and motion that recalls the gestural quality of ink on paper. Enlarged prints of Tong Yang-tze’s calligraphic lines are applied to the strips, extending the visual language of the original works onto the textile surface.\r
Through this series, the designer investigates what she defines as the “complicated and strong” strokes and movements characteristic of Tong Yang-tze’s calligraphy, translating their expressive intensity into materiality, structure, and volume within the garment."""@en ;
