    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/100> ;
    ns1:description """This performance takes place in Zhang Qiang's studio of Chongqing  in May 5th, 2004. Zhang Qiang writes on the body of his female counterpart Chi Wu 赤乌. After being "traced" by Zhang Qiang, the performer Chi Wu (whose real name is Ma Yuanyuan 马媛媛) performs the "sword dance" (jianwu 剑舞), one of the four classical dances used in Chinese opera. While holding the sword, Chi Wu has two red Chinese knots tied to her wrist. Chi Wu's lips are also red, but painted only in the middle, not completely. The performance is accompanied by traditional Chinese music in the background, played with the guqin 古琴 and the guzhen 古筝, two typical Chinese musical instruments.\r
The sword dance originated from Gansu. It was popular in folk before the period of Emperess Wu 武 (624-705). Then it was introduced to Chang An and became the imperial dance. The sword dance was not only a famous dance, but also had great influence on Chinese poetry and calligraphy art. The poet Du Fu 杜甫 (712-770) for example, ever wrote famous poem related to the dances of the sword dance. It is also said that the calligrapher Zhang Xu 张旭 (fl. 8th century) was enlightened by the sword dance and form his own style. """@en ;
