    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/526> ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/528> ;
    ns1:description """This performance is held in Macau in 2021. The artist Wu Xixia 吴析夏 enters in an airtight inflatable sphere that symbolizes the uterus and begins to write inside the “Orchid Pavilion Preface” (Lanting Xu 兰亭序) composed by Wang Xizhi 王羲之 (303–361)  in 353. The Lanting Xu 兰亭序 is known as "the world's largest calligraphy performance" (tianxia di yi xing shu 天下第一行书). During the Wu Xixia writing process, the different forms of the twenty zhi 之 characters present in the poem, which have always been praised and imitated by all Chinese calligraphers, were replaced with visible red ink male phalluses. The zhi 之 character is one of the most important calligraphic symbol in traditional Chinese culture. A lot of calligraphy masters are essentially men, except for a small number of female calligraphers such as, for example, He Xiangning 何香凝 (1878 - 1972). Therefore, in this performance the character zhi 之, as a symbol of order, represents the "authority" (quanwei 权威). The nightgown worn by the artist and the bruises all over her body also represent the state of domestic violence suffered by women from men in history. This performance art is defined by the artist as a "non-literary" work (fei wenxue xing 非文学性) and aims to shake the consciences of people who take part in it. During the writing process, the oxygen inside the sphere is gradually depleted, infusing it with carbon dioxide. Just before suffocating, the artist will ask for help to get out of the sphere. Wu Xixia calls this type of performance “a self-redemption with the aim of suffocating” (yi chang yi zhixi wei mudi de zi wo jiushu 一场以窒息为目的的自我救赎).\r
Wu Xixia has learned calligraphy from her grandfather since she was a child, and she considers it a great identity and cultural value. Calligraphic rules are determined by ancestors, tradition and authority. The patriarchal symbol of traditional culture has been ingrained in the minds of the Chinese people through ethics, morality and customs for thousands of years, penetrating every corner of society through the mass media, and has been handed down to the present day. The reason for Wu Xixia's choice to rewrite the “Orchid Pavilion Preface” is not only because it is the calligraphic collection that the artist has practiced the most since she was a child, but also because it pays homage to the work “Writing the 'Orchid Pavilion Preface' One Thousand Times” (Chongfu shuxie yi qian bian 'Lantingxu' 重复书写一千 遍 '兰亭序', 1990-1995) realized by Qiu Zhijie.\r
Another similar performance titled “Writing the Orchid Pavillon for the Second Time” (书写兰亭序第二遍) takes place during the Wuzhen Theater Festival (Wuzhen xiju jie 乌镇戏剧节) on November 27, 2022. In this case, Wu Xixia writes inside an airtight sphere placed in the water. The long snake-shaped strip that surrounds the sphere is an inflatable model that simulates the shape of an umbilical cord, on which are written some idiomatic sentences such as: “chibaole chengde 吃饱了撑的” (restless from overeating); “chi tai bao le 吃太饱了” (eat too full); “huazhong quchong 哗众取宠” (try to please the public with claptrap); “buru zhongtian 不如种田” (it is better to farm); “yishu yi si 艺术已死” (art is dead); “yishu shi laji 艺术是垃圾” (art is rubbish); “hebu shi rou mi 何不食肉糜” (to be oblivious to other people’s plight). \r
The writing process of this performance has three main characteristics: \r
1) The replacement of the four characters "Zhi hu zhe ye 之乎者也" present in the original text with a "secret" female writing style, defined as "nü shu 女书". \r
2) The character ye 也 is replaced with the female vagina symbol. In fact, in oracle bones and bronze inscriptions the original form of "ye 也" is a part of the female body resembling a vagina. As Xu Shen 许慎 (c. 58 – c. 148 BC) calligrapher of the Eastern Han dynasty wrote in his "Shuowen Jiezi 说文解字" (“Explaining Graphs and Analyzing Characters”) the character ye 也 really means "female vagina". \r
3) The writing process is combined with the audience's emotional participation in the performance."""@en ;
