    ns2:P571 "2021-12"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    ns2:P608 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1932> ;
    ns1:description """The installation "Her Mountain" (Ta shan 她山) is exhibited in Beijing in 2021 in occasion of the "Wu Xixia  Personal Art Exhibition" (Wu Xixia ge zhan 吴析夏个展) held from December 6 to 20, 2021 at the Song Yang Art Museum (Song Yang meishu guan 宋洋美术馆). The artist Wu Xixia creates a "mountain of blood" full of symbols linked to childbirth and the female womb (such as some foetuses lying in red wedges) to talk about gender issues in contemporary society. The form of the entire installation is combined with the Da Henggin Mountain 大横琴山 and Xiao Hengqin Mountain 小横琴山 (two mountain ranges of Hengqin Island, in Zhuhai, Guangdong Province) visible behind the glass windows. Just as gravel collects in a mountain range, women form a "group". The red-flecked stainless steel flowers blooming on "Her-Mountain" represent the "blossoming" of spiritual consciousness. In the installation, there are also the three closed spheres resulting from the three feminist-themed performative works realized bu Wu Xixia. The "mountains" and "spheres" represent respectively the "groups" and "individuals" in the process of awakening the female consciousness. \r
The three spheres resulting from the performance are: \r
1) "I" (Wo 我, 2020) a work that reconciles personal identity and gender. Wu Xixia carries out a reflection on women and herself, writing the labels of society’s goodwill and malice towards women with red ink, such as "whore" (biao zi 婊子) "old maid" (lao chunu 老处女), "good wife and loving mother" (xian qiliang mu 贤妻良母), "green tea bitch" (luchabiao 绿茶婊), "teenage girl" (yunu 玉女), "white lotus" (bailian hua 白莲花), etc. She answers questions such as: "Who are women? And who am I? Is my gender determined by birth or is it given by society? There is a womb here, but where is the "mother"?". Having been rejected by her family at the time of her birth as a female, she has been questioning her gender throughout her formative years, and the social labels she writes about are an attempt to reconcile herself with her gender identity.\r
2) "Darling" (2021) in which Wu Xixia performs in front of a building called "Darling". The building was covered with red ribbons, which spread all the way to the inflatable ball. Wu Xixia writes the words "jiu jiu wo 救救我" (help me); "qiuqiu ni 求求你" (I beg you), "bie sha wo 别杀我" (don't kill me); "bie S wo 别S我" (don't fuck me), etc. inside the hermetic sphere. The female body becomes an object of desire and "objectification" under the male gaze. In the process of doing performance art, passers-by, especially men, took photos with various poses and angles nearby. In the course of more than 40 minutes, the artist lay quietly on the ground to receive everyone’s gaze, to begin to write down the words for help. Red gives a signal of judgment, and the red ribbon is the concretization of the female body being stared at. The building covered with red ribbons symbolize the existing and massive examination of the female body. The artist wraps the red ribbon of the building on the inflatable ball, representing the current situation of women who are looking at fearlessly and pursuing physical freedom, but it is still difficult to escape from the scrutiny. In the past, the female body has always been hidden. Nowadays, with the awakening of female consciousness, women break free from the shackles and turn from invisibility to openness and fearless publicity. In this transition, the female body is no longer a private and invisible object, but exposed to the public eye. Just as the protagonist of this performance art puts herself under public scrutiny, it is a challenge, but cannot escape scrutiny. This performance aims to arouse people's attention to the predicament of women breaking free from physical examination (wuwei shenshi 无畏审视). A screen with the photo/video of the performance in progress is also present in the installation.\r
3) "Writing the Orchid Pavilion Preface One time" (Shuxie Lanting Xu yi bian 书写兰亭序一遍, 2021) which attempts to break the traditional rules of Chinese calligraphy. The characters are written with a brush dipped in black ink and the different forms of the twenty zhi 之 characters present in the traditional poem are replaced with visible red ink male phalluses.\r
The three independent spheres and the surrounding red mountains complement each other, presenting a utopia in which the "self" (inner world) and the whole (external world) "bloom" in the process of awakening the female consciousness."""@en ;
