    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/585> ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/609> ;
    ns1:description """This performance is realized by Echo Morgan in occasion of the 3rd Edition of the "Territori - Ibiza Performance Art Festival" (17-25 September, 2022) which takes place in Ibiza, Spain. In this performance, Echo Morgan's face is painted white and with a compound made of chlorophyll powder mixed with seawater, she writes "Anatomy de la Posidonia" on her face. She is dressed in white and starts to drip her hair in the compound made of chlorophyll powder mixed with seawater. After dipping her hair, she starts to drip the mixture all over her body and begins to move on a white sheet, about 11 metres long. With various body movements, accompanied by some verses and vocal sounds, the artist becomes a brush and leaves "imprints" and marks of her body's passage on the sheet. \r
Many people call chlorophyll "plant blood." This molecule is responsible for the ability of plants to convert sunlight into energy through photosynthesis. Through this gestures and body movements, the artist highlights the intelligence of nature and invites the public to re-immerse themselves in nature, giving life to a more "primitive" approach to the surrounding environment. In this performance, Echo Morgan reflects also on the femininity of Posidonia, one of the only plants that flowers and fruits in the sea. Just as a mother provides security, nourishment, and protection to her family, Posidonia provides stability for beaches, serves as a carbon sink, and provides a haven for marine fauna. It filters and oxygenates the water, much like women who organises and purify their homes and communities. Posidonia is also fragile, despite its 100,000 years of strength and wisdom, due to climate change and pollution from tourism. A scar from a large boat in the meadow can take a century to recover. Studies have found that 29% of Posidonia has been lost since 1879. This loss of Posidonia meadows due to human activity highlights the urgent need for greater respect and care for our natural environment and the feminine qualities it represents. It serves as a reflection of the scars that women often bear due to the damaging effects of patriarchy and misogyny. \r
As Echo Morgan explained in a recent interview: "The wild and abstract body movements depicted in the hair painting are closely associated with the subject of femininity and freedom - a source of power that also signifies vulnerability and subservience. The painting itself illustrates microscopic images of fertilized Posidonia seeds with eggs. At 100,000 years old, Posidonia is the oldest and largest living organism in the world, serving as one of the greatest sources of oxygen in the Mediterranean. Each square meter of plant generates between 4 and 20 liters of oxygen per day and provides a home to over 400 plant species and 1,000 animal species. Despite being cherished and protected by the local community, commercial developers and tourists often overlook its importance, making it a vulnerable and endangered species. This performance took place in front of the monument of Vara Del Rey - a Spanish war hero born in Ibiza in 1841 and who died during the Spanish-American War in 1898. Through my performance, I aimed to challenge the concept of his heroic status and ask whether he is also seen as a hero in the eyes of Cubans. Drawing inspiration from the Chinese political criminal, Nobel Peace Prize winner, and democracy activist Liu Xiaobo's quote, "Scepter's Terracotta Army. Amazed the entire world. A tomb that is more majestic than the palace. Our long history, relying on the emperor's grave to show off glory," I aimed to shift the focus towards celebrating and protecting other species rather than glorifying colonial rulers.This performance was also inspired by a local Catalan feminist funk band called Pussydonia".\r
\r
During the Festival, few days before this performance (17th September), Echo Morgan also did another performance on the beach entitled "Echo of Posidonia", where she painted her body white and green and became a Posidonia plant. During the performance, the artist had herself "chained" with 12 heavy metal yacht chains by the audience on the beach and delivered a speech about protecting the marine environment and the culture of the locals. The artist says: "[...] For the echoes of Posidonia, the scarred meadows under the sea. For the millions of refugees adrift in the ocean, some of whom drown in the Mediterranean Sea. For visitors to show more respect for the indigenous culture, Catalan language and their beloved sea".\r
\r
Echo Morgan has also realised other performances in which she "became brush" and makes use of ink-soaked hair, such as:\r
1) "I Am A Brush" (2011) at the Royal College of Art of London. This is the first artist's live performance and it lasted three and a half hours until the end of the roll had been reached. In 2011, Xie Rong's decision to return to full-time study and continue her Master of Arts, ended her seven-year marriage. Following the Chinese custom, the mother combs hair and blesses the bride the night before the wedding. Young women often cut their hair and put it in a small bag to give to their loved one. Using hair to make his first live art work was a symbolic reflection of the artist's changing personal life, it is like "cutting" ties with the past, an act of rebirth and separation. During the exhibition “Home” (Jia家) (18th March - 20th April 2017) Xie Rong decides to curate the long "expression" into individual pieces. As a metaphor for endings and beginnings, Xie deems the act of cutting the paper as akin to cutting one’s hair. \r
2) "Circle of Fire, the Dying Spite of Yi" (2018) at the Röda Sten Konsthall Gallery of Gothenburg (Sweden). In this performance, Echo Morgan is always dressed in white and paints on a circular sheet of paper with her hair dipped in red ink. She told the story of the Yi minority in Sichuan, where many people died of AIDS, leaving behind a large number of orphans and HIV-positive mothers.\r
3) "Painting until it becomes Marble - Love Never Dies" (2019) at the Museum of Fine Arts of Leipzig (Germany). Reverently and ceremoniously, the artist stood quietly in the huge empty space with extremely high floor-to-ceiling windows emitting a background of pure light. Dressed all in white, Xie Rong stood with two bowls at her feet, one filled with Chinese black ink and the other with pure blue pigment powder. The artist combed the ink through her long black hair, saturating it and began to paint on a very large paper on the floor. Rong’s barefooted dance began – at first light and graceful like a ballerina, then more intensely, furiously jumping as her drenched hair splattered paint in all directions and on herself. The chaotic calligraphy continued with her head to the floor, making brush strokes. Kneeling with her head down on the paper, submitting to the work in reverence, she made thicker strokes and swirls. \r
"""@en ;
