    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/585> ;
    ns1:description """This performance takes place in Linz (Austria) in 2022 in occasion of the "Performance Festival: Non-fungible Body?" (June, 16 - 19). The artist Echo Morgan, after colouring her body white, writes Chinese characters in red ink all over her body. Chinese words are logographic and their construction often explains their historical meaning. The Chinese word "ta 她"(she/her) is composed of the words "female" + "also". The usage of the word "ta 她" as a female pronoun did not become prevalent until the 1920s, when first introduced by Liu Bannong 刘半农 (1891-1934) for translating western literature purpose. Despite many controversy, the pronoun "ta 她" was an unique and an important symbol during the women’s movement in China. Sadly, there are many Chinese words with the character "nü 女" (female) that contain negative attitude towards to women. These words seem traditionally neutral but embodied the unconsidered misogynistic culture that women are so hunted. For this, Echo Morgan's body becomes a scroll of "patriarchal text" and she decides to cover her body with these kind of words, such as: "jian 奸" (rape); "nuan 奻" (stupid); "jian 姦" (evil); "yao 妖" (demon); "biao 婊"(bitch); "piao 嫖"(brothe); "pin 姘"(mistress); "chang 娼"(whore); "ji 妓"(prostitute); "nu 奴"(slave); "shua 耍"(play); "lan 婪"(greed); "ning 佞"(insincerity); "wang 妄" (foolish); "yu 娱"(entertainment); "xian 嫌"(fedup); "fang 妨"(harm); "ji 嫉" (envy) ; "du 妒" (jealous); "bi 妣" (dead mother); "ban 姅" (miscarriage).  She also covers her body with famous Chinese female saying such as: “Three Obediences and Four Virtues” (san cong si de 三从四德); “A woman's virtue is to have no talent" (nuzi wu cai bian shi de 女子无才便是德); "Men are superior and women are inferior” (nan zun nu bei 男尊女卑); "Beauty leads to disaster/femme fatale” (hongyanhuoshui 红颜祸水), etc. In this performance, the artist also contrasts the female symbol, linguistically associated with insulting or discrediting words (expressed by the Chinese characters written on the artist's body) and the Chinese knot symbol, which has always been linguistically associated with "positive" meanings of union and harmony such as: "jiehun 结婚" (marriage); "jiejiao 结交" (associate); "jieguo 结果" (bear fruit/results); "jieshu 结束" (finish); "tuanjie 团结" (union/solidarity); "jiu jie 揪结" (entangled); "xin jie 心结" (heart knot). After having painted herself, the artist ties her body to red curtains previously installed inside the building where the performance takes place, making sounds and creating different movements with the body. The red curtains represent the "trap" within which the woman's body is a victim of society and patriarchy and from which the artist will symbolically free herself at the end of the performance.\r
This performance  is part of a chapters series entitled "Jie: Chinese Knot" (Zhongguo jie 中国结) divided into six moments. As Echo Morgan explains, before the invention of writing, people in ancient China tied knots on a rope to memorise important life events. The knotted symbol represents happiness, unity, harmony and prosperity. Ironically, the way the character itself is written also echoes sadness, death and ending. This literal double-bind serves the artist to dive into the story of a woman chained in a doorless shack that went viral on Chinese Social Media in late January 2022. Her tragic case of human trafficking accumulated more than 10 billion views, rivalling those about the Beijing Winter Olympics that took place at the same time. In her performance Echo Morgan engages with this highly mediated incident. By activating the pictorial capacity of a Chinese knot installation, she engages its traditional motif to become an in-between space of social, political and artistic expression.\r
\r
The other five chapters are: 1) Chinese Knot - Chapter One: Become Little Plum Blossom (Jie - di yi zhang: Bian cheng xiaohua mei 结 - 第一章 : 变成小花梅); 2) Chinese Knot - Chapter Two: Know her name, remember her name (Jie - di er zhang: Zhixiao ta xingming, laoji ta xingming结 - 第二章 : 知晓她姓名, 牢记她姓名); 3) Chinese Knot - Chapter Four: The Non-interchangeable Body (Jie - di si zhang: Buke hu huan de shenti 结 - 第四章 : 不可互换的身体); 4) Chinese Knot - Chapter Five: Red Planet (Jie - di wu zhang: Hongse xingqiu 结 - 第五章 : 红色星球); 5) Chinese Knot - Chapter Six: Viennese Theater (Jie - di liu zhang: Weiyena juyuan 结 - 第六章 : 维也纳剧院)."""@en ;
