    ns1:description "This performance was held by Li Xinmo in March 2012, during the \"Bald Girls Exhibition\" (tutou ge nu 秃頭戈女) at the Iberia Contemporary Art Space of Beijing. She writes some big traditional characters of the famous Chinese dictionary named \"Shuowen Jiezi 说文解字\" realized by Xu Shen (c. 58 – c. 148 CE). These characters are accompained with their explanations written in smaller size. These big characters with female radical have all a negative meaning, or in any case words that relegate women to a simple \"role\" in society, for example: \"hai 妎\" (jealous/demanding); \"fu 妇\" (woman/wife); \"wang 妄\" (presumptuous/rash); \"lan 婪\" (be greedy); \"fang 妨\" (harm), \"hun 婚\" (married), \"yin 婬\" (lascivious/licentious); \"ning 佞\" (sycophant/toady); \"du 妒\" (jealous/envy);  \"qu 娶\" (take a wife); \"wei 委\" (throw away/cast aside); \"zi 姊\" (older sister) etc.  Characters are realized with white ink on a black wall in regular script. In addition, they are realized in non-simplified form and from top to bottom and from right to left, recalling ancient scriptures. In China female discrimination has a very ancient origin. It starts from writing and, consequently, from calligraphy, an artistic practice exclusive to male in China.This performance is very reminiscent of Echo Morgan's “Chinese Knot” (see the archive). The only difference is that here the canvas is not a body, but a black wall and the characters are not made in red but in white. All of this once again shows the misogynistic and derogatory nature of Chinese cultural tradition towards women."@en ;
    ns1:hasChineseConcept <http://w3id.org/write/thesaurus/junzi> ;
