    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/746.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """This visual artwork was created by Wang Dongling during his visit in the United States.  It is one example among his several innovative collages that integrate Western images with Chinese characters to form a composition expressing a simple concept. This collage consists of a New York Times article on the American dancer and choreographer Martha Graham, who reshaped the style of American dance with her free, rebellious and courageous spirit. On the English article about the dancer and on his picture, Wang wrote wu 舞, the character for 'dance', in a bold cursive script. The strokes of the character are inspired by Graham's physical posture in motion.\r
As backgroud text, Wang added smaller, finer orange-red characters in a dozen of vertical lines. As happens in other collages of the artist, the texts he uses are usually poems  selected as a form of supplementary linguistic commentary to produce a multi-layered work. In this case, the text is inspired to the story of Gongsun daniang 公孫大娘, which narrates of a famous sword-dancer of the Tang dynasty. It is said that Zhang Xu (active 710-750) invented his caoshu (cursive script) after watching her dance. This text emphasizes the connection between dance and calligraphy, both of which encapsulate the sense of movement and freedom. \r
In these series of works, Wang combines calligraphy, relating the form of the character to the human form, and also relating the movement of the brush to the movement of the human form. If the brush represents gestural language, he is directly and explicitly exposing that relationship to the body, with the form complementing the movement of the body. \r
"Dance" does not belong to a specific series, but since the creation of this artwork, Wang has embarked on a whole series of innumerable calligraphic works using art photography in books, magazines and newspapers sheets which, despite the content and the calligraphic forms being different, reflect many of the features present in the the current work. \r
"""@en ;
