    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1586> ;
    ns1:description """This performance took place on March 28, 2018 at the Braidense Library in Milan. to the notes of the voice of the shamanic singer Sainko Namtchylak, the calligrapher Silvio Ferragina begins to write on a white scroll placed vertically in the center of the room (fig. 269). Silvio Ferragina transcribes some verses of the “Heart Sutra” with different calligraphic styles: seal script (zhuanshu), cursive script (caoshu) and regular script (kaishu). During the performance, Silvio also uses a particular writing technique in which he employs a dry brush (resembling the feibai) that he then soaks in ink and drip onto the transcribed characters, reminiscent of the dripping technique.\r
The verses created by Silvio Ferragina are the same that the calligrapher had already proposed previously during the musicalligraphy performance realized with Alessandro Cerino in Hangzhou in 2016 (see the archive). The integration of shamanic music with the “Heart Sutra” creates a profound atmosphere that amplifies the spiritual and meditative qualities of both calligraphy and the text. The “Heart Sutra”, a central text in Mahayana Buddhism, conveys deep philosophical concepts regarding emptiness and the nature of reality. Sainko Namtchylak’s shamanic singing, characterized by ethereal and evocative qualities, complements the introspective nature of the sutra. The music can induce a trance-like state, encouraging both the calligrapher and the audience to connect more deeply with the meanings of the verses being transcribed. As Silvio Ferragina moves between different calligraphic style (seal script, cursive script, and regular script) each stroke can be seen as a visual manifestation of the rhythms and emotions of the music. The fluidity and expressiveness of the calligraphy reflect the improvisational elements of shamanic music, allowing for a dynamic interaction between sound and visual art.\r
"""@en ;
