    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/927.jpg> ;
    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/748> ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/928> ;
    ns1:description """This performance by Wang Dongling took place in occasion of Asia Contemporary Art Week (ACAW) on November 7, 2015, at the Brooklyn Museum  and was organized by the museum in collaboration with the INK Studio (an art gallery based in Beijing and New York). Wang Dongling writes the Heart Sutra (Xingjing 心经) , a concise and elegant articulation of the core tenets of Mahayana Buddhism in his Chaos Script (luan shu 乱书) making the characters mostly unrecognizable. The resulting amorphous and energetic chaos hovers on the brink of legibility, fully realizing the gestural and expressionistic potential of calligraphy. Spotlit at the centre of the museum’s Beaux-Arts Court, Wang Dongling worked on approximately 20 square meters of xuan paper placed on the floor. He chose a brush with a long and narrow bamboo stem, which sensitively translated his every movement. Throughout the performance, he held the brush at an angle and repeatedly let it run dry, causing its hair to split and rub against the paper surface to create tenuous traces. With impeccable timing and technical mastery, he wrote at an even pace for almost exactly 30 minutes, breaking stance after completing each character (268 in total).\r
\r
Wang Dongling wrote several versions of the Heart Sutra in Chaos Script in his performances, such as:\r
- in 2012 on the occasion of the performance held at the OCT Contemporary Art Center of Shenzhen;\r
- in 2016 on the occasion of the performance held at the British Museum in London;\r
- in 2016 on the occasion of the performance held at the Vancouver Art Gallery in Canada;\r
- in 2016 on the occasion of the performance held at the Auckland Art Museum in New Zealand."""@en ;
