    ns1:description """This artwork is part of the Corporeal Calligraphy (Shuti 书体) series composed of eight calligraphies on magazine sheets presented for the first time during the exhibition "Decanter Ink, Hangzhou Cross Year 2011-2012, Wang Dongling Solo Exhibition". The artwork is a clear example of the modernists' approach to the calligraphic material (Iezzi 2014, p. 410). The support is not the traditional sheet of xuan paper, but the page of a magazine written in English with the photograph "Nude on Sand, Ocean, 1936" in black and white by Edward Weston (1886-1958). The photo presents the model Charis Wilson (1914-2009), Weston's partner at the time, naked and lying on the beach. This photograph is the background of Wang Dongling's calligraphy work. The artist distributes the characters of the Heart Sutra in the sandy background carefully following the lines of the female model's body in an attempt to integrate different cultures, represented by the Chinese calligraphy and the Western photography. Wang also attempts to conciliate the dichotomy of tradition, embodied in the Heart sutra, a text of the philosophical tradition used as calligraphic material in all eras, and modernity, expressed with the use of photos and magazine sheets. \r
In 2012 Wang Dongling realized a second specific series, entitled Shuti_shu zhi ti (Shuti_Shu zhi ti 书体_书之体). This series consists of five works presenting five naked female models in five different positions, the lines of each body are associated to one specific calligraphic script (regular, semi-cursive, cursive, clerical, small seal). The parellism between script and the models' bodies is explicitely indicated by the title of the work, in which the character 'ti 体' refers both to writing scripts (shuti 书体) and to the body (shenti 身体). Shu zhi ti 书之体 is then understood as 'bodies/styles of writing'."""@en ;
