    ns2:P569 "1994-06-25"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P800 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1525>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1531>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1538> ;
    ns2:P856 <https://www.instagram.com/dohak625/> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q16572> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1523.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Dohak625, born in 1994 and currently residing in Guangzhou, is an emerging figure in the graffiti world. He speaks Cantonese, Mandarin, and Hakka, and despite having no formal training in calligraphy, art, or aesthetics, he has developed a distinctive graffiti style. His journey into graffiti began in 2020, though he only actively engaged with the practice four years ago. \r
His pseudonym, “Dohak625,” was derived from the Cantonese pronunciation of "daoke 刀客," which is "dou haak." Seeking a shorter name, they decided to abbreviate "dou haak" to "dohak." Interestingly, they later discovered that "dohak" closely resembles "Doha," the capital of Qatar, with just an added "k." Initially, they were unaware of the existence of Doha and didn't even know how to pronounce "dohak" correctly. Now, they find it amusing that it can be roughly pronounced like "Doha."\r
Dohak625 commonly uses the following three tags: "Daoke 刀客" (meaning "swordsman"), "Jianshi 剣士" (another way of saying "swordsman"), and "Ziluolan 紫羅蘭/ Lan蘭" (meaning "violet"). The choice of the two tags "Daoke 刀客" and "Jianshi 剣士" reflects his admiration for Chinese martial arts novels, which often feature tragic characters with difficult fates, such as in the film The One-Armed Swordsman released in 1967. Dohak625 also has a fondness for Stephen Chow's movies, particularly From Beijing with Love, where Chow plays a meat vendor who refers to himself as a "daoke 刀客" (swordsman). They appreciate the subversive imagination and humor in this portrayal.\r
The use of the tag "Ziluolan 紫羅蘭" is inspired by the love story of the late Chinese writer Zhou Shoujuan 周瘦鹃 (1895 – 1968). Despite the tragedy of Zhou's love story, Dohak625 was moved by his personal life and the way he expressed his feelings. Zhou was born in a time when marriages were arranged, and he was unable to marry the person he truly loved. Nevertheless, he did everything he could to express his love for this woman, whose English name was Violet, which in Chinese is "Ziluolan 紫羅蘭."\r
In an interview (Bisceglia 2024),  Dohak625 described his creative process as a “low-budget style,” deliberately avoiding expensive materials in favor of more affordable options. His graffiti-making aligns with a standard procedure: finding a spot, completing the work, and moving on. He often improvises, rarely sketching drafts before executing his work, which resonates with the anti-style trend in graffiti. This movement, which challenges traditional notions of technique, color schemes, and detail, finds echoes in Dohak625's art. \r
Dohak625’s style also conform to a contemporary trend in Chinese calligraphy known as “ugly calligraphy” (choushu 丑书), which emerged in the 1980s and is characterized by disharmony, asymmetry, and inconsistency.\r
In a interview (Bisceglia 2024) he describes his relationship with Chinese calligraphy as the relationship between father and son: "As a son, I am greatly influenced by my father, and this influence will likely last throughout my life. However, when the son turns 18 and steps out into the world, the time they spend together may gradually diminish, though their bond will always remain. The son will meet more and more people and continue to be shaped by these new influences." \r
His creative process often involves riding his bicycle through unfamiliar cities, stopping at potential graffiti sites. This improvisational approach rejects the repetitive practices typical in graffiti circles, as Dohak625 seeks to challenge established norms with his unique blend of chaos and creativity.\r
Dohak625’s work transcends the traditional boundaries of graffiti. He sees himself not merely as a graffiti writer but as an artist driven by a profound creative impulse. Unlike many graffiti artists who engage heavily in urban “bombing” – the rapid and prolific tagging of public spaces – Dohak625 prefers working in ruins and secluded locations, where he feels more freedom to express his artistic vision. Urban bombing constitutes only about 40% of his work, and although graffiti holds little commercial value in China, he remains committed to these public and wild spaces.\r
The influence of traditional Chinese calligraphy is apparent in Dohak625's work, though he views his artistic evolution as something that has progressed beyond it. He critiques the way many Chinese graffiti artists incorporate traditional elements into their art without engaging with the core aspects of the medium, such as tagging or throw-ups, instead focusing on large-scale, realistic works. For Dohak625, true graffiti exists in the tension between these influences and the raw, unpolished energy of street art.\r
Stylistically, Dohak625 admires GhettoSino, a fellow Chinese graffiti artist known for excelling in six key areas: quantity, location, style, creativity, skill, and content. Dohak625 holds GhettoSino in high regard for maintaining these qualities consistently over time, which he believes makes him a standout figure in the Chinese graffiti scene.\r
Although Dohak625’s work aligns with aspects of the anti-style or ugly calligraphy movements, he distances himself from rigid labels. His art is less about deliberately rejecting traditional aesthetics and more about embracing imperfections that reflect the chaotic, tragic, and humorous experiences of his life. Raised in a poor family, Dohak625's art is informed by personal history, low-budget materials, and an ethos that prizes authenticity over polished perfectionism.\r
As an artist, Dohak625 challenges the boundaries between beauty and decay, embodying a philosophy of anti-style that deliberately disregards conventional norms while maintaining a deep respect for the creative process. He sums up his view of artistic creation with a statement on writing:  \r
"I am serious about this topic. For me, being a writer is one of my ultimate goals in life. Literary creation is the highest form of creation, and writers who can produce classic literature are the most amazing creators. I love to talk, express, write, and record." (Interview with M. R. Bisceglia, 2024)\r
Dohak625 is also part of the "Chinese Emotional Graffiti" movement (Zhongwen qingxu tuya 中文情绪涂鸦), which focuses on graffiti that emphasizes written text as its main element. This form of graffiti often includes slogans, short stories, poetry, quotes, social and political commentary, or personal reflections, found on various public surfaces. It aims to convey messages or statements through language, using public spaces as a platform for expressing ideas, emotions, or social commentary. In Dohak625's early artistic phase, he mainly wrote long poems—both original and quoted—sometimes creating acrostics by highlighting the first column of characters to emphasize a concept or summarize the poem's theme."""@en ;
