    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1607.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """This artworks is part of the series "Ode of Eight Thousand Verses" (Baqian songge 八千颂歌). It combines  two writing systems: the Chinese characters and the Sanskrit script. The Sanskrit parts in black are extracts from the Sutra of the Perfection of Wisdom in 8,000 Lines, also called Transcendental Knowledge Sutra (Prajñāpāramitā Sutra), which is a collection of Buddhist texts fundamental for the creation of Mahāyāna Buddhism. The graphs in white outlined in black are reworkings of the Chinese character Fu 福 (Good Fortune). This character represents one of the most popular auspicious symbols in China: it is often affixed to the doors of the Chinese houses or appears in paintings hanging on the walls, also reworked in a creative way.  To shape the more than 20 different forms of this character used in this artwork, Luo Qi  took inspiration from the very popular "Baifutu 百福图" (One hundred handwritings of the character fu), a design composed of 100 different handwritings of the character fu 福 in the style of ancient and modern seal character, expressing good wishes. This diagram is also linked to the forms of the so-called "Daoist talismanic script" (fulu 符箓 or fuwen 符文), usually reported on talismans. These forms make the character not linguistically (and immediately) recognizable, giving it magical power.  The artist decided to write this character also because from 2010 it has been the source of inspiration of an important part of his artistic production focusing on various reworking of this character. Additionally, the character "fu" refers to an essential religious text of Western tradition, the Gospel, which in Chinese is called "Fuyinjing 福音经" (lit. ‘Classic of Fortunate Music’ or ‘Classic of Happy Sound’). This text is the main topic of another series created by Luo Qi in 2018 entitled "Fujing chaoben 福经抄本" (The transcription of the Gospel) inspired by European Medieval incunabula,  and the second character of its title "yin 音" (sound) brings us back to music, that is one of the main themes of the whole series. \r
This artwork was created in 2017 for the exhibition “Connections”  (Fort Adelaide - La Citadel, Port Louis, 08-16/09), and then displayed in the exhibition "Luo Qi: Writing Music“ (Bologna University Library - Bologna, 25/03-08/04/2019), and "Sounds of Ink: Luo Qi and 30 Years of Calligraphyism" (International Museum and Library of Music - Bologna, 12/10-05/11/2023).\r
The artist tries to use different linguistic signs that, juxtaposed, reiterated, mixed and outlined through expert calligraphic brushstrokes, create a visual rhythm that visually reproduces the musical rhythm of Buddhist litanies arisen from reading the transcribed sutras. The linguistic content of the work is so no longer important, but is only the means to create a sonically perceptible plot. It is made of signs that go beyond borders and unite worlds through their "visual sound". """ ;
