    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1665.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Beer1ne, also known by his various tags including "beer," "beer1ne," and "Hui jin 恢烬," is a 19-year-old graffiti artist from Huizhou, Guangdong, currently residing in Guangzhou. His initial tag "beer" originated from the English initials of his previous name, "PJ." Frequently typing "PJ" on the keyboard led the Chinese input method to suggest "啤酒" (beer), prompting him to adopt this moniker. Later, he transitioned to "beer1ne," a play on "beerone," creating a distinctive version to avoid confusion with other artists.\r
The tag "恢烬" carries profound significance for Beer1ne. It emerged during a period of emotional turmoil when he identified with the symbolism of "ash," representing decay and the remnants of something once vibrant. This feeling encapsulated desolation while also embodying the potential for transformation. Just as ash can settle into the earth or drift in the air, the concept signifies a cycle of rebirth. Thus, "恢烬" reflects both melancholia and an openness to change, with an element of playful interpretation on his part.\r
Beer1ne is affiliated with the crew WAS, informally known as "was dead?" and is also a member of WOD CLUB, which functions more like a collective than a structured group. \r
His artistic journey began around the age of 11 or 12, inspired by graffiti he encountered in anime, video games, and movies. At 15, a close friend’s social media post featuring their graffiti artwork motivated him to delve into the craft. He began learning graffiti techniques through alphabets shared by graffiti-focused public accounts on WeChat.\r
Shortly thereafter, Beer1ne and his friend purchased spray paint and respirators, eagerly seeking out public spaces to create their first pieces. Their initial foray into graffiti led to a humorous adventure in a residential area, where they narrowly escaped being reported by a delivery driver. This joyful experience remains a cherished memory for him, despite his novice status at the time.\r
Initially, Beer1ne favored clean and intricate wildstyle pieces. However, as repetition became uninspiring, he began to experiment with his own Chinese tag and embraced a more chaotic, rough visual approach, partly due to past struggles with clean lines. This evolution in style prompted him to explore a broader artistic direction, which he later learned leaned towards an "ignorant style" through feedback from Taiwanese writer Faso during a graffiti competition.\r
While he has not read extensively, Beer1ne considers the book "STREET FONTS" (Walde 2011) a significant source of inspiration. His creative process has shifted from meticulous sketches and pre-planned color choices to a more spontaneous approach, utilizing whatever materials are at hand. His primary motivation for creating graffiti is the thrill and enjoyment it brings, viewing it as a form of public art that can address social and political themes while celebrating the rebellious act of occupying public spaces.\r
Beer1ne's work remains deeply personal and self-directed, with no current engagements in commercial projects or commissions. He notes that the structure of most Chinese graffiti fonts tends to adhere to regular script (kaishu 楷书) but has previously experimented with integrating Qin Dynasty seal script (xiaozhuan 小篆) into his pieces. Although his work attracted imitation during that time, he eventually returned to simplified Chinese characters, feeling creatively exhausted by the seal script style.\r
He appreciates the blending of Chinese cultural elements with graffiti, viewing it as a colorful and inclusive art form that can enrich local cultural traditions. Beer1ne believes that a "Chinese graffiti style" begins with writing in Chinese fonts while also reflecting a more Chinese aesthetic.\r
Beer1ne perceives the current state of graffiti in China as relatively positive and peaceful, noting that the emphasis is often on visual presentation rather than the act of creation itself. While street bombing faces legal and moral restrictions, he observes that the Chinese scene primarily focuses on complete pieces, with less emphasis on throw-ups and handstyles. The Chinese graffiti environment is generally more formal and conservative, leaning towards street art rather than traditional graffiti, with plagiarism posing challenges for artists in developing unique styles.\r
Despite these challenges, Beer1ne admires several Chinese writers, such as Saeor, and respects the work of graffiti crews like MTS, KTS, 2G, and ABS, appreciating their outstanding contributions to the scene."""@en ;
