    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1685.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """TxT, an enigmatic graffiti writer and multifaceted artist, operates under a veil of anonymity that allows him to explore the fringes of street art while engaging in illegal writing. This examination will focus solely on his "calligraphic" works, revealing the depth and nuance of his artistic practice. A citizen of the United Kingdom, TxT is in his forties and has cultivated a diverse linguistic repertoire.\r
His calligraphic education is primarily self-taught, inspired by traditional artworks that he meticulously studies and emulates. This journey intensified during the various lockdowns of 2020, but his formal study began earlier when he trained in calligraphy under Chen Yu Lian, the daughter of the renowned Chinese ink artist Chen Hong Fei.\r
TxT's calligraphic creations have taken him across the globe from 2020 to 2024, with significant activities in Australia, the People’s Republic of China, Indonesia, Italy, and Germany. His preferred canvases include trains—often covering entire carriages—as well as abandoned urban spaces, where he seeks to establish a dialogue with the surrounding context. His materials of choice are acrylic paint, paint rollers, and brushes, tools through which he expresses his artistic vision.\r
In his unique blend of graffiti and calligraphy, TxT employs a distinctive motif: the red tag seal. This element appears in every piece he creates, symbolizing his identity and connecting his work to a broader cultural narrative. The combination of the letters "TT" with "X" forms his signature tag, TxT. The letter "X" serves not only as a vital component of his identity but also as a semiotic device, representing a hidden identity that is not legally recognized but still holds significance. This symbolism is particularly poignant, as the "X" often signifies a mark made by those who cannot write—an identity that transcends the constraints of language and legality.\r
The incorporation of the Chinese red seal further enhances the universality of TxT's work. The brush strokes that render this symbol evoke the fluidity of ink and brushwork in traditional calligraphy, bridging his artistic identity with Chinese culture. In his series titled “X,” the "X" transforms into a dynamic figure, as evidenced by the artwork “X-evolution,” where it is never static. This kinetic quality is amplified by his calligraphic approach, reinforcing the idea that art is a living, evolving dialogue rather than a mere static representation. Through these innovative practices, TxT invites viewers to engage with the intersections of identity, culture, and artistic expression.\r
"""@en ;
