<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1844> a ns3:Q464980 ;
    ns2:P10661 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1017>,
        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/814> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Ink Art and New Music Project  (水墨艺术与新音乐)"@en,
        "水墨艺术与新音乐"@zh,
        "Shuǐmò yìshù yǔ xīn yīnyuè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1384>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8646> ;
    ns2:P580 "2020"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns1:description """This project represents an important collaboration between the University of Hong Kong (HKU) and the M+ Museum of Hong Kong. It consists of a series of engaging activities, including lectures, mentorships, commissions, and performances realized from 2020 to 2022. This multi-phase initiative aims to foster meaningful conversations and inspire new compositions through a rich interplay of multimodality and cross-cultural exchange. By bringing together diverse perspectives and artistic practices, the project seeks to create a vibrant platform for innovation and dialogue within the arts community.  \r
\r
This project consists of three main phases:\r
1. Phase one – started in September 2021 with four virtual lectures,   conducted in a masterclass style, on composing for mixed ensembles inspired by ink art. The curator Dr. Lesley Ma 马唯中 (b. 1980) from the M+ of Hong Kong, Dr. Chen Yeung-ping from the South China Normal University, Prof. Chan Hing-yan 陳慶恩 (b. 1963) form the University of Hong Kong, and Dr. Shen Yiwen 沈逸文 from the Tianjin Juilliard School, each delivered a lecture to their mentees, the four composition students Austin Leung 梁騫仁, Samuel Mutter (b. 2001), OGA (b. 1999) and Wang Jing 王婧 (b.1992) selected from the Bard Conservatory of Music and the University of Hong Kong (HKU);\r
2. Phase two – a two-day conference titled “Ink and Sound: A Conference on Chinese Music and Visual Arts,” held at the Bard Conservatory in the USA on 9 and 10 April 2022. Inspired by the Ink Art Collection of M+ Museum, the new compositions by the selected students and their mentors were premiered by musicians of the Bard Conservatory of Music during the conference;\r
3. Phase three – a total of seven different musical compositions were created, inspired by both Chinese calligraphic and ink artworks: three commissioned compositions, created by composers of the University of Hong Kong (Chan Hing-Yan, Chen Yeung-Ping and Shen Yiwen 沈逸文, and four created by students selected from Bard Conservatory of Music and The University of Hong Kong: Austin Leung, Samuel Mutter, OGA and Wang Jing. The seven new compositions for mixed Chinese and Western instruments have then received their Asian Premiere in Hong Kong realized by the young talents of Ensemble Traversée.  In that occasion, images of the select artworks which the compositions are inspired by, were digitally projected to enhance the audience’s experience with ink and sound.  Many students’ composers and faculty composers were inspired by artists from Hong Kong’s so-called “New Ink Movement,” an artistic movement that emerged between the 1960s and 1980s, that revolutionized traditional ink art by integrating modern concepts and techniques, leaving a profound and lasting impact on Hong Kong’s art scene. As a British colony, periods of severed or limited ties with mainland China saw Hong Kong shift its cultural moorings toward Western Europe and North America. In this context, the artists carved out a unique trajectory that was distinct from traditional Chinese ink painting while acknowledging its roots, emphasizing individual expression over political, social, and historical dogma. This represents a very interesting starting point as regards artistic “contamination” between East and West.\r
Of these seven compositions involved in the project and their respective ink artworks, only four are closely inspired by calligraphy: 1) “Beyond Form” (Xiang wai 象外), inspired by the calligraphic work of the same title by Leung Kui Ting 梁巨廷 (b. 1945); 2) “Trace” by Jing Wang, inspired by an untitled work of the Chinese artist Li Yuan-chia 李元佳 (1929-1994); 3) “Song of Ink,” a composition by Chen Yeung-ping, inspired by five calligraphies by Tong Yang-tze; and 4) “Wild Grass,” a composition by Shen Yiwen ‘10, inspired by the work “Wild Grass II” by Hung Fai 熊辉 (b.1988). """ .

