    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1949> ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1951> ;
    ns1:description """On June 18, 2005, Zeng Laide and Rohan de Saram (1939-2024) participated in the international event called “The Music of Ink”, a completely experimental day held at the British Museum in London. Zeng Laide and Rohan de Saram met for the first time on the Tuesday before the event, June 14. They decided to perform a musicalligraphy piece, with Rohan de Saram playing the cello and Zeng Laide executing calligraphy. The performative combination of these two artists was entirely based on body language, as Rohan did not speak Chinese and Zeng did not speak English. \r
Rohan de Saram chooses to include Bach in his performance because he sees a deep connection between calligraphy and Bach’s music. He recognizes that calligraphy is clearly present in musical scores, which often reflect narrative and visual events, as in the case of Bach’s works. The structure and artistry of Baroque music, along with the graphic representation of notes, evoke the idea of “painting” with music, a concept that was common among musicians of that era. Furthermore, Bach represents an important reference point in the history of Western music, and his ability to combine complexity and beauty resonates with Zeng Laide’s intent to express aesthetic experiences through calligraphy. Thus, the choice of Bach serves to establish a link between tradition and innovation, between classical music and contemporary artistic forms. As Rohan explained:\r
"After Bach, there was very little written for the solo cello, especially in the nineteenth century, when composers were not really interested in unaccompanied string instruments. Then, in the twentieth century, there was the long piece for the solo cello by Kodály. In the past, whenever I have played this piece, especially the central slow movement, I have been transported to East Asian music, or perhaps I should say, to East Asian melody […]. Our rehearsals have been very shorts, and when I played various movements of Bach to see which Zeng Laide preferred, he chose the slower movements. So I shall start as he wishes with some of the slow movements, two sarabands of Bach, and then I shall do the last movement of Kodály and then go into the slow movement then I just mentioned" (Wang 2012, pp. 67-68).\r
Zeng Laide decides to transcribe a famous poem from the Tang dynasty written by Li Bai (701 – 762), titled “Cursive writing poetry” (Cao shu ge xing 草书歌行).  The poem, with its romantic tone and unique imagination, vividly recreates a scene of the calligrapher Huaisu drunkenly and passionately waving his brush, writing in a wild and exuberant manner. It carefully and wonderfully portrays Huaisu's bold and expressive personality traits. The entire poem vividly captures Huaisu’s free-spirited, passionate, and exuberant character. It holds significant reference value for later studies on Huaisu’s cursive script art. In this poem, Li Bai describes calligraphy as music. As Zeng Laide explained:\r
"In Chinese, the title of the poem is Cao shu ge xing. 'Ge xing' means music; ‘cao shu’ refers to a type of calligraphy, specifically the grass script style. Thus, the poem and the calligraphy bring together the classical, the contemporary, and the creative" (ibid., pp. 69-70).\r
"""@en ;
