        ns3:Q127418,
        ns3:Q23852,
        ns3:Q37681 ;
    ns2:P2047 "PT6H"^^xsd:duration ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11725> ;
    ns2:P585 "2016-10-08"^^xsd:date ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/279.jpg> ;
    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/100> ;
    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/327> ;
    ns1:description """In this performance, Zhang Qiang collaborates with a woman named Zhang Kaini. The performance takes place in Zhang Qiang's studio of Huxi University Town (Shapingba District, Chongqing, called Chongqing University Huxi Campus).\r
The performance consists of eight different stages:\r
1. Free and in-frame interactive writing (Ziyou yu kuangjia nei hudong shuxie 自由与框架内互动书写) where the act of writing takes place "within a frame" as established by Zhang Kaini's will. This wooden object, which looks like a frame, is the wooden structure of Zhang Qiang's collaboration with the Belgian artist Lia Wei used during the "Biface Graphy" (shuangmian shufa 双面书法). When the 100-metre roll is hung overhead, the finished section flows out from underneath, like a "long stream". Zhang Kaini and Zhang Qiang are standing within this "abandoned frame" and the performative act takes place within a confined space.\r
2. The scenery on the drawing table (Hua an shang de fengguang 画案上的风光)\r
Zhang Qiang joined two boxes of equal height and similar width to make a large horizontal long drawing table, which he usually uses to practice traditional calligraphy and Chinese painting. On this table there are the basic furnishings of a traditional Chinese literary studio, with brass and wooden paperweights, crystal brush holders, ink beds, water pots, brush sinks, printing beds, seals, clay for printing, etc. There are also brushes of various types, and paper and silk of various colors.  The studio doors are higher than the roof, like the size of a church-like western door (which is rarely seen in Chinese home decoration). When the two tall heavy doors are closed, a sense of mystery is created and when the two meters thick doors are half open, a special scene is created. When Zhang Qiang lights the sandalwood incense, the fragrance begins to permeate the room like the "lines of a calligraphic cursive". Kaini lays back on the table and begins to shift her movements as the sandalwood incense dances around her. This time, in a psychedelic atmosphere, the body begins to express its own language. In this way, a traditional calligraphic and classical style of painting becomes a platform for body language performance and expression. Inside and outside the tall, heavy doors, the dreamlike expressions of Kaini's performance become a theatrical ritual: Kaini's body movement launches classical Chinese painting and calligraphy into a labyrinth of amusement.\r
3. The meaning released by the "Study of ancient inscriptions" ("Jinshi xue" de yiwei shifang “金石学”的意味释放)\r
Stone inscriptions were widely widespread in ancient China and were made on wood, metal, stone and bamboo. The content of these inscriptions  has generally a historical and artistic value. Therefore, the surface of the supports of these inscriptions are full of natural, temporal and man-made "wounds", revealing for example traces of natural erosion (such as on cliffs). Zhang Qiang calls this phenomenon the "aesthetic of wounds" (Shangkou meixue 伤口美学). What is important is the meaning behind the "wounds" and the role of their time dimension. Hence, rather than an "aesthetic of wounds", it could also be termed as an "aesthetic of the time dimension'' (Shijian weidu di meixue 时间维度的美学). This is because the "wounds" of these natural supports were formed in different time and specific spaces. \r
In this phase of the performance, Zhang Kaini, originally from Shandong, interacts with three inscriptions of two of the most important calligraphers of Shandong: Zheng Daozhao 郑道昭 (? - 515)and the monk An Daoyi 安道壹 (?-580). In doing this, two different time dimensions come into contact: the past traces come back to life in a present performing action. The smiling silhouettes of Zhang Kaini, her ecstatic smiles, movements and facial expressions toward the inscriptions (the traces of the past) joined with the brushstrokes on her body and clothes (the traces of the present).\r
4. Framing body and strokes (Shenti kuangjia yu bifeng 身体框架与笔锋)\r
After the interactive writing in the wooden frame of the "Biface Graphy" (phase 1), in this phase of the performance Zhang Kaini began to conduct wanton action in this narrow space brandishing two huge brushes.\r
5. Performative writing, Traces of Desire and Body Vocabulary (Xingwei shuxie, yuwang zongji yu shenti cishu 行为书写、欲望踪迹与身体辞书)\r
During this fase, Zhang Kaini draws on a sheet of paper placed on the floor with two huge brushes and her body. While she writes consciously with her body on one side of the sheet, her hands unconsciously write on the other side of the sheet with the brushes. The sultry red high heels that Zhang Kaini is wearing have the same effect as seals in calligraphy, insinuating themselves firmly into various places. \r
In Zhang Qiang's Traceology Report, and also in this performance, the first thing that is subverted is the basic relationship between the subject and the object of representation (writing), where the subject writes and the paper anxiously awaits the writing. The writer who is also the performer, with her back to the object, abandons the expression of her will to write, while her body freely moves during the writing process, breaking the traditional writing relationship.\r
6.  Traces on face (Wenyang 纹样 )\r
In Chinese, the wen character "文" (wen) is interchangeable with "纹" (wen) and it indicates the action of "leaving a trace" by humans. Similarly, the word "样" not only has the meaning of "style/model/shape", but also indicates the aspect of a person's face. Zhang Qiang therefore uses the expression "纹样" for this part of the performance, because he leaves traces (wen 纹) on the face of a person (yang 样) who is Zhang Kaini. The ink, scattered on the face in a chaotic manner, interferes with the facial features, giving new expression to the subject through the performance action. \r
7. Paper shifts writing (Zhi mian nuoyi shuxie 纸面挪移书写)\r
In this phase, Zhang Qiang interacts with Zhang Kaini in the same way of the "Zhang Qiang Trace Report A/B Model" (1990 - 2000, see the "Series of Creative Works" in the archive). \r
8. Free styling/free form (Ziyou zaoxing 自由造型)\r
The so-called "free form" consists of arbitrary and random movements. This phase is based on accidental stimulations of a non-specific situation. The surprise of movement therefore depends entirely on inner strength, as well as on the coordination of imagination and movement. The selected backgrounds (e.g. a Persian carpet, a vase of Aloe plant, a black cloth, etc.)  reveal an unconscious choice between the "performing action" (xingwei dongzuo 行为动作) and the "selected shooting" (xuanze paishe 选择拍摄). For example, the movement of the Persian carpet motif in the background of some photographs of the performance easily recalls the notion of "free will" (ziyou yizhi 自由意志) and suggests that between the cultural psychological structure of the background motifs and the "individual freedom" (geti ziyou 个体自由) of the brush and ink, there is also the body of the performer as a mediating expression of the connection between the two. \r
\r
This performance is part of the "A/X series" (A/X 模型). This series consists of about thirteen "free performances" (ziyou xingwei “自由行为”) from 2015 to 2017. These performances place greater emphasis on the concepts and initiatives of the counterpart (Zhang Kaini, for example, pays close attention to express her thoughts during the performance). The A/X series is part of the A/C series but it is more experimental and "free", because puts more emphasis on the initiative of female collaborators.\r
A similar performance entitled: "Zhang Qiang Traceology Report A/X8 (South America) [张强踪迹学报告A/X8（南美)] took place at Sanya Bay Beach (in the south end of Hainan) on January 10, 2017. Photographs of the scene taken by professional photographers named Yan Lu and Zheng Jian clearly show how these South American dancers, under Chinese ink and brushstrokes, are able to reveal their sublime body language. """@en ;
