No.4 Jiuxianqiao Rd (Jiǔxiānqiáo Lù 酒仙桥路),  Beijing"""@en ;
    ns2:P88 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q483915> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/443.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The artwork “With Irresistible Force (Shirupozhu 势如破竹)” was created from February 4th to 6th, 2008 by EricTin, Nat, Yumi, and Quan at the Nike 706 Art Space in the 798 Art District. Marked by extensive use of charactering and Chinese calligraphy, the piece created by the Kwanyin Clan is part of a major project entitled “The Lebron Experience Center.” Sponsored by Nike China, the project promotes basketball in the country through outstanding American basketball figures. The project transformed the Nike 706 Art Space, established in January 2007 by Nike China to foster creativity, sport, and self-expression, into a modern interactive basketball court where the brand’s latest products were displayed, and videos of the famous American basketball player LeBron James, represented by a 3D hologram, were shown. In these video projections, the athlete described his six signature moves (dunk, steal, post-up, pass, fade away, and drive), explaining how they helped him become a champion. For the opening ceremony of the “Lebron Six Experiences” event, a huge hall of fame was mounted on the external wall of the art space to host six graffiti pieces. Each piece was created by a famous Chinese crew—Xianggang Fengwei, FLB crew, Kwanyin Clan, ABS crew, Beijing Penzi, and NGC crew—and focused on one of LeBron’s six signature moves. The subject of the Kwanyin Clan's piece was the “post-up,” known in Chinese as “tupo 突破.” Since the primary meaning of tupo 突破 is “to make a breakthrough, break through a defense,” the Kwanyin Clan chose the expression Shirupozhu 势如破竹 (With Irresistible Force) as the title of their work. \r
"With Irresistible Force" is a perfect example of the use of calligraphy and Chinese writing in the Kwanyin Clan’s work. The crew created a graffiti piece featuring Chinese characters embellished with calligraphic inscriptions and painted in the traditional colors of calligraphy—white, grey, black, and red (the color of seals). The central part of the work is composed of the four characters of the title (shirupozhu 势如破竹) written in a bold wild style. These characters form a Chinese "four-character idiom" (chengyu 成语) and are flanked by two other wild style pieces. The piece on the upper left is likely by EricTin, as indicated by the motif of the sphere with the traditional character “Guan 觀” in the center and the thin pinnacles sprouting from the intricate characters. The piece on the lower right is by Yumi, confirmed by the small tag written in red, and its undecipherable forms suggest a large fire whose flames burn and generate the entire artwork. The central piece is attributed to Natuo, as the letters of his name are interwoven into the strokes of the Chinese characters. In addition to the diagonal stripe composed of these three pieces, two other significant elements are present: the long black calligraphy in the top right corner and the red colophon in the bottom left corner. The insertion of the chengyu “shirupozhu 势如破竹” in the central piece of the work serves a specific purpose. As EricTin explains: "I like using four-character chengyu as the basis of my works because there is always a story behind them that exceeds the literal meaning of the four characters" (interview 2015). The literal meaning of shirupozhu is "having a power (shi) to (ru) cut down (po) a bamboo (zhu)." It originally refers to the act of smashing into enemy territory "like splitting a bamboo." This idiom describes incisive and "overwhelming power," referring to the traditional way of splitting bamboo by cutting a slit on the top, causing it to split in two halves. This reference to an "overwhelming power" likely alludes to the crew's motto, "push forward with an overwhelming momentum," recorded in their logo. This expression originates from the Book of Jin (Jinshu 晋书), specifically in the fourth chapter of the third section about Biographies (Lie zhuan 列传), which is the 34th volume (juan 卷) of the text. The second half of this chapter focuses on Du Yu 杜预 (222-285), a military general of the Wei state (220-265) during the late Three Kingdoms period (220-280) and early Jin Dynasty (265-420). In 265, the ruler of the Wei state abdicated in favor of Prince Sima Yan 司马炎 (236-290), who ended the Wei state and became the first emperor of the Jin Dynasty. Du Yu became one of his most powerful generals and led an army in the conquest of the Wu state (222-280), their bitter rival. In 280, he succeeded in reuniting the empire and used the expression, which literally means “with enough force to split bamboo,” to encourage his troops during the crucial battle before reunification. His speech had the desired effect. By using this chengyu, the Kwanyin Clan aims to connect Du Yu’s story with LeBron’s. In their different ways, both succeeded in "breaking through" due to their overwhelming force. \r
Another reference to battles and the tenacious stubbornness of soldiers is the long calligraphy on the upper right, written in xingcao 行草 style, a blend of semi-cursive and cursive script, arranged traditionally in vertical columns from right to left. According to EricTin (interview 2016), the content of this calligraphy is inspired by the poem "Full River Red" (Man jiang hong 满江红) composed by General Yue Fei (1103-1142). Yue Fei wrote this poem during one of the last battles against the northern barbarians, before the final withdrawal of his imperial army. In this poem, Yue Fei expresses his anger and wrath towards the enemy, as well as his regret for the emperor’s decision to withdraw his troops on the verge of reconquering lost lands. This inclusion of Yue Fei's poem underscores the themes of unwavering resolve and the longing for justice and retribution. It complements the overall message of the artwork by reinforcing the connection between historical military valor and contemporary achievements, drawing a parallel between Yue Fei's unyielding spirit and LeBron's relentless pursuit of greatness. The connection between Yue Fei’s courageous behavior described in the poem and the ‘post-up’ movement depicted in the graffiti work hinges on the four-character chengyu "shirupozhu" (势如破竹) portrayed in the center. Indeed, both the attitude of a basketball player in a post-up position and that of a general confronting enemies are characterized by "irresistible force". In basketball, to post-up means the player positions himself close to the basket with his back to the defender, using his body to shield the defender away and create space to receive a pass or make a shot. This action mirrors the determination to overcome obstacles and achieve a strategic advantage, much like Yue Fei's resolve to defend and reclaim territories with his army. Yue Fei’s desire to shield and advance to restore lands to the emperor resonates with the player’s effort to shield and position himself effectively on the court. Both scenarios exemplify the application of force with a purpose, whether in battle or in sports, encapsulated in the concept of "irresistible force" conveyed by the chengyu.\r
The presence of a red inscription on the lower left suggests a nod to the use of red seals in traditional Chinese art. This inscription combines Chinese characters and Latin letters, a characteristic seen in other works by the Kwanyin Clan. Vertically, the characters "Guan Yin 观音" are written in xingcao style (cursive-running script), resembling a traditional colophon with the artist's signature (Kwanyin) near the main calligraphy (the four characters "shirupozhu 势如破竹"). Horizontally, the four tags of the crew members who authored the artwork (ERICTIN, NAT, YUMI, QUANER) are written from left to right, following the typical format of graffiti pieces. Despite the difference in writing systems (Chinese characters versus Latin letters), the style of writing—both characters and letters—is consistent. This unified style blends Chinese and Western traditions seamlessly, showcasing a harmonious integration of cultural influences within the artwork. The parallel presentation of these inscriptions highlights the Kwanyin Clan's ability to merge diverse artistic elements into a cohesive and visually compelling composition, bridging East and West in their expression of graffiti art. \r
The choice of colors (black, white, and red) recalls the white paper and black ink of traditional Chinese calligraphy, with its different shades of gray, and the bright red of the seals creates a contrast of tones that balances the entire composition. In this case, red is used both to display the tags of the crew and the writers in the left corner of the work (Guanyin 观音 vertically and Erictin/Nat/Yumi/Quan Er horizontally) and to recall the title of the poem (Full River Red) and its content: the tumultuous battles and the meaning of the four-character chengyu that stands at the core of the entire work. The red flames at the bottom of the writing, from which the whole piece seems to arise, emphasize the fierce atmosphere of the battle. In this piece, the Kwanyin Clan created genuine cohesion between form and content: the upheaval of the battle and the burning passion expressed in the chengyu and in the poem are equally evoked by the rapid and passionate style of the calligraphy at the top, the use of the color red, and the same style used in the colophon at the bottom, as well as by the blazing flames and the impenetrable entanglement of wildstyle characters. White, black, and red are also the colors of the basketball court in the Nike 706 Art Space and of the Nike clothing worn by LeBron in the videos. The colors, shapes, and contents of the graffiti thus transport us into a new battlefield, one of new heroes and products breaking into the Chinese market and society."""@en ;
