    ns2:P580 "2019-03-25"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2019-04-08"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P664 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2901539> ;
    ns2:P856 <https://bub.unibo.it/it/bacheca/luo-qi-calligrafie-musicali-1> ;
    ns1:description """"Luo Qi: Writing Music" is the first personal exhibition of Luo Qi held in Bologna (Italy). The exhibition took place from March 25 to April 8, 2019 at the Library of the University of Bologna. It was curated by Adriana Iezzi and was organized by the Library of the University of Bologna and the Department of Interpreting and Translation of the University of Bologna. \r
Within the exhibition, the artist presents five series of calligraphic-musical works in the form of album sheets and long panels that cover the entire room of the Historic Hall of the Library and its entrance. The main series, entitled "Writing Music – Silent Melodies" (Xie yue – Wu sheng zhi ge写乐·无声之歌) , is a set of scrools in which the artist translates famous Italian songs such as “'O sole mio” and “Volare” and from opera arias such as “Rigoletto” and "La traviata" into a concatenation of calligraphic signs. These signs are inspired by one of the oldest  Chinese musical notation system named "Dunhuang Musical Notation System" (Dunhuang qupu/yuepu 敦煌曲谱/乐谱), dating back to the 10th century and made up of ancient characters. In these artworks, Luo Qi aims to representing the harmonious encounter between calligraphy and music and between Eastern and Western cultures. \r
During  the opening ceremony of the exhibition a musicalligraphy performance took place in the Historical Hall featuring Luo Qi and the Collegium Musicum Almae Matris. \r
"""@en ;
