        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8686> ;
    ns1:description """Zhang Huan is an internationally renowned Chinese artist, known for his pioneering work in performance art and sculpture, often focusing on the human body and its relationship with spirituality, pain, and transformation. In the 1990s, Zhang was one of the founders of the Beijing East Village, a small artistic community located on the outskirts of Beijing. This group of friends from art school became known for their radical and experimental performance art. Zhang’s works, in particular, often attracted the attention and censorship of Chinese authorities, who found them provocative and inappropriate.\r
Zhang’s performance art frequently revolves around his own body, usually naked, and explores themes such as endurance, suffering, and transformation. One of his most iconic works is "12 Square Meters" (1994), where Zhang sat naked in a small, prison-like room, his body covered with flies, and half of his head shaved. The performance reflected a raw meditation on pain and physical endurance, while the accompanying photographs portrayed a stoic yet vulnerable figure immersed in discomfort.\r
Another famous performance, "To Add One Meter to an Anonymous Mountain" (1995), featured Zhang and nine other artists climbing a mountain near Beijing, stripping off their clothes, and lying atop one another to form a second, symbolic peak. In "To Raise the Water Level in a Fishpond" (1997), Zhang invited 40 migrant workers to stand in a pond, their collective bodies raising the water level. These works reflect the artist’s desire to explore collective action and the relationship between the individual body and larger societal forces.\r
In 1998, Zhang moved to New York, where he continued his artistic exploration, still focusing on the naked body and endurance, but also confronting new cultural and personal challenges.\r
In 2006, Zhang returned to China and began to focus on sculpture after converting to Buddhism. His sculptural work retained his interest in the body, but with a deeper engagement with Buddhist philosophy and ritual. \r
Zhang has exhibited his works in numerous international events, including the 2002 Whitney Biennial and the "Rituals" exhibition at the Akademie der Künste in Berlin. His art continues to challenge the boundaries between body, spirituality, and cultural identity, blending personal suffering with universal themes of transformation and human endurance.\r
\r
Two most iconic performances including Chinese calligraphy are:\r
1) "1/2" (1998), in which Zhang Huan explores the duality of human nature, contrasting the rational and civil side with the primal and animalistic. Using the body as a medium to express the separation between mind and body, society, and culture, the work also reflects the artist's transcultural experience, torn between Asian and Western cultures (see the archive).\r
2) "Family Tree" (2000), where Zhang Huan invites three calligraphers to write words and phrases on his face, reflecting on cultural and political themes. As his face darkens with the setting sun, the writing, which includes references to Chinese fables and divination, progressively obscures his identity. The performance explores the tension between the body as a medium of communication and the impact of cultural conditioning on individual identity, questioning how an artist can redefine themselves beyond inherited traditions (see the archive).""" ;
