    ns2:P856 <https://www.xubing.com/en/work/details/206?type=project#206> ;
    ns1:description """Xu Bing’s "Book from the Sky" is a set of 4 books printed with more than a thousand different characters invented by the artists wihout any real meaning. The title also pertains to an installation whereby the book sets are amalgamated with scrolls cascading from the ceiling and expansive wall panels adorned with the artist's indecipherable characters. \r
The beginning of this work was in 1987, when Xu Bing closeted himself in his dormitory room-studio at the Central Academy of Fine Arts in Beijing, devoting a year to creating the first printed version of the "Book from the Sky", composed of 1250 invented characters. He systematically recombined elements drawn from Chinese characters with the aim of inventing new characters devoid of meaning. He then carved them into small wooden blocks suitable for typesetting and printed several forms of books, scrolls, and wall panels. In October 1988, he firstly diplayed them as an installation in his solo exhibition at Beijing's China Art Gallery. In February 1989, the "Book from the Sky" installation was also displayed at the China Art Gallery in Beijing as a centerpiece of the "China/Avant-Garde" exhibition - the first important exhibition of the '85 New Wave art movement.  After the first exhibition in 1988, Xu Bing decided to make a new and better set of characters, carving one thousand large and one thousand small characters and working with a small factory in Daxing to rpint and bind the sets of four books. These are the books that Xu Bing has exhibited as the "Book from the Sky" since 1991, after immigrating to the United States in 1990 (Erickson 2001, 33, 37, 40). \r
Since that time, the "Book from the Sky" installation has been composed of 3 elements strictly linked to Chinese written tradition: handscrolls, wall panels, and books.  The handscrools take the form of Chinese texts from two thousand years ago and are evocative of Buddhist sutra. The wall panels refer to hanging scrools of calligraphy and to wall posters that appeared throughout Chinese Cultural Revolution. The books are laid out in the form of historical books, printed on sheets of xuan paper, folded and bound with string binding according to the tradition, and the characters are rendered in Song style, a popular print style of the Ming dynasty (1368-1644) thet is very plain and clear (ibid., 44-45). This meticulous attention to detail pays homage to the rich history of Chinese scholarship and exegesis preserved in printed form. Xu Bing, weary of the endless philosophical debates surrounding modernism, pseudo-modernism, and postmodernism, that consumed young intellectuals in Beijing in the late 1980s, recognized that, after the Cultural Revolution that had been fueled by words, at that time, with the import of Western texts, words were again consuming young minds. This is why he decided to devote five years of his life to invent and carve unmeaningless characters in order to write unreadable books made of words devoid of any meaning. \r
The "Book from the Sky" installation, that consists of suspended scrolls, books, and printed panels arranged in an immersive environment, creates an interplay of space and form. The installations explore the tension between tradition and modernity, weight and emptiness, structure and ambiguity. Xu Bing’s conceptual deconstruction of text—rendering it visually compelling but devoid of semantic content—invites viewers to reconsider the act of reading itself. The artist uses this installation to challenge viewers' perceptions of language and meaning, prompting a reflection on the nature of communication in contemporary society.\r
The entire work was originally titled "Mirror to Analyze the World: The Century’s Final Volume" (Xi shi jian—Shiji mojuan 析世鉴－世纪末卷 ).  However, the artist decided to adopt the name that was already in popular use, "Tianshu". In Chinese, the term "tian shu" (divine writing) originally referred to certain kinds of religious texts, but is now used to mean "gibberish"; it has thus been suggested that nonsense writing would be a more appropriate translation of the title.\r
In 2021 Xu Bing and i-Space launched the "Xu Bing Tianshu Rocket", a valuable contribution to the constantly developing field of space art. This unprecedented "art rocket" pays tribute to the achievements of Chinese astronauts and embodies the innovative spirit in Xu Bing's works. The rocket is painted with fabricated text from the original work of "Book from the Sky", which is like a container, constantly questioning the randomness and limitations of language.\r
\r
""" ;
