    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q239>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q90> ;
    ns1:description """After a couple of years spent between architecture and anthropology studies in Brussels, she moved to China in 2007, to continue studying calligraphy and sigillography in China Academy of Arts, Hangzhou. She then moved to Chongqing in 2009, where she studied Chinese Painting and Printmaking.\r
In 2014-2015, Lia Wei lectured at the Art Theory department in Sichuan Fine Arts Institute on the ‘History of Sinology: The Study of East Asian Art in the West’, and at the Archaeology department at Renmin University of China on ‘Comparing Values in Cultural Heritage: Landscape, Identity and Authenticity’. In 2015-2017, she taught the MA ‘Art and Archaeology of the Silk Road’ at the department of the History of Art and Archaeology SOAS. In 2013- 2017, she launched a collaborative project in creative archaeology with geographer Rupert Griffiths entitled ‘Site_Seal_Gesture’.\r
Lia Wei has obtained her PhD, entitled ‘Rock-cut burials along the Upper Yangzi River, 2nd to 3rd century CE’ at the School of Oriental and African Studies (SOAS), University of London, and has accepted an appointment at the department of Archaeology in Renmin University of China, Beijing, from September 2018. This year, she joined the EAst/ULB platform and delivered a series of conferences entitled ‘Regard sur la Tradition Lettrée’ at Université Libre de Bruxelles, which combined theoretical and practical sessions on calligraphy, sigillography and landscape painting.\r
In April 2018, Lia Wei acted as academic supervisor for the first edition of the Ink Art Week in Venice, including a collective exhibition of contemporary ink art entitled Ink Riders in Magazzino del Sale n°3 hosted by the Accademia delle Belle Arti di Venezia and a conference hosted by Ca’ Foscari University. She coordinates the next edition of the Ink Art Week, planned for May 6th to May 11th in Brussels, Belgium."""@en ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Academic"@en ;
    ns1:isCalligrapherOf <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/367> .

