    ns1:description "“Cursive” is a contemporary dance choreography performed for the first time on December 1, 2001, at the “National Theater” of Taipei (Taiwan) and then staged again on September 6, 2009, in the same place.  With the study of the dancers and the awareness of their bodies, the choreographer Lin Hwai-min discovers that this type of study went beyond the very concept of dance, outlining itself as a real dance on the aesthetics of calligraphy. It is a journey to explore the body, which contains a set of unique languages never experienced before. The movements of the dancers’ bodies all originate from the inside out, just like calligraphy, which emphasizes the movement of the brush in contact with the air, leaving empty spaces around.  The ten chapters comprising “Cursive” were performed by twenty-two dancers. They dance in black costumes on a white stage, like ink on rice paper. The female dancers wore black jumpsuits and the male dancers loose black pants that were asymmetrical, with the right leg narrow and the left leg broad. Videos and presentations of traditional calligraphy are projected onto a huge white screen behind the dancers: calligraphies by Wang Xizhi 王羲之 (303–361), Huai Su 怀素 (737–799), Zhang Xu 张旭 (fl. 8th century) and other famous masters of past dynasties are presented to the audience in large scale, merging with the movements of the dancers in the foreground floor. The background music for “Cursive” was composed by internationally renowned contemporary composer Qu Xiaosong 瞿小松 (b. 1952), and was recorded in Heidelberg, Germany with an ensemble of cellos and percussion, creating a moving tension. Qu Xiaosong leaves a lot of empty space in the musical composition, merging calligraphy and dance into one and unique flow. The first chapter of the performance begins with a single female dancer in the corner of the stage with no music. She dances without fully stretching the torso and limbs. The movement of the body is very slow, concentrated, and precise, giving rise to involuntary physical vibrations, the so-called “qi vibration” (qidong 汽动). In the second chapter, female dancer Zhou Zhangning 周章佞 [Chou Chang-ning] dances in front of a projection of the Chinese character yong 永 (eternal).The yong font contains all the basic variations of brush strokes and gestural vocabularies commonly used in the standard style (kaishu 楷书), cursive style (caoshu草书), and wild cursive style (kuangcao 狂草) in Chinese calligraphy. The order of the movement of the brush serves as a sequential score of the dancer’s movement. She repeatedly writes/dances the yong character with her whole body. The gesture of writing in Chinese calligraphy is the metamorphosis of nature. Yong as the diagram of the foundation of the brushstrokes refers to: “a cloud formation stretching a thousand li; indistinct, but not without form; like a stone falling from a high peak, bouncing and crashing, about to shatter the ground; a withered vine, ten thousand years old; crashing waves or rolling thunder”. During the third chapter, a group of seven dancers vocalize their fast movement amplifying the sound of their breathing without music. The light gradually fades as the dancers continued to vocalize breathing and moving. In the fourth chapter, a male dancer starts dancing at the back of the stage, with a female dancer opposite. At first, they dance in unison, but the female moves by often resting her lower body on the floor, while the male stands in a higher position. The female gradually begins to move towards the male’s territory, dancing in front of him. Both dancers implement sequences of independent movements without physical contact and feed the tension between uniting and keeping apart, echoing the principles of Yin 阴 and Yang 阳, two opposite but interpenetrating parties that cannot live without each other. In the next fifth chapter, a nearly naked male sits on the floor behind a transparent screen with his back to the audience. The projected freeze frame is the beitie 碑帖 (a rubbing of an inscribed script on stone) of the white cursive words on a black background reproducing the poem entitled “Li Sao 离骚” (Encountering Sorrow) realized by Mi fu 米芾 (1051–1107). This is an ancient Chinese poem from the anthology Chuci traditionally attributed to Qu Yuan 屈原 (c. 340 – 278). On the dark stage, the dancers’ skin becomes a surface that absorbs light and reveals the calligraphic characters. As the dancers move their torsos, the characters become “liquid”, flowing over their skin. When the characters touch the dancers’ skin, the characters become brighter. A red circle faded in on the stage while the dancers transited into lower still postures, with the whole stage’s lighting faded out. The ninth chapter is a solo female dance by Chou Chang-ning 周章佞. Instead of dancing simultaneously with the projected character, this time Zhou dances in front of the panel placed in the centre of the stage, which portrays the work pan 磐 (hard stone) created by Tong Yang-Tze 董陽孜. The long black dress with 510 cm long sleeves worn by Chou Chang-ning is a reference to the elements of Chinese opera. Zhou begins with her back to the audience, then turns to the audience, which she resonated with the Chinese opera theatrics of liang xiang 亮相 (lit. “strike a pose on the stage”). Zhou moves the heavy long sleeves to evoke weightlessness, like a flying dragon and a dancing phoenix moving like a brush on the soft rice paper. "@en ;
