    ns1:description """The installation "The Bamboo Path" (Zhu jing 竹径) by Wang Dongling was first shown in 2017 as part of the exhibition "Wang Dongling: The Bamboo Path (王冬龄：竹径)" held at the OCAT Shenzhen Pavillion. The work pays  tribute to bamboo and immerses the viewer in a recreated natural environment. It consists of a short path, more than 20 meters long, crossing a bamboo grove with 201 bamboo poles and 30 propylene panels. Poles and panels are inscribed by the artist in various calligraphic styles. This installation examplifies Wang Dongling's exploration of diverse materials and supports  for his calligraphy. Writing on bamboo evokes the practices of pre-Qin scholars, who primarily used bamboo strips to read, write, and study. At the same time, the work emphasizes the cultural significance of bamboo in Chinese tradition, as a symbol of righteousness and a noble spirit in the eyes of the literati.\r
The installation evokes multive narratives from the Chinese cultural past. It recalls the Seven Sages of the Bamboo Grove (Zhulin Qi Xian 竹林七贤), a group of third-century scholars whose gatherings deeply influenced Chinese poetry, music, and art. The bamboo grove also resonates the Wang Xizhi's "Orchid Pavilion Preface" (Lanting Xu), a calligraphic masterpiece in which the calligrapher describes a poetic gathering among literati. During this event, participants composed verses while drinking wine in a landscape surrounded by high mountains, steep hills, dense forests and tall bamboos (chong shan jun ling, mao lin xiu zhu 崇山峻領, 茂林脩竹 ). On another occasion, the Tang dynasty poet Li He (790-816)  wrote the "Songs of Chu" (Chu Ci 楚辞) on bamboo. Similarly, Wang Dongling extends this lineage by writing his calligraphy on bamboo poles, measuring 5 m high and 12-15 cm in diameter. In addition, propylene panels with Chinese calligraphy written using white acrylic paint enrich the installation. The black ink on the bamboo poles interacts visually with the write acrylic on the propylene transparent panels. This creates a dialogue between different surfaces and materials, while projected shadows of the Wang's Chaos Script  intensify a sense of movement and rhytm.  \r
In Chinese traditional culture, bamboo is one of the "Four Gentlemen" (si junzi 四君子) together with the plum blossom, orchid, and chrysanthemum.  Flexible enough to withstand storms without breaking, bamboo has long been personified as a spiritual and cultural symbol of integrity, purity, modesty, and restraint, characteristics that Chinese gentlemen were expected to embody.\r
\r
The bamboo poles, together with 30  transparent proprylene panels of  the exhibition ”Wang Dongling: The Bamboo Path”, were also reused in the "Zi·Canglang ting (自·沧浪亭)" exhibition held in Suzhou in 2018 and in the "Between Bamboo (竹·间)" solo exhibition  held in Suzhou in July 2019. \r
\r
Calligraphies in white acrylic paint on transparent propylene panels were also displayed during other exhibitions and performances, such as:\r
- the performance "Zhuangzi · Enjoyment in Untroubled Ease (庄子 · 逍遥游)" at the "'Edge of The Earth' - Asian Contemporary Art Invitation Exhibition" ('De zhi yuan' - Yazhou dangdai yishu kaimu "地之缘" - 亚洲当代艺术開幕) held in Hangzhou, 2003;\r
- the "Dao Xiang-Wang Dongling Calligraphy Art Exhibition" (Dao xiang · Wang Dongling shufa yishu zhan 道象 · 王冬龄书法艺术展 ) held at the Imperial Ancestral Temple Art Museum in Beijing, 2016;\r
 - the calligraphic performance “The Way that Can Be Spoken” (Dao Ke Dao  道可道) at the Victoria and Albert Museum (2016) during the London Craft Week.\r
\r
"""@en ;
