    ns1:calligrapher <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/592> ;
    ns1:description """This “in concert” performance took place on May 30, 2011, in the Carlo de Carli Hall - Milan Bovisa Campus of the Politecnico di Milano as the inaugural event for the exhibition “Sans papier/son papier”, held from May 27 to July 14. An original exhibition at the Polytechnic University of Milan-Bovisa uses paper to define the utopia and the contradictions of contemporary society starting from the hopes of the “sans papier”. Paper, the privileged material of the culture of recycling, takes on multiple symbolic meanings, from the exploration of new forms of expression and construction to the search for identity and citizenship of the “sans papier”, migrants who aspire to integration into the societies of the rich West. In this exhibition, twenty-four paintings on handmade paper and cardboard prototypes of works by artists such as the creator of the project Gabriele Amadori, Giuseppe Spagnulo (1936-2016), Rodolfo Aricò (1930-2002), Davide Cantoni (b. 1965), Angela Glajcar (b. 1970), Hans Hermann (1858 – 1942) and Daniele Nitti Sotres (b. 1977) were exhibited. Some of the installations were created by the students of Gabriele Amadori, then professor of scenography at the Polytechnic of Milan. The concert “Song Papier” was written and directed by the jazz music master Enrico Intra who directed an ensemble composed of the students’ choir of the Polytechnic and percussionists (seven members of the “Scuola Civica di Jazz” of Milan and seven neophytes of the Polytechnic) engaged in playing paper objects, deliberately chosen among objects of common use, in homage to the philosophy of John Cage who said: “Everything is music”. They were then joined by the voice of Luana Tallarita and the choreography and dance of Irene Stellini, accompanied by the brush strokes of Silvio Ferragina and Gabriele Amadori.\r
The performance begins with seven sheets of paper placed in front of seven performers (men and women) who in turn wear clothes made of paper. Next to them, there is a group of percussionists (on the left), and a group of female choristers also dressed in clothes made of paper (on the right). At first, a female dancer, Irene Stellini, begins to perform, also clad in a dress made of paper with special cut strips, which sway to the rhythm of the notes played on the piano by jazz musician Enrico Intra. Silvio Ferragina then begins to calligraph with a brush and black ink on the seven sheets of paper placed in front of the performers. The characters he realized in zhuanshu are “meiyou shenfen没有身分” (lit. there is no identity). The Chinese word shenfen (identity, status, capacity) is used by the artist to express the concept conveyed by the exhibition itself, namely the search for identity and a place in society by the “sans papier”.  At a later stage, the artist Gabriele Amadori enters the scene and begins to paint some horizontal lines of vermilion red on the sheets of paper previously calligraphed by Silvio, giving life to a mix of black and red lines and graphic signs. At a third stage, Gabriele Amadori begins to paint with blue paint on the paper clothes worn by the seven performers and with white paint on the clothes worn by the choral group. Silvio Ferragina joins again and begins to calligraph on the clothes of the choristers, but with black ink. At this point, the two artists return to painting on the sheets of paper, mixing the black ink and the red paint on the paper support again. At the end, the singer Luana Tallarita performs dressed in black and gold, accompanying the movements of the dancer Irene Stellini.\r
Using paper as a symbolic material, these events explored the hopes and contradictions of the “sans papier,” offering a space for artistic expression that transcends cultural barriers. The synergy between calligraphy, dance, and music created an immersive environment, engaging the audience in a multisensory experience. The works of Silvio Ferragina and Gabriele Amadori, enriched by the dance of Irene Stellini and the voice of Luana Tallarita, demonstrated how art can engage with contemporary themes, stimulating a profound reflection on identity and integration.\r
In this context, the performance was not only a moment of entertainment but also a platform for discussion and exploration of the challenges faced by migrants in modern society. The fusion of different art forms thus created new narratives that invite a more empathetic understanding of human experiences, highlighting the importance of collaboration and creativity in building an inclusive future.\r
\r
"""@en ;
