<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1561> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1891>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q597> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Writing Music – Silent Melodies (写乐·无声之歌)"@en,
        "Calligrafie musicali – Melodie silenti"@it,
        "写乐·无声之歌"@zh,
        "Xiě yuè · wúshēng zhī gē"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/84> ;
    ns2:P580 "2018"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1561_1.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1561_2.jpg>,
        <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/1561_3.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """This series is composed of three different types of works: 1. paper folding books hung as long vertical scrolls or displayed as accordion fold album (2018, 2023); 2. big vertical scrolls in plastics (2018, 2023); 3. small square works in ink on paper (2018).  \r
In all of these works Luo Qi transcribes famous Italian arias, operas and songs using the oldest Chinese musical notation system, the "Dunhuang Musical Notation System" (Dunhuang qupu / yuepu 敦煌曲谱/乐谱). This system dating back to the 10th century consists of twenty symbols derived from ancient characters, traditionally used for notating the tablature for the pipa (Chinese lute). These symbols are reshaped according to the artist's personal and original interpretation, in a way that these musical compositions are transformed into a “visual and visible musical score,” strongly rhythmic and dynamic, with an inextricably calligraphic thread. \r
Among the 12 paper folding books hung as long vertical scrolls (2018), six are inspired by famous Italian operas, (“Rigoletto”, “La traviata”, “Turandot”, “Otello”, “Tosca” and “Madama Butterfly”), and the others are inspired by 6 famous Italian songs (“Santa Lucia”, “’O sole mio”, “Mamma”, “Torna a Surriento”, “Volare” and “Core ‘ngrato”). In addition to these 12 paper folding books, in 2023 Luo Qi also created two small folding books inspired by "The Barber of Seville" to be displayed as accordion fold albums.\r
In 2018 Luo Qi created 3 big vertical scrolls in plastic inspired by three famous Italian arias, which are “Se l'aura spira” by Girolamo Frescobaldi (1583 –1643), “Pur dicesti, o bocca bella” by Antonio Lotti (1667 – 1740) and “Amarilli, mia bella” by Giulio Caccini (1546-1618). These works are made of white acrylic paint on plastic sheets. In 2023 he realized again the same pieces, using white and black acrylic paint on plastic sheets.\r
Among the 20 small square works in ink on paper (2018), six are inspired by famous italian operas:  “La donna è mobile” from Rigoletto by Verdi, “Turandot” by Puccini, “Un bel dì, vedremo” from Madama Butterfly by Puccini, “Vissi d’arte” from Tosca by Puccini, “Sì. Mi chiamano Mimì” from Bohème by Puccini, and “O Mio Babbino Caro” from Gianni Schicchi by Puccini; one is inspired by a famous Neapolitan song "’O sole mio” by Giovanni Capurro, Eduardo di Capua e Alfredo Mazzucchi, and other famous arias of the 17th and 18th centuries, such as “Danza, danza, fanciulla gentile” and “Vergin, tutto amor” by Francesco Durante (1684-1755), “Per la gloria d’adorarvi” by Giovanni Bononcini (1670-1747), “Sebben crudele” by  Antonio Caldara (1670-1736), “Lasciar d’amarti” and “Caro laccio, dolce nodo” by Francesco Gasparini (1668-1727), “Ombra mai fu” and “Lascia ch'io pianga” by Georg Friedrich Händel (1685-1759), “Pur dicesti, o bocca bella” by Antonio Lotti (1667-1740), “Consolati, e spera” and “Le violette” by Alessandro Scarlatti (1660-1725),  “Tu lo sai” by Giuseppe Torelli (1658-1709), and a more recent “Serenata” by Enrico Toselli (1883-1926). \r
\r
There are two other series related to these works: the first is "Preface about the Dunhuang Musical Notation System” (Dunhuang qupu tiji 敦煌曲谱题记), which consists of eight pieces in which the artist illustrates the "Dunhuang musical manuscripts", the notation system they employ, and the genesis of the exhibited works inspired by them. The second is “Notes on Dunhuang Musical Notation System” (Dunhuang qupu biji 敦煌曲谱笔记), comprising 20 works in which the artist transcribes 20 of the 25 melodies from the Dunhuang P.3808 manuscript. This series represents an interpretative rewriting, where he applies his personal style to the symbols that form the Dunhuang notation system.\r
\r
The vertical folding books and the plastic sheets with white acrylic were displayed for the first time during the exhibition "The Music of Ink - Calligraphies by Luo Qi and Silvio Ferragina in dialogue with the Chinese archive collection of the Braidense Library" held in 2018 at the National Braidense Library. The 20 small square works were displayed for the first time during the exhibition "Luo Qi: Writing Music" held in 2019 at the University Library of Bologna. The two small folding books and the plastic sheets with black and white acrylics were realized for the exhibition "Sounds of Ink: Luo Qi and Thirty Years of Calligraphyism" held in 2023 at the International Museum of Music in Bologna. """ ;
