    ns1:createsArtwork <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/253> ;
    ns1:description """In this performance, Qiu Zhijie decides to devote himself  to copying the work "Lanting xu 兰亭序" (Orchid Pavilion Preface) composed by Wang Xizhi in 353 AD. Qiu Zhijie repeatedly copies the work a thousand times on the same piece of rice papersheet. Repeatedly writing over his free-hand interpretation of the original, Qiu turns the paper into a saturated black field. By focusing on the process of writing rather than on its literary content, Qiu asserts that the ultimate goal of calligraphic practice is a form of "written meditation".\r
By continuing to overwrite, even once the surface is completely blackened, Qiu Zhijie arrives at the total annihilation of the work, its non-existence. In this way, Qiu underlines how the work can not be viewed, like a tangible document, but it resides only in the memory and imagination of individuals who have handed it down. Calligraphy becomes a metaphor for social orthodoxy and cultural reproduction, reducing culture to a mere mechanical practice, in which there is no possibility of criticism and renewal.\r
Qiu Zhijie also carries out a very similar operation in another performance entitled "Heart Sutra" (Xin jing 心 经, 1999), copying the ancient Buddhist text of the Heart Sutra. In this performance, Qiu Zhijie copies the work hundred times until the page is completely obscured. Here the artist wonders about the veracity and reliability of a translated text (from Sanskrit) like the Chinese Heart Sutra that represents a fundamental text for the spread of Chan Buddhism in China.\r
\r
"""@en ;
