    ns2:P1476 "Ink Art: Past as Present in Contemporary China"@en ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1384>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q160236> ;
    ns2:P580 "2013-12-11"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2014-04-06"^^xsd:date ;
    ns1:description """"Ink Art" constitutes the first major exhibition of Chinese contemporary art ever mounted by the Metropolitan Museum of Art. Installed within the Museum’s permanent galleries of Chinese art, the exhibition offers a critical investigation into how contemporary works from a non-Western cultural context may be presented within an encyclopedic museum. The selected artworks are not approached as a radical departure from tradition, but rather as part of a continuous cultural and historical lineage deeply embedded in Chinese artistic practice.\r
While these works may also be interpreted through the lens of global contemporary art, the exhibition demonstrates that a reading grounded in Chinese historical artistic paradigms reveals additional layers of meaning and cultural significance that might otherwise remain obscured. The curatorial framework thus deliberately integrates transnational perspectives with culturally specific interpretations, suggesting that both viewpoints are essential to a more nuanced understanding of the artists’ creative processes.\r
For more than two millennia, ink has served as the principal medium of painting and calligraphy in China. Since the early twentieth century, however, the primacy of the “ink art” tradition has been increasingly challenged by artistic practices and media introduced from the West. Ink Art examines the work of a selected group of Chinese artists active from the 1980s to the present, whose practices have fundamentally transformed inherited traditions while maintaining an underlying identification with the expressive language of China’s artistic past.\r
The exhibition features approximately seventy works by thirty-five artists, encompassing a wide range of media, including painting, calligraphy, photography, woodblock printing, video, and sculpture, all produced over the past three decades. Organized into four thematic sections—The Written Word, New Landscapes, Abstraction, and Beyond the Brush—the exhibition highlights diverse strategies of reinterpretation, subversion, and expansion of traditional models. Ultimately, Ink Art affirms that China’s long-standing paradigm of cultural renewal through the reengagement with historical forms remains a vital and productive creative trajectory in contemporary artistic practice.""" ;
