<http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/524> a ns3:Q7725310 ;
    ns2:P1071 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q14773> ;
    ns2:P1476 "Uterus Series (子宫系列)"@en,
        "子宫系列"@zh,
        "Zǐgōng xìliè"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P170 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/526> ;
    ns2:P580 "2020"^^xsd:gYear ;
    ns2:P582 "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/524.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """The "Uterus Series" (Zigong xilie 子宫系列) takes place between 2020 and 2022. The series is a performative action composed of different performances that use calligraphy as a means of expression. Each performance tells a different female story: Wu Xixia writes Chinese characters while she is inside a hermetic transparent plastic sphere that symbolizes the mother's womb. As the performance progresses, the amount of oxygen is gradually depleted, until the sphere fills only with carbon dioxide. At that point, the artist requests that the sphere be broken before suffocating. The nightgown and bruises on her body, represent the state of domestic violence that women have been subjected to for thousands of years by men. “Writing” is an important symbol for this series and the use of different styles and brushes has specific meanings. \r
Before this series, the artist experiments with different media to explore the issue of gender. At first, the artist wraps her body and women objects with plastic film to express the concept of "suffocation" [e.g. “Plastic Women Series" (Su nu xilie 塑女系列) 2015-2018] then in 2021 she started using hermetic spheres during her performances, such as:\r
\r
1) "I" (Wo 我, 2020) in which Wu Xixia carries out a reflection on women and herself, writing the labels of society’s goodwill and malice towards women in red paint, such as "whore" (biao zi 婊子) "old maid" (lao chunu 老处女), "good wife and loving mother" (xian qiliang mu 贤妻良母), "green tea bitch (luchabiao 绿茶婊), "teenage girl" (yunu 玉女), "white lotus" (bailian hua 白莲花), etc. Through this performance, she wants to answer questions such as: "Who are women? And who am I? Is my gender determined by birth or is it given by society? There is a womb here, but where is the "mother"?". Having been rejected by her family at the time of her birth as a female, she has been questioning her gender throughout her formative years, and the social labels she writes about are an attempt to reconcile herself with her gender identity (see the archive).\r
\r
2)"Writing the Orchid Pavilion One Time" (Shuxie Lanting Xu yi bian 书写兰亭序一遍, 2021) in which calligraphy becomes an important symbol of the patriarchy. In this performance, the famous twenty different forms of zhi 之 character present in the original "Orchid Pavilion Preface" by Wang Xizhi are replaced by Wu Xixia with red images of male phalluses. The zhi 之 character, that is one of the most important in Chinese masculine calligraphic tradition, is used by Wu Xixia as a symbol of order and authority (quanwei 权威) (see the archive).\r
\r
3) "Writing the Orchid Pavilion for the Second Time" (Shuxie Lanting Xu di er bian 书写兰亭序第二遍, 2022) in which calligraphy again becomes a patriarchal symbol. Previously, on the inflatable outer part of the perimeter surrounding the sphere, ideomatic phrases were written such as: "chi bao cheng 吃饱撑" (having nothing better to do); "xingwei yishu 行为艺术" (performance art); "huazhong quchong 哗众取宠" (try to please the public with claptrap), etc. In this case, Wu Xixia writes inside an airtight sphere placed in the water and the character "ye 也" is replaced with the image of female vagina. Wu Xixia also writes some larger characters in "nüshu 女书" (women's script), a syllabic script derived from Chinese characters that was used exclusively among women in Jiangyong County in Hunan province of southern China. Using this characters' form, she wants to counterbalance the male predominance of Chinese writing with the female writing of nüshu 女书 (see the archive). \r
\r
4) "Darling" (2021) in which Wu Xixia performs in front of a building called "Darling". The building was covered with red ribbons, which spread all the way to the inflatable ball. Wu Xixia writes the words "jiu jiu wo 救救我" (help me); "qiuqiu ni 求求你" (I beg you), "bie sha wo 别杀我" (don't kill me); "bie S wo  别S我" (don't fuck me), etc. inside the hermetic sphere. During the performance, passers-by, especially men, took photos with various poses and angles nearby. In this “public act”, the female body transforms into an object of desire and “objectification” through the male gaze. For over 40 minutes, the artist lies still on the ground, inviting the audience's scrutiny while she begins to write down pleas for help (see the archive).\r
\r
5) "Right" (Zhengque 正确, 2021) where Wu Xixia invites the audience to write what they think is "right" outside the ball, while the artist repeatedly writes inside the ball the two characters "zhengque 正确" using the "large character calligraphy style" (da zi shuti 大字书体) until she is completely "enveloped" by the two characters. In this work, the acrylic paints and characters that read "Please write down what you think is right" on the ground attracted the attention of many viewers. Audiences of different ages and genders wrote words such as "justice" (zhengyi 正义)  and "love" (ai 爱) on the outside of a transparent airtight ball, and the artist inside the ball repeatedly wrote the word "correct" with red paint. The use of the large character calligraphy style also implies the existence of authority. The artist wants to focus on presenting the "gender troubles" in contemporary society. "How to define what is right?" and "Who defines what is right?" become the explicit themes of the performance, and the artist's gaze and being stared at become the invisible themes behind it.\r
\r
6) "Letter to Mom" (Yu mu shu 与母书, 2022) in which Wu Xixia interacts with twenty children. Before starting the performance, the artist guides the children to think and create characters or drawings, randomly combining images relating to figures or names that recall moments with their mother. The aim is to create a language that belongs only to the children and their mothers, and then write it outside the sphere. At the same time, Wu Xixia writes the Chinese character "ma 妈" (mother) inside the sphere in the traditional form.\r
\r
7) “Opposite/Image” (Dui/Xiang 对/像, 2023) where she is inside a hermetic red cube made of plastic and invites viewers to come closer to trace the outline of their body on the external surface of the cube. In the meantime, Wu Xixia composes the two characters “dui 对” and “xiang 像” on the inner surface of the sphere while observing what happens externally.\r
\r
8) “Confrontation” (Duihua 对话, 2023) which takes place at the “17th Macau International Live Art Festival (MIPAF)” (Di shiqi jie Aomen guoji xianchang yishu jie 第十七届澳门国际现场艺术节 - MIPAF), where Wu Xixia begins to write on a red humanoid mannequin with a brush the white characters “human” (ren 人) and “art” (yishu 艺术) to “give life” to it, announcing the beginning of the confrontation (see the archive).\r
\r
9) “Silencer” (Xiaoyin 消音, 2023) where the artist writes a series of words in an inflatable sphere and recites them through a loudspeaker, inviting spectators on site (both kids and adults) to participate.\r
Wu Xi Xia wrote about many women's secrets and the shame that keeps them "silenced". She collected some anonymous and shameful experiences of women not being accepted by society and wrote them into the sphere (see the archive).\r
\r
Some of these spheres then became part of the installation exposed in Beijing in 2021 in occasion of the "Xixia Wu Personal Art Exhibition" (Wu Xixia ge zhan 吴析夏个展 - December 6 to 20, 2021) called "Her Mountain" (Ta shan 她山, see the archive). In this installation, Wu Xixia creates a "mountain of blood" full of symbols linked to childbirth and the female womb (such as some foetuses lying in red wedges) to talk about gender issues in contemporary society. The form of the entire installation is combined with the Da Henggin Mountain 大横琴山 and Xiao Hengqin Mountain 小横琴山 visible behind the glass windows (two mountain ranges of Hengqin Island, in Zhuhai, Guangdong Province) visible behind the glass windows. Just as gravel collects in a mountain range, women form a "group". The red-flecked stainless steel flowers blooming on "Her-Mountain" represent the "blossoming" of spiritual consciousness. In the installation, there are also the three closed spheres resulting from the three feminist-themed performative works realized by Wu Xixia: "I" (Wo 我) a work that reconciles personal identity and gender; "Darling", a work on the fearless examination of women (of this work there is also a screen with the photo/video of the performance in progress); and "Writing the Orchid Pavilion Preface One time" (Shuxie Lanting Xu yi bian 书写兰亭序一遍) which attempts to break the traditional rules of Chinese calligraphy. The three independent spheres and the surrounding red mountains, that represent respectively the "individuals" and the "groups", complement each other, presenting a utopia in which the "self" (inner world) and the whole (external world) "bloom" in the process of awakening the female consciousness.\r
\r
Wu Xixia’s artistic practise strongly evokes that of Zhu Ming 朱冥 (b.1972), a key figure in the renowned East Village group and a central participant in the performance “Adding Another Meter to an Anonymous Mountain” (Wei wuming shan zenggao yi mi 为无名山增高一米, 1995). Zhu Ming, from 1994 to 2014, both in China and Western cities, created several performances by blowing into transparent plastic spheres and then entering them, experiencing suffocation; sometimes he also writes words and letters inside the spheres. Many of these performances are also performed in water. """@en ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/524.jpeg> ;
