        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8646> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/578.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Fung Ming Chip was born in Guangdong in 1951 and moved to Hong Kong in 1956 where he received only primary education before moving to New Jersey in 1977. His passion for seal carving started in New York in 1975, and this allowed him to broaden his horizons subsequently to calligraphy, studying as a self-taught. He moved to Taiwan in 1986 and finally in 2006 to Hong Kong, where he now lives and works; there he opened his art studio “Sibuguan 四 不管 Four no studio” in an area of the city called Sai Ying Pun.\r
Fung Ming Chip is a poet, a seal carver and a calligrapher.\r
His seal production can be divided into three phases: 1) carving stone seals on the basis of traditional models; 2) carving wooden boards; 3) creation of three-dimensional bronze sculptures. At a later time, Fung Ming Chip decided to study and practice calligraphy in order to discover everything that was hidden inside it, seeking its deep meaning and interpreting calligraphy as an artistic medium. In 1996 Fung Ming Chip began experimenting with calligraphy, creating over one hundred new calligraphic “script types” (xiefa 写法). These new “script types” have their own visual language and creative process, breaking with traditional concepts of calligraphy and allowing the viewer to observe calligraphy through a new perspective. The calligraphic production of Fung Ming Chip can be categorized according to three parameters: 1) the degree of legibility of the transcribed text, 2) the use of ink and 3) the spatial arrangement.\r
Among his many international exhibitions, Fung had a major solo retrospective at the Taipei Fine Arts Museum in 1999. He was artist in residence at Cambridge University, and his work is included in major private and institutional collections around the world, including The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York, White Rabbit Collection, Sydney and Israel Museum, Jerusalem. He has also been active as a painter, photographer, and sculptor. In 2008, he created Les Poemes du Mandarin tableware pattern for Hermes.\r
The calligraphic production of Fung Ming Chip can be categorized according to three parameters: 1) the degree of legibility of the transcribed text, 2) the use of ink and 3) the spatial arrangement.\r
1. Legibility: Fung Ming Chip creates artworks in which characters are completely, partially or not recognizable. In the first case, the artist does not detach himself from traditional calligraphy, he traces the characters in full; in the second case characters are partially traced, even if it is still possible to reconstruct them and/or extrapolate their meaning; in the third case the legibility of the characters is compromised, because they are not recognizable, it only remains signs of the characters, approaching a more abstract art.\r
2. The use of ink: by means of the dilution of ink, the artist creates new calligraphic “script types”. By using brush, water, ink and paper, he tries to experiment with different techniques with the aim of stimulating the viewer mind.\r
3. The spatial arrangements: Fung Ming Chip breaks down the setting traditional spatial arrangement, exploring new possibilities to expand or reshape the “space and time” elements present in his art.\r
These three categories are not used always separately, each of them creates new calligraphic “script types” that sometimes are used together in the same work. These continuous experiments demonstrate the intent of Fung Ming Chip to always go further, through his creativity and imagination."""@en ;
