    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/130.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "Gu Gan (1942, Changsha, Hunan Province - 2020), original name Zhang Shiqiang 张世强, was a prominent Chinese artist and calligrapher known for his pioneering contributions to modern calligraphy. He began his artistic training under Ye Qianyu, Huang Miaozi, and Zhang Zhengyu at the Central Academy of Fine Arts, where he studied until 1962. Influenced by Western artists like Kandinsky, Klee, and Miró, he sought to modernize Chinese calligraphy by experimenting with character forms, scripts, and the use of colored inks. In 1985, he organized the Modernist Calligraphy Exhibition and became the chairman of the Society of Modern Calligraphy. His innovative approach is described in his book,  \"Three Steps of Modern Calligraphy\" (1990). During the 1990s, Gu Gan incorporated Buddhist themes into his work, inspired by Dunhuang's ancient monastery art. Internationally recognized in 1996, he was the first Chinese artist to design the label for Château Mouton Rothschild’s grand cru. Within China, he continued promoting modern calligraphy, organizing the exhibition \"Bashu Parade: '99 Chengdu Retrospective of Chinese Modern Calligraphy at the End of the Twentieth Century\" in 1999. Over two decades, Gu Gan’s art evolved to include non-traditional materials and techniques, solidifying his legacy as a transformative figure in modern Chinese calligraphy."@en ;
