    ns2:P812 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11190> ;
    ns2:P937 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q8686> ;
    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/160.jpg> ;
    ns1:description "Wu Wei was born in Ezhou (Hubei Province) in July 1963. He earned a master’s degree in medicine from Tongji Medical University and currently lives in Shenzhen. Since 1993, Wu Wei has been studying the changes and transformations related to contemporary calligraphy. Wu Wei argues that since calligraphy has turned towards modernism, its subject (Chinese characters) has been denied as a subject and has acquired a new connotation. As a result, the ontological system of Chinese ideographic writing has collapsed and, along with it, that solid bond that unites the dots and strokes of the brush to the overall compositional structure and the chosen writing style in a calligraphic work has also collapsed. Chinese characters have therefore been reduced to pure “signs”, becoming simply “calligraphic material”.  The artist’s intent is to reflect on certain cultural issues related to man and his role in Chinese society. To do so, Wu Wei uses calligraphy, the traditional art par excellence. His works are largely “conceptual” in nature, focusing, until the middle 2000s, on performances that completely go beyond calligraphic methodology in the traditional sense." ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Self-Taught"@en ;
    ns1:image <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/160.jpg> .

