    ns2:P1476 "Sounds of Ink: Luo Qi and 30 Years of Calligraphyism (水墨的声音：洛齐与三十年的书法主义)"@en,
        "INKiostro di voci: Luo Qi e 30 anni di calligrafismo"@it,
        "水墨的声音：洛齐与三十年的书法主义"@zh,
        "Shuǐmò de shēngyīn: Luò Qí yǔ sānshí nián de shūfǎzhǔyì"@zh-Latn ;
    ns2:P1640 <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/594> ;
    ns2:P276 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1891>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3330623> ;
    ns2:P580 "2023-10-12"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P582 "2023-11-05"^^xsd:date ;
    ns2:P664 <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q131262>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3330623> ;
    ns2:P856 <https://www.museibologna.it/musica/rassegne/inkiostro-di-voci-27/> ;
    ns1:description """The exhibition “INKiostro di voci: Luo Qi e 30 anni di calligrafismo/Sounds of Ink: Luo Qi and 30 Years of Calligraphyism”(Shuimo de shengyin: Luo Qi yu sanshi nian de shufazhuyi 水墨的声音：洛齐与三十年的书法主义—— International Museum and Library of Music in Bologna, October 12 November 5, 2023), is dedicated to one of the most important living Chinese calligraphers, Luo Qi, and it celebrates 30 years of activity of one of the main currents of contemporary experimental calligraphy of which Luo Qi himself is the founder, called ‘calligraphyism’. This movement attempts to create a universally accessible asemic language based on calligraphic reflection. \r
The exhibited works all stem from the interaction between calligraphy and music, a key theme in Luo Qi’s art, often described as “music of written signs” or “music of the line”. These works occupy not only the exhibition area on the ground floor, but also the museum rooms on the main floor, the inner courtyard of the Sanguinetti palace and its monumental staircase, so as to create a true visual and auditory dialogue within the resonant cultural space that surrounds them, between the art of calligraphy and music, and between East and West.\r
In this exhibition, the artist presents 3 series: “Ode of Eight Thousand Verses” (Baqian songge 八千颂歌, 2017), “Writing Music – Silent Melodies” (Xie yue – Wu sheng zhi ge写乐·无声之, 2018-2013), and “Sound Images” (Shengyin de tuxiang 声音的图像, 2023). In the first series, inspired by one important sutra, Luo Qi combines Chinese and Sanskrit writings, thus creating visually the rhythm and the sound of the Buddhist litanies. In the second series, the melodies of famous Italian operas were translated as calligraphic signs inspired by the most ancient system for Chinese musical notation, the “Dunhuang musical score” (Dunhuang qupu 敦煌曲谱). In the third series, the artist reshapes antique Chinese pictograms creating strongly rhythmical visual plots.\r
The exhibition also includes a series of texts documenting the active engagement of calligraphyism, and two long vertical scrolls made by the calligraphers Luo Qi and Silvio Ferragina during the multimedia musicalligraphy performance “Sound MAP” that took place at the exhibition’s opening ceremony (October 12, 2023).\r
The series of events associated with the exhibitions embraces the international conference “Chinese Calligraphy and Music: Meeting of Voices” (October 13, 2023), book presentations, calligraphic workshops, guided tours, and the musicalligraphy performance “Inky Strings: Old Friends from Mountains and Rivers” by Echo Morgan and Zhang Yaqin (October 29, 2023).  \r
""" ;
