    ns1:description """Song Dong (Beijing, 1966) is one of the most well-known Chinese artists on the international scene. He was introduced to painting and calligraphy at a young age, which ultimately led him to study painting. In 1989, in fact, he graduated from the Fine Arts Department of Capital Normal University in Beijing . He was acutely aware of the 85 New Wave movement, which proposed a radical turn toward expressive and experimental innovations. Starting with oil painting, he then turned to avant garde and experimental art forms including performance and video. In his artworks, he explores themes of memory, self-expression, impermanence, and the transience of human endeavors. His projects are often composed with quotidian objects and ephemera, proposing a destabilization of material hierarchies in relationship to personal and global themes.\r
Among the performers, Song Dong is one of the firsts to mainly use calligraphic practices in his work. However, he replaces ink with water, an ephemeral and volatile substance that, vaporizing, clearly highlights the transient transitoriness of existence. There are three main series/works in which Song Dong narrates the impermanence of calligraphy and life through the use of water: “Writing Diary with Water / Water Diary Series” (Shui xie riji 水写日记系列, 1995 – 2012); “Writing Time with Water Series” (Shui xie shijian yi ge duo xiaoshi 水写时间一个多小时系列, 1995-2010) and “Stamping the Water / Printing on Water” ( Yin shui 印水, 1996). \r
Song Dong's works are collected by major museums and art institutions such as the Museum of Modern Art in New York, the Metropolitan Museum of Art in the United States, the Guggenheim Museum in New York, the Ullens Art Foundation in Belgium, and the M+ Visual Art Hong Kong Museum.  """@en ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Present (not specified)"@en ;
