        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1975> ;
    ns1:description """The “digital performance” of music and calligraphy entitled “Heaven, Earth, Mankind and Unity” (Tian di ren he天地人合) was realized on September 2, 2021 by the TENG company and recorded in the concert hall of the Singapore Chinese Culture Centre. The term “digital performance” for the TENG company refers to a performance not held in front of an audience, but created specifically to be recorded and reconstructed later, resulting in a digital video that represents the real work. During the recorded performance, the music played with different traditional Chinese and Western instruments was accompanied by calligraphic movements of the esteemed female Chinese calligrapher Grace Chen Liang.  From the recorded video, four different videos were extracted and created in post-production, each lasting five minutes. Together, they formed a single digital video with a total duration of twenty minutes that represents the real digital performance.\r
\r
The four videos that composed the digital performance, are designed to be watched one after the other, in a sequence consisting of four moments: “Heaven” (Tian天) , “Earth” (Di 地) , “Mankind” (Ren人)  and  “Unity” (He合). The music played during the performance, is composed by Chow Junyi 赵俊毅 (b. 1987).  \r
- Movement 1. “Heaven” (Tian天) – For Voice, Sheng, Pipa, Guzheng, Erhu, Cello, Guitar & Electronics. This movement is based on one of the most influential Tang poems entitled “Spring Blossoms on a Moonlit Night” (Chunjiang hua yueye 春江花月夜) composed by the Chinese poet Zhang Ruoxu 张若虚 (c. 660-c. 720).    Eight members of the company perform on stage, with mixed western and traditional Chinese instruments,   while the calligrapher Grace Chen Liang accompanies their notes and melodies with calligraphic movements. The characters written by Chen Liang are an excerpt from the original poem, “Yue zhao hua lin jie si xian 月照花林皆似霰” (“The Moonlight shines upon dewy petals, glimmering like white pearls”) which illustrates the view of a curving stream within the meadow and moonshine illuminating the grass like a layer of snow – emphasising the beauty and harmony that exists between heaven and earth.   This composition also plays with the concept of Yun 韵 (rhyme/musical sound), an aesthetic fluidity that is often found in traditional Chinese art.   Through three Guzheng solos throughout the piece, where an abundance of freedom is given to the musician to direct the course and flow of the music, Yun is personified. These sections act as a juxtaposition to meter-oriented parts in the piece, creating contrasting sections of tension and release throughout the work, almost signifying a floating cosmos that has neither defined shape nor form. \r
- Movement 2. “Earth” (Di 地) – For Guzheng, Cello, Guitar & Electronic. This movement is based on the traditional Chinese folk tune, “Intimate Guest” (zhi xin ke知心客) which is used in the Erhu solo, “Spring Scenery of Jiangnan” (Jiang nan chun se 江南春色). Describing the beauty of the lands south of the Yangtze river, this melody was also used in the Zhou Xuan 周璇 classic “The Wandering Songstress” (Tian ya ge nü天涯歌女).  The composition features two unlikely western instruments (guitar and cello), as leads in this arrangement which speaks of the sweetness of what nature has to offer. The piece features a melodic structure that evokes the feeling of meeting a kindred spirit or loved one. Inspired by the melody, Chen Liang birthed the words “The Heart Dances and Leaps” (xin feiwu 心飞舞) to capture the feeling of one’s heart leaping out in joy. Chen Liang shares that these words can be considered a contemporary expression, and she believes that these simple words give a sense of grounding and relevance today. Although the words may appear to not have roots in traditional text, its structure was actually inspired by Han Dynasty’s Liu Bang’s poem “Song of the Great Wind” (Dafeng ge大风歌).  As Liu Ban uses the three words “The Clouds Rose on High” (yun feiyang云飞扬), Chen too, took liberties in creating a similar syntax with “The Heart Dances and Leaps” (xin feiwu心飞舞). Innovating from the structure of this poem, Chen offers her new interpretation and draws from the spirit of TENG’s musical ethos - the re-imagination of tradition.\r
Movement 3. “Mankind” (Ren人) – For Sheng, Erhu, Cello, Guitar, Keyboard & Electronics. The Erhu solo piece written by Hua Yanjun, commonly known as Ah Bing 阿炳 (1893 – 1950)   “Reflection of the Moon on Erquan Spring” (Er quan ying yue二泉映月) is the melodic inspiration for the third movement. Ah Bing became a blind and destitute busker in the later stages of his life and wrote the piece to illustrate his grief and bitterness due to the severe challenges that came in his life. The thematic material of the piece is split into three phrases: the first phrase is written to be soft and soothing; the second illustrating agitation and aggression, while the third and final phrase evokes feelings of agony and utter despair. TENG portrays its signature fusion style by incorporating a jazz style of Blues into the piece. In the West, Blues are sung laments and the combination of the musical styles, add to the fragility of mankind portrayed in TENG’s interpretation. The fluctuating movements in this piece are profound and philosophical which guides the listener to close their eyes and find the depiction of mountains, waters, skies, and earth within the music. Chen Liang creates the two characters jing guan 静观 (observe calmly/mindfulness) using her left hand.  The work presents the idea of guidance by “stillness” (jing 静) and “observation” (guan 观) through the hustle and bustle of mankind in modern day. Chen recalls the memories of the Erhu solo, “Reflection of the Moon on Erquan Spring”, to be one of her father’s favourite pieces when she was a child. The fluctuating movements in the piece are profound and philosophical. The listener naturally closes their eyes to find the depiction of mountains, waters, skies, and earth within the music. \r
Movement 4. Unity (He合) – For Voice, Sheng, Pipa, Guzheng, Erhu, Cello, Guitar & Electronics. This movement is an original composition written for the entire TENG Ensamble. This composition drew its inspiration from the legendary Chinese martial Pipa solo, “Ambush from Ten Sides” (Shi mian mai fu 十面埋伏), reproducing the “Wu” 武 or martial style gestures, recalling the Battle of Gaixia in 202 BC during which General Xiang Yu 項羽 (c. 232–202 BC ) was defeated by Liu Bang 劉邦 (256 – 195 BC).  The piece was based on the textural aggressiveness of Ambush and unifies the ensemble through the rhythmic, harmonic, and melodic elements of the music. The composer uses polyrhythms, constant meter changes, as well as the constant passing of melodic lines between different instruments to show the co-operation within the ensemble. These challenging characteristics make the piece technically demanding and virtuosic. The effect from the electronics enhances the intensity of the music and showcases a fusion of both traditional and contemporary elements. To Chen Liang, this piece captures the unification of both the musical and calligraphic worlds. The energetic and tenacious nature thus inspired Chen Liang to brush the words “Endlessness” (Shengsheng bu xi 生生不息). At first glance, “Ambush” can be interpreted as a state in war, but from a broader perspective, it can also be the representation of the never-ending cycle of collisions between the old and the new. The cycle of growth and development and reincarnation aligns with the philosophy of Laozi’s Dao De Jing: “Yisheng er, er sheng san, san sheng wanwu 一生二， 二生三， 三生万物” (One begets Two (Yin and Yang), Two begets Three (Heaven, Earth and Mankind; or Yin, Yang and Qi), Three begets all things). It is with this ideology that the words “Sheng sheng bu xi 生生不息” (Endlessness) were befitting for this final movement.\r
It is interesting to note how the performance is made up of videos in post-production, themselves conceived as a performative concept. What the TENG company explains is that this video mode allows you to focus on more details, zoom in on a calligraphic detail or an overview of the artists that a live performance, according to the musicians, would not allow.  This involves, however, a temporal lag as regards the duration of the calligraphic performance (which is slower in the execution) while the musical performance seen in post-production is more faithful to the real time in which the performance was executed.  Also interesting are the cover arts, in which each movement has its own logo created through a Chinese character (corresponding to that movement) and the representation of a surrealistic playground with delicate steps and passageways coiling around a monolithic structure - a symbol of roots and tradition. An exquisite balance of opposites exemplifies the work that TENG does, depicting how it achieves equilibrium between its traditional and contemporary influences, the east and the west. The cover art of the performance is also created “in full” thus combining all four movements represented by the different four characters that make up the performance: tian 天, di 地, ren 人, he 合. As Samuel Wong explained:\r
"We wanted to show the interconnection between nature and man and between heaven and earth. The search for the “celestial” element passes through the “earthly” element: the building and the steps designed by man. The steps can be used both to go up and down, thus interconnecting the earth with heaven. This implies that we are all interconnected with each other, as part of humanity and nature. And here is the concept of final ‘unity’" (interview 2024).""" ;
