    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/741.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Pu Lieping was born in Chengdu, Sichuan Province, in 1959. His early life was deeply affected by the Cultural Revolution; at the age of eight, he witnessed his father being humiliated by the Red Guards. In 1980, Pu began private calligraphy studies with renowned calligrapher Bai Yunshu. A year later, at just twenty-two, he was recognized as one of the top ten calligraphers in the province.\r
Deeply inspired by historical masters Mi Fu and Huaisu, Pu sought to blend the richness of Chinese calligraphic tradition with a contemporary artistic language. Influenced by Gu Gan’s seminal book "The Formation of Modern Calligraphy" (1986), he explored ways to use calligraphy as a means of expressing cultural essence in a modern context. Over time, he moved away from readable characters, finding their form limiting to creative expression. In the 1980s, he developed the concept of "Visual Music," using distorted, expressive characters to evoke rhythm and movement akin to musical composition.\r
In 2000, he introduced a new technique based on calligraphic brushstrokes structured into dynamic compositions. Pu describes himself as a "Chinese character artist, critic, and designer." He is currently Director of both the "Research Centre for Chinese Character Ceramic Art" in Jingdezhen and the "Beida Peiwen Chinese Character Art Research Centre" in Beijing, the latter founded by him in 2017.\r
Pu Lieping curated the influential "Bashu Parade: 99 Retrospective Exhibition of Modern Chinese Calligraphy" in Chengdu (1999), and in 2002 participated in "Brushes with Surprise: The Art of Calligraphy in Modern China" at the British Museum. Together with Gu Gan, he introduced the concept of "Chinese Character Art" ("Hanzi Yishu"), which he has since promoted through education, exhibitions, and theoretical research. In 2004, he began teaching this concept at Peking University’s Resource College of Fine Arts and has worked to globalize it since 2009.\r
He is also the author of key texts such as "A Brief History of Modern Chinese Calligraphy to the Art of Chinese Characters (1985–2016)" and "Evaluation of Modern Calligraphy," and the founder of the journal "Art of Chinese Characters.\""""@en ;
    ns1:hasCalligraphicTraining "Academic"@en ;
