        <http://wendang-project.github.io/data/item/1817> ;
    ns1:description """Offering a unique and inspiring night at Sadler’s Wells in London on August 10, 2012, this concert, commissioned by the Chinese government to honour the Summer Olympic Games,  combines traditional Chinese calligraphy and modern ballet, featuring a live performance again from the Chinese calligrapher Li Binquan, accompanied by the New London Orchestra under the direction of Ronald Corp OBE,  and the Chantry Dance Company of London.  The performance is a one-night only show at Sadler’s Wells on the main stage. Here Chinese calligraphy is combined with modern dance, mixing a synergy of Eastern and Western culture. As calligraphy can be seen as visual interpretation of dance on paper, so the movements of a ballet dancer reflect the beauty and aesthetics of calligraphy.  The New London Orchestra played various pieces of well-known Western music, mixed with traditional Chinese ones (accompanied with the guqin).  Accompanied by these different melodies and compositions, Master Li Binquan realizes his calligraphies. To four of the pieces played by the orchestra, dancers from the Chantry Dance Company  performed contemporary dance choreographies inspired by the movements and lines of Master Li’s calligraphy.\r
The pieces that Chantry Dance performed in are: \r
1) “Die Libelle, Polka-Mazurka”, by Josef Strauss\r
2) “Soirées Musicales (Tarantella and Canzonetta)” by Benjamin Britten\r
3) “The Haunted Ballroom” by Geoffrey Toye\r
4) “On Hearing the First Cuckoo in Spring”, by Frederick Delius. \r
In the pieces from Soirees Musicales, the dancers’ movements represent the techniques employed by the calligrapher. The "Tarantella" choreography is inspired by the straight lines, flicking, and dabbing of the brush, and the "Canzonetta" choreography is inspired by the circular technique and the manipulation of the brush by the artist. The choreography for “On Hearing the First Cuckoo in Spring” represents the meditative/flow state the artist experiences when writing calligraphy. The choreography was partly inspired by the shapes of specific calligraphy characters, which influenced the type of movements created. Sequences of movement were then developed to accompany the music. In this process, characters from each of the poems they danced to were used as starting points for the movement, and the work was further developed in conjunction with the music. The Chantry Dance, on the other hand, only uses characters from the poems that Master Li was working with. He has his own unique style, based on the cao shu (cursive) style.\r
Chantry Dance had five dancers performing in this occasion: two British, Rae Piper and Louis McMiller; one Italian, Alessia Lugoboni; one Spanish, Melanie Lopez and one Portuguese, Catarina Carvalho. \r
Another dance company from Beijing, the Na’er Dance Company (NDC) (Na’er si xiandai wu tuan 娜尔斯现代舞团) under the choreographer Jiang Keyu 蒋可钰   also performed in other pieces of music. During one of these pieces, the Benjamin Britten’s “Midnight Sounds” (Suite), the dancers Na’er Dance Company used body language to express the emotionality of the melody, accompanying the Li Binquan’s calligraphic performance. Another time the Na’er Dance Company performed during the “Qin Song”. In this phase, a roll of paper is wrapped around the body of the dancer, who, moving, begins to unroll it, while the calligrapher begins to write on it. The interaction between calligrapher and musicians allows dancers to personally experience the beauty of musical calligraphy and the artistic charm of the integration of calligraphy, music, and dance.\r
During the event, Li Binquan wrote different texts such as:\r
1. The phrase “Yingguo Zhongguo youyi wangguchangqing 英国中国友谊万古长青” (Britain-China Friendship will Last Forever) together with the Professor Roger Mason (b.1941) : the first part “Yingguo Zhongguo youyi 英国中国友谊” (Britain-China Friendship) was written by Roger Mason, while the second part “wangguchangqing 万古长青” (will last forever) was written by Li Binquan.\r
2. The poem “Drinking” (Yinjiu 饮酒) by Tao Yuanming 陶渊明 (365 – 427),   a famous scholar in the Eastern Jing Dynasty.\r
3. The poem “Prelude to Water Melody” (Shui diao ge tou · mingyue jishi you 水调歌头 · 明月几时有), composed by the Song dynasty poet Su Shi (1036-1101), who wrote these lyrics while he was drunk and missing his younger brother (Su Zhe) on the Mid-Autumn Festival of the year Bingzhen (1076). \r
4. The poem “Drinking Along Under the Moon (Yue xia duzhuo 月下独酌) by Li Bai, about loneliness and companionship, and the way alcohol blurs the line between the two. \r
5. The poem “Qinyuan Spring · Snow” (Qinyuan Chun · Xue沁园春·雪) written during the “Die Libelle, Polka-Mazurka”, by Josef Strauss and composed by Mao Zedong during the Eastern Expedition. \r
6. Some excerpts from “A Tale of Two Cities” (Shuangchengji 双城记) by the English writer Charles Dickens (1812-1870) written in Chinese. One example is: “It was the best of times, it was the worst of times” (in Chinese “Na shi zui meihao de shidai, na shi zui zaogao de shidai 那是最美好的时代、那是最糟糕的时代”). \r
"""@en ;
