    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/585.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Echo Morgan is the English name of Xie Rong 谢蓉, a Chinese artist born in Chengdu in 1983 and living in London. She moved to UK when she was 19. Echo Morgan works with the stereotypes of “Chineseness” and femininity to revolutionize them. Echo Morgan studied at the High School of the Sichuan Fine Arts Institute before moving to London where she gained a BA in Graphic Design at Central Saint Martins and an MA in Fine Arts at the Royal College of Art. Her work has featured in a debut exhibition “Home家” at the Galerie Huit of Hong Kong, and a group show, Engender, at Alaska Projects, Sydney (both 2017). Her performance works have been seen in China, Sweden and the UK. In her work, she takes on the role of director, performer, narrator, and filmmaker. She collects and collages encounters, voices, texts, images, and objects from my culture roots in China and her daily life in UK. Through the mediums of performance and painting, she explores the intrinsic and complex relationship between violence, beauty, and vulnerability, re-examining how these antipodal constructs impact ideas of the “self” and the body. Her narrative is rooted with her family history, she explores her individual memory that is deeply embodied with China’s complex society – one that had undergone a series of philosophical, ideological and political transformations. She often invites viewers to participate, drawing strength from the audiences’ emotional vulnerability and feelings of uneasiness to complete the performance. Her emotions become entwined with the audiences’, creating a symbiotic relationship based on control and power. Juxtaposing English narration with Chinese traditional songs, she plays with her identity and difficult childhood. She explores the territory between gesture and stillness, between her past and present, between abstract mark making and personal story telling and between public space and intimate privacy. \r
In her performamces, Echo Morgan usually writes lyrics about her on her skin using red lipstick, black Chinese ink, jasmine tea-based white “ink”, and her own mother’s milk after giving birth to her second child. She played with chinoiserie tropes, painting her naked body to look like blue-and-white porcelain, and then inviting the audience to violently wash away her motifs by throwing water-filled balloons at her. She narrates her childhood, family, marriage, and motherhood experiences, as well as those of her ancestors.  In Echo Morgan’s works her own body, her hair, even her breast milk, become a language; she is writing a woman’s story of suffering with a subtext of strength and courage. She says: “I do really respect the power and strength of women, but I think in my work you see a lot of fragility. And that is how I feel, as a daughter, as a wife, as a mother”. \r
Xie Rong has presented performances in gallery spaces, museums and festivals in the UK, France, China, Sweden, Egypt, Germany, Switzerland Australia and Korea. In 2021, the Museum of Far Eastern Art (Israel) dedicated a solo-exhibition to her performance archive. """@en ;
