    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/249.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Qiu Zhijie 邱志杰 was born in Fujian province in 1969. In 1992, he graduated from the printmaking department at Zhejiang Academy of Fine Arts, Hangzhou. He now lives and works in Beijing and is currently a professor at the School of Inter-media Art of China Art Academy, the Director of Total Art Studio and member of the supervisor team in the Art and Social Thoughts Institute. \r
As an artist, Qiu Zhijie is known for his calligraphy and ink painting, photography, video, installation and performance works. His art is representational of a new kind of experimental communication between the Chinese literati tradition and contemporary art, social participation and the power of self-liberation of art.\r
His works are collected by major museums and institutions around the world, including the Guggenheim Museum in NYC , the Metropolitan Museum of Art, Asian Art Museum of San Francisco, Fondation Louis Vuitton, Foundation by Christian Dior, Ullens Foundation, Neuer Berliner Kunstrerein, and the White Rabbit Gallery in Sydney. He was awarded Artist of the Year of the Award of Art China in 2009.\r
He was also the curator of the first video art exhibition in China in 1996, and curated a series of Post-sense Sensibility exhibitions during 1999 and 2005 promoting the young generation of Chinese artists. In 2012 he was the chief curator of the 9th Shanghai Biennale “Reactivation”, in 2017 he is the chief curator of the Chinese Pavilion of the 57th Venice Biennale.\r
\r
Some of his most important calligraphic works include:\r
1) "Writing the 'Orchid Pavilion Preface' One Thousand Times" (1990-1995), where Qiu Zhijie copies Wang Xizhi's "Lanting xu" a thousand times on a single piece of rice paper, gradually blackening it through repetition. By focusing on the act of writing rather than the content, he explores calligraphy as a form of "written meditation" and critiques cultural orthodoxy and reproduction (see the archive).\r
2) "Ten Tang Poems" (2000-2001) where Qiu Zhijie writes ten Tang Dynasty poems in reverse, with the text and stroke order written from left to right and bottom to top. When the performance video is played backward, the poems are erased as they are written, transforming calligraphy into "anti-calligraphy" (see the archive);\r
3) "Dictionary Series" (2000), where Qiu Zhijie blends a conceptual approach with sophisticated painterly techniques, creating calligraphy that dissolves into the background and overlaps in a way that evokes natural landscapes or phenomena. The works feature characters selected from a dictionary, studied, and reinterpreted through ink painting, with a focus on the diagonal movement in the composition (see the archive);\r
4) "Left/Right: Deviation from the Centre" (2001) held at a museum in Oslo, where Qiu Zhijie is blinded and wears shoes with "right" and "left" engraved on the soles. He must navigate a narrow hallway marked with an ink line, where walking off course leaves shoe prints behind. The performance explores the concept of disorientation, symbolizing how contemporary individuals have lost their sense of direction and certainty (see the archive).\r
5) "Light-writing calligraphy series" (2005-2010) in which Qiu Zhijie reimagines calligraphy by blending traditional techniques with photography and performance. Using light painting, he preserves the performative aspects of calligraphy while introducing new media, site-specific elements, and a different temporality. The resulting works, captured through long exposure, are both calligraphic and photographic, engaging public space and modern distribution methods like social media, offering a more accessible and viral experience compared to traditional calligraphy.\r
6) "Confucianism, Daoism, Buddhism, Shintoism, Zen Buddhism Series" (2017), where the artist creates an archive of esoteric symbols and scriptures from various philosophical and religious traditions. Using a layering process, he carves different scripts into concrete, prints them by rubbing ink onto paper, then covers them with new layers, erasing the previous inscriptions (see the archive).""" ;
