    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/277.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Shao Yan is a Chinese calligrapher born in 1962 in Wendeng, Shandong Province (China). At the age of six, he started studying and practising traditional calligraphy under the aegis of his father with the intention of surpassing the ancient calligraphy masters. When he realised that he would not succeed through mere imitation of the great masters, he felt it was time to move away from tradition and embrace new opportunities and artistic ways to show his identity and uniqueness. He studied Fine Arts at Shandong Laiyang University in 1981 and at the Central Academy of Fine Arts in 1990. In 1991 he moved to Beijing where he currently lives. Recently, he holds many important positions including vice-President of the Calligraphy Research Institute of the Jiangsu Academy of Fine Arts and Researcher at the National Painting Academy of China. Shao Yan took part in numerous exhibitions and won several awards like the first prize in the National Exhibition of young and middle-aged calligraphers (Quanguo zhongqingnian shufa zhuankezhan 全国中青年书法篆刻展) both in 1995 and 1997.\r
Shao Yan's artistic production, that lies between tradition and modernity, can be divided in three different periods: in the first period (1985 - 1995) the artist executed works 'with few characters' inspired by modern Japanese calligraphy (see "Bright Snow", Qing xue 晴雪). Influenced by Western abstract painting, he also started breaking down characters until they become unreadable, but he 'pictorially' reproduced their meaning that the observer can grasp through visual perception (see "Surrounding", Wei 圍 / 围); in the second period (1995 - 2005) the artist continued to carry out artworks “with a few characters” (see "Army", Jun 军) but also experimented  with calligraphy “with several characters” (“Peach blossoms fall in a chaotic rain of red dust”, Taohua luanluo hongchengyu 桃花乱落红尘雨). In addition, he introduced the technique of ink “washed” in water (shui de "chongxi" 水的 "冲洗") (see “Sea”, Hai 海 and “Flying Good Luck”, Fei lai hao yun 飞来好运); finally, in the third period (2006 - ongoing), he has begun to use a syringe with a small ink reservoir or more syringes instead of the brush (see “The Thirty-Six Stratagems of using ink”, Mofa sanshiliuji 墨法三十六计 and “Chaos”, Hundun 混沌) and has also executed works that carry meanings related to important social issues.\r
In recent years, Shao Yan has staged several calligraphy performances using the syringe to squirt ink (see the performance “Ink shooting”, Shemo 射墨) and has begun to realize calligraphy on Chinese ceramics and porcelain, using different techniques, tools and styles (see “Watching the sea and Listening to the Waves”, Guan hai ting tao 观海听涛; “Best Wishes”, Ruyi 如意 and “Ceramic ink shooting", Shemo ciping  射墨瓷瓶).\r
"""@en ;
