    sch:thumbnailUrl <https://cloud.ditlab.it/public.php/dav/files/b49pfgPgPXNDFtc/thumbs/748.jpg> ;
    ns1:description """Wang Dongling, born in 1945, is a distinguished figure in contemporary Chinese calligraphy. He is Full Professor of Calligraphy at the China Academy of Art in Hangzhou and serves as the President of its Modern Calligraphy Research Center. Additionally, he is the Chairman of the Hangzhou Calligraphers Association (Hangzhou shi shufajia xiehui 杭州市书法家协会) and the President of the Lanting Calligraphy Society (Lanting shufa shehui 兰亭书法社会). \r
Wang Dongling's early education was in painting at the Teachers' College in Nanjing, the capital of his home province, Jiangsu. However, the Cultural Revolution led to the closure of the institution in 1966. During this period, he worked at the cultural and educational office, promoting mass education through art. \r
During the Cultural Revolution, Wang Dongling realized big-character posters. He considers this period crucial in his artistic development, as it provided a sense of artistic freedom and exposure to new ideas. After the Cultural Revolution, Wang studied under renowned calligraphers Lin Sanzhi, Lu Weizhao, and Sha Menghai, learning how to balance classical techniques with innovative expressions.\r
In 1981, Wang graduated from the first calligraphy graduate class at the China Academy of Art (formerly Zhejiang Academy of Fine Arts) with a Master of Arts degree and began teaching there. From 1989 to 1992, he taught Chinese calligraphy at the University of Minnesota and served as a visiting professor at both the University of Minnesota and the University of California, Santa Cruz. During his time in the United States, Wang's style became more experimental and international, influenced by Western  emphasis on composition, line, and color. He also began experimenting with mixed-media works and collages, integrating Western imagery with Chinese characters. He returned to the China Academy of Art in 1994.\r
Wang Dongling argues that calligraphy should evolve through a critical synthesis of tradition and contemporaneity, in an international context, while preserving its cultural essence. His project aims to redefine a calligraphic practice that blend traditional skills with modernist and avant-garde approaches, approaching a more performative and abstract art form. Major exhibitions in Hangzhou and Beijing in the late 1980s showcased his evolving style, including large-scale works such as "Confucianism, Daoism, and Buddhism" and "View from Heaven".\r
In the late 1990s, Wang developed his distinctive  "Chaos Script" (Luanshu 乱书). This style is characterized by powerful, dynamic brushwork that creates a sense of depth and movement. It has brought him international recognition and positioned him as a leading figure in modern and experimental calligraphy."""@en ;
